2 CONT Janet Milburn (Schultz) Nathaniel Foote "the Settler" is my 10th great grandfather.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life Sketch
2 CONT   •  
2 CONT Nathaniel Foote, the emigrant ancestor, born in England in 1593, came to New England with his wife Elizabeth born about 1595. Nathaniel died in Nov 1644. He married Elizabeth Deming born about 1595, and died 28 Jul 1683. ( She was
2 CONC  the sister of Mr. John Deming, also one of Weth's first settlers...and one of the patentees named in its Charter) whom he had married about 1514, in England. They had seven children, all, with, perhaps, the exception of the young
2 CONC est, born in England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr Foote... was one of the first ten men, known as "adventurers, " who absolutely first settled here... His wife was Elizabeth Deming, whom he married in England about 1615. He died in 1644, age about 51 years; his widow then marr
2 CONC ied about 1646, Mr. Thomas Welles, Magistrate, afterwards Governor of the Colony, whom also she survived, dying 28 July 1683, age about 88 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT A conspicuous feature in the history of the first generations of the Foote family, is the deaths, sufferings and captivities of its members and of those connected with them by marriage, at the hands of the Indians.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their children were:
2 CONT Nathaniel born about 1620, married Elizabeth Smith, who married second William Gull
2 CONT Robert born about 1627 married Sarah, she married second Aaron Blachley
2 CONT Elizabeth born about 1616 married Josiah Churchill
2 CONT Mary born about 1623 married first John Stoddard, second John Goodrich, third Lt. Thomas Tracy
2 CONT Frances born about 1629 married first John Dickenson, second Francis Barnard
2 CONT Sarah born about 1632 married Jeremiah Judson, he married second wid. Cath. Fairchild
2 CONT Rebecca born about 1634 married first Lt. Phillip Smith, second Aaron Cooke ("The history of ancient Wethersfield")
2 CONT
2 CONT _______________
2 CONT
2 CONT Below was written by Linda Mac.
2 CONT Born in Colchester, Essex, England to Jonathan & Elizabeth (Gilbert) Deming. First married Nathaniel Foote about 1615 in England.
2 CONT They had 7 children: Elizabeth Churchill, Nathaniel, Mary Stoddard Goodrich Tracy, Robert, Frances Dickinson Barnard, Sarah Judson, & Rebecca Smith COOKE.
2 CONT Married, second, about 1646 to Governor Thomas Welles of Connecticut as his second wife.
2 CONT Elizabeth may be sister of John Deming {1636, Wethersfield} 
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From footefamilycompr011907foot.pdf:
2 CONT FOOTE HISTORY AND GENEALOGY
2 CONT
2 CONT DESCENDANTS OF NATHANIEL FOOTE OF
2 CONT WETHERSFIELD, CT.
2 CONT 1. NATHANIEL FOOTE. The first settler. He was b. abt. 1593; m. in Eng-
2 CONT land abt. 1615, Elizabeth Deming, sister of John Deming, who was one of the first settlers of Wethersfield. She was b. abt, 1595. He d. abt. 1644. She m. abt.
2 CONT 1646, Thomas Welles. He was magistrate, afterwards Governor of the Colony.
2 CONT He d. . She d. July 28, 1683.
2 CONT I. NATHANIEL FOOTE, one of the first settlers of Wethersfield, Ct., belongs,
2 CONT not to that class of men who fill a large place in the world 's history, because called by
2 CONT some great emergency into positions of power and influence,—but to that more meri-
2 CONT torious class of pious and excellent persons, who, born to the great inheritance of
2 CONT labor, walk meekly along the paths of common life, perform every duty, public or
2 CONT private, love and help their fellow men, and act always as if in their Great Task
2 CONT Master's eye. It is to such men that society owes at once its peace, stability and
2 CONT progress, and yet history takes no note of such, and hence
2 CONT '
2 CONT ' The world knows nothing of its greatest men. '
2 CONT '
2 CONT His business in life was that of agriculture,—necessarily the leading pursuit
2 CONT of New England in its early history, when the forests were to be elled, the soil broken up, the seeds of all the grains, and plants and fruits which constitute the food
2 CONT of men and beasts to be sown, and its great staples of commercial exchange supplied.
2 CONT And in every period of society the agriculture population has proved of the highest
2 CONT importance to the wealth, dignity and strength of a State. It is from this class of
2 CONT the population that the city and the village, that commerce and the arts, are ever drawing the bone and muscle of their laborers, and much of the energy of their
2 CONT directing force. In no other of the leading pursuits in society are there the same
2 CONT facilities for cultivating bodily energy, and the force and vigor of mind conse- quent upon a vigorous constitution. The pure air, the rough exposure, the health-
2 CONT ful toil, the constant call for thought and reflection, the walking with God in the
2 CONT open field, the study of his laws as unfolded in the circuit of the seasons, and in
2 CONT the growth of the seed and ripening of the harvest, the better domestic training
2 CONT under which children can be reared in the country,—all these things are favorable
2 CONT for converting the agricultural population into an element of conservation, much
2 CONT needed to give stability to the ever restless desire of change which animates a young community, and to uphold society in moments of danger and trial. It is the boast of Connecticut, and of Wethersfield in particular, to have had 
2 CONC from the
2 CONT beginning a large population of intelligent, industrious and pious farmers in her
2 CONT population, and that the ranks of her merchants, her mechanics, her seamen and
2 CONT her professional men have been replenished by contributions drawn from this source. To this she undoubtedly owed her reputation for steady habits, and the
2 CONT domestic peace which has reigned so continuously in her borders. To this class of
2 CONT her population Mr. Foote belonged;—he was an intelligent, pious and industrious
2 CONT farmer, and, like all of that class of farmers, he was from time to time summoned
2 CONT to the discharge of public trusts by his neighbors and townsmen.
2 CONT From all that we can learn Mr. Foote came from Shalford, in Colchester, Eng.,
2 CONT and settled in Watertown, Mass. The first mention I find made of his name is in the Kecords of the Colony of Massachusetts Bay, in 1633, when he took the oath
2 CONT of freeman. In the "Eecords of the Grants and Possession of the Lands in
2 CONT Watertown," (in which town Mr. Foote first located himself) the following entry 
2 CONT is made:
2 CONT "1. An homstall of sixteen acres by estimation, bounded ye north and north-
2 CONT west wth ye highway, the south and southwest wth Jeremiah Norcross, granted
2 CONT to him.
2 CONT "2. Two acres of marsh by estimation, bounded ye south wth ye River, the
2 CONT north wth Henry Curtis, the east wth John Firmin, and the west wth John Smith,
2 CONT granted to him."
2 CONT Whether Mr. Foote was among the pioneers from Watertown, who made the
2 CONT first lodgment in, or before, 1635, on the banks of the Connecticut at Pyquag, is not known, but his name is found in its first Eecords, and among those to whom
2 CONT the first distribution of land was made; and he, therefore, must have shared all the
2 CONT dangers and privations of that long and toilsome journey through the wilderness
2 CONT in 1636, and have encountered all the horrors and trials of the first winter in
2 CONT their new home. And how difficult it is for us, in our comfortable dwellings, or traversing with every means and appliance of comfort, the distance between
2 CONT Wethersfield and Boston, in half as many hours as they consumed days, to realize
2 CONT the sufferings of that journey and of that first winter here! We never can be
2 CONT too thankful that courage and strength was meted out to them in proportion
2 CONT to their trials. For them, the trail of the Indian, too narrow for teams or herds,—for them, the unbridged stream and morass,—for them, the steep hill,—for
2 CONT them, the dangers from wild beasts, or from savage men, were not enough to cause them to turn back. It was not 'till winter had come down from the north
2 CONT to lock up the streams, 'till the fire in their temporary lodgments could not keep
2 CONT out the biting cold, 'till famine stared them in the face, that they turned again
2 CONT for food and shelter to the coast; and when spring returned, they were again on
2 CONT their way, with their thinned ranks recruited, to commence anew the work of
2 CONT settlement.
2 CONT In a few years we find them, with their brethren in Hartford and Windsor,
2 CONT laying the foundations of a Commonwealth in which they aim "to maintain and
2 CONT preserve the liberty and purity of the Gospel of our Lord Jesus," and "to be
2 CONT governed and guided by such laws, rules, orders and decrees as shall be made,
2 CONT ordained and declared" by the General Court, to be appointed by the freemen
2 CONT of the Commonwealth. To found a State under any circumstances has ever been
2 CONT counted among the great works of great men, but to found a State, in which
2 CONT the equal rights of all men are so well recognized and guarded, in the wilderness,
2 CONT amid the trials of frost and famine, and with daily, hourly, constant apprehension
2 CONT of assault and butchery from the savage, is no common event in the world's
2 CONT history, and should be ever remembered by those who have enjoyed its protection.
2 CONT In the original distribution of the lands of the town, as recorded in 1640, Mr,
2 CONT Foote had assigned him a house lot of ten acres on the east side of Broad street, near the south end of the street. A part of this lot is now owned and occupied
2 CONT by Mr. Josiah Adams. Mr. Foote became the owner of several other tracts of
2 CONT land, laying partly in the Great Meadow east of his house lot, and containing in
2 CONT the whole, upwards of four hundred acres. The cultivation of his land constituted
2 CONT his main business, although he was called by his neighbors to participate in the
2 CONT public trusts of the town, and in 1644 was appointed a delegate to the General
2 CONT Court.
2 CONT Mr. Foote married in England, about the year 1615, to Elizabeth Deming,
2 CONT sister of Mr. John Deming, who was one of the first settlers of Wethersfield, and
2 CONT for many years one of the magistrates of the Colony of Connecticut, and one of the patentees named in its charter. His children were all boru in England, except
2 CONT perhaps the youngest. Mr. Foote died in 1644, aged about 51 years, and was
2 CONT buried in the ancient burying ground in the rear of the Meeting House, where are gathered together the ashes of nine generations. He left behind him, surviving, a widow, two sons and five daughters. He left no will. At a Particula
2 CONC r Court
2 CONT held at Hartford, November 20, 1644, the following inventory of his property and
2 CONT distribution of his real estate were exhibited, and an order of court granting
2 CONT administration on his estate and directing a distribution to be made, was passed.
2 CONT An inventory of the goods and lands of Nathaniel Foote, of Wethersfield,
2 CONT deceased, being truly taken and indifferently prised by Eichard Tratte,
2 CONT Samuel Smith and Nath: Dickinson.
2 CONT Imprs his purse and apparrell 7 . 16 . 00
2 CONT It in neat cattell and in hay 93.00.00
2 CONT It in horsse fleshe 34 . 00 . 00
2 CONT It in hoggs 66.00.00
2 CONT It in debts 29.00.00
2 CONT It in Englishe corne 70.00.00
2 CONT It in goats 3.15.00
2 CONT It in carts, ploughs and the furniturr belonging theretoo 6.00.00
2 CONT It in nayles 1.10.00
2 CONT Ite in Indian corn 8 . 00 . 00
2 CONT It in old wheat and pease 6 . 06 . 00
2 CONT It for certen things in the chamber 2 . 00 . 00
2 CONT It ffor amunition 5.00.00
2 CONT Ite for fewer beds wth the furniture 13 . 06 . 08
2 CONT le in fyne Lynnen 5 . 10 . 00
2 CONT Ite 2 table boards, 2 chests, Trunke wth other Implets 5.00.00
2 CONT It pewter and brasse and other useful vessell 12.00.00
2 CONT It in husbandry tools 3 . 00 . 00
2 CONT It in beife butter and cheese and other necessary pvision for the howse 8.14.00
