2 CONT The age of the 5 children Dwelling wth their mother.
2 CONT Nath: ffoote 24 years
2 CONT Eob ffoote about 17 years
2 CONT Frances about 15 years
2 CONT Sarah about 12 years
2 CONT Eebecka about 10 years
2 CONT The wyddowe of the said Nath: ffoote is admitted to administer the Estate,
2 CONT and the eldest sonne is to have the lands before mentioned as they are valued at
2 CONT 1261. 10s. wch is to be made uppe 1481, and the youngest sonne the pticular landes
2 CONT above mentioned for him at 651. wch is to be made uppe 741. and the daughters dis- posed in niarriedge are to have 301. a peece wch they have receevede made uppe
2 CONT 741. and the other children are to have 741. a peece privded it is lefte at the dispose
2 CONT of their mother to detracte from any of them if shee see just cause 51. of the
2 CONT portion here sett downe, and to adde yt to such of the other as best desearve yt.
2 CONT "At a Particular court holden December 12, 1644. Present,—Edward Hop-
2 CONT kins, Esq., Governor; John Haynes, Esq., Deputy Governor, and the several Magis-
2 CONT trates.
2 CONT "Mr, Heynes and Mr. Willis are desired to consider of the estate of Nath:
2 CONT flfoote, deceased, and to take in what help they please from any of the neighbors
2 CONT to advise how yt may be Disposed of, and to report there apprehensions to the
2 CONT . next Court."
2 CONT This last vote is of many evidences which might be quoted from the Eecords
2 CONT to show the confidence reposed in the leading men of the Colony, and how natur-
2 CONT ally the people turn to such men for help when public business is to be done, or private affairs even are to be regulated.
2 CONT Mrs. Foote, the widow of Nathaniel Foote, was married about the year 1646,
2 CONT to "Mr. Thomas Welles, Magistrate," afterwards Governor of the Colony, whom
2 CONT she likewise survived.
2 CONT Governor Welles died Jan. 14, 1659-60, leaving a will. When in life, he gave
2 CONT a good estate to each of his children, except his son John, who settled in Stratford,
2 CONT Ct. Mr. John Welles died before his father, and in his will gave him his son Eobert, then a child, to whom Governor Welles, in his will, which is dated Novem-
2 CONT ber 7, 1659, gave the bulk of the large estate of which he died seized. Mrs. Welles died July 28, 1683, aged about 88 years. She left a will which was exhibited to and approved by the "Particular Court," August, 1683. The
2 CONT following is a copy of that Instrument:
2 CONT "I, Elizabeth Welles, of Wethersfield, in the county of Hartford, in the
2 CONT colony of Connecticut, Widdow, Being stricken in yeares & in expectation of my
2 CONT Solemn Change but of Good and perfect memorie blessed by allmighty God for
2 CONT the setleing of the Temporall estate God hath lent me, & that peace may be con- tinued amongst my relations when I am gathered to my Fathers, doe make, con-
2 CONT stitute & ordain & declare this to be my last will & Testament in Manor & form
2 CONT following, revoking & adnulling by these presents all former & other will or
2 CONT wills, Testament or Testaments by me heretofore made and declared by word or writeing & this to be taken onely for my last will & Testament & none other, &
2 CONT first I committ my soule to allmighty God my Saviour & redeemer in whome & by
2 CONT the merits of Jesus Christ I trust and believe to be saved & to have forgiven of
2 CONT my sins, & that my Soule with my body at the Generall day or resurection Shal
2 CONT be reunited againe, & through the meritts of Christ's death and passion possesse
2 CONT & Inheritt the kingdom of heaven prepared for the Elect, & my body to comely
2 CONT and Christian Buriall as my overseers shall see meet, & my estate I disspose as
2 CONT followeth. I will that all those debts I ow in right or consciens to any man or men be well and Truly contended & payd out of my estate in the first place. My
2 CONT fourteen acres of land in the great meadow & Thirty acres in the west field I give unto my son Eobert foote and to his heirs forever prohibiting him the sale of the same, he paying for these lands forty five pounds to be payed to 
2 CONC the children
2 CONT of my Daughter Sarah Judson deceased nine pounds, & to my foure daughters,
2 CONT viz. my daughter Churchall, my daughter Goodrich, my Daughter Barnard & my
2 CONT daughter Smith, to each of them Nine pownds a piece, I give unto my son Nathaniel
2 CONT foots Eldest son and his Brother eleven pownds, & to their children. To Daniel
2 CONT forty shillings, & to Elizabeth fewer pownds which legacies, bothe the eleven
2 CONT pownd forty shillings & fower pownds shall be payed out of The money Nathaniel
2 CONT Graves owes me By Bill, I give and bequeth unto my Grand Son John Studder
2 CONT halfe my Great lott which lyes at the farther Bownds of the Towne & the other
2 CONT halfe of the sayd lott I give unto my Grandsons Joseph & Benjamin Churchall &
2 CONT theire heirs forever. The remaynder of my estate (when a legacy is pd to my
2 CONT overseers out of it) shall be divided into five parts one part I give to my daughterJudsons children to be to them and their heirs for ever, & to my daughter Churchall
2 CONT & her children one fifth part, & to my Daughter Goodrich & her children one fift part, and to my Daughter Barnard & her children one fift part, and to my
2 CONT Daughter Smith & her children one fift part, it is my will that what I give my
2 CONT four<3 daughters shall be wholly at their disspose, to disspose among their children as they see Good, I do nominate & appoynt my welbeloved Captaine John Allin
2 CONT to be my Executor, & my beloved Brother, Mr. John Deming, senr, & my Grand
2 CONT Sonn Henry Beck to be the desired overseers of this my will, and as a token of my
2 CONT respect to them I give them Thirty Shillings a piece out of my estate, & for the
2 CONT confirmation of the premises I have hereunto Set my hand this 28 day of March,
2 CONT 1678. memorandum it is my will that the Nine pounds a peice I give my foure
2 CONT daughters & the fift part of my estate I give them shall be divided among the
2 CONT children of each of them the one halfe of it imediatly after my deccasse.
2 CONT Elizabeth Welles
2 CONT E. W. L. S. her mark
2 CONT This signed and declared to be the last will and Testament of Mrs. Elizabeth
2 CONT Welles In presence of us: Joseph Eowlandson.
2 CONT John Deminge.
2 CONT Memorandum: I give unto my grandson Nath: ffoott: the eldest son of my
2 CONT sonn Nathll; the one half of my fourteen acres of Medow & one half of my thirty acres of upland lying in the West field; wth liberty of takeing the first choice, he
2 CONT paying one half of the Legacyes wch were to be pd by my sonn Kobt had he lived
2 CONT to possess ye sd land.
2 CONT my will is that that part of ye eleven pownds (wch I formerly will'd to my
2 CONT sd grandson Nathll & his Brothr,) wch belonged to him by will, shall be equally
2 CONT distributed between my foure daughters above mentioned, and for the memorandum
2 CONT all rents of Land due to me, I will to be divided equally amongst my foure fore- mentioned daughters & their heirs;
2 CONT Elizabeth Welles
2 CONT her mark
2 CONT Witnessed by us Samll Tallcott
2 CONT John Deminge
