2 CONT From *P* member Barbara Lange GGCW13A Jan 92 "From the Torrey book: New England Marriages Prior to 1700... Foote, Nathaniel (1593-1644) m Eliz. Deming (1595-1683), in England, bef 1616, resided at Watertown.  She m/2 Thomas Welles
2 CONC .  Nathaniel Foote, s/o Robert and Joane (Brooke) Foote was b in Eng in 1593.  He's listed as being of Shalford Parish, Essix and St. Mary Bothaw parish, London.  He was m about 1615 to Elizabeth Deming.  She was the d/o John of S
2 CONC halford Colchester, England and was b there ca 1595.  John Deming is reported to have been connected with the hugenots, as desc's of this man are members of the "Federation of Hugenot Society", also dec's of this John Deming are s
2 CONC aid to be listed in Wurts; "Magna Charta" books.  Nathaniel and Eliz. Foote came to New England at a very early data.  The 1st record of him is at Watertown, MA where his name is mentioned in the "Records of the Colony of Mass.," 
2 CONC May, 1633, when he took the "Oath of Freeman."
2 CONT From "The Macomber Family", Harold Haze Macomber, Ventura, CA 1981 ... p 9 ... "on 21 Sep 1608/9 he apprenticed himself for 8y to Samuel CROYEYE, a Colchester grocer.  Nathaniel was a grocer himself, as of 18 Oct 1619, when he bou
2 CONC ght a messuage from Beatrice Barker, Esq., located on East Street, St. James Parish, Colchester.  In 1630 the family was living in St. Mary Bothaw Parish, from whence they immigrated to New England on board the FORTUNE.  They were
2 CONC  in Watertown, MA by 03 Sep 1633, when Nathaniel took the freeman's oath and owned a homestead of 16 acres with a 2 acre salt marsh....in 1635 the family removed to Wethersfield, CT where Nathaniel was one of the ten "adventurers"
2 CONC  who bought the land directly from the Wongunk Indians, an area located along the CT River known as Pyguaq, later named Wethersfield.  Early Wethersfield had only one street, Broad Street, later called Front Street.... Nathaniel w
2 CONC as primarily a farmer, but also served as Deputy to the Generral Court 1641-44.
1 NOTE Born September 21, 1592 in Shalford Colchester Esses, England. He wasthe son of Robert Foote of Shalford, County of Essex, A Yoeman andgrandson of John Foote of Royston. Royston is located near ColchesterEngland. Nathaniel s mothe
2 CONC r's name was Joan (Brooks). She was thedaughter of John and Elizabeth Brooks of London. He resided inShalford Parish, county Essex and St Mary Bothaw Parish, London.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: A Short time after he finished his apprenticeship training,he was married to Elizabeth Deming (In January of the year 1616) inColchester, Essex, England. She was the sister of John Deeming, whowas one of the first settle
2 CONC rs of Wethersfield Conn. in the year of1615. John Deeming was for many years the one of the magistrates ofthe "Colony of the Connecticut" and one of the Patenees named in it'scharter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathaniel's Apprenticeship
2 CONT Nathaniel Foote The Settler resided in Shalford Parish, county Essexand St Mary Bothaw Parish, London. at the age of sixteen wasapprenticed on 21 September, 1608 to Samuel Croyle of Colchester, a"Grocer" and "Free Burgess", for a 
2 CONC period of 8 years or until his 24thbirthday in the year 1616. when he brought a message from BeatriceBarker, Esq., located on East Street, St James Parish, Colchester. Hewas a grocer himself as of 18 Oct 1619.
2 CONT
2 CONT A short time after he finished his apprenticeship training, he wasmarried to Elizabeth Deeming (In January of the year 1616) inColchester, Essex, England. She was the sister of John Deeming, whowas one of the first settlers of Wet
2 CONC hersfield Conn. in the year of1615. John Deeming was for many years the one of the magistrates ofthe "Colony of the Connecticut" and one of the Patenees named in it'scharter.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Movement to America
2 CONT Sometime before the year 1633 the family moved from London England toBoston, Massachusetts. He settled first, upon arrival in the NewWorld, at Watertown, Mass.; where he took the freeman's oath 3 Sep1633. According to the records 
2 CONC of "The Original Distribution of LandsAround Wethersfield" recorded in 1640, a short time after arriving inWethersfield in 1635, Nathaniel received a ten acre house lot on theeast side of Broad Street. This land was near the south
2 CONC  end of thestreet. This land was purchased directly from the Wongunk Indians at aplace located along the Connecticut River that was called Pyguaq,later renamed Wethersfield. Additionally, he became the owner ofseveral other tracts
2 CONC  laying in part in the great meadow east of hishouse and containing close to 400 acres of land. In 1641, he wasappointed a delegate to the General Court. He was a juror in 1643 and1644.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Part of his land is now a public park at the foot of BroadStreet.
1 NOTE Ancestral File Number:<AFN> HJPG-4D
1 NOTE There is much in print on Nathaniel Foote and his family; see the references in TAG, January 1997, which also has a tree showing many of the relationships.
2 CONT Came from Shalford, Essex, to Watertown; removed to Wethersfield 1635. Freeman 3 Sept 1634. Deputy for Wethersfield to the Conn. General Court in 1641 and later.. He seems to have died intestate; his inventory, taken 24 Nov 1644, 
2 CONC came to £380 17s, not including real estate.
1 NOTE Age 16 and a apprentice to Samuel CROYLE, grocer of Colchester, on Sep. 21, 1608.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathaniel arrived at Watertown, MA in 1630, and was a freeman there in 1634.
2 CONT
2 CONT He removed to settle at Wethersfield, CT in 1636.
2 CONT
2 CONT Married about 1615, England.
