1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://homepages.rpi.edu/~holmes/Hobbies/Genealogy2/ps28/ps28_225.htm
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/28849487/elizabeth-foote_welles
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Elizabeth Deming 
2 CONT *Birth:  1600 Colchester, England 
2 CONT *Death:  Jul. 28, 1683 Wethersfield, Hartford County, Connecticut, USA 
2 CONT *Seen as Born in Colchester, Essex, England to  https://www.geni.com/people/Jonathan-Deming/6000000002447173197?through=6000000010621722232 & https://www.geni.com/people/Elizabeth-Deming/6000000004993949138?through=600000001062172
2 CONC 2232 (Parents unproven). 
2 CONT *Probably the sister of https://www.geni.com/people/John-Deming/6000000002930827132?through=6000000010621722232
2 CONT
2 CONT First married Nathaniel Foote about 1615 in England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They had 7 children: Elizabeth Churchill, Nathaniel, Mary Stoddard Goodrich Tracy, Robert, Frances Dickinson Barnard, Sarah Judson, & Rebecca Smith COOKE.
2 CONT
2 CONT Married, second, about 1646 to Governor Thomas Welles of Connecticut as his second wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth may be sister of John Deming {1636, Wethersfield} (bio by: Linda Mac) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Family links: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouses:
2 CONT *Nathaniel Foote (1593 - 1644)
2 CONT *  Thomas Welles (1590 - 1659)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Children:
2 CONT *   Elizabeth Foote Churchill (1616 - 1700)*
2 CONT *   Nathaniel Foote (1620 - 1655)*
2 CONT *   Mary Foote Tracy (1623 - 1685)*
2 CONT *   Sarah Foote Judson (1632 - 1673)*
2 CONT *   Rebecca Foote Smith Cooke (1634 - 1701)*
2 CONT
2 CONT *Calculated relationship
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: Wethersfield Village Cemetery  Wethersfield Hartford County Connecticut, USA 
2 CONT
2 CONT Created by: Ryan Curtis Record added: Aug 07, 2008  Find A Grave Memorial# 28849487 
2 CONT
2 CONT __________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Family of Jonathan Deming (411) & Elizabeth Gilbert
2 CONT
2 CONT 1108. Elizabeth Deming. Born in Oct 1595 in Shalford, Essex, England. Elizabeth died between 16 Aug 1682 and 3 Sep 1683 in Wethersfield, CT.4
2 CONT
2 CONT That Elizabeth “was a woman of character and a good wife, is evidenced by the fact that her first husband (Foote) dying intestate, she was by the Particular Court to whom the inventory of his estate was presented, ‘admitted to adm
2 CONC inister the estate;’ and by the will of her second husband (Welles) ‘she was to enjoy and improve’ his whole estate, so long as she remained a widow, ... ‘that she may keep the better hospitality.’”167
2 CONT
2 CONT In Jan 1615 Elizabeth first married Nathaniel Foote (1221) , son of Robert Foote (430) (ca 1553-Feb 1608/9) & Joane Brooke (1555-aft 1609), in Colchester, Essex, Eng. Born on 21 Sep 1592 in Colchester, Essex, Eng. Nathaniel died i
2 CONC n 1644 in Wethersfield, CT.12
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathaniel was admitted freeman 3 Sep 1634, a proprietor of Watertown in 1642. Previous to this date, he went to Wethersfield, CT, where he was deputy 1641, and juror 1743-1644.11
2 CONT
2 CONT This most reputable Conn. family, commenced its American history in Weth., yet, owing to an apparently inconquerable migratory tendency in its earlier representatives, the name had entirely disappeared form the town by the end of 
2 CONC the third generation. Through its various early intermarriages with other Weth. families, however, the history of these early generations possess Colchester, Conn., and Hadley, Hatfield, Deerfield and other old towns in Western Ma
2 CONC ss. The family has also been fortunate in having had its history wirtten by a competent had, half a century ago. [The Foote Family, by Nathaniel Goodiwn, 1849.]
2 CONT
2 CONT “A conspicuous feature in the history of the first generations of the Foote family, is the deaths, sufferings and captivities of its members, and of those connected with them by marriage, at the hands of the Indians.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is by no means certain that Mr. Foote, as some have asserted, was the first settler at Weth., but it is probably true that he was one of the first ten men, known as ‘adventurers,’ who absolutely first settled here; and that he 
2 CONC was the largest holder of so-called ‘Adventurer’s lands.’ In the original lay-out of the town, 1640, he received a home-lot of ten acres, at South End of Broad St., East side, and gradually became the owner of other pieces of ld.,
2 CONC  partly in the Great Meadow, east of his home-lot, amounting in all to over 400 acres. ... In 1641-2-4, he represented the town in the General Court, an evidence of the respect and confidence in which he seems to have been held by
2 CONC  his fellow-townsmen. In May 1637, when the little army under Capt. John Mason was being provisioned for the memorable Pequot campaign, it was ‘ordered yt that there shalbe 1 hogg prvided att Wythersfeild for the design in hand, w
2 CONC eh. [i.e. the hogg, not the expedition] is conceived to be Nathaniell Footes’—a compliment, certainly form the Col. authorities, to Mr. Foote’s ability in raising good pork!”167
2 CONT
2 CONT When Nathaniel was 15 he was an apprentice to Samuel Croyle, grocer of Colchester. He arrived at Watertown, MA, in 1630, a freeman there in 1634. He removed to Wethersfield, CT, in 1636.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathanield brought from England his wife Elizabeth and children Nathaniel, Elizabeth, Mary, Robert, Frances, and Sarah; had REbecca, born probably at Watertown. Freeman 3 Sep 1634, then removed to Wethersfield 1636, where he was r
2 CONC ep. 1641-44.2
