2 CONT Second Marrage:
2 CONT    GOV Thomas Wells In 1646, Wetherfield, Hartford,Connecticut 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeths Will:
2 CONT    Will Elizabeth Deming
2 CONT
2 CONT The Deming Family Very little is known of the Deming family before they left England. Since the first Puritans left England to secure a better place to practice their religion. It might be assumed that the Demings left England for
2 CONC  similar reasons. 
2 CONT
2 CONT That they held strong religious convictions is evident in the records they left in Connecticut. Elizabeth was born in England in the last part of the 16th century. In January of the year 1616, a short time after he finished his ap
2 CONC prenticeship training, she married Nathaniel Foote in Colchester, Essex, England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the birth of their sixth child Nathaniel decided to sell his grocer business in Colchester and immigrate to the New World. According to the "Wethersfield Historical Society," Nathaniel Foote is considered to be one of the fi
2 CONC rst settler of Wethersfield. 
2 CONT
2 CONT We do know he was one of ten men who settled along the bank of the Connecticut River and named their settlement, Wethersfield. They are know as the "Ten Adventurers"
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth was the sister of John Deeming, who was one of the first settlers of Wethersfield Conn. in the year of 1630. John Deeming was for many years one of the magistrates of the " Colony of the Connecticut " and one of the pate
2 CONC ntees named in it's charter. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Since Elizabeth Deming married Nathaniel Foote who spent his early life in Shalford, Colchester, England, it can be assumed that 
2 CONT
2 CONT (1.) John and Elizabeth lived in the same area of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT (2) Elizabeth and Nathaniel were known to have been in the Colony of Massachusetts Bay and residing in Watertown when it is recorded Nathaniel took the oath of a freeman. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (3.) The Foote family must have joined with the Demings in feeling some dissatisfaction with the manner of life in Watertown and joined with others in making the 100 mile trek in 1635 through the forests of the New World until the
2 CONC y arrived at Pyquag on the western shore of the beautiful Connecticut River. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathaniel Foote was one of those named in the charter of patentees of Wethersfield. The Foote family became one of the leading families of the little Connecticut Colony. He became a magistrate, a leading land owner, eventually own
2 CONC ing more than 500 acres of land in Wethersfield, some of the great meadow, and his home on the south end of the green, next to the present Broad Street.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Nathaniel and Elizabeth Foote No.	Ancestor's Name	Parent	Birth	Death	Wife (22)	Elizabeth Foote	(10)	1616	1700	Josiah Churchill (23)	Nathaniel Foote	(10)	1618	1655	Elizabeth Smith (24)	Mary Foote	(10)	1623	1687	John Sto
2 CONC ddard (25)	Robert Foote	(10)	1627	1681	Sarah Potter (26)	Frances Foote	(10)	1629	1673	John Dickinson (27)	Sara Foote	(10)	1632	1672	Jeremiah Judson (28)	Rebecca Foote	(10)	1634	1701	Lt Phillip Smith
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth's Second Marriage The family was saddened by Nathaniel's death at age 52. Elizabeth was so respected that she was allowed to be executor of his estate. Elizabeth was left a wealthy widow, but did not remain in that statu
2 CONC s for long. In 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gov Thomas Wells - In 1646 Thomas married Elizabeth Foote, widow of Nathaniel Foote who died in Wethersfield in 1643, and sister of Joseph Deming of Wethersfield. She was unwilling to leave the homestead of many acres she was mana
2 CONC ging after her husband's death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As a result, one of the highest officers in the colony left his home in the center of Hartford and moved to Wethersfield with his younger children, Samuel and Sarah who were raised with her younger children Frances, Sarah, and Reb
2 CONC ecca.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas wrote his will on 7 Nov 1659. He seemed to be in good health on the evening of 14 Jan 1659/60, being well after supper, but dead by midnight. His will left his wife the use of half his housing and orchard, with her own land
2 CONC  to be returned to her. His own land and house went to his grandson Robert, the only child of his oldest son to live in Wethersfield. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He left land to sons Samuel and Thomas, and to Thomas son of the deceased son John, 20 pounds to Thomas, Samuel, Mary's children, Anne, Sarah, and 10 pounds to Mary Robbins' children. Elizabeth lived another 22 years, leaving her 
2 CONC estate to her children and grandchildren by Nathaniel Foote.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1646 she married Thomas Wells who was a widower with several children from his first marriage. Thomas Wells served as Governor of Connecticut Colony for two terms, 1655-1658. When he was not serving as governor he was a Deputy Gov
2 CONC ernor. He died during his last years of being deputy governor, 14 January 1659/1660. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth was again a widow, having two families instead of one. She was in control of a large estate from both husbands. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Welles was a tenacious and feisty old woman. She had not only survived a perilous voyage from England but while tending to six exuberant children and a husband, she had made a new life for herself and her family in a wor
2 CONC ld they knew nothing about. This world was inhabited by Indians who were not always friendly with those pale face people. The rigors of life and managing a household did not daunt her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Things went quite well through the intervening years since arriving on shores of the newly discovered continent, until she reached old age. In 1676 as she approched the age of 80 years, she ran into trouble with one of her step-gr
2 CONC and children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This was Robert Welles, a favorite of grandfather, Governor Thomas Welles when the governor was alive. Robert had arrived at the Governor's home, there to be taken care of and educated. 
2 CONT
2 CONT But now his grandfather was dead and Robert and his step-grandmother disagreed. Maybee she did not think him old enough to be married at age 24. Never-the-less it was 1676 when Elizabeth brought Robert Welles to court, because he 
2 CONC "...hath dammyfield her Barne by Parting with the other part of the Barne that did adjoin to it." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Exactly what he did to her barn is not clear. The court's decision was clear. He was ordered to repair the barn and also to pay his step-grandmother rent for it. Elizabeth made sure the barn incedent was not here last word. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Two years later, in 1678, she made sure all of the Welles were taken care of when she made her will. She left them nothing. She stated someone outside the family would be executor of her will. Everything she had she left to her ow
2 CONC n family. That is the family she and Nathaniel has raised and nurtured. The Welles family got nothing.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth died in 1683, at the age of 88. The estate was devided among the Footes. One of the documents in the Probates Court was that of the final disposition, that during that same year Robert Welles won a lawsuit against his st
2 CONC ep-grandmother's will that he would have to be paid by those who had been named in the will. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources http://www.langeonline.com/
2 CONT
