2 CONT SECOND GENERATION
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Peleg TRIPP was born about 1642 in Portsmouth, Newport, RI. He died on 13 Jan 1713/14. He was christened. He was buried. ! (Austin 208; Randall, James Tripp 5-7). 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married to Anne SISSON about 1665. Anne SISSON was born about 1647/48 in Dartmouth, Bristol, MA. She died on 13 Jan 1713/14. She was christened. ! She was dau of Richard and Mary (Freeman) Sisson. (Austin; Bristol County Pr
2 CONC obates 1:57,72; Carol Clark Johnson, "Hand, Sisson and Scott--More Yeomen Ancestors" [1981] 217-8).
2 CONT
2 CONT (http://www.trippgenealogy.org/TrippFamily/Bock/d429.html#P550 )
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE: Type: Periodical
2 CONT
2 CONT Title: The Genealogist
2 CONT
2 CONT Author: Neil D. Thompson(editor)
2 CONT
2 CONT Publication: Association for the Promotion of Scholarship in Genealogy, New York, New York
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: #7 & #8 - Had nine children. Lived at Dartmouth MA and Portsmouth RI. He was very active in town affairs: constable in 1667, surveyor of highways 1672-3, member of the Town Council in 1667-9 and 1683, and Deputy from Po
2 CONC rtsmouth to the General Assembly in 1680, 1681 and 1686. InJanuary 1678 he was a member of a committee to audit and adjudge all accountsbetween Newport and Portsmouth "relating and growing only by reason of the lateIndian wars". I
2 CONC n 1681 he helped work out the assessments; in 1684 he was Overseer of the Poor and a member of several commissions dealing directly with the Governor of Rhode Island. In 1687, during the Andros administration, he was on acommittee
2 CONC  to assess taxes on the real estate of Rochester, alias Kingstown. Heis called "Mr." and on occasion "Major" in the records.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: Copied from Internet - Herman William Tripp Remembering:
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: 1. Peleg TRIPP was born in 1642 in Portsmouth, Newport County, Rhode Island. He died on 13 Jan1714 in Portsmouth, Newport County, Rhode Island. He was christened. Peleg Tripp is my 7th Great Grandfather References: Rand
2 CONC all, Peleg manuscript from the New Bedford Library; Breffni Wheland, decendant who specializes on daughters andtheir offspring; Peleg Tripp was child number 2 of John Tripp the Founder. I donot know where this name came from, but 
2 CONC it was quite prevalent in the 16 and 17 hundreds.( I have since learned from my first cousin Frances Tripp Conyers that the name came from the bible. She has also contributed much information, particularly for editing. Peleg recei
2 CONC ved from his father, while John was still living, one quarter section of land that John had purchased from John Alden. PelegTripp married Anne Sisson and had 10 children, including Job who was my directancestor. Peleg was left onl
2 CONC y 5 pounds sterling in John's will because John had already deeded land to him prior to his death. Herman William Tripp--Remembering<nowiki>----</nowiki>Grandpa Peleg
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: The following is typed verbatim from Valentine Research Studio, of Washington D.C., written by Caroline Valentine, and published in 1932. Page 67. The year before the death (1677) of John Tripp the Founder, Peleg, the s
2 CONC econd son of John the Founder, appears on the town Council, at the age of 35. In the October meeting of this year, his brother, Joseph, waschosen "to bee of the jury for the Court of Tryalls". Peleg was at once placedon a committe
2 CONC e, and set at work for the Colony. In 1678, Peleg, Will Wodell andJacob Mott are on the town Council. At this time, Abiel, next younger, is admitted to be a Freeman of Portsmouth. The Tripp Boys are coming on. A special Town Meeti
2 CONC ng in December puts the town in touch with the General Assembly. The town has been "warned" to meet, "to here the Acts and Orders of the Court of Generall Assembly (held in October last) read, and also to make a rate of sixty eigh
2 CONC t pounds." "This, by the said court was ordered for this Towne of Portsmouth topay, as their part and share of A Colony Rate of three hundred pounds, etc."    
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of Peleg Tripp, dated 6 Nov 1713; proved 8 Feb 1714.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Executrix wife Anne.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Overseer, kinsman William Sanford.
2 CONT
2 CONT     To wife, dwelling house and a third of great orchard for life and my garden
2 CONT
2 CONT     and L18 yearly for life paid by son John.
2 CONT
2 CONT     To daughter Priscilla Tripp, L15.
2 CONT
2 CONT     To daughter Sarah Rogers, L15.
2 CONT
2 CONT     To son Job, rest of land where I dwell, he paying rents and legacies and at
2 CONT
2 CONT     death of wife he to have other land, great scales, weights and bed.
2 CONT
2 CONT     To son Peleg, 5s.
2 CONT
2 CONT     To daughter-in-law Sarah Tripp, 1s.
2 CONT
2 CONT     To daughter Mary Smith, 10s.
2 CONT
2 CONT     To daughter Anne Rogers, 10s., and land in Portsmouth.
2 CONT
2 CONT     To daughter Mehitable Thurston, 10s.
2 CONT
2 CONT     To daughter Priscilla Tripp, feather bed, &c.
2 CONT
2 CONT     To wife Anne, 14 acres.
2 CONT
2 CONT     To son Richard, land in Dartmouth, he paying his mother L 5, yearly.
2 CONT
2 CONT     To wife Anne, rest of moveable estate, money, cattle, sheep , swine, household
2 CONT
2 CONT     goods, &c.
2 CONT
