2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT == Biography == <ref>Profile created the import of GEDCOM's as noted in '''Acknowledgements'''; later merged.  Added information from Sharon, Massachusetts history researcher Dann-242| Nick Dann, 13 Jan 2019.</ref>
2 CONT '''From: ''Genealogical Record of the Hodges Family'', Pgs. 75 - 77:''':'''3 - Henry Hodges''' (2) [Wm. (1)], b. 1652, Taunton, Mass.; d. 1717 Sept. 30, Taunton, aged 65; m. 1674 Dec. 17, Taunton, Ester Gallop, dau. of John & Hann
2 CONC ah (Lake) Gallop, b. 1653 July 21, Taunton. :11 children born in Taunton: - ::'''9.''' i. Mary (4) Hodges, b. 1675/6 Feb. 3. ::'''10.''' ii. Esther (4) Hodges, b. 1677/8 Feb. 17. ::'''11.''' iii. William (4) Hodges, b. 1679/80 Mar
2 CONC ch 18, ::'''12.''' iv. Charity (4) Hodges, b. 1682 April 5. ::'''13.''' v. John (4) Hodges, b. 1684. ::'''14.''' vi. Henry (4) Hodges, b. 1685 or 1686. ::'''15.''' vii. Joseph (4) Hodges, b. 1688 or 1689. ::'''16.''' viii. Benjami
2 CONC n (4) Hodges, b. about 1691. ::'''17.''' ix. Ephraim (4) Hodges, b. about 1693. ::'''18.''' x. Elizabeth (4) Hodges. ::xi. Abigail (4) Hodges. :Henry (3) Hodges lived near the residence of his brother John. His house, it is stated
2 CONC  in the first edition of this genealogy, was "within a few yards of the place where a red school house stood in 1820. At the time there were some indications on the surface of the earth of the spot where the cellar had been." The 
2 CONC site of the red school house, on High street, opposite the Central Railroad Station, is now occupied bythe old Niagara Engine House, used at present as a ward room, and during the winter months, the Niagara Evening School is held 
2 CONC here. The exact location of Henry Hodge's house is difficult to fix. Henry Hodges certainly owned the ground here, and the city authorities have placed on the engine house a tablet inscribed "Homestead of Elder Henry Hodges, 1681 
2 CONC - 1717." From an examination of the ground, a consideration ofthe manner in which the roads were laid out in this vicinity, and thetestimony of persons (some now deceased) who have been well acquainted with the locality for many y
2 CONC ears, it seems probable that Elder Henry's house stood where the freight house of the railroad now stands, on the corner of Tremont and Oak streets. :Henry Hodges was a leading man in all the affairs of the settlement. He was Capt
2 CONC ain of the military company, held the highest town offices for very many years, and was a deacon and Presiding Elder of the Church, occupying, it is said, a seat in the pulpit with Rev. Samuel Danforth. He acquired a large amount 
2 CONC of real estate, and his farm of "Crooked Meadow," at Burt's Brook, adjoining the boundary line of Norton and Taunton, is often referred to in the records. He was administrator of s lsrge number of estates, and from his prominence 
2 CONC in the matter of allotment of lands, it would seem that he was a surveyor. He was on a coroner's jury at court held at Plymouth, Oct. 30, 1678, and on the Grand Jury, June 6, 1683. In 1681 he was appointed constable at Taunton. He
2 CONC  was selectman for 28 years (1687 to 1701, 1703 to 1709, 1711 to 17160, was  member of the General Court 5 years (1704, 1713, 1715, 1716, and 1717). His name appears, April 8, 1682, in the roster of the 3rd squadron of the militar
2 CONC y company, ordered to bring arms to church on Sundays. He was elected Ensign of the First Military Company in March, 1690, when the town was greatly excited over the question as to who should command the company. At some undetermi
2 CONC ned date prior to 1703, the Second Military Company was organized. He was the first captain of his company and retained command until 1714. He was a subscriber to the fund for the Canada Expedition of 1690 under Sir William Phipp.
2 CONC  When theNorth Precinct of Taunton was established, although he had been opposed to the separation, he donated land as an inducement for a minister to settle in the new parish. He was a shareholder in the first Tauntoniron works. 
2 CONC He was buried in the "Neck of Land Burying Ground," whereis still to be seen his gravestone inscribed "Here lies the body of Elder Henry Hodges, Died Sept. 30, 1717. Aged 65 years." :[Continues with biographies of Esther (Gallup) 
2 CONC Hodges' parents, John Gallop and Hannah (Lake) Gallop.]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Last Will and Testament''':Henry Hodges, Sr. of Taunton. Dated 17 Sep 1717. Presented 4 Nov 1717. Names sons William, John, Joseph, Henry, Benjamin and Ephraim (namedyoungest son). Beloved wife Esther, five daughters Mary Keith
2 CONC , EstherSouthworth, Charity Leonard, Elizabeth Hodges, Abigail Hodges. Son Ephraim named executor. Witnessed by Samuel Danforth, Jonathan Harvey and Samuel Danforth, Jr.<ref>Bristol County, MA: Probate File Papers, 1686-1880. Onli
2 CONC ne database. AmericanAncestors.org. New England Historic Genealogical Society, 2017. (From records supplied by the Massachusetts Supreme Judicial Court Archives. Digitized mages provided by FamilySearch.org). [https://www.american
2 CONC ancestors.org/DB2724/i/48300/13106-co9/68257188 Case 13106: Last Will and Testament]]</ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==*''Genealogical Record of the Hodges Family of New England, Ending December 31, 1894'', compiled by Almon D. Hodges, Jr., Third Edition, printed for the family by Frank H. Hodges, Boston, 1896. Henry Hodges is Record 
2 CONC #3, Taunton Branch, Pg. 75.<ref>[https://archive.org/details/genealogicalreco00hodg/page/74  Genealogical Record of the Hodges Family, Page 75.]</ref> *''Abridgement of the Genealogical Record of The Hodges Family of New England, 
2 CONC ending December 21, 1954'',  Compiled by George F. Hodges andother members of the Family, page 18, 19. Appears without publishing information, printed sometime after 20 Jan 1956, the last date in the book. (T. H. Bredehoft, privat
2 CONC e library collection) No. 43 of 200 printed.* ''New England Marriages Prior to 1700'', by Clarence Almon Torrey (Boston: New England Historic Genealogical Society, 2011), Vol. II, p. 769. HODGES, Henry (1652-1717) & Hester/Esther 
2 CONC ______ GALLOP (1653-); 17 Dec 1674; Taunton. 
2 CONT
