2 CONT == Disputed Origins ==
2 CONT :It has been suggested, without evidence, that Hannah was the daughterof [[Mead-433|John Mead]] and [[Love-190|Cisely Love]].:A 2004 ''TAG'' article identifies her maiden name as Langford.<ref name=Hatcher>Patricia Law Hatcher, "E
2 CONC nglish Origin of the Potter and Beecher Families of New Haven, Connecticut," in [https://www.americanancestors.org/DB283/i/13262/28/24793693 TAG, 79:28] (2004): starting on page 28</ref>: A 2006 "TAG" article explores the possibil
2 CONC ity of her origins. Her father remains unknown, but he may have had brothers William, Richard and ____ (father of Stephen).<ref>Hatcher, Patricia Law. "Langford Origins Redux." The American Genealogist [https://www.americanancesto
2 CONC rs.org/DB283/i/13264/133/235868582 81:133-140] (2006)</ref>
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT Hannah was born Ann Langford about 1584, based on her marriage date.<ref name=Hatcher /> Ann Langford married William (not John) Potter 6 Oct 1607 in St. Thomas at the Cliffe, Lewes, England.<ref name=StThomas>Hatcher, citing St.T
2 CONC homas Parish records</ref><ref>"England Marriages, 1538–1973 ," database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:NK4Z-7V9: 10 February 2018), William Potter and Ann Langford, 06 Oct 1607; citing Saint Thomas, Lewes,
2 CONC  Sussex, England, reference , index based upon data collected by the Genealogical Society of Utah, Salt Lake City; FHL microfilm 1,067,241.</ref>William was buried there 14 Aug 1619.<ref name=StThomas /> They had four children:#Wi
2 CONC lliam bpt 28 August 1608<ref name=StThomas /> William, his wife Francis and son Joseph immigrated to New England aboard the "Abigail" in 1635.<ref name="Hotten">Hotten, John Camden (editor). ''The Original Lists of Persons of Qual
2 CONC ity: Emigrants, Religious Exiles, Political Rebels, Serving Men Sold for a Term of Years, Apprentices, Children Stolen, Maidens Pressed, and Others, who Went from Great Britain to the American Plantations, 1600-1700.'' London: Joh
2 CONC n Camden Hotten, 1874. [https://books.google.com/books/reader?id=VN_A5wlsjQQC&printsec=frontcover&output=reader&source=gbs_atb_hover&pg=GBS.PA97 p. 97,98]</ref>#John bpt. 10 Jan 1608/9<ref name=StThomas /> John also went to New En
2 CONC gland<ref name=Hatcher />#Mary bpt 12 March 1611/12<ref name=StThomas /> No further Record.<refname=Hatcher />#Steven bpt 1 Aug 1614.<ref name=StThomas /> No further Record.<ref name=Hatcher /> Hannah married in January 1619/20, a
2 CONC lso at St. Thomas at the Cliffe, Lewes, England, her second husband John Beecher.<ref name=StThomas /> John had possibly an unknown first wife, by whom he had Richard and John Beecher.<ref name=Hatcher />: "It is generally suppose
2 CONC d that her husband, John Beecher, was one ofthe seven whom Eaton sent to New Haven in advance of the colony and who died before the rest of the colony arrived." <ref>"Genealogies of Connecticut Families from The New England Histor
2 CONC ical Genealogical Register" vol III Painter--Wyllys,selected and introduced by Gary Boyd Roberts, Genealogical Publishing Co., Inc.,Baltimore, 1983, p 150-151.</ref><ref name=Atwater>History of the Colony of New Haven to Its Absor
2 CONC ption Into Connecticut By Edward Elias Atwater, Lucy M. Hewitt, Bessie E.Beach. Meriden, Connecticut: 1902. (death of John Beecher- [https://books.google.com/books?id=q9MLAAAAYAAJ&pg=PR7&lpg=PR7 see p 63]); list of estates p. 108-
2 CONC 111; seats pp 544, 549.</ref> Their children were: ::5. Isaac Beecher b. between 1620 and 1627, probably about 1623, wentto New England with his parents.<ref name=Hatcher /> Isaac Beecher was thought by some to be Hannah's step-so
2 CONC n from her second marriage,<ref name=Shepard>Shepard, James, "The New Haven (Conn.) Potters 1639. in"Genealogies of Connecticut Families, from The New England Historicaland Genealogical Register" Vol III. Previously published as "
2 CONC The New Haven (Conn.) Potters, 1639." NEHGR 54:20. 1900. [https://www.americanancestors.org/DB202/i/11679/21/23519389 p. 21]</ref> although Jacobus in Families of Ancient New Haven gives no such impression.<ref name="Jacobus">Jaco
2 CONC bus, Donald Lines (compiler). ''Families of Ancient New Haven, Vol I-VIII. and Index Vol IX'' New Haven: 1931. Reprint, Baltimore: Genealogical Publishing Co., Inc., 1974, 1981, 1997. Originally published as New Haven Genealogical
2 CONC  Magazine, Volumes I-VIII. Rome, NY and New Haven, CT 1922-1932.</ref> Hatcher shows she was his mother based on his estimated birth date relative to her marriage to his father. She names him as her son in her will and gave him on
2 CONC e-third of her property.<ref name=Hatcher />  ::6. Thomas Beecher, b. by 1627. No Further Records <ref name=Hatcher /> Widow Beecher, aka Widow Potter, was early in New Haven, Connecticut. Her sons John and William Potter both sig
2 CONC ned the Plantation Covenant in New Haven, Connecticut on June 4, 1639,<ref name=Hoadley1>Hoadley, Charles J, MA. (editor) [http://books.google.com/books/reader?id=Ng0GbNFf8B0C&printsec=frontcover&output=reader&source=gbs_atb_hover
2 CONC  Records of the Colony and Plantation of New Haven, From 1638 to 1649.] Hartford: Case, Tiffany and Company, 1857. [https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=miun.afk3880.0001.001;view=1up;seq=25 Covenant signers p. 17], [https://bab
2 CONC el.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=miun.afk3880.0001.001;view=1up;seq=100 estate list, p. 92]</ref> just nineteen years after the Pilgrims settled at Plymouth, Massachusetts. She was a midwife in 1641 and 1646. She is called 'Sister Pott
2 CONC er the midwife,' in seating the meeting house in 1646.  There are among the early records of New Haven Colony a list of estates. It falls within the pages of 1643, but it is by analysis an earlierdocument from the end of 1640.<ref
2 CONC  name=Atwater> This list contains the following entries::Widow Potter: 2 persons, estate L30, 20 & 1/4 acres and a rate of L 0.5.01.<ref name=Hoadley1 /> The second person is most likely her son Isaac Beecher.:Joh: Potter: 4 perso
2 CONC ns, L25 estate, about 30 acres and a rate of L 0.7.09<ref name=Hoadley1 />:Will Potter: 4 persons, estate of L40, about 58 acres and a rate of L0.8.09<ref name=Hoadley1 /> Another document from 1646, known as the book of Alienatio
2 CONC ns, shows her share as listed under the name of Widow Beecher, proving conclusively that Widow Potter and Widow Beecher are the same person.<ref>Great Migration Newsletter, V.1-20.(Online Database: AmericanAncestors.org, New Engla
2 CONC nd Historic Genealogical Society, 2015.) Vol 13. pp 9, [http://www.americanancestors.org/databases/great-migration-newsletter-v1-20/image/?pageName=15&volumeId=21157 15]</ref>  In 1646, Sister Potter the midwife was assigned the l
2 CONC ittle cross seatewith Old Sister Nash, and in 1656, Goodwife Beecher the elder was assigned a seat in the alley for convenience of hearing.<ref name=Atwater/> The Widow Potter had some trouble about her fence and was fined 12d for
2 CONC  a gap on 6 Aug 1650<ref>Dexter TR1: [https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=wu.89067363812;view=1up;seq=53 p. 43]</ref> Then about 7 months later 10 Mar 1650/51: "It is ordered that the treasurer paye out of the Towne stocks for 
2 CONC the making of 5 or 6 rod of fence, for Widdow Beecher ye midwife<ref>Dexter TR1: [https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=wu.89067363812;view=1up;seq=77 p. 67]</ref> It is a measure of the value of the midwife to the towns well-bei
2 CONC ng that the town paid for this repair and again 4 Aug 1651. "It is ordered that the Treasurer take care and see that widow Potter the midwives house be mended, and paid for out of the Treasury.<ref>Dexter TR1: [https://babel.hathi
2 CONC trust.org/cgi/pt?id=wu.89067363812;view=1up;seq=90 p. 80] The footnote claims this is the widow of John Potter, however John Potter's widow married Edward Parker in 1646, and would have had no need of someone to tend her house.</r
2 CONC ef> Widow Beecher (4 Nov 1651) testified that John Bishop, deceased, was sane enough to make a will. <ref>Dexter TR1: [https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=wu.89067363812;view=1up;seq=106 p. 96]</ref> and 4 May 1652, Goodwife Be
2 CONC echer ye midwife testified in a case of fornication<ref>Dexter TR1: [https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=wu.89067363812;view=1up;seq=134 p. 124] </ref> Hannah Beecher died before 2 Mar 1658/9 in New Haven, when her inventory wa
2 CONC s appraised, but after she wrote her will 13 June 1657.<ref>Hoadley, Charles J, MA. (editor) ''Records of the Colony or Jurisdiction ofNew Haven, From May 1653 to the Union'' Hartford: Case, Tiffany and Company, 1858. [https://pla
2 CONC y.google.com/books/reader?id=FMRSAAAAcAAJ&printsec=frontcover&source=gbs_atb_hover&pg=GBS.PA357 p. 357]</ref>  The will of Hannah Potter Beecher was proved April 5 1659, and is recorded in first part, vol i, p 80 of New Haven Prob
2 CONC ate Records.:"I Hannah Beecher of New Haven, expecting my great change do make this my last will and testament, I bequeath my soul unto the hands of my Lord Jesus Christ by whose meritt I hope to be saved and my body to beburried 
2 CONC at the discretion of my Son William Potter my Executor. And for my worldly goods I give unto John Potter my Grand Child twenty shillings and to Hannah Blackly, my Grand child, wife to Samuel Blackly, twenty shillings, And to Samue
2 CONC l Potter my Grand child twenty shillings to be paid to them within three months after my decease. And for the rest of my estate I give one third part to my son Isaac Beecher and twothirds to my eldest son William Potter, making hi
2 CONC m my Executor, desiring him to be as a father to his younger brother and his children. Andin dividing my goods my will is that my son William should have my feather bed with that belongeth to it, unto his part and that the rest be
2 CONC  divided at the discretion of my Overseers with the assistance of Sister Wakeman and sister Rutherford and I desire my loving freinds Mr. Mathew Gilbert and John Wakeman to be overseers of this my last will whereunto I have set my
2 CONC  hand this 13th day of June, Anno 1657." Witnesses: Mathew Gilbert, John Wakeman, Sarah Rutherford. Hannah Becher signed by mark.<ref name=Shepard /> 5 Apr 1659  "The last will & testament of Hannah Beecher, late of Newhaven decea
2 CONC sed, was by her sonne Wm Potter presented, wch being read…to be ye last will of the deceased,… It was judged legall." An inventory was presented in the amount of L55.05.06, attested to by WilliamPotter. At some time later Isaac Be
2 CONC acher acknowledged receiving his third part.<ref>Dexter TR1: [https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=wu.89067363812;view=1up;seq=409 p. 399]</ref>
