1 NOTE {geni:about_me} --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT '''John Tripp''' was Quaker. He was employed. He was born on 6 February 1610 at Lincolnshire, England; Bigelow has Month, says Northumberland, not Lincolnshire County.? He was the son of John Tripp and Isabel Moses . John Tripp im
2 CONC migrated in 1630 to Newport, Newport, RI. He was a founder on 30 April 1639 at Aquidneck, Newport, R.I.. He married Mary Paine, daughter of Anthony Paine and ? Susannah Potter, circa 1639 at Portsmouth, Newport, RI; Portsmouth, "T
2 CONC rapp" has Torrey;,m b 1640. John Tripp was resident in 1648 at Providence, RI. He died on 28 October 1678 at Portsmouth, Newport, RI, at age 68; Bigelow has Month, 1678, not 1677. [498] has 1719. His estate was probated on 28 Octo
2 CONC ber 1678 at Portsmouth, Newport, RI.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From ''The Genealogist'' 
2 CONT
2 CONT A source finds the family roots in the Canterbury area of co. Kent and that John came on the same ship as William Hall. He signed a covenant for human love and liberty dated at Providence in December 1647. On April 30, 1639 he sig
2 CONC ned the Compact of Portsmouth with 28 others.
2 CONT
2 CONT On March 18, 1649/50, John and his wife Mary (Paine) gave a rreceipt to Rose Weeden, Mary's step-mother, for a legacy under the will of Anthony Paine, Mary's father.
2 CONT
2 CONT John served 
2 CONT *as a juror in 1649, 1650 and 1653. 
2 CONT *As clerk of the weights and measures in 1651. 
2 CONT *On the Court of Commisioners in 1661. 
2 CONT *Many years as the commissioner ot the General Court in Providence. 
2 CONT *On the General Court of Commissioners in Warwick in 1662. 
2 CONT *On the Grand Jury in 1666. 
2 CONT *As a deputy to the General Assembly for many years between 1648 to 1675. 
2 CONT *Moderator of the Portsmouth meeting. 
2 CONT *The prover and sealer of weights and measures in 1675. 
2 CONT *John was a freeman in Portsmouth in 1655.
2 CONT
2 CONT John's will was dated 6 10 mo. (December) 1677 and proved 28 8th mo. (October) 1678. He said his age was about 67 and named his wife Mary as executrix.
2 CONT
2 CONT *.1 He was born on 6 February 1609/10 at Horkstow near Barton on Humber, Lincolnshire, England, United Kingdom.[1],[2] 
2 CONT *He removed to on the ship TrueLove, Newport, Newport Co., RI, on 10 June 1635 had been apprentised to the house carpentry trade and came with his Master Randall Holden.[2] 
2 CONT *He married Mary Paine, daughter of Anthony Paine and Susannah Rose Potter, on 4 April 1639 at Portsmouth, Newport Co., RI.[1] 
2 CONT *John Tripp died on 12 February 1678 at Portsmouth, Newport Co., RI, at age 68.1,2
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of John Tripp and Mary Paine
2 CONT *1. John Tripp+1,2 b. 1640, d. 20 Nov 1719
2 CONT *2. Peleg Tripp+1 b. 1642, d. 13 Jan 1714/15
2 CONT *3. Joseph Tripp+1 b. 1644, d. 17 Nov 1718
2 CONT *4. Mary Tripp+1 b. 1646, d. a 1716
2 CONT *5. Elizabeth Tripp+1 b. 1648, d. a 1701
2 CONT *6. Alice Tripp+1 b. 1650, d. 1698
2 CONT *7. Isabel Tripp+1 b. 1651, d. b 1716
2 CONT *8. Abiel Tripp+1 b. 1653, d. 10 Sep 1684
2 CONT *9. James Tripp+1 b. 1656, d. 30 May 1730
2 CONT *10. Martha Tripp+1 b. 1658, d. a 1717
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Date of birth has also been (erroneously?) reported to be February 6, 1609.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000186869593833&size=large}}
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000186869754841&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT * ''John Tripp, 1611 - 1678: A Glimpse into Our Founder's Life''. by Janet Tripp Andrews edited by Jan E. Tripp © November, 2016. < [https://portsmouthhistorical.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/11/John_Tripp_1611-1678_V11-10-16.pdf PD
2 CONC F] >
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tripp[1]
2 CONT *M, b. 6 February 1609/10, d. 12 February 1678
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tripp From a book on the history of Portsmouth, RI 'Richard Searl, a seaman in the early 1600's sold his three acre tract of land to Mary Paine for one pint of wine. She married John Tripp and later, on part of this land, the
2 CONC y lived in a house and operated a ferry to Bristol. Their home is now the present address of the Pocasett Country Club.'
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tripp also had a ferry here and the first mention made is in 1676 when Captain Church of Tiverton, the famous Indian fighter, crossed to Bristol on Tripp's ferry. John Tripp died in 1677 and his wife Mary, continued the busin
2 CONC ess probably with male help. She recieved a license in 1679 to sell food, drink and entertainment.'
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT From The Document of John Tripp in the New Bedford, MA library: 
2 CONT *The resolution of John 
2 CONT *the said John Tripp concerning the places of his abode. First I was born in Covetown (Horkstow near Barton on Humber) in Lincolnshire 3 miles from Benton Harbor, and 
2 CONT *my father's name was John Tripp and 
2 CONT *my mother's name was Isabel Moses and before she married my father when she was a maid and 
2 CONT *they had about twelve children and much kindred we had and 
2 CONT *when I grew in years I was put an apprentice to a shapt (ships?) carpenter whose name was John Baaty (Beety, Beedy?) of [Horys]bury three miles of from Alsand in Lincolnshire aforesaid where i served years and sometime after wrou
2 CONC ght with him and 
2 CONT *after that I bound myself to owe Frances East for 4 pounds a year for 4 years who sold me and I had served him and his assign for about a year and a half. 
2 CONT *He sold me to Robert Jafra (Jeffery?) then living in Boston and Boston church members persecuted some to the offending of others. My master came to Rhode Island with the said persecuted people and I with him and his wife being si
2 CONC ckly and they could not get their maid to Rhode Island with them. People whom the said members and expelled from them. 
2 CONT *Therefore my master was forced to sell me to Randall Houlding of Portsmouth on Rhode Island and I served a while and after bought out the rest of time of him and 
2 CONT *after a while I married a wife whose maiden name was Mary Paine. I being about thirty or twenty eight years old or thereabouts, and the Lord hath given us eleven children of when one is dead. 
2 CONT *The eldest is 29 years old and <nowiki>----</nowiki> this 17 on the second month 1670. 
2 CONT *Praised be thee our Rock who hath been help and unto us at all times give what thou pleased. It is mercy from thee to rerceive anything for the earth and sea is all thine and the fullness thereof.
2 CONT
2 CONT Chronology of John Tripp 
2 CONT *1610-1635 Lincolnshire 
2 CONT *1635-36 Boston 
2 CONT *1636-37 Providence 
2 CONT *1637-1677 Portsmouth 
2 CONT *1640 md. Mary Paine 
2 CONT *1678 died
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Dale Thompson, Tripp Gedcom - D.Thompson.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Illean Hammar and Earnest Klein, Tripps of Quinte, page v.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography of John TRIPP
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tripp was baptized 8 Sep 1611/1612 at Horkstow Parish, Lincolnshire, England according to a deposition (loose folder dated 1670; New Bedford Library) concerning his personal history. He states Horkstow as his birthplace. 
2 CONT
2 CONT George Randall, early biographer (1950's) of the Tripps of Rhode Island, cites his birthplace as Northumberland, England. Randall's source is likely the same deposition (not the Horkstow deposition) cited by Sumner wherein John Tr
2 CONC ipp gives a deposition 6 Feb 1660/1661 regarding his "Uncle Potter" (who was in fact uncle to Mary Paine). This deposition reads "I, John Tripp, born in Northumberland County, England, the son of John and Isabel Moses of Lincolnsh
2 CONC ire, born in 1610, was apprenticed to the house carpentry trade and with my master, Randall Holden, came to Newport or Portsmouth in 1630...". 
2 CONT
2 CONT The deposition of 1660/1661 has not been located. At present, there appears to be no way to reconcile these two conflicting depositions. The baptismal record for John Tripp at Horkstow Parish has been located thus until further ev
2 CONC idence, we are recording the birthplace of John Tripp as Lincolnshire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of about twelve children of John Tripp and Isabel Moses, John Tripp was apprenticed to John Baats of Thorsby, Lincolnshire to learn ship carpentry and served seven years and more. Thereafter, he bound himself (in 1633/1634) to
2 CONC  Frances East for four years. After about one and one half years of service (1635/1636), East sold the contract to Robert Jafra of Boston. Jafra fled Boston, with John Tripp in tow, to Rhode Island to avoid religious persecution. 
2 CONC Thereafter, Jafra sold the contract to Randall Holden of Portsmouth, RI. After some time, John Tripp paid off (probably 1637/1638) the remaining time on the contract.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is likely that John Tripp was first apprenticed at about the age of fifteen thus serving his first apprenticeship until 1632/1633. It is probable that he was yet in Lincolnshire for his next one and one half years of service un
2 CONC til 1635/1636 when he immigrated to Boston on the same ship as William Hall (Abiel Tripp, son of John Tripp, married Deliverance Hall, daughter of William Hall). This likely sequence agrees with the immigration date of 1635 cited 
2 CONC by Dr. Carolyn Syron Valentine in "Trip Wills, Deeds and Ways", 1932. The Passenger and Immigration Lists Index, 1500s-1900s (page 296) has the immigration place of Rhode Island in 1638, this not being a conflict with immigration 
2 CONC date of 1635 to the colonies.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Registry of Freemen of Portsmouth, Colony of Rhode Island (Aquidneck Island), John Tripp is listed as: John Tripp, Gentleman. Since the term "Freeman" refers to landholding, voting rights, and the privilege to serve in publ
2 CONC ic office, then it is likely that he was admitted Freeman about 1641. The deposition (wherein he discusses his origins and life history) of John Tripp housed in the New Bedford Public Library, Genealogy Dept and Archives, was ente
2 CONC red about 1670.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1640, John Tripp married Mary Paine, daughter of Anthony Paine. Her mother is unknown. Her step-mother was Rose Potter.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 1 Mar 1641, John Tripp purchased land next to Thomas Groton and then later resided next to Ralph Earle. In 1651, John Tripp and Ralph Earle settled a boundary dispute and this document was witnessed by Benedict Arnold (not of t
2 CONC he American Revolution) and others. In 1657, he had planting land on Hogg Island for a period of seven years. On or before 1658, John operated a ferry because he was paid for transporting members of the General Assembly that year.
2 CONC  This was no doubt the beginnings of the famous Tripp Ferry. In 1662, he purchased a quarter share of the Dartmouth Purchase from John Alden and then divided and conveyed this interest to his sons in 1665.
2 CONT
2 CONT Active in public affairs, John served in a number of public offices over the years.
2 CONT *1651: Clerk of Weights and Measures
2 CONT *1655: Commissioner Of The Colony
2 CONT *1661: Served At Court Of Commissioners At Portsmouth
2 CONT *1666: Serves Grand Jury;Also Ratemaker,Surveyor Of Cattle,Deputy-Genl Assembly
2 CONT *1667: Deputy Of General Court
2 CONT *1668: Deputy Of General Court
2 CONT *1669: General Assembly At Newport
2 CONT *1672: Apr and Oct Assemblies;Moderater Of Portsmouth Mtg To And Including 1675
2 CONT *1675: Prover And Sealer Of Weights And Measures
2 CONT *1676-1678: Numerous Offices
2 CONT *1678: Last Appearance In Records
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tripp made his last will and testament 16 Dec 1677, proved 28 Oct 1678, leaving five male descendants some of whom would remain in Rhode Island and become prominent in many areas including the whaling industry and some of who
2 CONC m would begin a restless migration first to New York continuing west. His daughters, of whom less is known, married into some of the first 100 families of Rhode Island.
2 CONT
2 CONT © 2006 Carolyn Fields-Collins and Mark L. Fields. This work may be reproduced and redistributed, in whole or in part, without alteration and without prior written permission solely for genealogy research purposes, provided all cop
2 CONC ies contain the following statement: "© 2006 Carolyn Fields-Collins and Mark L. Fields. This work is reproduced and distributed with the permission of Mark L. Fields."
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT 1, John Tripp signed (with others) the Aquidneck Covenant and the following covenant, a politico-human document that could serve us as well today as then. This covenant was signed in Providence and was probably the General Assembl
2 CONC y.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. "That we are not wilfully opposite, nor careless and senceless, and thereby meanes of our own an others ruine and destruction. And especially in testimonye of our fidelitye and cordiall affections unto one another heere present
2 CONC , so that ther may be a current, peaceable and comfortable proceedinge."
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. An industrious man, John Tripp was, amongst other enterprises, a husbandman. His brand (livestock mark) was entered into the Portsmouth town records: "The Eare (ear) marke of John Tripp Senyor (Senior) is a Crop on ye Left Eare
2 CONC  (and) a hapeny (half-penny) one (on) (th)e side of ye same Eare (u)nder Crope of Nere(.) (U)pon 30 res standinge (residents attending?) and re(a)d (u)pon Record the nd day of december by me Richard towne Clarke (Clerk)"
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. John Tripp, probably living in or near Newport, RI was one of about 100 purchasers of Conanicut Island from the Indians in 1657. His 350 year old signature can be seen on the original purchase agreement page at
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.jamestownhistoricalsociety.org/index.cfm?id=38
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT For more information about the purchase, see
2 CONT
2 CONT http://jamestownhistoricalsociety.org.whsites.net/jhs1/index.php?page_id=44
2 CONT
2 CONT Exhibits - Jamestown Land Agreement
2 CONT
2 CONT Permanent Exhibit in The Town Hall
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1657 Land Agreement is the basis for Jamestown land titles. The prepurchase document among the buyers of Conanicut Island details what percentage of land on the island would belong to each purchaser after the purchase of the l
2 CONC and from the Narragansetts was finalized.
2 CONT
2 CONT When property is purchased today a title search is done to show a legal chain of ownership from the current owner back through the last 350 years to this first land title document of Jamestown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Above right is the first page of the document. Below it is the 7th and last page, which contains 74  of the 101 signatures on the document.  The Land Agreement is displayed in the Town Hall.
2 CONT
2 CONT  The Story
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1657, 100 colonial Rhode Islanders negotiated the purchase of Quononaqutt, now Conanicut, Island from the Narragansett Indians. Before completing the purchase, the group met to establish how the land would be divided among them
2 CONC . This contract is known as the Quononaqutt Land Agreement. Multiple copies of the agreement were made.
2 CONT
2 CONT A transcribed copy with a list of the signers but without their signatures rests in the land records of the Town of Jamestown and is the starting place for all titles searches for island property. The town copy has been badly dama
2 CONC ged and is virtually unreadable.
2 CONT
2 CONT In late April 2005, Arlene Petit, the Jamestown Town Clerk, notified the Society that a copy of the agreement with the original signatures was to be auctioned in New York City. A scanned copy of the document was obtained and exami
2 CONC ned by knowledgeable members of the Society. At an emergency meeting on May 11, the Board authorized our President, Bill Burgin, to bid up to $20,000 to buy the document. The auction was held on May 12. When the bidding went above
2 CONC  the authorized amount, an individual Society director, Ken Newman, stepped in to guarantee the additional $4,000 required. " It was bought at an auction the Swann gallery in New York on May 12, 2005. Total cost, including commiss
2 CONC ion, was $27,740.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tripp Genealogy
2 CONT
2 CONT Name origin: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Unproven and contested family tradition says Lord Howard's fifth son was given the name Tripp by King Henry V after the storming of a castle during the siege of Boulogne. Hearing that Howard tripped up the castle wall the king sai
2 CONC d that his name shall be Tripp, no longer Howard. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Available information indicates that only two Lord Howards had five sons. One was Thomas, Lord Howard of Effingham. His fifth son was Richard Howard, born about 1494. This date is not given, but chronologically Richard's birth dat
2 CONC e is between the dates of two siblings, Edward 1476 and William 1511. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard's date,1494, is not compatible with the lifetime of King Henry V (1386-1422), but it is compatible with the regency of King Henry VIII, 1509-1547, and the siege of Boulogne 1544. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The other Lord Howard was William, Lord Howard of Effingham, whose fifth son was Edward, born 1550, too late to co-exist with any King Henry or be at the siege of Boulogne. So, if it happened, it was Richard Howard who became Rich
2 CONC ard Tripp(1), and absent other Tripps existing at that time, the ancestor of all Tripps.
2 CONT
2 CONT George Tripp(2) was born 1525 prob. in Horkstow, Lincolnshire, England and died about 1580.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married about 1545 Margarie
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT *1. Thomas of Horkstow
2 CONT *2. Agnes born about 1545
2 CONT *3. John(3) born about 1550
2 CONT *4. Edward born about 1555 of Eaglesham
2 CONT *5. Margaret born about 1561
2 CONT *6. John Tripp(3) was born about 1550, probably in Horkstow, Lincolnshire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT *1. Robert born about 1579
2 CONT *2. Thomas born about 1581
2 CONT *3. William born about 1583
2 CONT *4. John(4) born about 1587
2 CONT *5. Elizabeth born about 1561
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tripp(4) was born about 1587. On 30 Jul 1609 he married Isobel Moyses (Moses), chr. 20 Mar 1584 in Horkstow, England, daughter of Nicholas Moyses. They resided in Lincolnshire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT John TRIPP The Founder was born on 6 Feb 1610 in Horkstow, Lincolnshire, England. He died on 12 Feb 1678 in Portsmouth, Newport County, Rhode Island. He was christened.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The first Tripp to come to America is the ancestor of so many of us that someone appropriately tagged him " The Founder ". By his own hand, he wrote that he had 11 brothers and sisters, so he evidently did not have much to start w
2 CONC ith. For that reason he indentured himself to Randall Holden so that he could learn the carpenter trade. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was admitted as an inhabitant of the island of Aquidneck, (Later Rhode Island), and on April 30, 1638 signed a compact with twenty-eight others as follows: "We whose names are underwritten do acknowledge ourselves the legal sub
2 CONC jects of His Majesty king Charles, and in his name do hereby bind ourselves into a civil body politic, unto his laws according to matters of justice". 
2 CONT
2 CONT Records show that he accumulated land in Dartmouth, (that he purchased from John Alden), land in Narragansett, land in Westerly and land on Hog Island. This island got its name from the fact, that all of the hogs that were not con
2 CONC trolled by their owners by fences or buildings, were hauled over and turned loose on Hog Island! I don't know what John did with them, when he started farming the island! I guess this was the first Tripp to raise pigs in this coun
2 CONC try! 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tripp was active in several aspects of the government, including Commissioner. He was Deputy for the following years: 1648, 1654, 1655, 1658, 1661, 1662, 1663, 1664, 1666, 1667, 1668, 1669,and 1672. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He left in his will various lands, buildings plus orchards, and fencing. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tripp married Mary Paine in 1639 and during the next 21 years had, and then reared eleven children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From the New Bedford Massachusetts Library we have secured records for first generation of all the children and subsequent years for John Tripp Junior, Peleg Tripp, Joseph Tripp, Abiel Tripp, James Tripp, and Sylvanus Tripp. Toget
2 CONC her with additional information about Abiel Tripp from Warren Forrest Tripp, of Wheatridge Colorado and Paul Tripp of Pennsylvania who recently moved to Provo Utah; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Flash--New News Robert Tripp and his wife Dortha Tripp just called me to tell me the news! They recently visited the Tripp House in Scranton Pennsylvania that was evidently built by Isaac Tripp's son Isaac Tripp. The house is purp
2 CONC orted to be built on and around the original log house built in 1778. That was the year that Grandpa Isaac was killed by the Indians with nine spears put through him! 
2 CONT
2 CONT The new news though, is that records from this house indicate that John Tripp the Founder set sail on June 10th 1635, on the ship named TrueLove, to come to this country. This is our first information about his leaving England dat
2 CONC e and And on which ship he sailed. Good news it is! 
2 CONT
2 CONT Herman William Tripp--Remembering John Tripp, Gentleman, Pioneer, At Work by Caroline Valentine writing in the Valentine Research Studio, in Washington D.C., published in 1932. Verbatim: As the centuries have wheeled into the past
2 CONC , five hundred men, or possibly a thousand, have answered to the unassuming name, John Tripp. It may be supposed that none of these ever once dreamed, that from him should come a man to sit on the American throne. Yet this honor c
2 CONC ame to John the Founder, in prospect, and in very fact to one of his descendants. 
2 CONT
2 CONT All that we find recorded of him shows that John Trippe of Portsmouth did his daily task with his might, looking for the Light, and in the fear of God as He was conceived by them of the Friends' persuasion. As plain country squire
2 CONC , at many points in old England; as lawyer; as physician; as clergyman of the established, stereotyped sort; as colonial deputy; as Mayor and representatime of his King, John Tripp was always "John Tripp, Gentleman". Manifesting i
2 CONC n multifarious forms, he played always an estimable part, and no records of John Tripp show that he was ever less than an honorable man, strictly "on the job" that fell to him in his then sphere. But the very formal term, John Tri
2 CONC pp, Gentleman, found in the old records, was brought over from England, even as was John himself. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The line back into the mists of the conquror's time is so long, that no one may now tell how many John Tripps trod English soil. The family name was certainly John, and a common custom of giving the names of the founder of the fam
2 CONC ily, and of the father and grandfather to the elder sons in every family, held for untold generations among the Tripps. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancient deeds long held by English Tripps, even in west England, testify to the family grip on the soil throughout many centuries. English genealogists seem to agree that Canterury was the place of inception as far as history show
2 CONC s, and one of the stanard English Genealogical firms informs us that no Tripp family there known can be traced to any but the one root in Kent. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It is of record that a Tripp of 1325 held right to a coat of arms; but the date of its conferment is missing. Sir Charles Tripp, who bore it, was a well-known lawyer of the Middle Temple, London, whose arms are both shown and desc
2 CONC ribed. He was son of that English John Trippe, Gentleman, who lived both in the sixeenth and the seventeenth centuries. His father (and probably he, himself) saw life as it was keenly lived in France as well as in England. For the
2 CONC  father, according to the English heralds, was John Trippe, Vice-Marshal of Calais. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A very gorgeous John Tripp, Gentleman, in crimson and gold, executed the city ordinances of Hull, as its Mayor, in 1669. we find him under the heading "John Tripp, 1660" in a most quaint old English book of notables who had been s
2 CONC choolmates in Lincolne county or elsewhere. The style is often humorous, but the English owe the author a great debt for historical items lacking elsewhere. This John was Chamberlain; then Sheriff; then Mayor of Hull, 1669. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tripp, Gentleman, Pioneer At Work 
2 CONT
2 CONT John the Founder's appearance in Portsmouth, was in 1638; in Providence, under date of May 16, 1648. He had come over in the same ship with William Hall in 1635. His name appears on the organization list of Portsmouth as John Trip
2 CONC pe. On this list appeared the names of eleven families with whom the Tripps were to intermarry. One of these was that of Anthonie Paine, John's father in law; another, that of William Haule (Hall), three of whose children became "
2 CONC in laws" to John Tripp, later. Thus, the founding of Portsmouth seems rather a family affair. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The remnant of the earliest records of Portsmouth now available contains no less than 80 references to John Tripp, the Founder of the New England line of English Tripps. The indexers agree that the spellings Trip, Tripe, and Tripp
2 CONC  belong also to the family of Trippe. In 1649, we find John serving on a jury; as also in 1650, 1653, etc. By this time, three fourths the recorded names are of those related, or to be related by marriage to the first John Tripp, 
2 CONC and to assist in carrying on his family line. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1651, he is clerk of "the wayghts and measures". In the same year he makes an agreement with Ralph Earle concerning a line fence. This precise paper fills one and one half pages of the records, as printed. It was signed in the 
2 CONC presence of four witnesses, one of whom was Benedict Arnold. The meat of it was that each signer should play fair with the other, as to reciprocity on stones and in the work of building the stone fence; a chief object being to "ma
2 CONC ke there sayed landes several to each of their private uses." John was between two fires, in that Ralph Earl's land joined him on both the north and the south sides. One point of the agreement read thus: "From yeare to yeare, so l
2 CONC ong as those sayd lots ly open together, Earl's to Tripp's aforenamed, that they will not on either of these said lots damnify each other by Cattell there put by their order." 
2 CONT
2 CONT This agreement was considered so important that the heirs of both signers are included in its terms, and a forfeit of twenty pounds sterlng is laid on the failure; to be "payed by the Ptie defective, unto the other Ptie engaged th
2 CONC erein". Formal seals completed the document, when signed. It was a wise move to block trouble with bounding neighbors. I can recall, as grandchild of New York pioneers six generations later, the threats of law-suits and the loud w
2 CONC ords following the neglect of fences, which led to raids by neighbors' cattle, on growing grain. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Having served his town freely and well whenever needed, John Tripp came to the honor of being chosen Committeeman for the Generall Court. This was an important term, for this committee was authorized to meet with those of the othe
2 CONC r towns, "at the day Nuport neighbors shall apoint, with as full power to act as if the towne were present." The same "Meetinge of the Inhabitants of Portsmouth" that chose John Tripp as representative, voted also, "that Assamequi
2 CONC n shall have his coate payed him forthwith for his rent of the medows on the maine land, on the north side of Roade Ilande". 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1655, John Tripp was chosen "commissioner of the Colony, to transact the business of the Generall Court to be held at Providence at the usuall tyme". He here appears as "Mr. John Tripp." Serving with him were John Roome, John B
2 CONC riggs, Thomas Lauton and Mr. Thomas Brownell. Portsmouth, by this time, was becoming important. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Even among this goodly people, thieves were abroad, it seems; who, if they could but remove cattle from the island, could then snap their fingers at all authority. The ferries became increasingly important. Thus, in August of the 
2 CONC year 1666, the town meeting appointed John Tripp, his son in law's father, William Hall and two others "to survaie and view all cattell that shall be henceforth transported off the Iland and to take the names of all such as transp
2 CONC ort cattell, the day of the month when, the number of the Generall Cattel, with there severall ear marks and if any have cattel that have not their own eare marke or that have other markes than ther owen the survayers are hereby r
2 CONC equired to make stopp of them, unless thay give satisfaction to the survayers how they cam by those Cattel, so differently marked. and the survayeers are to see the Cattell boated and if any carry cattell off the Land in the night
2 CONC , though survayed before, shall have a survayer to see them at ther going of shore or shall forfeit tenn pounds and stand to further sensuer of the toune". 
2 CONT
2 CONT Serving frequetly on the Town Council, John was chosen also in 1661, with five others, to serve as a "Court of Comitioners" at Portsmouth. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The following year he was chosen to serve on the next General court of Commissioners to be held at Warwick. The report of the town meeting for February, 1663 notes that four "Comitioners or deputyes" were chosen to serve at a Gene
2 CONC ral Court to be held at Newport. Of these, was Mr. John Tripp. Another was his brother in law Mr. Lott Stange; who had married Alice Paine, sister to John Tripp's wife Mary. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the meantime, John's family is increasing and maturing. The year 1666 sees John appear on the records as "Mr. John Tripp senior". he serves this year on the Grand Jury. A new duty--of Rate making is laid upon him in 1666. He is
2 CONC  chosen for the Grand Jury (with two others). He is kept, as in most years, in the post of Surveyor of Cattle, and is again chosen deputy to the General Assembly. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1667, the town chooses him to serve, with Albro, Cooke, Aly, William Hall and Sanford, to devise a method of preventing "the destruction of wood and timber of this township" and some method of redress for the town. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In June of 1667, John Tripp is once more chosen as one of the four Portsmouth "deputies for ye next Generall Court, as deputy. In 1669, he serves again in the General Assembly, held at Newport in May of that year. In 1672 he serve
2 CONC s in the April Assembly, and also in that of October. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tripp, the Founder, is now aging. He has but six more years in which to prove his value. But during these years he is allowed no surcease of work. In this year of 1672, he becomes moderator of the Portsmouth meeting; to which
2 CONC  office he is chosen each year therafter up to and including 1675. As rate maker; as surveyor of cattle; as keen eyed representative of his town in many capacities, he does his unhurried and faithful duty. As moderator, he is hamp
2 CONC ered by rules hitherto unknown to the presiding officers; since, in the last previous meeting, the town council voted that for the future, "noe Towne meetinge shall have power to act in affaires Relatinge to the Towne Except there
2 CONC  be at least the number of fifteene prescent at the Said meeting. And further ordered that for the future, noe Moderator of the Towne Meeting Shall presume to desolve Such Said Meeting without the Major vote of the said Meetinge. 
2 CONC And if any Moderator shall from the meeting withdraw himself, without the leave of the major part of the meeting, it shall be in the power of that meetinge to elect another to the place of Moderator." 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1675, Mr. John Tripp was chosen by the town council for three several offices besides that of moderator. One of these was that of prover and sealer of weights and measures, "accordinge as the law of this Collony hath provided."
2 CONC  in 1676, 1677 and 1678 he is still filling important town offices. This last year sees his last appearance on the records. In April of the year 1679, "the widow Mary Tripp" receives from the Town Council" a License for one year t
2 CONC o Sell Victuals and drink to Travelers and to afford them entertainment as may bee needful and Conveient, they first giveing bond according to law for the keeping of good order and do also pay into the Treasury ten Shillings for e
2 CONC ach License". 
2 CONT
2 CONT So far as the records indicate, the public work of John Tripp was never spectacular; but these were sober-minded men, and the same might be said of almost any man of record at Portsmouth during this period. this John was most sure
2 CONC ly a man of keen mind and great personal integrity, for his name comes into prominence in every essential matter, and wherever money is the crux of the situation, except in connection with the Treasury. 
2 CONT
2 CONT But William Wodell was long the Treasurer for the town of Portsmouth. he was also many times a deputy from Portsmouth. As, at this period, every priviledge that high authority could offer was for members of the established church,
2 CONC  the church of England, and that every indignity and tax that could be conceived was visited upon non-members, under many of the colonial governments, we may imagine how much need the quiet group of Friends had of the stiff neck w
2 CONC hich was supposed to be their crowning attribute. The "Sufferings", at one period, any Friend came to be ranked very high in the estimation of his Order. John Simcock, close friend and valued counselor of William Penn, was a notab
2 CONC le "sufferer"; nor did the least conspicuous escape his tax of scorn and obloquy, anguish and financial loss. Costly, indeed, was it to "follow The Light", in their time. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1658, Roger Williams wrote: "It was not price nor money that could have purchased Rhode Island. Rhode Island was obtained by love; by the love and favor which that honorale gentleman, Sir Henry Vane, and myself, had with that g
2 CONC reat Sachem, Miantonomie". 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nine years after John Tripp signed the Aquidneck group covenant, he set his name to a marvelous politico-human document in Providence. its basis and tenor were human love and human liberty. It, too, was a covenant, its purpose bei
2 CONC ng to place upon record: "That we are not willfully opposite, nor careless and senceless, and therby meanes of our own and others' ruine and destruction. And especiallly in Testimonye of our fidelitye and Cordiall affections unto 
2 CONC one another heere present, so that there may be a currant, peaceable and Comfortble proceedinge." 
2 CONT
2 CONT John, Peleg and Joseph Tripp, Citizens At the time when the first John appeared in the Generall Court. Mr. Roger Williams was chosen to be assistant. This Assembly was called by the Islanders the "Generall Court of Election". It w
2 CONC as then ordered that six men should be chosen for each town, in whom the General Court should be continued. Also that the Generall Court of Tryall should be held "by course", wherever the actions should arise, and at such times as
2 CONC  the committee should choose. This seems suplementary to the previous work of the General Assembly. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On this committee appeared the names of Weeden, lawton, Almy, Briggs, Wilbor and Green: into whose families the descendants of John Tripp were soon to marry. Continued under Peleg Tripp. Bock: (American Families 2; Newport Histori
2 CONC cal Magazine 4 [1883]:50-7; Arthur D. Dean, Genealogy of the Tripp Family Descended from Isaac Tripp of Warwick Rhode Island and Wilkes Barre, Pennsylvania [Scranton PA:1903]; George L. Randall, Tripp Genealogy: Descendants of Jam
2 CONC es son of John Tripp [New Bedford:1924] 5; Valentine Research Studio, Tripp will, Deed, and Ways [Washington DC:1932]; James Savage, "Genealogical Dictionary of the First Settlers of New England" [Baltimore, 1977] IV:330-1; Beamon
2 CONC  Newport County Mar from Probates [Portsmouth Rec., 315-6; Portsmouth Land Evidence 2/1:129]; John's Resolution from New Bedford Library; Letter from Neil Thompson; Horkstow Register [bp]). 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genforum.genealogy.com/tripp/messages/860.html
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married to Mary and Susannah.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT --John Tripp's apprenticeship as a carpenter for 7 years (abt 1625-1634) started in Thoresby, Lincolnshire undgr John Baats. About 1634 he bownd himself to Francis East. Aftgr a year and a half East sold the contract to Robert Jaf
2 CONC ra then living in Boston. Jafra and his wife left Boston due to persecution and sold the contract to Randal Houlding of Portsmouth, about 1638. John eventually bought out the remainder of his contract. He and wife Mary Paine had 1
2 CONC 1 children.
2 CONT
2 CONT He became an original founder and proprietor of Portsmouth, in 1638; he was a signer of the Compact on 30 April 1639. Rhode Island Gen. Assgmbly 1648, 1551-58, 61-64, 66-69, 72; Cowrt of Comoissioners 1655; member Gov.'s Council 1
2 CONC 670, 1673-75. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He owned land in Portsmouth, Narragansett and Westerly and Dartmouth in Plymouth Colony. According to a deposition of 1666 Richard Searl sold a three acre lot just above the town ferry to Mary Paine, afterward wife of John Tripp, 
2 CONC for a pint of wine, the sale taking place at the house of Wm Bawlstong, where apparently Mary was a barmaid. John Tripp later ran the ferry from this site.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: Immigration: ABT 1630 Newport, Rhode Island
2 CONT
2 CONT Occupation: carpenter
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: 6 7 8 9 He came to America in the 1630s as a carpenter's apprentice; he settled in Portsmouth, RI. He was a signatory of the Aquidneck Group Covenant and the Compact of Portsmouth. John Tripp ... landed Newport 1630; one of 
2 CONC signers of Compact entered in Portsmouth 30 Apr. 1639; Deputymany years from 1648 to 1672; left land in Dartmouth, Portsmouth, Narraganset,and Westerly to each of his 11 children and 79 Grandchildren; was a Quaker; (the Compiler h
2 CONC as read a paper which appears to be a copy of his own statement--viz., I, John Tripp, b. Northumberland Co., Eng., son of John and Isabel (Moses)of Lincolnshire; b. 1610; was apprenticed to the house carpentry trade and with his M
2 CONC aster, Randall Holden, came to Newport or Portsmouth in 1630; after completing his time he married Mary Paine, dau. of Anthony, and had 11 children--the paper ends by naming 1st. ch.) Called "Mr" in the records, John was active in
2 CONC the public affairs of Portsmouth. He served as a juror in 1649, 1650, and 1653, as clerk of the "wayghts and measures" in 1651, in 1661 on the Court of "Comitioners" and for many years commissioner to the General Court in Providen
2 CONC ce. In1662 he served on the General Court of Commissioners at Warwick, in 1666 on the Grand Jury, from 1648 intermittently to 1675 as deputy to the General Assembly. He also served as moderator of the Portsmouth meeting and in 167
2 CONC 5 prover andsealer of weights and measures. He was a freeman in Portsmouth in 1655. John Tripp, according to undocumented tradition the son of John and Isabel (Moses) Tripp of Lincolnshire, born in Northumberland, England, in 1610
2 CONC  ... Some sourceslist an additional son Sylvanus who married Margaret Diamond and lived in Kittery, Massachusetts (now part of Maine).
2 CONT
2 CONT The first Tripp to come to America is the ancestor of so many of us thatsomeone appropriately tagged him The Founder . By his own hand, hewrote that he had 11 brothers and sisters, so he evidently did not havemuch to start with. F
2 CONC or that reason he indentured himself to RandallHolden so that he could learn the carpenter trade. He was admitted as aninhabitant of the island of Aquidneck, (Later Rhode Island), and on April30, 1638 signed a compact with twenty-
2 CONC eight others as follows: We whosenames are underwritten do acknowledge ourselves the legal subjects of HisMajesty King Charles, and in his name do hereby bind ourselves into acivil body politic, unto his laws according to matters 
2 CONC of justice.Records show that he accumulated land in Dartmouth, (that he purchasedfrom John Alden), land in Narragansett, land in Westerly and land on HogIsland. This island got its name from the fact, that all of the hogs thatwere
2 CONC  not controlled by their owners by fences or buildings, were hauledover and turned loose on Hog Island! I don't know what John did withthem, when he started farming the island! I guess this was the firstTripp to raise pigs in this
2 CONC  country! John Tripp was active in severalaspects of the government, including Commissioner. He was Deputy for thefollowing years: 1648, 1654, 1655, 1658, 1661, 1662, 1663, 1664, 1666,1667, 1668, 1669, and 1672. He left in his wil
2 CONC l various lands, buildingsplus orchards, and fencing. John Tripp married Mary Paine in 1639 andduring the next 21 years had, and then reared eleven children. From theNew Bedford Massachusetts Library we have secured records for fi
2 CONC rstgeneration of all the children and subsequent years for John TrippJunior, Peleg Tripp, Joseph Tripp, Abiel Tripp, James Tripp, and SylvanusTripp. Together with additional information about Abiel Tripp from WarrenForrest Tripp, 
2 CONC of Wheatridge Colorado and Paul Tripp of Pennsylvania whorecently moved to Provo Utah; We have attempted to collect and install inthis computer as many of John Tripp's decendants as may be found, so thatall Tripps anywhere, may ha
2 CONC ve an opportunity to connect and have the sameknowledge of their ancestory that we now enjoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Flash--New News Robert Tripp and his wife Dortha Tripp just called me totell me the news! They recently visited the Tripp House in ScrantonPennsylvania that was evidently built by Isaac Tripp's son Isaac Tripp.The house is purport
2 CONC ed to be built on and around the original log housebuilt in 1778. That was the year that Grandpa Isaac was killed by theIndians with nine spears put through him! The new news though, is thatrecords from this house indicate that Jo
2 CONC hn Tripp the Founder set sail onJune 10th 1635, on the ship named TrueLove, to come to this country. Thisis our first information about his leaving England date and And on whichship he sailed. Good news it is! Herman William Tripp
