2 CONT     1651: He is clerk of "the wayghts and measures". In the same year he makes an
2 CONT
2 CONT     agreement with Ralph Earle concerning a line fence. This precise paper fills
2 CONT
2 CONT     one and one half pages of the records, as printed. It was signed in the
2 CONT
2 CONT     presence of four witnesses, one of whom was Benedict Arnold. The meat of it was
2 CONT
2 CONT     that each signer should play fair with the other, as to reciprocity on stones
2 CONT
2 CONT     and in the work of building the stone fence; a chief ofject being to "make
2 CONT
2 CONT     there sayed landes several to each of their private uses."
2 CONT
2 CONT     John was between two fires, in that Ralph Earl's land joined him on both the
2 CONT
2 CONT     north and the south sides. One point of the agreement read thus: "From yeare
2 CONT
2 CONT     to yeare, so long as those sayd lots ly open together, Earl's to Tripp's
2 CONT
2 CONT     aforenamed, that they will not on either of these said lots damnify each other by
2 CONT
2 CONT     Cattell there put by their order." This agreement was considered so important
2 CONT
2 CONT     that the heirs of both signers are included in its terms, and a forfeit of
2 CONT
2 CONT     twenty pounds sterlng is laid on the failure; to be "payed by the
2 CONT
2 CONT     Ptie defective, unto the other Ptie engaged therein". Formal seals completed
2 CONT
2 CONT     the document, when signed.
