2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Came to America in 1635 as an apprentive carpenter with his master Randall Holden, who was from salisbury, Wiltshire, England.  He went to Portsmouth in 1638, and was a signer of the RI Compact.  He owned lands in Portsmouth (havi
2 CONC ng been granted three acres next to Thomas Gorton on 03/01/1649), Narragansett and Westerly in RI, and Dartmouth in Plymouth Colony.  He signed a covenant for human love and liberty in Providence RI in December 1647.  A Quaker, he
2 CONC  served as a juror in 1649, 1650 & 1653, and a clerk of "wayghts and measures" in 1651 [Margaret Buckridge Bock, The Genealogist, 4:60], and he was Deputy to the RI Assembly from 1648 to 1672, and Assistant from 1670 to 1675.  He 
2 CONC also served as a member of the town council for many years.  On 02/06/1600 he testified he was about 49 yuears old that that he had heard his uncle Robert Potter say that he (Potter) has dold a certain house and land to John Antho
2 CONC ny.  According to a deposition made by William COllinge in 1666, Richard Searl sold a three acre lot just above the town ferry to Mary Paine, afterwards the wife of John Tripp, for a pint of wine, the sale taking place at the hous
2 CONC e of WIlliam Baulstone, where apparently Mary was a barmain, but at the time of the sale, no ded was drawn so the Court of Commissioners confirmed the sale on 12/08/1666.  John Tripp later ran the ferry from this site.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Another source also indicates John was born in England and came to America as an indentured servant.  He was bought and sold three times and then bought his freedom.  He was among a group of Quakers who founded Portsmouth, RI.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Landed in Newport in 1630 on the Mayflower. May or may not have been the same Mayflower of 1620 fame.
2 CONT
2 CONT Came to Newport(or Portsmouth) with his Master Randall Holden, he was an apprentice in the house carpentry trade.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Immigration abt 1635 in Canterbury, Kent,Eng. He was a carpenter. He was 
2 CONT
2 CONT a Quaker. From a book on the history of Portsmouth RI Richared Searl, a 
2 CONT
2 CONT seaman in the early 1600's sold his three acre tract of land to Mary 
2 CONT
2 CONT Paine for one pint of wine. She married John Tripp and later, on part of 
2 CONT
2 CONT this land, they lived in a house and operated a ferry to Bristol. Their 
2 CONT
2 CONT home is now the present address of the Pocasett Country Club. John Tripp 
2 CONT
2 CONT also had a ferry here and the first mention made is in 1676 when Captain 
2 CONT
2 CONT Church of Tiverton, the famous Indian fighter, crossed to Bristol on 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tripps ferry. John Tripp died in 1677 and his wife Mary, continued the 
2 CONT
2 CONT business probably with male help. She received a license in 1679 to 
2 CONT
2 CONT sell food, drink and entertainment.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Resided on Aquidneck Island.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1635- He came to New England in the same ship as William Hall.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1639, April 30- One of those who signed the Portsmouth, RI Compact to form a government in preparation for settlement of Portsmouth, RI and Newport, RI. His name was spelled John Trippe at the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1648, May 16- He was listed among Providence, RI  inhabitants, but subsequently returned to Portsmouth, RI.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1648-72: Deputy to the General Court for many years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Owned land in Dartmouth, MA, Portsmouth, RI, Narragansett, RI, and Westerly, RI.
2 CONT
