1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} >'''NOTES'''
2 CONT >'''I agree with''' [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Sisson-12 '''Wikitree'''] in not accepting Snaith as Richard's birth/baptism place. Moreover, FamilySearch repository queries made on 17 May 2024 for Snaith Births/Baptisms come u
2 CONC p empty. 
2 CONT >'''"George" appearing in the secondary header above is without merit.''' No primary record of in Colonial America (or a well-respected second source) shows "George as an alternate name. I don't think it's wise for Geni to be an o
2 CONC rigin or perpetuator of internet myth.
2 CONT >'''For''' [https://www.familysearch.org/search/record/results?count=20&q.anyDate.from=1600&q.anyDate.to=1615&q.anyPlace=Snaith%2C%20Yorkshire%2C%20England&q.givenName=Richard&q.surname=Sisson&c.birthLikePlace1=on&f.birthLikePlace
2 CONC 0=9&c.birthLikePlace2=on&f.birthLikePlace1=9%2CEngland&f.birthLikePlace2=9%2CEngland%2CYorkshire '''Richard Sisson'''] limited to Yorkshire for years 1600-1615 brings up ONE Rychard Sisson – baptized in 1615 in Saxton, Yorkshire. 
2 CONT >'''Alternatively,''' entering [https://www.familysearch.org/search/record/results?count=20&q.anyDate.from=1600&q.anyDate.to=1615&q.anyPlace=Snaith%2C%20Yorkshire%2C%20England&q.givenName=George&q.surname=Sisson&c.birthLikePlace1=
2 CONC on&f.birthLikePlace0=9&c.birthLikePlace2=on&f.birthLikePlace1=9%2CEngland&f.birthLikePlace2=9%2CEngland%2CYorkshire '''George Sisson'''] limited to Yorkshire for years 1600-1615 brings up NO George Sisson except outside the year-r
2 CONC ange boundary in 1588 in Howden, Yorkshire.
2 CONT >'''Anderson's''' ''New England: The Great Migration Begins'' series doesn't include a bio for George Sisson. Whether an addenda volume will ever be published is anyone's guess. Since Richard came over the pond to New England, why
2 CONC  did Anderson and colleagues omit a bio for him? — Ken Shelley, 17 May 2024
2 CONT ——————————
2 CONT
2 CONT ID: I28280
2 CONT Name: Richard Sisson
2 CONT Sex: M
2 CONT Birth: 1608 in England
2 CONT Death: BEF 26 FEB 1683/84 in Dartmouth, Bristol Co., Massachusetts
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestry Hints for Richard Sisson
2 CONT 4 possible matches found on Ancestry.com	Ancestry.com
2 CONT '''Marriage''' 1 Mary ?...? b: ABT 1610 in England
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Children
2 CONT # George Sisson b: 1644 in Dartmouth, Bristol Co., Massachusetts
2 CONT # Elizabeth Sisson b: ABT 1647 in Dartmouth, Bristol Co., Massachusetts
2 CONT # John Sisson b: ABT 1652 in Rhode Island
2 CONT # Anne Sisson b: ABT 1654 in Dartmouth, Bristol Co., Massachusetts
2 CONT # James Sisson b: ABT 1656 in Dartmouth, Bristol Co., Massachusetts
2 CONT # Mary Sisson b: ABT 1656 in Dartmouth, Bristol Co., Massachusetts
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source:''' RI Genealogy-Biography, Beers, 1908, Pg 2000 
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Descendants of Richard Sisson: Generation No. 1
2 CONT * RICHARD1 SISSON (Source: Family Group Sheets from John Sisson.) was born ABT 1608 in England (Source: Family Group Sheets from John Sisson.), and died Feb 26, 1683/84 in Portsmouth, Newport, RI (Source: Family Group Sheets from 
2 CONC John Sisson.). He married MARY (Source: Family Group Sheets from John Sisson.) ABT 1644 in Dartmouth, Bristol, MA (Source: Family Group Sheets from John Sisson.). She was born ABT 1615 (Source: Family Group Sheets from John Sisson
2 CONC .), and died Sep 22, 1692 in Dartmouth, Bristol, MA (Source: Family Group Sheets from John Sisson.).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Notes for RICHARD SISSON:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The first american settler of the name Richard Sisson, came hither in the early days of the colony (RI), and was made a *Freeman in Portsmouth in 1653. He afterward resided in Dartmouth, MA, where he was made a *Freeman in 1655. T
2 CONC hat he was active in public affairs is shown by the frequency with which his name appears in the records. At one time he owned considerable interest in **Conanicut and the Dutch Islands. Born in 1608 he died in 1684. The christian
2 CONC  name of his wife was Mary, and their children were George, Elizabeth, James, John, Anne, and Mary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref: RI Genealogy-Biography, Beers, 1908, Pg 2000
2 CONT
2 CONT The founder of the family, Richard Sisson, was born in 1608, probably in England and died in 1684, in Dartmouth, MA. He was a prominent man in both colonies and an extensive landholder, and filled numerous official positions. He w
2 CONC as made a *Freeman in Dartmouth, May 17, 1653. In 1658 he purchased one three hundredth share in the **Conanicut and Dutch Islands, and subsequently purchased another like share, both of which he sold in 1660. In 1667 he was a gra
2 CONC nd juror at Dartmouth and surveyor oh highways June 5, 1671. He first settled in Portsmouth, RI where he had extensive land holdings which were inherited by his descendants. His will, made Oct 18,1683, was proved Feb 26, 1684, mad
2 CONC e careful provisions for his wife Mary; sic.. Bequeathed to his sons James. . . . . . House and land in Dartmouth; to daughter Anne Tripp Land; to John all his housing and land in Portsmouth; to his son George, five pounds; to dau
2 CONC ghter Eliabeth Allen, five pounds; to his indian servant, a horse; to his grandchild Mary Sisson, three cows, a bed and other property. The inventory of his estate amounted to six hundred pounds, nineteen shillings, including live
2 CONC stock valued at one hundred fifty-eight pounds, five shillings. His widow survived him by eight years and her will made April 15, 1690, proved December 1692, disposed of an estate of one hundred and ninety pounds, including one hu
2 CONC ndred twenty pounds in silver.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref: Genealogy of New England, Vol IV, pg 2313
2 CONT
2 CONT *Freeman: "In Colonial days this term referred to any male over twenty-one years old who owned personal property or real estate valued at a prescribed amount. He also was obliged to be a peaceful man, endorsed by a majority of the
2 CONC  town's Freemen. Only after this vote at a semi-annual meeting of the selectmen, could the man take the Freeman's Oath. His duties were to elect deputies to the General Assembly of the colony and to elect new Freemen. He was a mem
2 CONC ber of a local church and had the right to vote and pay taxes."
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref: A to ZAX, A comprehensive dictionary for Genealogists & Historians, Barbara Jean Evans, 1995, Hearthside Press, pg 121.
2 CONT
2 CONT **Conanicut Island is in southern Rhode Island, between Newport (Newport Island) and the mainland of Rhode Island.
2 CONT
