1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Not a known daughter of https://www.geni.com/people/George-Potter-of-London/6000000004180351320?through=6000000029206804397 & https://www.geni.com/people/Martha-Potter/4603783137940025031?through=6000000029206804397
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT * Do not confuse:
2 CONT **Martha (–?–) (Hazard), first wife of Thomas-1 Hazard
2 CONT ** Martha (–?–) (Sheriff) (Hazard) Hues, second wife of Thomas-1 Hazard
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Date of birth has also been (erroneously?) reported to be circa 1600.
2 CONT
2 CONT Place of birth has also been (erroneously?) reported to be Rhode Island.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Martha Hazard'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Born:''' circa 1611
2 CONT * '''Died:''' 1669
2 CONT * '''Note:''' Some sources have her surname as Potter but nothing so far proves that. The Hazard book does not give her surname, nor does Torrey.
2 CONT * '''Marriage:''' BEF 1635
2 CONT * '''Spouse:''' Thomas Hazard,  b: bet 1608 and 1610, of Orset, Nottinghamshire, England; d. aft 1680,  Portsmouth, Newport, Rhode Island
2 CONT * '''Children''' 	
2 CONT **Robert Hazard,   b. 1635, Portsmouth, Rhode Island; d. 1710, Portsmouth, Rhode Island   Age: 75 years
2 CONT **Thomas HAZARD b: ABT. 1636 in Portsmouth, Newport, Rhode Island
2 CONT **Hannah Hazard,   b. 1636-1637, Boston,  Mass; d. 1685, Portsmouth,  Rhode Island, Age: 48 years
2 CONT **Elizabeth Hazard,  b. Abt 1637, Portsmouth, Rhode Island;  d. 8 Nov 1711, Portsmouth, RI
2 CONT **Martha Hazard,   b. 1638, Boston,  Massachusetts;    d. 1686, Kingston, Rhode Island,  Age: 48 years
2 CONT **Jonathan Hazard,   b. 1638, Portsmouth, Newport, Rhode Island;    d. 1711  Age: 73 years
2 CONT **George Hazard,   b. 1646, Portsmouth, Newport, Rhode Island  d. 1743  Age: 97 years
2 CONT **Nathaniel Hazard,   b. Abt 1647, Portsmouth, Newport, Rhode Island
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sources [1] :'''''''''
2 CONT
2 CONT * "The Hazard Family of Rhode Island, 1635-1894"  Robinson, Caroline E. 
2 CONT * "New England Marriages Prior To 1700" Clarence A. Torrey, (as found on NEHGS CD-ROM, Boston, 2001) "HAZARD, Thomas (1610-1680+) & 1/wf Martha _____ (-1669+); by 1635; Boston/Portsmouth, RI
2 CONT * Babcock-Westwater Ancestry - http://www.babcockancestry.com/SS2-o/p101030.htm#c101030.5
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sources [2] (Potter) :'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * http://worldconnect.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=:1912853&id=I0254
2 CONT * http://www.ourfamilyhistories.org/getperson.php?personID=I12669&tree=00
2 CONT * http://www.genealogyofnewengland.com/f_1ee.htm#173
2 CONT * Potter: Descendants of George Potter of Portsmouth, RI (Kenneth G. Potter), Potter, Kenneth G., ((Boston : A. Mudge & Son, 1888., reprinted 1992); CS71.P868 1888).
2 CONT * Title: New England Families Genealogical and Memorial: Third Series, Volume IV, Page: 1684
2 CONT
2 CONT ==comments==
2 CONT
2 CONT No proof to support parents of [http://www.geni.com/people/George-A-Potter/6000000004180351320 George Potter] and [http://www.geni.com/people/Martha-Potter/4603783137940025031 Martha]. Disconnected July 2014
2 CONT  ************************** http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~streeter/hazard.pdf THOMAS HAZARD OF PORTSMOUTH, RHODE ISLAND © 1999 Perry Streeter  3273 NYS Route 248 d Canisteo, NY 14823,  perry@streeter.com  www.perry.stre
2 CONC eter.com  18 Feb 2001
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.'''THOMAS1 HAZARD''' was '''born''' about 1610 and '''died''' in 1680. 
2 CONT * He married first MARTHA. Martha '''died''' in 1669; 
2 CONT * Thomas married second MARTHA (	) SHERIFF; she '''died''' in 1691; she was the '''widow of Thomas Sheriff'''.
2 CONT * At Portsmouth, on 30 January 1658, Thomas Hazard deeded as dower, with his daughter Hannah Hazard, to Stephen Wilcox of Portsmouth, 34 acres adjoining Thomas' farm. On 10 December, Thomas deeded to Thomas Sherriff, a house, 30 a
2 CONC cres, orchard, etc. in Portsmouth for £20 and a quarter of a share in Misquamicut. The Sheriffs were not to take possession of the deeded property until Thomas Hazard died. 
2 CONT ** Ironically, Thomas Sheriff died first and his widow, Martha, married Thomas Hazard. (Austin, 320)
2 CONT * On 30 November 1669, in '''a will that was voided by subsequent ones''', Thomas named wife Martha, daughters Elizabeth, wife of George Lawton, Hannah, wife of Stephen Wilcox of Misquamicut, and Martha, wife of Ichabod Potter, so
2 CONC n Robert Hazard, and grandson Thomas Hazard (Austin, 320). 
2 CONT * On 16 October 1674, Thomas testified in a dispute regarding the corn mill at Pawtuxet between Stephen Arnold and Samuel Reape. Thomas stated that he was sixty-four years old (Austin, 320). On 29 May 1675, Thomas made a formal st
2 CONC atement prior to declaring marriage intentions with Martha (______) Sheriff:
2 CONT * This is to notify all men whom it may concern, whereas there is a promise of matrimony betwixt Thomas Hazard and Martha Sheriff, yet I, the aforesaid Thomas Hazard, do take the same Martha Sheriff for her own person, without hav
2 CONC ing anything to do with her estate, or anything that is hers for matter of goods, etc. (Austin, 320)
2 CONT * This prenuptial agreement of sorts was probably intended to reassure the heirs of Thomas and Martha (  ) Sheriff that Thomas Hazard would not interfere with the inheritance they had envisioned. Apparently, relations between Thom
2 CONC as and his only son, Robert, were strained at one point. Thomas made another formal declaration on 6 August 1677:
2 CONT ** That whereas son Robert claims right to my lands by virtue of a writing as he saith by me formerly passed and given him, &c.—this is denied. I, Thomas Hazard, do by these presents solemnly protest and affirm that I never made a
2 CONC ny writing, only in a will drawn by John Porter at John Lawton's house about thirty years past, wherein if I had then deceased, being sick and weak, my said son was to have had my lands and my other children my movables; nor made 
2 CONC any writing to said son only in a will 1669, Nov. 30, in which I appointed him executor, since which time have seen cause to make null and void, and do absolutely abolish the two wills and have made another will dated 1676, Nov. 6
2 CONC . (Austin, 320)
2 CONT * In his will, proved 1680, his wife Martha, whom he calls his "beloved yoke- fellow," is sole executrix, and he gives her "all movable and immovable estate, as housing, goods, cattle, and chattels, etc." To his son Robert he give
2 CONC s 1s. To his daughters, Hannah Wilcox and Martha Potter, wife of Ichabod Potter, 1s. (Source?)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children, by Martha Potter:'''
2 CONT * ii. '''ROBERT'''2, '''b.''' 1635; '''m.''' MARY BROWNELL.
2 CONT * iii. '''ELIZABETH''', '''m.''' GEORGE LAWTON.
2 CONT * iv.  '''HANNAH''', '''m.''' STEPHEN WILCOX, son of Edward and (  ) Wilcox. 
2 CONT * v.  '''MARTHA''', '''m.''' (1) ICHABOD2 POTTER (Nathaniel1), son of Nathaniel1 and Dorothy (  ) Potter; 
2 CONT ** '''m.''' (2) BENJAMIN MOWRY, son of Roger and Mary (  ) Mowry.
2 CONT
2 CONT  ************************** '''Find A Grave Memorial# 37073770'''; http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=37073770
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Martha Hazard'''
2 CONT * '''Birth:''' unknown
2 CONT * '''Death:''' unknown
2 CONT * Thomas Hazard married (1) by about 1628 Martha _____.
2 CONT * They had four children: Robert, Elizabeth Lawton, Hannah Wilcox, & Martha Potter Mowry.
2 CONT ** With the exception of daughter Hannah whose baptism is recorded in Boston, our estimates for the dates of births of the daughters are based on very crude approximations. Even so, we note the gap of about seven years between the
2 CONC  first two children and the last two. 
2 CONT ** This might indicate that the immigrant had an earlier wife who was the mother of the first two children, but the evidence in hand is insufficient to make this more than a suggestion.   '''Family links:''' 
2 CONT *  '''Spouse:''' Thomas Hazard (1610 - ____)* 
2 CONT *  '''Children:'''
2 CONT ** Elizabeth Hazard Brightman (1626 - 1711)*
2 CONT ** Robert Hazard (1635 - ____)*
2 CONT * '''Burial:'''  Unknown
2 CONT  ************************** 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT This person migrated to New England during the Puritan Great Migration (1620-1640). (see The Great Migration (Series 2), by R. C. Anderson, vol. 3, p. 294-8)
