2 CONT ==Notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Thomas Hazard was one of the first settlers of Aquidneck, and was appointed, with Nicholas Easton and Robert Jeffries, to lay out the town of Newport. The colonial records of Massachusetts contain the following entries, which ha
2 CONC ve been furnished me by Mr. Hazard Stevens, son of the late General Stevens, late Governor of Washington Territory: "September 3,1634, Mr. Nicholas Easton admitted freeman [at the General Court]"; "May 25, 1636, Thomas Hassard adm
2 CONC itted freeman."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children and Wives info:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT http://jrm.phys.ksu.edu/Genealogy/Needham/d0005/I3485.html
2 CONT
2 CONT '''two marriages:'''
2 CONT * 1st wife: Martha ( widow of Thomas Sheriffe ) surname unknown.
2 CONT * 2nd wife: Martha Potter Abt. 1633 probably St. Bride, Middlesex, England, UK
2 CONT
2 CONT From:  http://thesaltysailor.com/rhodeisland-philatelic/rhodeisland/stampless38c.htm ''The Hazard Family Letters 1832-1950''  History of the Hazard Family of Peace Dale, Rhode Island 
2 CONT
2 CONT * The Hazard family of Peace Dale Rhode Island were among the original settlers in Rhode Island. The first Hazard in America was Thomas Hazard who emigrated from Wales around 1630. He originally settled in the colony of New Jersey
2 CONC  and then moved to Boston, Massachusetts in 1636. Sometime in between 1636 and 1638 he founded Newtown on Long Island, New York and then moved to Rhode Island in the spring of 1639. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''The Hazard Family motto''' is "Be Just and Fear Not." 
2 CONT * '''Thomas had 7 sons:''' Robert, George, Jeremiah, Benjamin, Stephen, Jonathan and Thomas.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From:'''  http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~streeter/hazard.pdf 
2 CONT
2 CONT * There is a long line of descendants from this daughter [of Thomas1 and Martha ( ) Hazard], Martha, and Ichabod Potter, with frequent intermarriages in the Hazard family. 
2 CONT * In the early history of the family it was almost an exception to find a Hazard who did not marry a cousin, and it is a curious fact that the lines in which these marriages were the most frequent, were often marked by the stronge
2 CONC st men and women, both mentally and physically. (Robinson, 1-2)
2 CONT * Robinson included another Hazard researcher's assessment of the family in her genealogy:
2 CONT ** The Hazards are a strongly marked race, handing down and retaining certain peculiarities from generation to generation. One is, a peculiar decision of character, a certain amount of pride, and a pronounced independence, coupled
2 CONC  with a slight reserve. Physically they are strongly marked. Generally speaking, they are of good stature and vigorous frames with rather a square head, high forehead, brown hair, blue eyes, straight or aquiline nose, and with wil
2 CONC l shown by a firmly set jaw. Their complexion is fair, a little inclined to florid. (Thomas R. Hazard, Recollections of Olden Times as quoted by Robinson)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married Martha _____? Emigrated to Boston in 1635. 
2 CONT * '''Occupation:''' Ship's Carpenter. 
2 CONT * He was admitted a freeman in Boston 25 Mar 1638. 
2 CONT * He was a freeman of Portsmouth, Rhode Island in 1640. 
2 CONT * On 28 Apr 1639 he signed, along with eight others, the contract for the settlement of Newport, Rhode Island. 
2 CONT * On 12 Mar 1640 he was appointed a member of the General Court of Elections. 
2 CONT * He became a member of Governor's Council 10 Dec 1654. 
2 CONT * He was a member, for Newton, L. I., of the Provincial Convention of November and December, 1653.  '''Source:''' http://worldconnect.rootsweb.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=wdwrth&id=I498) 
2 CONT
