1 NOTE {geni:about_me} HISTORY OF THE TOWN OF STONINGTON, County of New London, Connecticut, from its first settlement in 1649 to 1900, by Richard Anson Wheeler, New London, CT, 1900, p. 631
2 CONT ----
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Almy-23
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Almy-23 created 7 Feb 2011 | Last modified 5 Feb 2022
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Katherine (Almy) Brown (bef. 1629 - abt. 1703) 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Katherine "Catharen, Catherine, Katherin" Brown formerly Almy aka Almey, West===
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Born before 18 Mar 1629 in South Kilworth, Leicestershire, England [uncertain]
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter of William Almy and Audrey (Barlowe) Almy
2 CONT
2 CONT Sister of Anne (Almy) Greene, Christopher Almy, John Almy and Job Almy
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife of Bartholomew West — married 1652 (to before 1675) [location unknown]
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife of Nicholas Brown — married 1675 (to 1703) [location unknown]
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother of Stephen West, William West, John West and Anne (West) Wooley
2 CONT
2 CONT Died about 1703 in Shrewsbury, Monmouth, Province of New Jersey
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT To Be Worked
2 CONT
2 CONT "Katherin Almey the daughter of Will[ia]m Almey and... baptized the [18?] day of March in the year 1628[/9]."[1] This baptism was located after the publication of Anderson's PGM entry on her father.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Catherine, b. say 1630; m. about 1652 Bartholomew West"[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In her father's will of 28 February 1676/7, "Catharen" was willed the "cattle and moveables" with her sister and two brothers (after the death of her mother). He gave to "'my grandchild Bartholomew West' £20 when he comes to age."
2 CONC [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT "Nicholas Brown, son of Nicholas, was of Shark River, where he died about 1714. He took the Oath in 1668, and was appointed Ensign by the Dutch in 1673. In 1683 he brought an action against Captain Christopher Almy. He was three t
2 CONC imes married. The name of his first wife has not been recovered. Katherine Almy, widow of Bartholomew West, became his second wife, and she dying in 1703, Mary daughter of John and Mary Chambers." (Hornor)
2 CONT From Anderson's Great Migration.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In a letter of estimated date 23 May 1650 to John Winthrop Jr., Roger Williams, in describing the activities of the crew of a French prize vessel, reported that "one of them (having lain with Mr. Amie's daughter of Portsmouth) is 
2 CONC like now to marry her. The parents and the English are troubled greatly." This cannot be daughter Annis, who had married John Greene by 1649, so, unless there is a third daughter otherwise unrecorded, this record must refer to dau
2 CONC ghter Catherine. She does not appear on the 1635 passenger list with her parents and two siblings. If she was born in New England, then, it could be no earlier than the latter half of 1635, which would make her no more than fourte
2 CONC en at the time of the incident noted above. The probable resolution is that Catherine was born in England just before or after her brother Christopher, and either was omitted from the passenger list or came at a later time. We the
2 CONC refore estimate her date of birth as about 1630, although a date around 1634 would also be consistent with the other evidence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Furthermore, if this record does apply to Catherine, then she could not have been married to Bartholomew West in 1650. Pending more detailed investigation of the family of Bartholomew West, we estimate that the marriage took place
2 CONC  about 1652.
2 CONT Land Records
2 CONT
2 CONT The following can be retrieved from the New Jersey State Archives; PDFs are available for most.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Anno 1675
2 CONT 2 November: a warrant to the Surveyor Generall to lay out for Stephen, William, and Adey West in or about ye limits & bounds of the towne of Shrewsbury to each of them sixty acres of upland & meadow in proportion and  ??? a certif
2 CONC icate there of ????? ye secretaries' office and etc
2 CONT "Ditto. A warrant to lay out for Katherin Browne the late widow of Bartholomew West within the limits and bounds of the towne of Shrewsbury for right of her deceased husband and her selfe from ye yeare 1666, 180 acres of upland & 
2 CONC meadow in proportion and etc.
2 CONT In 1679, Katherine Brown was listed as a neighboring landholder in deeds of Judah Allen, Francis Borden, Joseph Parker, Stephen West, John Chamnis, and John Havens.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Katherine owned land in Shrewsbury, Monmouth County, and her husband was a resident of Shrewsbury when he wrote his will in 1711, so this is likely her place of death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Per Horner, Katherine died before 1703; she was certainly deceased before 1710 when Nicholas Brown had a daughter with his third wife.
2 CONT
