1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Her parents are unknown.  Do NOT add parents.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Source for Mary Blucks as wife of this John King is  [http://books.google.com/books?id=rLg-AAAAYAAJ&dq=Mary%20Blucks%20John%20King&pg=PA357#v=onepage&q=Mary%20Blucks%20John%20King&f=false  The New England Historical & Genealogical
2 CONC  Register and Antiquarian Journal, Volume 11 (Google eBook)  S.G. Drake, 1857] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Be cautious, the writer says "It is said that  his father's name was John King and that he married Mary Blucks in England but whether they came to America is not known.."  So there is NO evidence for Mary Blucks other than what th
2 CONC e writer has heard.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth surname possibly Blucks?
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary, first wife of John King of Weymouth (1600 - 1669) was likely from Dorchester, Dorset, England.  Her parents are unknown.  She was the mother of all of his children:  John, Samuel, Mary, Abigail, and Philip.  She died circa 1
2 CONC 645.  He remarried twice afterwards.  He married his second wife circa 1647 and Philip was born 1645, so Mary died 1645 or slightly later.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first Weymouth, Massachusetts settlers, facing starvation, Indian attacks and no leadership from colony founder Thomas Weston, scattered up and down the seaboard.
2 CONT
2 CONT John King Returned to Weymouth
2 CONT
2 CONT John King, and perhaps some of the other 1622 settlers, returned to Weymouth. King went to a Maine fishing village in 1623, then to England. He returned to Weymouth with his wife, Mary, and became an extensive landowner. A mariner
2 CONC , he traveled throughout New England and his name is on several early town records. Weymouth’s King’s Cove, King’s Grove and King Oak Hill are named for him. Descendants include brothers William King, an early Maine governor, and 
2 CONC U. S. Constitution signer Rufus King.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ben M. Angel notes: I've removed the burial location of the Austrian-Hungarian Empire. Having difficulties imagining how that political entity would have even figured in to this person's life, much less how to justify how her Engl
2 CONC ish-born deceased body would have been shipped from the New World to the European Continent.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT {{Unsourced}}
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT This person is highly doubtful or the records are being confused;in 1646 Northampton and what is now the county of Hampshire, Massachusetts did NOT exist.Northampton was established in 1653 and the John A King(1629–1703) associate
2 CONC d with its naming is not the same nor is related to this John King of Weymouth.<br>
2 CONT This John King in Weymouth may have been associated with the Saugus Ironwork and later the Braintree Iron works. His son Philip established the Taunton ironworks with the Leonards and Deans.<br>
2 CONT The Ancestry of Mrs Florence Dean Stickney 1901 at the Old Colony Historical Society does say that John King of Weymouth has a wife named Mary or maybe Dorothy, but this is uncertain and more family tradition than documentation. T
2 CONC hese Ancestry Family tree GEDCOMs are very unreliable especially for this time period and are often confuse and mix up people and places. Best Regards David Blackwell 2020.
2 CONT VItal Records of Weymouth Massachusetts to 1850 published 1910. King Marriages:https://archive.org/details/vitalrecordsofwe02weym/page/108/mode/2up<br>
2 CONT Not here.<br>
2 CONT King family deaths:<br>
2 CONT https://archive.org/details/vitalrecordsofwe02weym/page/292/mode/2up/<br>
2 CONT Not here.<br>
2 CONT VItal Records of Weymouth Massachusetts to 1850 published 1910. King birth records<br>
2 CONT VItal Records of Weymouth Massachusetts to 1850 published 1910https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=mdp.39015010559204&view=1up&seq=165Show John King with the birth of daughters Abigail 1641 and Mary 1639;but no wife is mentioned
2 CONC .<br>
2 CONT  The Samuel and Sarah King family with records in the VRW are thought to be his son and daughter in law.<br>
2 CONT  They are believed to have been involved in the Braintree Ironworks which harvested bog ore from the area of Smelt Brook, Cranberry pond, up to what is now Great Pond in Weymouth (which is larger than the original pond). The King 
2 CONC family were bloomers and forgers. The bloomery melted and purified the bog ore into cast iron which could be worked in the forge. <br>
2 CONT   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bog_iron <br>
2 CONT  In addition to the early and often incomplete vital records of Massachusetts towns county records of deeds, court cases and will proving often are a source of documentations. <br>
2 CONT  These are often idiosyncratic  of the recorder. Here is a good pampleton the early recorders of Suffolk county Massashacusetts<br>
2 CONT https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=loc.ark:/13960/t3nv9mg5p&view=1up&seq=5   <br>
2 CONT  Some Suffolk county  record are  online here http://genealogyink.blogspot.com/2018/03/suffolk-deed-books-suffolk-county.html <br>
2 CONT
2 CONT Here is a good list of sourceshttps://www.familysearch.org/wiki/en/Suffolk_County,_Massachusetts_Genealogy 
2 CONT
2 CONT And there is an index https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/008729804
2 CONT
2 CONT == Name ==: Name:  Mary /Blucks. <ref>Source: [[#S2]] Text:  http://trees.ancestry.com/pt/AMTCitationRedir.aspx?tid=26914960&pid=2006652844
2 CONT Note: [[#N1343]]</ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT : Marriage Date:  1626: Place:  Northampton, Hampshire, Massachusetts.  <ref>Source: [[#S291503039]] Page:  Source number: 8199.000; Source type: Electronic Database; Number of Pages: 1; Submitter Code: BFO.  Note:  http://trees.a
2 CONC ncestry.com/rd?f=sse&db=worldmarr_ga&h=695687&ti=0&indiv=try&gss=pt  - Text:  Birth date:  1600Birth place:  MA Marriage date:  1626 Marriage place:  MA. APID:  7836::695687</ref> : Mary Blucks 1614 - 1640, married John Goodman Ki
2 CONC ng 1600 - 1670 had ason Samuel King 1640 - 1711. <ref>Source <span id='S2'>S2</span>Title:  Ancestry Family Trees. Publication:  Name: Online publication - Provo, UT, USA: Ancestry.com.  Original data:  Family Tree files submitted
2 CONC  by Ancestry members.  Repository: [[#R1]] Repository <span id='R1'>R1</span></ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Comments==
2 CONT The  death date for Mary does not agree with the birth of four their children. According to the dates provided, Mary was about 12 years old when she married John. Given that there is no source for Mary's birth,she could have been 
2 CONC older. Their first child was born seven years after John and Marry married which seems like a long interval but there could have been children born before John in 1633. They could have diedin infancy but they are not recorded here
2 CONC . Samuel was born in 1640, Abigail was born in 1641 (a year or so after Mary's death), Philip was born in 1645 (5 years after Mary died) and Thomas was born 10 years after Mary died. 
2 CONT
