2 CONT According to the "John Whitman Of Weymouth, Mass." book by Charles H. Farnam, John (1) left London, England on either the ship the "True Love" or the "Confidence". It is said that '''John (1) left his wife Ruth, whom he married in
2 CONC  1625, and their four children and came over on the "True Love"''' with his brother Zachariah, but John's name does not appear on the manifest. Zachariah's does, along with his wife Sarah and one child. The "Confidence" also does 
2 CONC not contain John's name.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''History of the Descendants of John Whitman of Weymouth, Mass'''
2 CONT '''by Charles Farnam  1889'''
2 CONT
2 CONT John Whitman, one of the earliest settlers of the town of Weymouth, in the State of Massachusetts, is the Ancestor, if not of all, yet of nearly all of the name of Whitman, as well as of an equal number, at least, bearing other na
2 CONC mes, in this country. He came from England, but from what particular part of it cannot be ascertained. He may have originated from Holt, in the county of Norfolk. The name of Whiteman has existed there. In the list of those copied
2 CONC  into Winthrop's Journal, Avho were admitted freemen, the name of the above John is spelled with an e, as are those of Holt. Governor Winthrop and his company came principally from the vicinity, and, probably, some of them, from t
2 CONC lie town of Holt. And John came over, if not at the same time, yet not many years after, and attached himself to the same company, and, it may be presumed, was allured thither by his neighbors and friends, who either came with or 
2 CONC had preceded him a few years. The "e" may have been omitted by him, as he and his posterity have ever written their names without it.
2 CONT
2 CONT The exact date of his settlement in Weymouth has not been ascertained, but he appears among those who received allotments of land in the list of 1642.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Weymouth records is found this entry :
2 CONT "died 18.17, 1662, Ruth, wife of John Whitman;" there is evidently an error as to date, which should probably read: " d. 8.17, 1662;" some think this was the wife of John, while others think it refers to Ruth Reed, wife of John". 
2 CONC The date of the birth of the eldest child of John, viz: Ruth, b. Feb. 1, 1663, would, however, indicate that it must be the wife of John. The loss of the early church records on which the name of the wife of John would appear dest
2 CONC roys any opportunity of clearing the mystery regarding- her name.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had four sons and five daughters, all but one of whom survived him, and six of whom lived to be over eighty.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Coat of Arms was obtained from Thomas Culleton of London, a well-known genealogist. It has been impossible to procure absolute proof of the right of this family to use the same. A similar shield with varying crests but the sam
2 CONC e motto has, however, been in the possession of several branches since the latter part of the last century. This fact has induced the writer to insert the plate, hoping that at some future time more absolute proof can be obtained.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
