1 NAME James /Leonard/ of Taunton
2 GIVN James
2 SURN Leonard
2 NSFX of Taunton
2 _MARNM Leonard
2 NICK Col. James Leonard
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1621
2 ADDR
3 CITY Pontypool
3 STAE Wales
1 BURI
2 DATE AFT 18 SEP 1691
1 DEAT
2 DATE 18 SEP 1691
2 ADDR
3 CITY Taunton
3 STAE Province of Massachusetts
1 OCCU Iron Manufacturing, proprietor of Taunton Ironworks
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://bradsport.com/aJamesLeonard1620/
2 CONT
2 CONT James Leonard’s father was Thomas Leonard, a fyner who traveled about the west of England working at various ironworks. His grandfather was Henry Leonard, about whom almost nothing is known. James was not a descendant of Sampson L
2 CONC ennard, Lord Dacre. More about the deeper ancestry of the Leonards is to be found at Origins of the MA Leonards.  James settled inTaunton, MA, after initially working at the Massachusetts Bay Colony’s ironworks in Lynn/Saugus and 
2 CONC Braintree.
2 CONT
2 CONT James’ descendants test in Haplogroup J2, also known as J-L70. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His descendants here number about 7,000+ through 10 generations.  Some in the 11th generation and later generations are still alive and are not listed for that reason.
2 CONT
2 CONT The main source for this descendancy is the unpublished typescript and charts compiled by Elisha Clark Leonard 1819-1894 and his grandson, George Marston Leonard 1880-1959. I’ve checked their information against other sources, suc
2 CONC h as NEHGS and VR’s, and added to their information from personal contacts, census data, and ancestry.com.  Corrections and additions welcome!
2 CONT
2 CONT Possible sources of confusion include the descendants of Solomon Leonard 1610-1671 who lived in Bridgewater, MA, neighboring Taunton, Raynham, Norton, and Middleborough where the Iron Leonards resided.  Also, the descendants of Wi
2 CONC lliam Leonard ca.1685-aft.1750 and Sarah Bolton inhabited Bridgewater and neighboring towns in the 1700’s.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT [https://minerdescent.com/2011/11/14/richard-martin-sr/]<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT James’ parents were  Thomas Leonard, 1577-1638, and Lydia White. He emigrated  from Pontipool, Monmouthshire, Wales, with his brothers  Henry, and Philip.   These immigrants were the founders of the first successful iron-works in 
2 CONC America.
2 CONT
2 CONT “It is said that the Leonards had been in the iron industry for twelve hundred years, since the days of the “forestsmiths” of Germany, where the name Leonard is found in old German records of the sixth century. The Saxon Leonards,
2 CONC  workmen in metals, came to England very early and settled among the iron hills of Kent and Sussex. Later, as the mines in this vicinity were less productive, some of them removed to the iron mining districts of Wales from whence 
2 CONC James and Henry Leonard came, leaving their forges in England “plastered with mortgages,” not only at Pontipool but also at Belaton, Stafford County. In the nineteenth century the Leonards might have redeemed their title to this p
2 CONC roperty, but it would have involved an expensive and lengthy suit in the Court of Chancery, which was not undertaken.
2 CONT
2 CONT “Being well versed in the iron lore, the secrets of which had been long handed down from father to son, James and Henry Leonard, on their arrival in America, at first found employment with one John Winthrop at his bloomery near Ly
2 CONC nn, established by Adam Hawkes in 1630. The following entry has been found in an old account book of Winthrop’s dated 1651. “James Leonard, fifteen days’ worke in ye forge oe 1.13.0.”
2 CONT
2 CONT “After a short connection with John Winthrop’s iron effort at Braintree, the Leonard Brothers struck out for themselves, testing the streams and ponds for chalybeate evidence, little Thomas and James who had come holding onto “Unc
2 CONC le Henry’s finger,” probably having the time of their lives fishing with birch rods on these expeditions. Their elders found large deposits of bog iron, particularly in Quittacus Lake, Middleboro, which were extracted by means of 
2 CONC great tongs from the lakes and swamps. They made a contract with the town of Taunton to set up a bloomery there. A stock company was formed, one of the stock holders being Elizabeth Pole, who had bought Taunton from the Indians fo
2 CONC r a peck of beans.
2 CONT
2 CONT “The Leonards called their bloomery Raynham forge, doubtless from Raynham in England, which is the station where one alights to visit Belhus mansion at Aveley Easex, the head quarters of the English Leonards where the beautiful po
2 CONC rtraits are of our English ancestors. The owner, Sir Thomas Barrett Leonard, is a landed proprietor of at least 10,000 acres of land inherited from the early Leonards. It may be that James and Henry Leonard lived here in their boy
2 CONC hoods and had childhood’s associations with Raynham, for which they named their forge. The site of this old forge which was carried on by seven generations of Leonards, was pointed out to me by my father, when as a child I rode wi
2 CONC th him through Raynham to Taunton.
2 CONT
2 CONT “This was the parent forge for many others not only in this vicinity but all over the Atlantic seaboard of the United States, substantiating the famous saying “Wherever you find iron works you will find a Leonard.” The proudest ac
2 CONC complishment of these various forges was in 1775, when Eliphalet Leonard of the fifth generation made in Easton the first bar of American steel.
2 CONT
2 CONT “James Leonard was a warm friend of the good Indian chief Massasoit who used frequently to visit him, sleeping under his roof and eating his bread. James gave him every assistance in the repair of his guns and making his weapons a
2 CONC nd tools. Massasoit, before his death, required a solemn oath of his son Philip that he would never harm a Leonard, and Philip in 1675 in an imposing meeting in Taunton Church at which James Leonard was present, affixed his mark t
2 CONC o a document promising peace with the men of Taunton. Philip’s tribe molested the white settlers in Middleboro and New Bedford, but the inhabitants of Taunton and Bridgewater suffered little in King Philip’s war, and no harm was d
2 CONC one to the Leonards with Philip’s consent. Thus the name of Leonard represents to Taunton not only splendid enterprise, but the hospitality and friendliness which secured safety for the town at a critical period. King Philip had a
2 CONC  summer home near the Leonards, and Lake Nipenicket between Raynham and Bridgewater was a favorite fishing ground of his. There is a tradition that Philip’s head was secreted after his death under the old Leonard house in Raynham.
2 CONT
2 CONT “James Leonard, the immigrant, died in 1691. His wife, Mary Martin, had died earlier and he had married a second wife named Margaret.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=25564368
2 CONT
2 CONT James LEONARD and Margaret FORD were married before 1662 in Taunton, Bristol, MA.3 Margaret FORD (daughter of William FORD and ANNA) was born about 1632. She died before 9 Apr 1701 in Taunton, Bristol Co., MA. Was her last name Fo
2 CONC rd? Will of Margaret Leonard of Taunton, widow,about the 68th year of her age, dtd. November 12, 1700, prob. April 9,1701. Mentions son-in-law Uriah Leonard's wife Elizabeth and his daughter Margaret. Daughter-in-law Hannah Deane 
2 CONC and her daughter Abigail Terry,son-in-law James Leonard's daughters Abigail and Prudence Lewis.Son-in-law Thomas Leonard's daughters Elizabeth and Johanah. Cousin Eleazer Carver to be exec. (She was probably step-mother, not mothe
2 CONC r-in-law). (2:30). Problem: Thomas Leonard didn't have a daughter named Johanah. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ONLY Known Friend/Documented Of the Great Sachem (Chief) Massasoit of the Wampanoag People  was James Leonard Sr. Frequently Entertained Massasoit and King Philip&Families ,Hunted Together, etc.  Who journeyed from Mt. Hope to the
2 CONC  hunting grounds at Fowling Pond. Fowling Pond is in Raynham, was one mile north of the Ancient Iron Works on present-day King Philip's Street near the end of Mill Street. Fowling Pond was said to be two miles long and three-quart
2 CONC ers of a mile wide in King Philip's time, but today has disappeared. James repaired their guns and conferred favors that led to a lasting friendship. King Philip conveyed to James Leonard about two hundred and fifty acres at Matta
2 CONC poisett Neck in Swansea in October 1665, but the deed was lost by the Plymouth Court. Tradition says that out the outbreak of King Philip's War in 1675, Philip gave strict orders that his men were never to harm a Leonard (although
2 CONC  young Uriah Leonard was almost shot by King Philip's men early in the war, a bullet having passed through his hat as he rode his horse to escape an attack). It is conjectured that because of the Leonards Taunton was not attacked 
2 CONC during the war. (Philip'sorders were actually not to disturb certain families including those of James Leonard, John Brown, and Capt. Thomas Willett, all of Taunton --Hurd, p. 346). 
2 CONT
2 CONT One peculiarity to check out: although several Leonards were officers in the militia of the time, there's little mention of Leonards fighting in Philip's War. Bodge in Soldiers of King Philip's War mentions Jacob as serving under 
2 CONC Capt. Woodworth, Thomas credited under Capt. Thomas Brattle October 19, 1675, and Thomas at Lynn, August 24, 1676. More research needs to be done on the activities of the Leonards during Philip's War. 
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the garrison houses used during King Philip's War was the Samuel Leonard house erected in 1653 by James Leonard at the site of Taunton's Ancient Iron Works Company now in Raynham. A memorial plaque marking the spot is locat
2 CONC ed seven-tenths of a mile east from Route 44 along the southside of Route 104. One of the garrison houses used during King Philip's War was the Samuel Leonard house erected in 1653 by James Leonard at the site of Taunton's Ancient
2 CONC  Iron Works Company now in Raynham. A memorial plaque marking the spot is located seven-tenths of a mile east from Route 44 along the southside of Route 104. James came to America in 1643. Erected first forge furnace in Plymouth C
2 CONC olony (Taunton, MA)     Photo added by Gr. Grandson- Elwin C. Nickerson- /ECN/ James Leonard frequently entertained Massasoit and King Philip, who journeyed from Mt. Hope to the hunting grounds at Fowling Pond. Fowling Pond is in 
2 CONC Raynham, was one mile north of the Ancient Iron Works on present-day King Philip's Street near the end of Mill Street. Fowling Pond was said to be two miles long and three-quarters of a mile wide in King Philip's time, but today h
2 CONC as disappeared. James repaired their guns and conferred favors that led to a lasting friendship. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT There is uncertainty as to when and how James Leonard first came to America.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Land Patent Books of Virginia, Book 1, page 23, shows Robert Bennett granted 700 acres...for transportation of 14 persons, including James Leonard, June 26, 1635. One theory is that James came first to Virginia,then to Marylan
2 CONC d, then to Providence, then to Lynn. 
2 CONT *Source: The Leonard Dictionary, Volume III (manuscript). Duplicate record August 18, 1637.
2 CONT *Another source is Charles Edward Banks' "Topographical Dictionary of 2885 English Emigrants to New England 1620-1650," Baltimore, Genealogical Publishing Co., 1963, 3rd edition, p. 148, citing Various References: NEGR 5/104. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Apparently, employees and recruits of John Winthrop were sometimes not listed as passengers, since they were not paying passengers on those particular vessels crossing the Atlantic.
2 CONT
2 CONT He arrived before 1650 from Pontypool, Wales, although some sources say he first came to Providence, RI, in 1645. He was paid for bringing his goods from Providence by the Lynn/Saugus Ironworks in 1651. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On January10, 1645/6 in Providence, 25 acres of land were granted to a number of inhabitants, including James Leonard, but his name had been crossed out. He was the Ironmaster of Taunton, having first participated development of t
2 CONC he iron works at Braintree and Saugus.
2 CONT
2 CONT But there appear to have been Leonards in the Pontypool area since the early 1600's. A Thomas Leonard mentioned in deed of July 29, 1633, bordering lands of John Powell, John Gerbon, and Phillip Morgan in Trevethin (Parish near Po
2 CONC ntypool, with a bridge near swamp and pool there in 1490 -- pool later became forge pond). An ironworks was in operation before 1634, and there's a record of a complaint against John Wylde for failure to collect monies from it, in
2 CONC stead selling iron at a discount to his friends. Thomas Morgan was recorded as selling charcoal to it in 1640. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The works were apparently owned by the Hanburys, probably Richard. August 1618. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *Thomas, son of Jacob Leonard, was baptized January 9,1699; 
2 CONT *William, son of Jacob Leonard, was baptized July 23, 1696; 
2 CONT *Gwenllian, wife of Thomas Leonard, buried March 15, 1656; 
2 CONT *Mary Leonard married Alexander Lewis January 26, 1656; 
2 CONT *a son of Philip Leonard was born October 27, 1656. 
2 CONT *Sarah, daughter of James Leonard, baptized September 1, 1705; 
2 CONT *Ann, daughter of James Leonard, baptized March 13, 1702. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Local records include a mention of a Thomas Leonard in 1790, a John and Mary Leonard who died at age 84 in 1774. 
2 CONT
2 CONT These indicate there were Leonards and ironmaking in the Pontypool area after James and Thomas left. These Leonards had names identical to or similar to those who emigrated to America. There was even a Theophilus Leonard, iron ref
2 CONC iner,who died March 31, 1900 in nearby Pontnewydd, Wales, perhaps just a coincidence. 
2 CONT *(Source: old documents at the Monmouthshire County records office near Pontypool, October 2003. A researcher with more time could probably find some interesting material here.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisha Clark Leonard paid 5 pounds to a clergyman in Pontypool to check the records for James and Henry, but he reportedly found nothing. GML reported that later researchers found nothing about them either. So the theory is that J
2 CONC ames and Henry were not in Pontypool very long.
2 CONT
2 CONT Probably James and his young family (and his older brother Henry) were also ironworkers in the Bilston, Staffordshire (Cheshire?), area prior to their immigration. Bilston became a center of the "Black Country" iron industry. Geor
2 CONC ge Marston Leonard includes a note on one of his tables that "James, son of Thomas, son of Henry of Billston, Staffordshire..."from McKenzie, Colonial Families, Vol. IV. Apparently, the Leonards left a claim to the ownership of so
2 CONC me heavily mortgaged ironworks there, moving on as the mining districts became less productive. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Years later (1821?) an ironworker in Bilston by the name of James Leonard sent a letter to James Leonard, ironworker in or near Taunton, MA stating that the extensive iron works there in Bilston belonged to the Leonards. The Leona
2 CONC rds in Taunton decided not to undertake the expense of an extended suit to regain the works. The Leonards may also have been involved in some of the ironworks in Somersetshire, England, and Pontypool, Monmouthshire, Wales, as well
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT James Leonard was but a short time at the Saugus Ironworks and at Braintree for a longer time. At sale of the Braintree works, he became a partner. With the invitation from Taunton, he moved there, erected a forge and furnace, and
2 CONC  continued as master workman, a position he held for the rest of his life. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ECL believes Oliver Purchase was the one who induced Henry and James along with Ralph Russell to come to Taunton. He conveyed the two hearths at Taunton to his sons, Thomas and James, and they in turn conveyed them to their sons. 
2 CONC He purchased a lot on Mill River and erected a one-hearth forge, which he called Whittinton Forge. His son Joseph was the master workman at Whittinton Forge. His two other sons, Benjamin and Uriah, were also trained as "bloomers."
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT About 1682 James Leonard built a house for himself a short distance from the Taunton (Raynham) Ironworks on the north side of the road. It was a gambled roof house two stories in front and running back to one story in the rear. Wh
2 CONC en he died in 1691, he left an estate valued at 500 pounds, a very respectable sum in those days (from Elisha Clark Leonard and George Marston Leonard's unpublished manuscript).
2 CONT
2 CONT More about the involvement of James and Henry Leonard in early ironworks in Massachusetts and New Jersey can be found in Bill Barton's articles,"The Establishment of the Iron Industry in America," "Pre-American Ancestry of Our Leo
2 CONC nard Ironworkers," and "Leonard Siblings Henry, James,Philip, Sarah, and Thomas in America and Some of Their Descendants,"<freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~bart/LEONARD1.htm>,
2 CONT
2 CONT James Leonard was allowed to keep an "ordinary" (bar) in Taunton. The license was revoked in 1664/5, but later conveyed to his son, Thomas.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Leonard frequently entertained Massasoit and King Philip, who journeyed from Mt. Hope to the hunting grounds at Fowling Pond. Fowling Pond is in Raynham, was one mile north of the Ancient Iron Works on present-day King Phili
2 CONC p's Street near the end of Mill Street. Fowling Pond was said to be two miles long and three-quarters of a mile wide in King Philip's time, but today has disappeared. James repaired their guns and conferred favors that led to a la
2 CONC sting friendship. 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Philip conveyed to James Leonard about 250 acres at Mattapoisett Neck in Swansea in October 1665, but the deed was lost by the Plymouth Court. Tradition says that out the outbreak of KIng Philip's War in 1675, Philip gave str
2 CONC ict orders that his men were never to harm a Leonard (although young Uriah Leonard was almost shot by King Philip's men early in the war, a bullet having passed through his hat as he rode his horse to escape an attack). It is conj
2 CONC ectured that because of the Leonards Taunton was not attacked during the war. (Philip's orders were actually not to disturb certain families including those of James Leonard, John Brown, and Capt. Thomas Willett, all of Taunton --
2 CONC Hurd, p. 346).
2 CONT
2 CONT One peculiarity to check out: although several Leonards were officers in the militia of the time, there's little mention of Leonards fighting in Philip's War. Bodge in Soldiers of King Philip's War mentions Jacob as serving under 
2 CONC Capt. Woodworth, Thomas credited under Capt. Thomas Brattle October 19, 1675, and Thomas at Lynn, August 24, 1676. More research needs to be done on the activities of the Leonards during Philip's War.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the garrison houses used during King Philip's War was the Samuel Leonard house erected in 1653 by James Leonard at the site of Taunton's Ancient Iron Works Company now in Raynham. A memorial plaque marking the spot is locat
2 CONC ed seven-tenths of a mile east from Route 44 along the south side of Route 104.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another traditional story is that Philip's head was deposited in the basement of Leonard's house for safekeeping before being sent to Plymouth. However, none of the early historians indicated anything but that the head was sent di
2 CONC rectly to Plymouth for display. (Philip was shot by Alderman, a Sakonnet Indian, on August 12, 1676, in a swamp at the foot of Mt. Hope in Bristol. His head was set on a pole in Plymouth and stayed there for a generation. For more
2 CONC  on King Philip's War, see Eric B. Schultz and Michael J. Tougias, "King Philip's War: The History and Legacy of America's Forgotten Conflict (Woodstock, VT: The Countryman Press, 1999)."
2 CONT
2 CONT ECL notes that James had 68 grandchildren. A chart gives those present at a Thanksgiving family party in 1690, and I've checked all the grandchildren alive then against the chart (there were 45 living in 1690).
2 CONT
2 CONT Account of Estate of James Leonard of Taunton dtd. August 24, 1697. Agreement about estate among Isaac and Hannah Dean, Joseph Leonard, Uriah Leonard, Thomas Leonard, Benjamin Leonard, James Leonard, John and Abigail Kingsley, and
2 CONC  Isaac and Rebecca Chapman. (1:44).
2 CONT
2 CONT William Reed Deane in "Genealogical Memoir of the Leonard Family" lists all of the children but John (NEHGS Reg. 1851:414(3).
2 CONT
2 CONT *James LEONARD and Mary Jane MARTIN were married in 1640 in England.[2] 
2 CONT *Mary Jane MARTIN2 (daughter of Isaac MARTIN) was born before 1625. 
2 CONT *She died on 25 Feb 1663/64 in Taunton, Bristol Co., MA. 
2 CONT *She was also referred to as Margaret and Jennie Martin. A family of Martyns lived in Newport, not far from Pontypool. Henry Martyn, yeoman,1573, John, William, Thomas, Morgan, Mary, Edmund, Catherine, 
2 CONT *1583.William was keeper of the keys in Newport. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Further research at Newport and the Monmouthshire County Records Office might turn up a relationship, although church records in that area do not go back to this era. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --source:  http://www.bradsport.com/jamesleonarddescendants1107/b1684.htm#P6254
2 CONT ----
2 CONT http://www.bradsport.com/Leonardorigins.htm What we know of the origins of James Leonard comes from Hannah Leonard Deane, his daughter, who made a deposition to her grandnephew, Zephaniah Leonard, as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT “February 2, 1732-3, Hannah Deane, sister to Capt. James Leonard late of Taunton deceased, gave to the subscriber the following account of her relations, etc –
2 CONT
2 CONT             1st Namely her Great Grandfather’s name was   Henry Leonard
2 CONT
2 CONT             2nd Her own Grandfather’s name was                            Thomas Leonard
2 CONT
2 CONT             3rd  Her Father’s name was                                           James Leonard            
2 CONT
2 CONT                    Her Grandmother’s name was                                White              
2 CONT
2 CONT                    Her Mother’s name was                                        Martin
2 CONT
2 CONT                    Her Father’s brothers’ names were the eldest,        1. Henry Leonard        
2 CONT
2 CONT                                                                                                 2. William Leonard      
2 CONT
2 CONT                                                                                                 3.  John Leonard
2 CONT
2 CONT                                                                                                 4.  Philip Leonard
2 CONT
2 CONT                                                                                                 5.  Thomas Leonard                
2 CONT
2 CONT                                                                                                      Margery
2 CONT
2 CONT                                                                                                      Joan
2 CONT
2 CONT                                                                                                      Sarah
2 CONT
2 CONT James, her Father, lived and died at Taunton, New England
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas was drowned at Piscataway.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry went to New Jersey.      
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip lived at Marshfield and died at Taunton.
2 CONT
2 CONT William & John never came out of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Margery married Henry Samson of Ireland, Lt. of the City of Gallaway.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah died at New Salem.       
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan never came out of England.          
2 CONT
2 CONT Said Hannah said her eldest brother was Thomas Leonard, next James, Joseph, Benjamin, John, Uriah.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sisters were Abigail and Rebecca.
2 CONT
2 CONT They all had children save John, who died at about 20 years of age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry, who removed to New Jersey, lived and married his wife in Lynn in New England, from thence he removed to Toppsfield, then removed by way of Taunton to the Jerseys.  When he was in Taunton, he had seven likely children, namel
2 CONC y,
2 CONT
2 CONT             The eldest Samuel married Sarah Brooks         
2 CONT
2 CONT                              Nathaniel
2 CONT
2 CONT                              Thomas married his wife in Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT                              Henry
2 CONT
2 CONT                              John married A. Almy (originally)
2 CONT
2 CONT             The daughters, the eldest married Throgmorton; next Mary.
2 CONT
2 CONT             So far Hannah Deane, originally Hannah Leonard, gave me an account.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Settler
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonial Daughters of the 17th Century (26031)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT  James Leonard sent a letter to James Leonard, ironworker in or near Taunton, MA stating that the extensive iron works there in Bilston belonged to the Leonards. The Leonards in Taunton decided not to undertake the expense of an e
2 CONC xtended suit to regain the works. The Leonards may also have been involved in some of the ironworks in Somersetshire, England, and Pontypool, Monmouthshire, Wales, as well.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Leonard was but a short time at the Saugus Ironworks and at Braintree for a longer time. At sale of the Braintree works, he became a partner. With the invitation from Taunton, he moved there, erected a forge and furnace, and
2 CONC  continued as master workman, a position he held for the rest of his life. ECL believes Oliver Purchase was the one who induced Henry and James along with Ralph Russell to come to Taunton. He conveyed the two hearths at Taunton to
2 CONC  his sons, Thomas and James, and they in turn conveyed them to their sons. He purchased a lot on MillRiver and erected a one-hearth forge, which he called Whittinton Forge.His son Joseph was the master workman at Whittinton Forge.
2 CONC  His two other sons, Benjamin and Uriah, were also trained as "bloomers." About 1682 James Leonard built a house for himself a short distance from the Taunton(Raynham) Ironworks on the north side of the road. It was a gambled roof
2 CONC house two stories in front and running back to one story in the rear.When he died in 1691, he left an estate valued at 500 pounds, a veryrespectable sum in those days (from Elisha Clark Leonard and George Marston Leonard's unpubli
2 CONC shed manuscript).
2 CONT
2 CONT More about the involvement of James and Henry Leonard in early ironworksin Massachusetts and New Jersey can be found in Bill Barton's articles,"The Establishment of the Iron Industry in America," "Pre-AmericanAncestry of Our Leona
2 CONC rd Ironworkers," and "Leonard Siblings Henry, James,Philip, Sarah, and Thomas in America and Some of Their Descendants,"<freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~bart/LEONARD1.htm>,
2 CONT
2 CONT James Leonard was allowed to keep an "ordinary" (bar) in Taunton. Thelicense was revoked in 1664/5, but later conveyed to his son, Thomas. James Leonard frequently entertained Massasoit and King Philip, who journeyed from Mt. Hope
2 CONC  to the hunting grounds at Fowling Pond. Fowling Pond is in Raynham, was one mile north of the Ancient Iron Works on present-day King Philip's Street near the end of Mill Street. Fowling Pond was said to be two miles long and thre
2 CONC e-quarters of a mile wide in King Philip's time, but today has disappeared. James repaired their guns and conferred favors that led to a lasting friendship. King Philip conveyed to James Leonard about two hundred and fifty acres a
2 CONC t Mattapoisett Neck in Swansea in October 1665, but the deed was lost by the Plymouth Court. Tradition says that out the outbreak of King Philip's War in 1675, Philip gave strict orders that his men were never to harm a Leonard (a
2 CONC lthough young Uriah Leonard was almost shot by King Philip's men early in the war, a bullet having passed through his hat as he rode his horse to escape an attack). It is conjectured that because of the Leonards Taunton was not at
2 CONC tacked during the war. (Philip's orders were actually not to disturb certain families including those of James Leonard, John Brown, and Capt. Thomas Willett, all of Taunton --Hurd, p. 346).
2 CONT
2 CONT One peculiarity to check out: although several Leonards were officers inthe militia of the time, there's little mention of Leonards fighting in Philip's War. Bodge in Soldiers of King Philip's War mentions Jacob as serving under C
2 CONC apt. Woodworth, Thomas credited under Capt. Thomas Brattle October 19, 1675, and Thomas at Lynn, August 24, 1676..
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the garrison houses used during King Philip's War was the Samuel Leonard house erected in 1653 by James Leonard at the site of Taunton's Ancient Iron Works Company now in Raynham. A memorial plaque marking the spot is locat
2 CONC ed seven-tenths of a mile east from Route 44 along the south side of Route 104. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://www.bradsport.com/jamesleonarddescendants1107/b1684.htm#P1686
2 CONT
2 CONT James LEONARD1 was born about 1620 in Pontypool, England/Wales. He died on 1 Sep 1691 in Taunton, Bristol Co., MA.2 There is uncertainty as to when and how James Leonard first came to America. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Land Patent Books of Virginia, Book 1, page 23, shows Robert Bennett granted 700 acres...for transportation of 14 persons, including James Leonard, June 26, 1635. One theory is that James came first to Virginia,then to Marylan
2 CONC d, then to Providence, then to Lynn. Source: The Leonard Dictionary, Volume III (manuscript). Duplicate record August 18, 1637.Another source is Charles Edward Banks' "Topographical Dictionary of 2885 English Emigrants to New Engl
2 CONC and 1620-1650," Baltimore, Genealogical Publishing Co., 1963, 3rd edition, p. 148, citing Various References: NEGR 5/104. Apparently, employees and recruits of John Winthrop were sometimes not listed as passengers, since they were
2 CONC  not paying passengers on those particular vessels crossing the Atlantic. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He arrived before 1650 from Pontypool, Wales, although some sources say he first came to Providence, RI, in 1645. He was paid for bringing his goods from Providence by the Lynn/Saugus Ironworks in 1651. On January 
2 CONT  10, 1645/6 in Providence, 25 acres of land were granted to a number of inhabitants, including James Leonard, but his name had been crossed out.He was the Ironmaster of Taunton, having first participated development of the iron wo
2 CONC rks at Braintree and Saugus. 
2 CONT
2 CONT But there appear to have been Leonards in the Pontypool area since the early 1600's. A Thomas Leonard mentioned in deed of July 29, 1633,bordering lands of John Powell, John Gerbon, and Phillip Morgan in Trevethin (Parish near Pon
2 CONC typool, with a bridge near swamp and pool therein 1490 -- pool later became forge pond). An ironworks was in operation before 1634, and there's a record of a complaint against John Wylde for failure to collect monies from it, inst
2 CONC ead selling iron at a discount to his friends. Thomas Morgan was recorded as selling charcoal to it in 1640. The works were apparently owned by the Hanburys, probably Richard b. August 1618. Thomas, son of Jacob Leonard, was bapti
2 CONC zed January 9,1699; William, son of Jacob Leonard, was baptized July 23, 1696; Gwenllian, wife of Thomas Leonard, buried March 15, 1656; Mary Leonard married Alexander Lewis January 26, 1656; a son of Philip Leonard was born Octob
2 CONC er 27, 1656. Sarah, daughter of James Leonard, baptized September 1, 1705; Ann, daughter of James Leonard, baptized March 13,1702. Local records include a mention of a Thomas Leonard in 1790, a John and Mary Leonard who died at ag
2 CONC e 84 in 1774. These indicate there were Leonards and ironmaking in the Pontypool area after James and Thomas left. These Leonards had names identical to or similar to those who emigrated to America. There was even a Theophilus Leo
2 CONC nard, iron refiner,who died March 31, 1900 in nearby Pontnewydd, Wales, perhaps just a coincidence. (Source: old documents at the Monmouthshire County records office near Pontypool, October 2003. A researcher with more time could 
2 CONC probably find some interesting material here.) Elisha Clark Leonard paid 5 pounds to a clergyman in Pontypool to check the records for James and Henry, but he reportedly found nothing. GML reported that later researchers found not
2 CONC hing about them either. So the theory is that James and Henry were not in Pontypool very long. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Probably James and his young family (and his older brother Henry) were also ironworkers in the Bilston, Staffordshire (Cheshire?), area prior to their immigration. Bilston became a center of the "Black Country" iron industry. Geor
2 CONC ge Marston Leonard includes a note on one of his tables that "James, son of Thomas, son of Henry of Billston, Staffordshire..."from McKenzie, Colonial Families, Vol. IV. Apparently, the Leonards left a claim to the ownership of so
2 CONC me heavily mortgaged ironworks there,moving on as the mining districts became less productive. Years later(1821?) an ironworker in Bilston by the name of James Leonard sent a letter to James Leonard, ironworker in or near Taunton,
2 CONC  MA stating that the extensive iron works there in Bilston belonged to the Leonards. The Leonards in Taunton decided not to undertake the expense of an extended suit to regain the works. The Leonards may also have been involved in
2 CONC  some of the ironworks in Somersetshire, England, and Pontypool, Monmouthshire, Wales, as well. 
2 CONT
2 CONT James Leonard was but a short time at the Saugus Ironworks and at Braintree for a longer time. At sale of the Braintree works, he became a partner. With the invitation from Taunton, he moved there, erected af orge and furnace, and
2 CONC  continued as master workman, a position he held for the rest of his life. ECL believes Oliver Purchase was the one who induced Henry and James along with Ralph Russell to come to Taunton. He conveyed the two hearths at Taunton to
2 CONC  his sons, Thomas and James, and they in turn conveyed them to their sons. He purchased a lot on Mill River and erected a one-hearth forge, which he called Whittinton Forge.His son Joseph was the master workman at Whittinton Forge
2 CONC . His two others ons, Benjamin and Uriah, were also trained as "bloomers." About 1682 James Leonard built a house for himself a short distance from the Taunton(Raynham) Ironworks on the north side of the road. It was a gambled roo
2 CONC f house two stories in front and running back to one story in the rear.When he died in 1691, he left an estate valued at 500 pounds, a veryr espectable sum in those days (from Elisha Clark Leonard and George Marston Leonard's unpu
2 CONC blished manuscript). 
2 CONT
2 CONT More about the involvement of James and Henry Leonard in early ironworksin Massachusetts and New Jersey can be found in Bill Barton's articles,"The Establishment of the Iron Industry in America," "Pre-American Ancestry of Our Leon
2 CONC ard Ironworkers," and "Leonard Siblings Henry, James,Philip, Sarah, and Thomas in America and Some of Their Descendants,"<freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~bart/LEONARD1.htm>, 
2 CONT
2 CONT James Leonard was allowed to keep an "ordinary" (bar) in Taunton. Thelicense was revoked in 1664/5, but later conveyed to his son, Thomas. 
2 CONT
