2 CONT Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT James and his family came to America in 1645 and settled in Providence and in Lynn, MA where entries in an account book of 1651 kept by the Lynn Company stated: "James Leonnarde, 15 days worke about finnerey Chimneye and other wor
2 CONC ke in ye forge, 1:13:0. To ditto Leonard for dressing his bellows 3 times, 1:10:0. To ditto soe much allowed him for bringing his goods from Providence, 2:0:0.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family moved to Taunton by 1652 where he and his brother, Henry, set up an iron forge on Two Mile River," this being the first forge in the country for the manufacture of iron. This "bloomerie" was owned by a number of men who
2 CONC  bought shares in it and it began operation in 1656. It was sometimes called the Raynham Works.
2 CONT
2 CONT "James was a friend of the Indian Chief, King Philip, often entertaining him at his home. In October of 1665, King Philip conveyed about 250 acres of land at Mattapoisett Neck in Swansea to James. Tradition says that it was becaus
2 CONC e of this friendship that, at the outbreak of King Philip's War, King Philip gave strict orders that his men were never to harm a Leonard, and conjecture says that because of this, Taunton was not attacked during the war.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Leonard could not read or write when he came to America but he was able to amass property amounting to 500 pounds during his lifetime."
2 CONT
2 CONT James remarried a Margaret after Mary's death.
