2 CONT [[Category: Original 77 Owners of the South Purchase]]
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT ===England===The birth dates and location(s) for James Leonard and brother Henry, two of the founders of the Taunton Iron Works, are unknown. Their father Thomas moved the family several times while working in the iron refining in
2 CONC dustry in England and possibly Wales. It is widely reported that James was born in Pontypool, Monmouthshire but there is no evidence of this. He may have come to New England after working at Pontypool, but the earliest evidence of
2 CONC  his father was in Cleobury Mortimer, Shropshire.  (The primary source of information regarding the Leonard family parent and sibling relationships is James' daughter, Hannah Leonard Deane.)<ref>Deane, William Reed. Genealogical M
2 CONC emoir of the Leonard Family. Boston, MA: Self-Published for the New England Historic-Genealogical Register, 1851. [https://archive.org/details/genealogicalmemo00dean/page/21/mode/2up Page 21.]</ref>
2 CONT Possibly James and older brother Henry also worked  in the Bilston, Staffordshire area prior to their emigration. Apparently the Leonards left a claim to the ownership of some heavily mortgaged ironworks there.An ironworker in Bil
2 CONC ston by the name of Leonard sent a letter to his "loving cousin," (James' descendant) Samuel Leonard, stating that extensive iron works situated there belonged to the Leonards. Charles Richmond, Esq. of Taunton, investigated the l
2 CONC egal situation in Bilston. The outcome was that the New England Leonards decided not to undertake the expense of an extended suit.<ref> Everett, Alice Allen. "Leonards of Monmouthshire and Somersetshire, England." The American Gen
2 CONC ealogist,Volume 53, 1977. ''By Subscription'' (Online database. AmericanAncestors.org. New England Historic Genealogical Society, 2009 - .)[https://www.americanancestors.org/DB283/i/12345/104/0 Page 104.]</ref>
2 CONT Before his emigration, James married (at an unknown date and location)and had at least one son in England. On 8 August 1641 Thomas Leonard was baptized at Kinver, Staffordshire, England. The mother's name is transcribed as Jane or
2 CONC  Jean.<ref name=Birth>"England Births and Christenings, 1538-1975", database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:J94T-XFC : 19 March 2020), Thomas Leonard, 1641.</ref> (Kinver is only 17 miles from Cleobury Mort
2 CONC imer where some of James' siblings were baptised.)
2 CONT ===Leonards and the New England Iron Industry===A group of investors led by John Winthrop Jr. spearheaded efforts to build the first iron works in New England at Braintree.<ref>Bowman, Robert E. "Glimpses into the English and Cont
2 CONC inental Ancestry of Certain Braintree and and Saugus Ironworkers of about 1650." The Essex Genealogist, Volume 20. 2018. Page 63.  (Online database. AmericanAncestors.org, New England Historic Genealogical Society, 2018.) https://
2 CONC www.americanancestors.org/DB396/i/12957/63/0</ref>The land was purchased in September of 1645, with the furnace likely built the following year. It is unknown when the Leonard brothers arrived, but it is thought that Henry Leonard
2 CONC  superintended the erection ofthe Braintree Iron Works.<ref>Swank, James M. History of the Manufacture of Iron in All Ages, and Particularly in the United States from Colonial Times to 1891. Philadelphia, PA: The American Iron and
2 CONC  Steel Association, 1892. [https://archive.org/details/historyofmanufac00swanrich/page/112/mode/2up?q=%22scarcity+of+ore%22 Page 113.]</ref> A largeroperation was built at Lynn on the Saugus River and the first documented records 
2 CONC of James Leonard's presence in America appear in the Lynn Iron Works papers dated 1651: § To: James Leonnarde p 15 dayes Worckeabout ye finnerey Chimneye, and other worcke in ye forge £1-13-00§ To: ditto Leonarde p dressing his be
2 CONC llows, 3 times £1-10-__ § To: Ditto. Soe much alloued him forr breingeinge his goods from Prouidence: £2-00-00.<ref> Swank, 1892.  [https://archive.org/details/historyofmanufac00swanrich/page/110/mode/2up?q=ditto+leonard  Page 110
2 CONC .]</ref> In1653 Henry and James were some of the most skilled and highest paid workers.<ref>Griswold, William and Linebaugh, Donald. Saugus Iron Works. The Roland W. Robbins Excavations, 1948-1953. Saugus Iron Works National Histo
2 CONC ric Site.  [https://www.nps.gov/sair/learn/historyculture/upload/RobbinsBookComplete.pdf Page 44.]</ref> 
2 CONT ===Taunton===In October of 1652, the Plymouth Colony town of Taunton offered the Leonard brothers "free consent to come hither, and join with certain of our inhabitants to set up a bloomery work on the Two Mile River."<ref>Baylies
2 CONC , Francis. An Historical Memory of the Colony of New Plymouth.Vol II. Boston, MA: Hilliard, Gray, Little, and Wilkins.1830.  [https://books.google.com/books?id=is8TAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA268&dq=baylies+new+plymouth+imprimis&hl=en&newbks=1&
2 CONC newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwitiJ7c_srrAhWQHM0KHavyA5wQ6AEwAHoECAUQAg#v=onepage&q=baylies%20new%20plymouth%20imprimis&f=false Page 268.]</ref> Their role seems to have been that of skilled workers, with James owning a one-half s
2 CONC hare and Henry owningnone.<ref> Swank, 1892. [https://archive.org/details/historyofmanufac00swanrich/page/114/mode/2up?q=Two-mile+river Page 114.]</ref> Henry moved on to New Jersey, with James remaining in Taunton where he contin
2 CONC ued at the iron works along with their brother Philip. James eventually purchased land and built the Whittington Iron Works which he operated with sons Joseph, Benjamin, and Uriah.<ref>Emery, Samuel Hopkins. History of Taunton, Ma
2 CONC ssachusetts From its Settlement to the Present Time. Syracuse, NY: D. Mason & Co., Publishers 1893. [https://books.google.com/books?id=iqsvAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA383&dq=%22philip+leonard%22+taunton&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2a
2 CONC hUKEwjTqfOUwcPrAhXSXM0KHdlKAoAQ6AEwAXoECAMQAg#v=snippet&q=%22whittington%20Forge%22&f=false Page 630.]</ref> (Probably not coincidentally, Whittington is an iron works town in Shropshire.)
2 CONT In 1659 "inhabitants within the Township of Taunton" were awarded a division of land. For the sum of 18 shillings, James was to receive 58 acres for "ten heads" (members of the household), suggesting that two adults and eight chil
2 CONC dren were included.<ref>Emery, 1893.   [https://books.google.com/books?id=iqsvAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA120&cad=3#v=snippet&q=Jan.%205%2C%201659%20orphans&f=false Page 130.] </ref>
2 CONT Running the Taunton Iron Works seems to have given James some trouble,some advantages, and some civic obligations:* On 6 Oct. 1659 James Leonard and Phillip Lenard and others, all of Taunton, were warned to personally appear at th
2 CONC e court "to bee holden the first Tuesday in March next" to answer for sundry misdemeanors committed in Taunton.<ref>Shurtleff, Nathaniel B. Records of the Colony ofNew Plymouth of New England, Court Orders Vol. III 1651-1661. Bost
2 CONC on,MA: Legislature of the Commonwealth of Massachusetts, 1855.  [https://archive.org/stream/recordsofcolonyo0304newp#page/n189/mode/2up/search/Phillip+Lenard Page 176. ]</ref>* By order of the Court in June of 1662, James was "fre
2 CONC ed from training in the milletarie company of Taunton, in reference to his calling, being a bloomer…"<ref>Shurtleff, 1855.  [https://archive.org/stream/recordsofcolonyo0304newp#page/14/mode/2up/search/index  Page 15.]</ref>* James
2 CONC  Leonard was awarded the privilege to keep an "ordinary" (a public location for alcoholic beverages) in Taunton. The license was revoked in March of 1663/4 as a result of his "having buryed his wife and in that respect not being s
2 CONC oe capable of keeping a publicke house..." Several months later the license was awarded to his son Thomas.<ref name=Lic>Shurtleff, 1855.  [https://archive.org/stream/recordsofcolonyo0304newp#page/54/mode/2up/search/index  Page 54.
2 CONC ]</ref>* James served as a juror in a potential murder case on July of 1664. (He signed the verdict with his mark.)<ref> Shurtleff, 1855. [https://archive.org/stream/recordsofcolonyo0304newp#page/70/mode/2up/search/index  Page 71.
2 CONC ]</ref> In 1679, James Sr. and son Thomas participated in the "South Purchase"of land from King Philip (alias Metacum, Chief Sachem of Pakanaukett.) <ref>Emery, 1893. [https://books.google.com/books?id=iqsvAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA120&cad=3#
2 CONC v=onepage&q=%22addition%20of%20assonet%20neck%22&f=false Page 121. ]</ref> It's reported in several genealogical and historical publications that James built a home close to the Taunton Forge and was helpful to King Philip, whose 
2 CONC favored bird hunting pond was on James' land.  According to Cutter, "...he was a great friend to King Philip... and when the town was burned his house was spared."<ref>Cutter, William Richard. New England Families Genealogical and
2 CONC  Memorial. ThirdSeries, Volume I. New York, NY: Lewis Historical Publishing Company, 1915. [https://books.google.com/books?id=1tAUAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA389&dq=%22james+leonard%22+raynham+king+philip&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=
2 CONC 2ahUKEwi26KzR2cvrAhW9Ap0JHXwBAsgQ6AEwBHoECAMQAg#v=onepage&q=%22james%20leonard%22%20raynham%20king%20philip&f=false Page 389.] </ref>
2 CONT ===Marriage===There remains uncertainty regarding James' wives; Hannah Leonard Deaneidentifies her mother as ___ Martin. That leaves two possibilities: 1) James' first wife "Jane/Jean" died and Mary Martin was a second marriage or
2 CONC  2) there's an issue with Thomas' birth record. We know from Colony records that Mary died before March of 1663/4,<ref name=Lic/> within months of Uriah's birth. No details are known about James' next marriage to Margaret (birth s
2 CONC urname unknown) except that he predeceased her and is named in his will.<ref name=NEHGR>"Abstracts from the FirstBook of Bristol County Probate Records." New England Historical and Genealogical Register, October 1909, [https://boo
2 CONC ks.google.com/books?id=VBJAAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA327&dq=bristol+county+probate+files+%22james+leonard%22+1691&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjH3OKC3s3rAhUJVc0KHbpKAIMQ6AEwAnoECAEQAg#v=onepage&q=bristol%20county%20probate%20
2 CONC files%20%22james%20leonard%22%201691&f=false Page 327.]</ref>
2 CONT ===Children===Combining the statement given by James' daughter Hannah and the discoveries of genealogical researchers, the known children of James Leonardare (order uncertain):
2 CONT # Thomas bap. Kinver 8 Aug 1641;<ref name=Birth/> m Mary Watson# James b ca 1643 prob in England;<ref>Deane, 1861. [https://archive.org/details/genealogicalmemo00dean/page/17/mode/2up Page 17.]</ref> m (1) Hannah (2) Lydia Gullive
2 CONC r# Abigail;<ref name=Deane>Deane, 1861. [https://archive.org/details/genealogicalmemo00dean/page/22/mode/2up Page 22.]</ref> m John Kingsley
2 CONT # Rebecca;<ref name=Deane/> m Isaac Chapman
2 CONT # Joseph;<ref name=Deane/> m Mary Black
2 CONT # Benjamin;<ref name=Deane/> m Sarah Thresher
2 CONT # Hannah;<ref name=Deane/> m Isaac Deane
2 CONT # John; died at about twenty years old<ref name=Deane/># Uriah b 10 Jul 1662;<ref>"Massachusetts Births and Christenings, 1639-1915", database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:FCVZ-7HQ : 15 January 2020), Uri
2 CONC ah Leonard, 1662.</ref> m ElizabethCaswell
2 CONT ===Death===James died in 1691; and the estate was settled on November 6, 1691. Joseph "to have one fourth of his father's iron works, "with some adjacent land, and "to pay 20 shillings in money, and 400 of iron annually to his mot
2 CONC her-in-law;" Uriah "to have the rest of the Whittington Iron Works, dwelling house and land," and "pay 600 of iron per annum to his mother-in-law Margaret as long as she continued his father's widow ;"James "to have some tracts of
2 CONC  land, "the old home lot," and "his father's half share in the Taunton Iron Works", also £4 9s. more from the estate; Benjamin to have certain parcels of land named, the shop tools, old iron, his father's clothing and as much more
2 CONC  from the estate as to make £26 9s. Abigail and John Kingsbury, Rebecca and Isaac Chapman, and Hannah and Isaac Dean (daughters and husbands) to have certain tracts of land and proceeds of sales from the estate, to make for each£2
2 CONC 6 9s. Thomas to have the dwelling house near the iron works on Two Mile River, after the death of his mother-in-law, and to administer the estate, to pay all bequests to the heirs, and have the remainder. One third of all the mova
2 CONC bles were assigned to Widow Margaret, she "to reside in the house where she lives as long as she remains a widow." Margaret gave a statement on November 10, 1691 stating her agreement.<ref>"Abstracts from the First Book of Bristol
2 CONC  County Probate Records." The Genealogical Advertiser, Volume 4, 1901.[https://books.google.com/books?id=DppQAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA124&dq=estate+%22james+leonard%22+1691&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjylu3J3s3rAhXNVs0KHWNz
2 CONC DMQQ6AEwAHoECAEQAg#v=snippet&q=%22John%20Eddy%22&f=false Page 123.]</ref>
