2 CONT Robert Goodell was born 16 August 1601 in Dennington, Suffolk, England and died 27 June 1682 in Salem, Essex, Massachusetts. Children with Catherine Kilham:
2 CONT
2 CONT 10.  Elizabeth  b: ABT 1649 in Salem, Essex, Massachusetts.  Married 1) John Smith.  2) Henry Bennett.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT  iv ELIZABETH GOODALE, b. say 1636; m. (1) by 1658 John Smith [ELR 1:39-40]; m. (2) Salem [blank] March 1674/5 Henry Bennett (this marriage record as published from the court copy gives the groom's name as "William," but in deeds 
2 CONC he is called "Henry," and these seem to be the more reliable records [ELR 6:66, 9:64]). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth married John Smith, had 5 children by him, then married William Bennett and had one daughter, Grace.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of her father's death in 1682 he names only his daughter Elizabeth Bennett (last name from her second marriage) and her son John Smith. (Her father had already provided for his other children via gifts during his lifet
2 CONC ime.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT #  John Smith. He m. Ann Skerry, Feb. 6, 1689. He was a maltster (brewer) in Salem in 1692. 
2 CONT # Mary Smith. She m. Mark Haskell of Beverly, carpenter, March 20, 1677/8. 
2 CONT # Sarah Smith, b. Oct. 20, 1660; m. John Clark of Beverly, weaver. 
2 CONT # Elizabeth Smith, b. June 5, 1662; m. Humphrey Horrill of Beverly, Jan. 10, 1687/8. 
2 CONT # Abraham Smith. By deed and release from his brother and sisters he acquired the Goodale homestead, which had been left by his grandfather to his mother, and also the property deeded to her in 1678. He conveyed the estate to his 
2 CONC son Nathan in 1740. (Essex County Deeds 80: 75.) 
2 CONT # Grace Bennett, b. Feb. 1676; d. young.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT * The will of Robert Goodell of Salem, 1682, reads as follows:  "I, Robert Goodell being now aged & weake in body, as alsoe my wife and my daughter Elizabeth Bennett, hath taken care of me and therefore my will & desire is & I doe
2 CONC  will & bequeath unto my daughter Elizabeth Bennett & my grand child, John Smith, m y house & the orchard & all the meadowes that I now possess with the pasture which is about eight acres of upland be it more or lesse, all which h
2 CONC ouse, land & meadows my daughter Elizabeth Bennett, and my Grand child John Smith, shall enjoy after the lease, or terme, that it is now let for, is expired, they or either of them paying as much rent, yearly as the wife of the ab
2 CONC ovesaid Robert Goodell hath let it for which is to the value of twenty shillings in currant pay, dated the twelfth of October one thousand six hundred eighty two; & after my daughter Elizabeth's decease, the whole lands shall be m
2 CONC y grand child's John Smith. In witness whereof I have hereunto set my hand & seal.
2 CONT * From "The Ancestry of Lydia Harmon 1755-1836":  iv. ELIZABETH. She m. John Smith of Salem Village. It seems probable that after their marriage they lived with her father, Smith helping in the management of the farm. The evidence
2 CONC  in the case against him and his sister-in-law, the wife of Zachariah Goodale, in 1672, would seem to indicate that the marriage was not a particularly happy one. Smith d. before March, 1674/5, when Elizabeth m. William Bennett of
2 CONC  Salem. As her father states in his will, he and her stepmother lived with this daughter until his death. She was a widow in 1692, living with her son Abraham. 
2 CONT
