2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT {{Puritan Great Migration}}
2 CONT ==Disputed Origins==There are many publications and family trees which definitively state that a Robert Watson, immigrant of 1632, was father to George and brother of John Watson of Roxbury. According to Robert Charles Anderson's 
2 CONC "Phantom File", '''"this family construct should be rejected totally'''." An assignment of a servant by 'Elizabeth Watson, widow, … unto Thomas Watson, on 8 Nov 1638 [PCR 1:102], is cited in partial support of this claim; but this
2 CONC  record says nothing about a Robert, nor does itconnect to records of other Watson immigrants."<ref>Anderson, Robert Charles. The Great Migration Begins: Immigrants to New England 1620-1633, Volumes I-III. Boston, MA: New England 
2 CONC Historical and GenealogicalSociety, 1995. ''By Subscription'' [https://www.americanancestors.org/DB393/i/12107/2103/235200512 Page 2103.]</ref> 
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT === Origin ===According to Robert Charles Anderson's authoritative research on the Great Migration, George Watson's birth location, date, and parents are unknown.<ref name="GMB">Anderson, Robert Charles, [http://www.americanancest
2 CONC ors.org/databases/great-migration-begins-immigrants-to-ne-1620-1633-vols-i-iii/image/?volumeId=12107&pageName=1944&rId=235198127 "George Watson," Featured Name] (Vol I-III, Page 1944) ''The Great Migration Begins: Immigrants to Ne
2 CONC w England 1620-1633, Volumes I-III.'' (Online database accessed April 16, 2015: AmericanAncestors.org, New England Historic Genealogical Society, 2010)</ref> George was born about 1602 (based on age at death, though Anderson suspe
2 CONC cts this was probably "exaggerated by about 5 years").<ref name="GMB"/> 
2 CONT === Immigration ===The first record of George Watson's presence in New England is a deposition he gave on July 19, 1631 wherein George refers to himself and "fellowe servaunts."<ref>Proceedings of the Massachusetts Historical Soci
2 CONC ety, Vol. 45, 1911-1912. Boston, MA: Massachusetts Historical Society, 1912. [https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=njp.32101013658339&view=1up&seq=527&q1=George%20Wattson Page 527.]</ref> They were interviewed regarding illegal 
2 CONC trading of guns and munitions to the Natives at the Castine/Macchabiticus (Penobscot) trading post in Maine where they were employed. Trader Edward Ashley was found guilty and returned to England where he was imprisoned.<ref>Plymo
2 CONC uth Ancestors Website."https://www.plimoth.org/sites/default/files/media/pdf/ashley_edward.pdf" Plimoth.org accessed 8 Sep 2020.</ref>
2 CONT The Maine trading post was ceded to the French in 1635.<ref>Spencer, Wilbur Daniel. Pioneers on the Maine Rivers with Lists to 1651. Baltimore, MD: Genealogical Publishing, 1935. [https://archive.org/details/pioneersonmainer00spen
2 CONC /page/384/mode/2up?q=40+leagues Page 385.]</ref> By that time George was already a Freeman at Plymouth (possibly becausehe had served out his indenture) likely in March of 1634.<ref>Anderson, 1995. ''By Subscription.'' [https://ww
2 CONC w.americanancestors.org/DB393/i/12107/1944/235198127 Page 1547]</ref>
2 CONT === Family ===George married c. 1635 to [[Hicks-26|Phebe Hicks]], daughter of RobertHicks. They had seven known children, all born in Plymouth. <ref name="GMB"/> Phebe died at Plymouth in 1663.<ref>Shurtleff, Nathaniel B. Records 
2 CONC of the Colony of New Plymouth of New England. Miscellaneous Records 1633-1689. Boston, MA: Legislature of the Commonwealth of Massachusetts, 1857. [https://archive.org/stream/recordsofcolonyo0708newp#page/n35/mode/2up/search/%22Ph
2 CONC ebe+Watson%22+ Page 23.]</ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT # [[Watson-790|John Watson]]; b ca 1636
2 CONT # [[Watson-35|Phoebe Watson]] b ca 1638; m Jonathan Shaw
2 CONT # [[Watson-791|Mary Watson]] b ca 1642; m Thomas Leonard# [[Watson-796|Samuel Watson]] b 18 Jan 1648;<ref name=Shurtleff>Shurtleff, 1857. [https://archive.org/stream/recordsofcolonyo0708newp#page/n17/mode/2up/search/Watson Page 5]
2 CONC </ref> d Aug 1649<ref>Shurtleff, 1857. [https://archive.org/stream/recordsofcolonyo0708newp#page/n21/mode/2up/search/Watson Page 8.]</ref># [[Watson-414|Elizabeth Watson]] twin;<ref name=Shurtleff/> m Joseph Williams# [[Watson-225
2 CONC 89|Jonathan Watson]] b 9 Mar 1652<ref>Shurtleff, 1857.  [https://archive.org/stream/recordsofcolonyo0708newp#page/n25/mode/2up/search/Watson Page 13]</ref># [[Watson-925|Elkanah Watson]] b 25 Feb 1656<ref>Shurtleff, 1857. [https:/
2 CONC /archive.org/stream/recordsofcolonyo0708newp#page/n29/mode/2up/search/Watson Page 16.]</ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT Not mentioned by Anderson and not proven as George's child: [[Watson-797|William Watson]]{{Citation needed}}
2 CONT === Life in Plymouth===In 1635 George purchased a home and garden, the first of roughly twenty transactions acquiring and trading land throughout his life. He wasentrusted with many civic duties, including serving on a grand jury,
2 CONC  coroner's juries, and as arbiter. He held various offices, including selectman and constable. 
2 CONT George's profession was mariner; he owned at least a partial interest in and was master of a [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barque barque].In May of 1653 a "counsel of warr" made preparations to launch an expedition against Hollan
2 CONC d if necessary. George, his vessel and his mates were "pressed to attend the expedition."<ref>Shurtleff, Nathaniel B. Records of the Colony of New Plymouth of New England, Court Orders Vol.III 1651-1661. Boston, MA: Legislature of
2 CONC  the Commonwealth of Massachusetts, 1855. [https://archive.org/stream/recordsofcolonyo0304newp#page/n41/mode/2up/search/%22two+barkques%22 Page 29.]</ref>
2 CONT George died on January 31, 1688/9 "being 87 yeares of age.<ref> Plymouth Church Records, 1620-1689, Part 1. Boston, MA: The Colonial Societyof Massachusetts, 1920.  [https://archive.org/details/publicationsofcov22colo/page/261/mod
2 CONC e/2up?q=%22george+watson%22 Page 262.]</ref> His son Elkanah administered the estate.<ref>Plymouth County, MA: Probate File Papers, 1686-1881.Online database. AmericanAncestors.org. New England Historic Genealogical Society, 2015.
2 CONC  (From records supplied by the Massachusetts Supreme Judicial Court Archives.) ''By Subscription.''[https://www.americanancestors.org/DB1558/i/20959/22233-co1/426519540 Page 22233.4]</ref>
2 CONT == Sources ==
