2 CONT The following information is from "Genealogy of the Gallup Family", by John D. Gallup, pub. 1893.
2 CONT
2 CONT   The John Gallop, Sr. family sailed from Plymouth, England on March 20, 1630, in the Mary and John.
2 CONT
2 CONT   "John Gallup, son of John and Christobel Gallup, was born in England, and came to this country in 1633. He married in  1643, at Boston, Hannah Lake, daughter of John and Margaret Lake. Madam Margaret Lake was the daughter of  Ed
2 CONC mund Read, Esq., of Wickford, Essex county, England, and sister of Elizabeth Read, who married John Winthrop, jr.,  Governor of Connecticut. In early life he showed signs of the bravery which afterwards distinguished him as an Ind
2 CONC ian  warrior. It is supposed he was with his father and assisted him in the capture of John Oldham's vessel, off Block Island.
2 CONT
2 CONT   With Massachusetts forces he engaged with his father in the Pequot war and bore himself so bravely that the General  Court of Connecticut in 1671 gave him a grant of 100 acres of land. He came to New London in 1650 or '51.
2 CONT
2 CONT   "Having these large grants of land he removed with his family in 1654 to the east side of the Mystic river, now  Stonington, where he had taken up the land granted him. He was one of the early settlers of that town. His homestea
2 CONC d  place was bounded on the west by Mystic river, south by Captain Stanton's homestead place and Captain Denison's  land, east by Denison's land and the town lots, and on the north by Robert Park's land. He represented the town at
2 CONC  the  General Court in 1665 and 1667. He was also an Indian interpreter. When King Philip's war broke out, although he was  over sixty, age had not quenched his martial ardor. new London county having raised seventy men under Capt
2 CONC ain John  Mason of Norwich, Captain Gallup jaoined with him at the head of the Mohegans. These troops forming a junction with  those of the other colonies, were engaged in the fearful swamp fight at Narragansett, December 19, 1675
2 CONC  (within the  limits of the present town of South Kingston, R.I.)
2 CONT
2 CONT   In storming this fort he led his men bravely forward and was one of the six captains who fell in this memorable fight. A  complete victory was here gained over the savage foe, but with great loss of life on both sides. Capt. Gal
2 CONC lup was a  brave and valuable officer and was loved and respected by his men.
2 CONT
2 CONT   The division made of his estate by order of the County Court was to the widow, 100 pounds; to the oldest son John,  137 pounds,; five daughters, 70 pounds each. Mrs. Hannah Gallup had also a large grant of land from the General 
2 CONC  Court in consideration of her great loss." 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT [[Category: English Immigrants to America]] [[Category: Stonington, Connecticut]] [[Category: Griffin, sailed 1633]] [[Category: US President Direct Ancestor]]
2 CONT {{Puritan Great Migration}}
2 CONT == Biography == 
2 CONT {{Roll of Honor
2 CONT | image = Roll of Honor Template-15.jpg  | category = Killed in Action, King Philip's War | description = KIA | war = King Philip's War }} Note: John Gallop is an ancestor of U.S. Presidents George H.W. Bush and George W. Bush. Jo
2 CONC hn was born or baptized 25 Jan 1619 / 1620 at St. Mary's Parish, Bridport, or Mosterne, Dorset, England. John Gallup immigrated to America with his mother, two brothers and sister Joan, in the ship ''Griffin'', arriving at Boston 
2 CONC on the 4th day of Sept., 1633. He married Hannah Lake, daughter of John and Margaret Lake, and sister of Elizabeth Read, who married John Winthrop, Jr., Governor of Connecticut. She also immigrated with her mother, in the ship ''A
2 CONC bigail'', arriving Oct. 6, 1635, after a passage of ten weeks.<ref>Richard Anson. ''[https://play.google.com/store/books/details?id=tvILAAAAYAAJ  History of the Town of Stonington, County of New London, Connecticut, From Its First
2 CONC  Settlement in 1649 to 1900].'' Press of the Day Publishing Co.,New London, CT, Jan. 1900.  Page: 382</ref> He removed from Boston to Taunton, MA, 1640, to New London 1651. In 1654 to his residence, Whitehall, built upon a tract o
2 CONC f land on the Mystic River.<ref>Memorial Gravestone Inscription; MORE INFO SOUGHT</ref> He married about 1642/43 probably in Boston, Massachusetts [[Lake-117|Hannah Anna Lake]]. He died 19 Dec 1675 OR 1676 at South Kingston, Washi
2 CONC ngton, Rhode Island, killed in King Phillip's War, and supposedly buried at Narragansett, Washington County, Rhode Island.<ref>{{FindAGrave|10866775}} for Capt. John Gallup.</ref><ref>Wikipedia article on [http://en.wikipedia.org/
2 CONC wiki/Great_Swamp_Fight the Great Swamp Fight].</ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From: ''Genealogical Record of the Hodges Family'', Pg. 76:''':John Gallop (or Gallup), father of Ester (Gallop) Hodges, was son of Capt. John Gallop, the noted Boston pilot and sea-captain. He was bornin England and came to Ma
2 CONC ssachusetts with his mother, Christabel Gallop, in 1633, three years after his father's first arrival. He settled in Taunton some time previous to August 1643, when he was enrolled among those able to bear arms. He went to New Lon
2 CONC don, Conn., apparently in 1650 or 1651, but probably did not take his family there for severalyears, as his daughter Ester's birth is recorded at Taunton among thechildren of Richard Burt. In 1654 he settled in Stonington, Conn., 
2 CONC ofwhich town he was representative in 1665 and 1667. He was employed often as Indian interpreter and in his career had much to do with the aborigines. He was a bold fighter and is supposed to have aided his father in what has been
2 CONC  called the first naval engagement in New England waters, when, in 1636, "two men and two boys" attacked and captured thepinnace manned by 14 Indians who had murdered John Oldham. This murder led to the Pequot war in which John Ga
2 CONC llop fought again, and for hisservices was granted 100 acres of land by the General Court of Connecticut. When King Phillip;s war broke out, he was called on still againand given command of a Connecticut company, and was one of th
2 CONC e six captains killed in the Great Narragansett Swamp Fight, Dec. 19, 1675, the hardest battle of this war. :[Continues with biographical information for Henry Hodges, and biography of Esther (Gallup) Hodges' mother, Hannah (Lake)
2 CONC  Gallop.]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''From: ''Genealogy of the Gallup Family'', Page 21 (abridged):''': John Gallup, son of John and Christobel Gallup, was born in England,and came to this country in 1633. He married in 1643, at Boston, Hannah Lake,daughter of John
2 CONC  and Margaret Lake. Madam Margaret Lake was the daughter of Edmund Read, Esq., of Wickford, Essex county, England, and sister of Elizabeth Read, who married John Winthrop, jr., Governor of Connecticut.In early life he showed signs
2 CONC  of the bravery which afterwards distinguished him as an Indian warrior. It is supposed he was with his father and assisted him in the capture of John Oldham's vessel, off Block Island. With Massachusetts forces he engaged with hi
2 CONC s father in the Pequot war and bore himself so bravely that the General Court of Connecticut in 1671 gave him a grant of 100 acres of land. He came to New London in 1650 or '51. :Having these large grants of land he removed with h
2 CONC is family in 1654 to the east side of the Mystic river, now Stonington, where he had taken up the land granted him. He was one of the early settlers of that town. His homestead place was bounded on the west by Mystic river, south 
2 CONC by Captain Stanton's homestead place and Captain Denison's land, east by Denison's land and the town lots, and on the north by Robert Park's land. He represented the town at the General Court in 1665 and 1667. He was also an India
2 CONC n interpreter. When King Philip's war broke out,although he was over sixty, age had not quenched his martial ardor. new London county having raised seventy men under Captain John Mason ofNorwich, Captain Gallup jaoined with him at
2 CONC  the head of the Mohegans.These troops forming a junction with those of the other colonies, were engaged in the fearful swamp fight at Narragansett, December 19, 1675 (within the limits of the present town of South Kingston, R.I.)
2 CONC  :In storming this fort he led his men bravely forward and was one of the six captains who fell in this memorable fight. A complete victory was here gained over the savage foe, but with great loss of life on both sides. Capt. Gall
2 CONC up was a brave and valuable officer and was loved and respected by his men. :The division made of his estate by order of the County Court was to the widow, 100 pounds; to the oldest son John, 137 pounds,; five daughters,70 pounds 
2 CONC each. Mrs. Hannah Gallup had also a large grant of land from the General Court in consideration of her great loss.
2 CONT
