2 CONT
2 CONT aka: John Gallup II
2 CONT
2 CONT John Gallup came to America with his mothers, two brothers, and a sister in 1633. He left Boston in 1640 and went to Taunton, then a part of Plymouth Colony, where he remained until 1651, when he moved with familyto New London. Th
2 CONC ey lived there until 1654, when he went to what is now Stonington and settled upon a land grant given by that town in recognition of the service of himself and his father in the Pequot war.
2 CONT He died in South Kingston, RI, leading troops in King Philip's war. The General Court afterwards made several land grants to his widow and children. [History of the Town of Stonington]
2 CONT
2 CONT 2nd acount for Capt. John Gallop Jr.:
2 CONT Capt. John Gallop, Jr.  was born 1618 at Bridgeport, Dorsetshire, England and was baptised 25 January 1619/20 at St. Mary's, Mosterton, Dorsetshire, England. He died 19 December 1675 at Narragansett Fort (now South Kingston, RI) w
2 CONC hile participating in the Narragansett Swamp Fight during King Philip's War and was buried near the scene of the fight. He arrived in Boston, MA aboard the Griffin 4 September 1633. He was a Ship Owner and took part in the coastal
2 CONC  New England trade. He married Ann or Hannah Lake 1643 at Boston, Suffolk Co., MA. She was baptised 3 July 1621 at North Benfleet, Essex, England and died 19 December 1675 at Stonington, New London Co., CT. Shearrived in New Engla
2 CONC nd aboard the Abigail 6 October 1635. She is buried at Whitehall Cemetery, Mystic, CT.
2 CONT (source for 2nd account: http://www.geocities.com/Heartland/Plains/4803/gallup.htm )
