1 NOTE {geni:about_me} According to the Nye Family of America, there is no record of Benjamin's birthplace or parents.
2 CONT
2 CONT recently found at this link: https://archive.org/details/genealogyofnyefa00nyeg/page/n29/mode/2up/search/%22Benjamin+nye%2C+son+of+thomas+and+agnes+nye%22?q=%22Benjamin+nye%2C+son+of+thomas+and+agnes+nye%22 Son of Thomas Nye and A
2 CONC gnes Nye Benjamin Nye was born at Bidlenden Kent, England, May 4, 1620. __________________________________________ http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~hayes/people/nye/nyes.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Denmark:  The name Nye as a family name made its first appearance about the middle of the thirteenth century in the Sjelland Section of Denmark. The prefix Ny in the Danish language, means new or new comer.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first record of a Nye is Lave Nye. He is said to be of descendant of the royal house of Sweden. He became a man of great prominence and in 1316 was Bishop of Roskilde in the Sjelland Section.
2 CONT
2 CONT SVEN NYE is mentioned in 1346 as the heir of his father, the Bishop of Roskilde. In 1363 he had in Tudse, Holbeck Bailiwick, Sjelland Section a grant of land. He probably died the same year, since his son,
2 CONT
2 CONT MARTEN NYE was in that year declared heir of his father in Tudse.
2 CONT
2 CONT NILS NYE was a son of Marten Nye and in 1418 is mentioned in the Landnamabok as possessing land in Tudse, which he then deeded to his son.
2 CONT
2 CONT BERTOLF NYE is mentioned in 1466 as the son of Nils Nye of Tudse. Bertolf had two sons James and Randolf. James had a duel with Tostig, son of Gytha, from the consequences of which he fled to England accompanied by his younger bro
2 CONC ther Randolf and settled in Wiltshire, afterward removing to Hampshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT RANDOLF NYE settled in Sussex in 1527. He had one son named William.
2 CONT
2 CONT WILLIAM NYE married Agnes Tregian and had a son named Ralph.
2 CONT
2 CONT RALPH NYE married Margaret Merynge on June 18, 1555. They had five children.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Thomas  2. Edmundus  3. Ralph  4. Anne  5. Mary
2 CONT
2 CONT THOMAS NYE married Katherine Poulsden September 9, 1583. They had four children.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Henry  2. Philip  3. John  4. Thomas THOMAS NYE married his second wife (first wife unknown) Agnes Rye (widow of Henry Rye) June 10, 1619.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Benjamin  2. Thomas
2 CONT
2 CONT ''United States
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin Nye was born at Biddenden, Ken, England May 4, 1620, the first son of Thomas and Agnes Nye.  He came to Massachusetts as a lad of fifteen and is believed to have came on the ship "Abigail" in the year 1635 in the company 
2 CONC of Thomas Tupper. The Abigail of London, Richard Hackwell, Master, sailed from Plymouth about August 1 with two hundred and twenty persons on board and many cattle. She arrived at Boston about October 8, ten weeks later, but was h
2 CONC eld in port several days due to an outbreak of smallpox among the passengers. On October 18, 1640, Benjamin, age 20 and Thomas' daughter, Katherine Tupper, age 18, (born in England January 11, 1623) were married. Following his mar
2 CONC riage, possibly as early as 1640, Benjamin erected a house at Spring Hill to the east of Sandwich and it was here that he and Katherine raised their 8 children. He later gave this house to his son John. He then built another house
2 CONC  that stayed in the family until 1924 when it was deeded to the State for a fish hatchery. In 1962 the house and a small parcel of the original grounds were acquired by the Nye Family of American Association which maintains it in 
2 CONC a restored condition as a memorial to Benjamin. They had 8 children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Mary  2. John  3. Ebenezer  4. Jonathan  5. Mercy  6. Caleb  7. Nathan  8. Benjamin
