2 CONT It is believed that Thomas Tupper Sr. came over on the ship Abigail in 1631.  Some of the descendants of Thomas Tupper & his wife Ann married descendants of those who came over on the Mayflower in 1620.  Records show that it took 
2 CONC the Mayflower 65 days to cross the Atlantic with 102 passengers and supplies.  During the first year 50 of those died.  However, it is estimated that 35 million people in America today can claim to be direct descendents to the fir
2 CONC st Mayflower passengers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note:  no Tupper is listed on the Abigail voyage in 1631 - http://www.packrat-pro.com/ships/abigail.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas, b. in Bury, Sussex County on 28 (7) January 1578. Birth date 27 January 1577/78 (16). As a ship's carpenter and member of the crew, he is believed to have voyaged to America first about 1621, again in 1624 when he remained
2 CONC  a year, a third time in 1631 when he stayed at Cape Ann for an uncertain period, and finally to Sandwich, Mass. in 1637 where he settled and remained until his death 28 March 1676 (1678 according to (16)). This sequence is from (
2 CONC 3) but it does not seem entirely correct: note that he married in January 1627/28 in MA and his wife died in Topsfield, MA in 1634. According to (2), about 1635 he emigrated to Massachusetts to obtain "liberty of conscience", deni
2 CONC ed to him under the reign of Charles I of England. This seems however like a bit of an embellishment since we know he first came to America as a hired tradesman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Tupper married first in the Parish of Chelmsford, Essex County, 29 April 1622, to Katharine Gator. They had two children, both born in England (for Thomas' children see next section). It is not clear if his wife ever came t
2 CONC o America. Secondly, he was married, in Topsfield, Mass., 25 January 1627/28, to Susan Turner, a widow. Thomas and Susan had two children, both born in England (1628 and 1633), so at least Susan must have returned to England. She 
2 CONC must have later returned to the New World because she died in Topsfield in 1634. Thirdly, in Ipswich, Mass., 21 December 1634, Thomas married Anne Hodgson (Thomas would have been about 56). Anne was a widow, of Topsfield, born abo
2 CONC ut 1588 (between 1588 and 1598 in (16) and written "Hodgeson") and who died in Sandwich 4 June 1676. They had one child. If the birthdates are correct, Anne would have been about 49 or 50 when she had her son Thomas; this seems a 
2 CONC bit unlikely.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas was one of the incorporators (2) of the town of Sandwich, MA and became an outstanding citizen of that town. He served in the general court in 1644 and later was deputy for 20 years, from 1647 to 1667; he served on juries, 
2 CONC local boards and commissions, was a charter member of the church and for three years starting in 1667 was Selectman of the town (i.e., a member of a board of officers annually elected to manage various local concerns in a New Engl
2 CONC and town). In his later years, when there was no settled minister, Mr. Tupper conducted religious services as a layman and he was deeply interested in religious work among the Indians. The source (2) adds that he cared especially 
2 CONC for the Indians who were "living in spiritual darkness and savagery" and that he built the Indians a place of worship and established a congregation at Herring Pond. He was shrewd trader and invested heavily in real estate and hel
2 CONC d large holdings at his death.
2 CONT
