2 TITL Kember English COA
2 NOTE Origin Displayed: English - Spelling variations of this family name include: Kimber, Kember and others. First found in Cornwall where they were seated from very ancient times, some say well before the Norman Conquest and the arriv
3 CONC al of Duke William at Hastings in 1066 C.E. Some of the first settlers of this family name or some of its variants were: Henry and Mary Kimber arrived in Philadelphia in 1753; John Kimber settled in Charles Town in 1764.
3 CONT (From www.HouseOfNames.com Archives copyright © 2000 - 2009). Motto Translated: Thou may'st break, but shalt not bend me. This interesting and unusual surname is of Anglo-Saxon origin, and has two possible sources. The surname may
3 CONC  derive from the Old English pre-7th Century "cemban", to comb, and would have been an occupational name for a comber of wood or flax. Job descriptive surnames originally denoted the actual occupation of the name bearer, and later
3 CONC  became hereditary. The other possible derivation is from a metronymic, formed from the Old English female personal name "Cyneburh", composed of the elements "cyne", royal, with "burh", fortress, stronghold. This name was borne by
3 CONC  a daughter of the 7th Century King Penda of Mercia, who, in spite of her father's staunch opposition to Christianity, was converted and founded an abbey, serving as its head. She was venerated as a saint in the Middle Ages, and c
3 CONC hildren were named after her. The surname is first recorded in the early half of the 14th Century and can also be found as Kember and Kimbrough. On 31 January 1581, Edward Kimber was christened in London. Isaac Kimber was a genera
3 CONC l baptist minister who conducted "The Morning Chronicle" (1728 - 1732), and also edited Ainsworth's "Latin Dictionary" (1751), and published the "Life of Oliver Cromwell" (1724). The Coat of Arms granted to the family is a silver 
3 CONC shield with three black Cornish choughs, beaks and legs red and on a black chief three silver mullets, the Crest being a bull's head affrontee proper. The first recorded spelling of the family name is shown to be that of Roger le 
3 CONC Kembar, which was dated 1327, in the "Subsidy Rolls of Sussex", during the reign of King Edward 2nd, known as "Edward of Caernafon", 1307 - 1327. Surnames became necessary when governments introduced personal taxation. In England 
3 CONC this was known as Poll Tax. Throughout the centuries, surnames in every country have continued to "develop" often leading to astonishing variants of the original spelling.
