1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Francis Cooke''' (c 1583 – April 7, 1663) was a [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/English_Dissenters Leiden Separatist] who went to America in 1620 on the Pilgrim ship [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mayflower ''May
2 CONC flower''], arriving at Plymouth in what became the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plymouth_Colony Plymouth Colony]. He was a founding member of the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Massachusetts_Bay_Colony Massachusetts Bay Colony],
2 CONC  and a signer of the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mayflower_Compact Mayflower Compact].  This early settler is one of the twenty-six male [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pilgrims_(Plymouth_Colony)_ Pilgrims] known to have descend
2 CONC ants, and the longest living male Mayflower Pilgrim. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT >There is no evidence to support [https://www.geni.com/people/Edward-Cooke/6000000010955389061 Edward Cooke] and Alice Canton as his parents.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Caleb Johnson’s [http://mayflowerhistory.com/cooke-francis/ “Mayflower History”]:
2 CONT
2 CONT > Francis Cooke was born about 1583. It is probable he was born in England, perhaps  from the Canterbury or Norwich areas.  He married Hester le Mahieu on 20 July  1603 in Leiden, Holland; she was a [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/
2 CONC Walloons French Walloon] whose parents had initially  fled to [http://www.machadoink.com/Walloon%20or%20French%20Church.htm Canterbury, England]; she left for Leiden sometime before 1603.  Francis  Cooke and Hester le Mahieu's mar
2 CONC riage occurred in [https://www.smithsonianmag.com/travel/the-pilgrims-before-plymouth-111851259/ Leiden, Holland] six years  before the Pilgrim church made its move there, so he was living there long  before their arrival and must
2 CONC  have met up with and joined them afterwards.  What brought Francis to Holland in the first  place is unknown: religious persecution of Protestants in England did not really  begin until after King James took power in 1604. ([http
2 CONC s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_Puritans_under_King_James_I Wikipedia]).
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biographical summary 
2 CONT
2 CONT “Signer of the Mayflower Compact" From:  [http://www.mayflowerfamilies.com/mayflower/francis_cooke_family.htm “Mayflower Families”] (dead link)
2 CONT
2 CONT >… Other than being identified as a bachelor and a "wool comber," little to nothing is known of his early life. He and his son John originally embarked on the [https://pilgrimhall.org/ap_voyage_mayflower_speedwell.htm ''Speedwell'
2 CONC '], but were transferred to the Mayflower. Hester and the other children would follow in the Ann in August 1623. Leading an apparently exemplary life, Francis Cooke was among the signers of the Mayflower Compact, a purchaser in th
2 CONC e rights of the London "Adventurers," a freeman, and in general a good citizen frequently called upon and appointed to various committees to perform various duties. He and his wife Hester toiled in this new land for some 40-plus y
2 CONC ears before leaving their legacy to others that would follow.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family  
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis Cooke married some time after July 20, 1603 at Leiden, Holland to [https://www.geni.com/people/Hester-Cooke/6000000006333221372?through=6000000003613328328 Hester le Mahieu] (c. 1585-7 April 1663, Plymouth, daughter of Jea
2 CONC n Le Mahier and Jeanne (unknown). (Leiden records [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000011801178235&graph_node_id=profile-5822623&mode=tagged link])
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis Cooke came on the Mayflower from Leiden with his eldest son John in 1620 and as such was one of the signers of the Mayflower Compact. His wife, Hester, soon followed on the [http://mayflowerhistory.com/anne ''Anne'' and ''
2 CONC Little James''] in 1623. She brought with her three additional children: Jacob, Jane, and Hester. Another child, Mary, was born in 1626.  Hester died after June 8, 1666, and was buried at Burial Hill in Plymouth, Mass.
2 CONT
2 CONT The seven children of Francis Cooke and Hester Mahieu (updated July 2021 from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_Cooke#Family Wikipedia]):
2 CONT
2 CONT The birth order of the Cooke children is uncertain.
2 CONT
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/people/John-Cooke-Mayflower-Passenger/6000000000426575370?through=6000000003613328328 John] was baptized in Leiden, Holland between January and March 1607 and died in Dartmouth on November 23, 1695. He marr
2 CONC ied Sarah Warren on March 28, 1634, in Plymouth and had five children. She died after July 15, 1696.  “ And his son John, which came over with him, is married, and hath 4 children living."
2 CONT # a [https://www.geni.com/people/Child-Cooke/6000000008833005959?through=6000000000426575370 child] was buried in Leiden on May 20, 1608.
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/people/Jane-Mitchell/6000000000796814453?through=6000000003613328328 Jane] was born about 1609 in Leiden. She married Experience Mitchell in Plymouth after May 22, 1627. Her date of death is unknown, as is 
2 CONC the date of his second marriage. But his first three children are generally considered to be hers.
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/people/Elizabeth-Cooke/6000000014054357641?through=6000000008833005959 Elizabeth], was baptized in Leiden on December 26, 1611. There is no further record.
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/people/Jacob-Cooke/6000000003582715205?through=6000000014054357641 Jacob] was born about 1618 and died in Plymouth in December 1675. He was buried at Tyler Point Cemetery, Barrington, R.I. He married 1. Dam
2 CONC aris Hopkins shortly after June 10, 1646, in Plymouth and had seven children. Her father was Mayflower passenger Stephen Hopkins. 2. Elizabeth (Lettice) Shurtleff on November 18, 1669, in Plymouth and had two children.
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/people/Hester-Wright/6000000004630832136?through=6000000003582715205 Hester] was born about 1620 in Leiden and died between 1669 and 1691. She married Richard Wright in Plymouth in 1644 and had six children
2 CONC . She was buried at Burial Hill in Plymouth, Mass.
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/people/Mary-Tomson/6000000000426575449?through=6000000004630832136 Mary] was born in Plymouth about 1625 and died in Middleborough on March 21, 1714. She married John Tomson on December 26, 1645, in Plymout
2 CONC h.   Both Mary and John were buried at Nemasket Hill Cemetery, Middleborough, Massachusetts.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Occupation
2 CONT
2 CONT There is no record at Plymouth of what kind of work Cooke was engaged in. He did have an apprentice, John Harmon, for seven years starting in 1636. Francis Cooke was also on the 1643 Plymouth list of those who were able to bear ar
2 CONC ms. (Plymouth Colony Records 8:187-196 [https://static1.squarespace.com/static/50a02efce4b046b42952af27/t/50a93ec2e4b0726ad3f136ca/1353268930507/MenAbleToBearArms1643.pdf PDF])
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Reputation 
2 CONT
2 CONT ''In 1651, William Bradford wrote of him:''
2 CONT >"And seeing it hath pleased Him to give me [William Bradford] to see thirty years completed since these beginnings, and that the great works of His providence are to be observed, I have thought it not unworthy my pains to take a 
2 CONC view of the decreasings and increasings of these persons and such changes as hath passed over them and theirs in this thirty years ...
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Francis Cooke is still living, a very old man, and hath seen his children's children have children. After his wife came over with other of his children; he hath three still living by her, all married and have five children, so t
2 CONC heir increase is eight. And his son John which came over with him is married, and hath four children living."     William Bradford, Of Plymouth Plantation 1620-1647, ed.
2 CONT Samuel Eliot Morison (New York : Knopf, 1991), p. 75-6. ([https://books.google.com/books?id=MRF5AAAAMAAJ&focus=searchwithinvolume&q=“Francis+Cooke GoogleBooks])
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Estate
2 CONT
2 CONT Cooke appears to have been granted many different parcels of land in and around Plymouth. Some of this land he gave to his sons Jacob and John, which they sold portions of. Francis even sold some land to William Bradford. His neig
2 CONC hbors included Isaak Allerton, Edward Winslow, and Thomas Prence as well as his 2 sons John and Jacob.
2 CONT
2 CONT On December 7, 1659, Cooke made out his will, describing himself as "at present weak and infirm in body." He had a very simple will that just gave everything to "Hester my dear and loving wife." 
2 CONT
2 CONT from  The Will and Inventory of Estate of Francis Cooke, deceased 1663 
2 CONT
2 CONT >Francis’ will was made  10 Jul 1659. He makes his wife Hester and son John executors. It is witnessed by Howland and Alden. Inventory was taken 1663 by Eph. Tuckham and Wm Crowe.       
2 CONT
2 CONT >>7 December 1659 - The last Will and Testament of ffrancis Cooke of Plymouth late Deceased: exhibited before the Court held att Plymouth aforsaid the fift day of June 1663 on the oathes of mr John Aldin and mr John howland; 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>The Last Will and Testament of ffrancis Cooke made this seaventh of the tenth month 1659 
2 CONT
2 CONT * I being att prsent weake and Infeirme in body yett in prfect memory throw mercy Doe comitt my soule unto god that gave it and my body to the earthe; which my will is should bee Intered in a Decent and comly manner;  
2 CONT * As for such goods and lands as I stand posessed of I Doe will and bequeath as followeth;  
2 CONT * 1 My will is that hester my Dear and loveing wife shall have all my moveable goods and all my Cattle of all kinds; viz: neat Cattle horsekind sheep and swine to be att her Dispose  
2 CONT * 2 my will is that hester my wife shall have and Injoy my lands both upland and meddow lands which att prsent I posesse During her life  
2 CONT *  3 I Doe ordaine and appoint my Deare wife and my son John Cooke Joynt exequitors of this my said will  
2 CONT * Witnes      John Aldin   ffrancis Cooke   John howland 
2 CONT
2 CONT >Francis Cooke died in Plymouth on April 7, 1663, and was (probably) buried at [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cole%27s_Hill Cole’s Hill] in Plymouth.  (Cenotaph at Burial Hill in Plymouth, Massachusetts ([https://images.findagrave
2 CONC .com/photos/2018/326/34825341_a3cdb364-c2da-44cf-8601-b62d0c81bea9.jpeg image]).  (The  first internment at Burial Hill was in the early 1670's - after the death of Francis Cooke.)
2 CONT
2 CONT > An inventory was taken of his possessions by Ephraim Tinkham and William Crow on 1 May 1663. His total net worth at that time was 86 pounds, 11 shillings, and 1 pence. From his estate inventory, it appears that he was involved w
2 CONC ith sheep and wool as he had sixteen sheep and five lambs, a "woolen wheele & scales," three pairs of sheep shears, and twenty pounds of wool.  Besides the housing and land; the goods and Chattels amount to eighty six pounds eleve
2 CONC n shillings and a peney; apprised by us, Ephraim Tinkham his E T , William Crow. (From [https://pilgrimhall.org/pdf/Francis_Cooke_Will_Inventory.pdf “Pilgrim Hall Museum - The Will of Francis Cooke”]).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notable descendants  
2 CONT
2 CONT Notable descendants of Francis Cooke include Cephas Thompson, William Drew Washburn, Mrs. Anna Mary Robertson ("Grandma Moses"), (George) Orson Welles, Abel Head "Shanghai" Pierce (Texas cattleman who introduced the Brahman cattle
2 CONC  breed into Texas), Actor Richard Gere, and Beach Boys Brian, Carl, and Dennis Wilson. (See more at [https://famouskin.com/famous-kin-menu.php?name=6474+francis+cooke FamousKin.com]).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000193587047835&size=large}}
2 CONT
