2 CONT * [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~mcneillyandco/genealogy/old_occupations.htm Old occupations] - WOOLCOMBER. … It was an apprenticed trade, a seven year apprenticeship being the norm in the mid 18th century with apprenticeship sta
2 CONC rting at about the age of 12 or 13. 
2 CONT * [http://www.histarch.illinois.edu/plymouth/GLOSSARY.htm Plymouth Colony Glossary] - Freeman. A term which designated citizenship of the Colony, which was restricted to adult males. A freeman possessed the right to vote for the G
2 CONC overnor and Assistants and the right to hold office.  Initially all stockholders in the joint-stock company that financed the Colony were freemen, but not all freemen were stockholders. (Fennell 1998: 7). 
