1 NOTE MAYFLOWER PASSENGER
1 NOTE
2 CONT This information follows the research of others and confirmations by census and family tree info. ALL the info I have is on this database, however, I appreciate any corrections or additions.
1 NOTE Passenger on the Mayflower.
1 NOTE Francis Cooke (1583 - 1663)    MAYFLOWER
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=5b4be6d4-9781-4d78-9ace-a7c5e35cdfd8&tid=11048819&pid=-503794048
1 NOTE A MAYFLOWER PASSENGER.
1 NOTE OCCUPATION: Woolcomber, Husbandman, Surveyor of Highways 
2 CONT The Cookes are ancestors of FDR, both Presidents Bush, Attorney General Elliot Richardson, painter "Grandma Moses," Orson Welles, Gen. Leonard Wood (commander of the Rough Riders), Josiah Granville Leach (Civil War veteran and ori
2 CONC ginator of Flag Day), George M. Pullman (Founder of the Pullman Car Co.), Chef Julia Child, Marjorie Betty Crocker Child, folk singer Pete Seeger, poet William Cullen Bryant, Wild Bill Hickok, John Bartlett of Bartlett's Quotation
2 CONC s, Tonight Show host Johnny Carson, and possibly Sir Winston Churchill. Marilyn Monroe is presumed to be a descendant.
2 CONT MAYFLOWER PASSENGER
2 CONT There were 102 passengers that embarked on the Mayflower, including three pregnant women.  The Mayflower left Plymouth, England on 6 September 1620, and sighted land on 9 November 1620.  Landfall was made on 11 November 1620.
1 NOTE !Mayflower pilgrim.  Historical information included in notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis COOKE
2 CONT of Plymouth
2 CONT A Biographical Research Profile
2 CONT by David Haas
2 CONT Background
2 CONT Francis Cooke came on the Mayflower with his eldest son John in 1620 and as suchwas one of the signers of the Mayflower Compact. His wife, Hester, soon followed on the Anne in 1623. She brought with her three additional children: 
2 CONC Jacob, Jane, and Hester. Another child, Mary, was born in 1626.
2 CONT Freemen
2 CONT Francis Cooke was also listed on the original list of freemen for Plymouth and was found on this list again in 1633, 1637 and 1658. As a freemen he had severalduties which were thrust upon him. He served twice on the Grand Inquest
2 CONC , once in1638 and a second time in 1640. Cooke also served on numerous juries from the years 1638-48. His most notable case was that of Allis Bishop. She admitted to murdering her four year old daughter by slashing her throat and 
2 CONC windpipe with a knife. His major service to the community, however, seemed to come in the highwayrealm. In 1637 he was appointed to the committee to lay out highways. He followed this appointment with the job of surveyor of the hi
2 CONC ghways for Plymouth in 1641, 1642 and again in 1645. He even served on a committee to find the best route for a new road.
2 CONT Occupation
2 CONT There is no record or what kind of work Cooke was engaged in. He did have and apprentice, John Harmon, for seven years starting in 1636. Francis Cooke was alsoon the 1643 Plymouth list of those who were able to bear arms.
2 CONT Estate
2 CONT Francis Cooke was not as active in court as his jury duty. He won a judgment against John Browne, the elder, for abusing his cattle. Along with twelve others, he was given 4 black heifers and two shee goats in the 1627 cattle divi
2 CONC sion. Cooke appears to have been granted many different parcels of land in and around Plymouth. Some of this land he gave to his sons Jacob and John, which they sold portions of. Francis even sold some land to William Bradford. Hi
2 CONC s neighbors includedIsaak Allerton, Edward Winslow, and Thomas Prence as well as his 2 sons John and Jacob.
2 CONT The End
2 CONT The will of Francis Cooke, dated 7 October 1659, leaves all his possessions to his wife Hester with Hester and his son John as joint executors of the will. JohnAldin and John Howland were witnesses to the will. Francis Cooke passe
2 CONC d away on7 April 1663 at the age of approximately 80. An inventory was taken of his possessions by Ephraim Tinkham and William Crow on 1 May 1663. His total net worth at that time was 86 pounds, 11 shillings, and 1 pence.
2 CONT Primary Source Reference
