2 CONT ==About Plymouth Colony==
2 CONT
2 CONT History of the Plymouth Colony from Micrsoft, "Encarta" 96 Encyclopedia, 1993-1995, Microsoft, Corp/Funk & Wagnalls Corp. says:
2 CONT
2 CONT "The foundation of the Plymouth Colony was one of the major events in the early history of the British colonies in North American.  In the reign of Elizabeth I, queen of England, one of the sects of Puritans known as "Brownists", 
2 CONC separated from the new Church of England and after much persecution took refuge in the Netherlands.  They finally determined to emigrate to America (after 11 years in Leiden, the Netherlands).  A group of London investors financed
2 CONC  them in exchange for most of their produce from American during their first six years.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Their ship, the Mayflower, taking on many other passengers (such as Richard Warren), to fill the boat, sailed from Plymouth, England, for America on September 16, 1620.  (Mayflower Quarterly Publications indicate originally they w
2 CONC ent from Leiden, Holland, to England, planning on taking the "Mayflower" and the "Goodspeed", both named for wild flowers, to the New World.  
2 CONT
2 CONT However, the Goodspeed was leaking badly by the time they reached Londen, so they sold it, and continued on in only the Mayflower.  The new owners of the Goodspeed replaced the sails with smaller ones, which stopped the leakage, a
2 CONC nd they continued using the Goodspeed in the shipping industry of England for many more years.)  When the Pilgrim "Saints" (Brownists), and "Strangers" (like Richard Warren), reached the American coast, strong winds drove the Mayf
2 CONC lower into what is now Provincetown harbour, at the end of Cape Cod rather than their original destination in Virginia.  They wrote and signed the Mayflower Compact, forming the first constitutional political democracy in the Amer
2 CONC icas.  After some exploration they settled on the site of what is now Plymouth, Massachusetts.  The Plymouth Colony later united with other New England colonies to form the Massachusetts Bay Colony in 1691.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==of Pilgrims and Clothing==
2 CONT
2 CONT How do we know what the Pilgrims actually wore? The Pilgrims were certainly knowledgeable of fabrics and clothing construction. In looking at the occupations of the Pilgrims we find that Isaac Allerton and James Chilton were tailo
2 CONC rs, William Bradford was a fustian-maker, Edward Tilley was a cloth-maker, John Tilley was a silk worker, Francis Cooke and William White were wool combers or carders, and Digory Priest had been a hatter in London. In addition to 
2 CONC those clothing-related trades we know that William Mullins was a boot and shoe merchant, and Thomas Rogers was a camlet merchant. Other merchants who may have dealt in similar goods were John Carver, John Turner and Richard Warren
2 CONC .
2 CONT
