2 CONT More Info at:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Warren <nowiki>--------------------</nowiki> Signatory to the Mayflower compact.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was one of the nineteen signers of the Compact who survived the first winter. A contemporaneous authority described him as 'grave Richard Warren,' 'a man of integrity, justice and uprightness, or piety and serious religion,' an
2 CONC d as 'a useful instrument during the short time he lived, bearing a deep share in the difficulties and troubles of the plantation.'
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mayflower_Compact
2 CONT
2 CONT When he came over on the Mayflower, he left behind his wife and five daughters, planning to have them sent over after things were more settled in the Colony. His wife and daughters arrived in America in 1623, on the ship Anne.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~barbpretz/PS03/PS03_210.HTM
2 CONT
2 CONT More erroneous information has been published about Richard Warren than any other Mayflower passenger, probably because he has so many descendants (note that all seven of his children grew up and married). It is time here to debun
2 CONC k many of the mistakes that have been published over the past hundred years. The only concrete things we know about Richard Warren's ancestry are that he was a merchant of London--whether he was born there or not is an entirely di
2 CONC fferent question. We also know that his wife was named Elizabeth. He had five daughters baptized in England somewhere, and perhaps the true records will some day be brought to light.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------- Arrived on Ship Mayflower 1620 Richard arrived in Plymouth. Buried Burial Hill Plymouth, Massachusetts.
