1 NOTE From Ancestral File (TM), data as of 2 January 1996.
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-20792
1 NOTE "Mayflower" passenger. His wife and 5 daughters came on the "Anne" in 1623. Then 2 sons b. at Plymouth.
1 NOTE Standish, Carver, the Tilley's, Richard Warren and others circulated the deep Bay of Cape Cod, soon after landing at what was then known as Plymouth.
2 CONT Mayflower Passengers
2 CONT  Richard Warren
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: Probably in Hertford, England. Mayflower Families: Richard Warren for Four Generations, contains the best, most thorough and completely researched genealogy on Richard Warren.  It covers every descendant of his for the first
2 CONC  four generations.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mayflower Families: Richard Warren's Fifth Generation for children Mary, Ann, and Elizabeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage:
2 CONT Elizabeth Walker, 14 April 1610, Great Amwell, Hertford, England, daughter of Augustine Walker.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: 1628, Plymouth.
2 CONT Children: Mary, Ann, Sarah, Elizabeth, Abigail, Nathaniel, and Joseph.
2 CONT Biographical Information
2 CONT Richard Warren's English origins and ancestry have been the subject of much speculation, and countless different ancestries have been published for him, without a shred of evidence to support them.  Luckily in December 2002, Edwar
2 CONC d Davies discovered the missing piece of the puzzle.  Researchers had long known of the marriage of Richard Warren to Elizabeth Walker on 14 April 1610 at Great Amwell, Hertford.  Since we know the Mayflower passenger had a wife n
2 CONC amed Elizabeth, and a first child born about 1610, this was a promising record.  But no children were found for this couple in the parish registers, and no further evidence beyond the names and timing, until the will of Augustine 
2 CONC Walker was discovered in December 2002 by Edward Davies.  In the will of Augustine Walker, dated April 1613, he mentions "my daughter Elizabeth Warren wife of Richard Warren", and "her three children Mary, Ann and Sarah."  We know
2 CONC  that the Mayflower passenger's first three children were named Mary, Ann, and Sarah (in that birth order), and that they were born c1610, c1612, and c1614, so this put the nail in the coffin and we can say with near certainty tha
2 CONC t Richard Warren of the Mayflower married in Great Amwell, Hertford to Elizabeth Walker, daughter of Augustine Walker.  Additional research is currently being sponsored by MayflowerHistory.com to see if anything further can be lea
2 CONC rned about these families.
2 CONT
2 CONT Very little is known about Richard Warren's life in America.  He came alone on the Mayflower in 1620, leaving behind his wife and five daughters.  They came to him on the ship Anne in 1623, and Richard and Elizabeth subsequently h
2 CONC ad sons Nathaniel and Joseph at Plymouth.  He received his acres in the Division of Land in 1623, and his family shared in the 1627 Division of Cattle.  But he died a year later in 1628, the only record of his death being found in
2 CONC  Nathaniel Morton's 1669 book New England's Memorial, in which he writes: "This year [1628] died Mr. Richard Warren, who was an useful instrument and during his life bare a deep share in the difficulties and troubles of the first 
2 CONC settlement of the Plantation of New Plymouth."
2 CONT
2 CONT All of Richard Warren's children survived to adulthood, married, and had large families: making Richard Warren one of the most common Mayflower passengers to be descended from.  Richard Warren's descendants include such notables a
2 CONC s Civil War general Ulysses S. Grant, President Franklin D. Roosevelt, and Alan B. Shepard, Jr. the first American in space and the fifth person to walk on the moon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Additional Resources
2 CONT
2 CONT MayflowerHistory.com, Copyright © 1994-2003. All Rights Reserved.From "Of Plymouth Plantation 1620 1647", by Gov. William Bradford, printed 1996, p. 68 which gives account of Great Grandfather Richard Warren on one of their early 
2 CONC explorations of the land while the Mayflower lay at anchor in the bay:
2 CONT
2 CONT "The month of November being spent in these affairs, and much foul weather falling in, the 6th of December they sent out their shallop again with ten of their principal men (see footnote 7 below) and some seamen, upon further disc
2 CONC overy, intending to circulate that deep bay of Cape Cod. The weather was very cold and it froze so hard as the spray of the sea lighting on their coats, they were as if they had been glazed. Yet that night betimes they got down in
2 CONC to the bottom of the bay, and as they drew near the shore they saw some ten or twelve Indians very busy about something. They landed about a league or two from them, (8) and had much ado to put ashore anywhere--it lay so full of f
2 CONC lats. Being landed, it grew late and they made themselves a barricado with logs and boughts as well as they could in the time, and set out their sentinel and betook them to rest, and saw the smoke of the fire the savages made that
2 CONC  night. When morning was come they divided their company, some to coast along the shore in the boat, and the rest marched through the woods to see the land, if any fit place might be for their dwelling. They came also to the place
2 CONC  where they saw the Indians the night before, and found they had been cutting up a great fish like a grampus, (9)" being some two inches thick of fat like a hog, some pieces whereof they had left by the way. And the shallop found 
2 CONC two more of these fishes dead on the sands, a thing usual after storms in that place, by reason of the great flats of sand that lie off.
2 CONT So they ranged up and down all that day, but found no people, nor any place they liked. When the sun grew low, they hasted out of the woods to meet with their shallop, to whom they made signs to come to them into a creek hard by, 
2 CONC (1), the which they did at nigh water; of which they were very glad, for they had not seen each other all that day since the morning. So they made them a barricado as usually they did every night, with logs, stakes leeward, partly
2 CONC  to shelter them from cold and wind (making their fire in the middle and lying round about it) and partly to defend them from any sudden assaults of the savages, if they should surround them; so being very weary, they betook them 
2 CONC to rest. But about midnight (51) they heard a hideous and great cry, and their sentinel called "Arm! arm!" So they bestirred them and stood to their arms and shot off a couple of muskets, and then the noise ceased. They concluded 
2 CONC it was a company of wolves or such like wild beasts, for one of the seamen told them he had often heard such a noise in newfoundland."
2 CONT .  Richard Warren was born 1580, and died 1628.  He married Elizabeth 1608 in Kent, England.  She was born 1583 in Kent, England, and died 22 Oct 1673 in Plymouth, MA.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for Richard Warren:
2 CONT Arrived on board Mayflower - 1620. Wife and children stayed behind and came later.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Richard Warren and Elizabeth are:
2 CONT
2 CONT         i.  Mary Warren, b. 1610, Greenwick, Kent, England; d. 27 Mar 1683, Plymouth, MA.
2 CONT
2 CONT         ii.  Ann Warren, b. 1612, Kent, England; d. 19 Feb 1675, Marshfield, Plymounth County, MA.
2 CONT
2 CONT        iii.  Sarah Warren, b. Abt. 1614, Kent, England; d. 25 Jul 1686, Plymouth, Plymouth Co, MA.
2 CONT
2 CONT        iv.  Elizabeth Warren, b. 20 Jan 1617, Kent, England; d. 09 Mar 1670, Hingham, Plymouth County, MA.
2 CONT
2 CONT        v.  Abigail Warren, b. Abt. 1618, Kent, England; d. 03 Jan 1692.
2 CONT
2 CONT       vi.  Nathaniel Warren, b. 1625; d. 1667.
2 CONT
2 CONT       vii.  Joseph Warren, b. 22 Mar 1627, Plymouth, MA; d. 04 May 1689, Plymouth, MA.
