1 NOTE Richard Warren (c.1580 - 1628) a passenger on the Mayflower (old &quot;May Floure&quot;) in 1620
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=bf96395d-e1a5-4632-9455-de3fc77fb61a&tid=1579541&pid=-1056950401
1 NOTE Biographical Summary
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=f560cd51-9d2a-4e86-a8d8-a6bad8bb886a&tid=1579541&pid=-1056950401
1 NOTE Online Biography
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=f6cb0bb1-5c5b-4ac8-9cdc-c0b3d1e706fb&tid=1221135&pid=-1967007796
1 NOTE MAYFLOWER PASSENGER
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Richard and Elizabeth Warren are the ancestors of FDR, U.S. Grant, Civil War Heroes Commander William B. Cushing, Lt. Alonzo Cushing (Cushing's Battery), and Lt. Howard B. Cushing (Indian Fighter), Attorney General Elliot Ri
2 CONC chardson, political economist Edward S. Mason, astronaut Alan Bartlett Shepard, poet Henry Wadsworth Longfellow, astronomer Maria Mitchell, artist Winslow Homer, Admiral Samuel Eliot Morison (Pulitzer Prize-winning historian), Rev
2 CONC . Hosea Ballou (Father of American Universalism), George M. Pullman (Founder of the Pullman Car Co.), biographer Gamaliel Bradford, Roger Nash Baldwin (Founder of the ACLU), publisher Frank Nelson Doubleday, authors Henry David Th
2 CONC oreau and Laura Ingalls Wilder, John Bartlett (of Bartletts Quotations), inventor Elias Howe, political economist Edward S. Mason, Orson Welles, Gen. Leonard Wood, illustrator Charles Dana Gibson, the Wright Brothers and Amelia Ea
2 CONC rhart, Chef Julia Child, Tonight Show host Johnny Carson, The Beach Boys (Brian Wilson & Co.), and possibly Sir Winston Churchill.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Warren was a merchant and haberdasher in London before coming to America. He was not one of the Leiden Puritans fleeing religious persecution, but rather one of the so-called "strangers" who were picked up in London and co
2 CONC mprised more than half of the passengers on the Mayflower. In Plymouth, he served as Assistant Governor of the Colony and was the 12th signer of the Mayflower Compact.
