2 CONT WILLIAM ALMY gentleman (vide marriage license 1626) of South Kilworth, b. probably at Dunton-Bassett, in Leicestershire, England, the earliest known home of the Almey's—removed to Lynn, Massachusetts, Sandwich, and Portsmouth, Rho
2 CONC de Island; b. 1601, d. 28th February, 1676; will proved 23d April, 1677; m. in 1626 Audrey BARLOWE, of Lutterworth; b. 1603, d. 1676; he was in New England prior to 1631; in 1635 having been home to England he returned to New Engl
2 CONC and, in the ship “Abigail”; Freeman, 1655; Juryman, 1656; Commissioner, 1656-1657, 1663; Foreman of Grand Jury. William's father Christopher Almey, gentleman (vide will, 2d October, 1624), b. 1575; d. 4th October, 1624 at South Ki
2 CONC lworth. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [p.354] Robert ALMEY, husbandman, of Dunton—will 11th December, 1579. Proved 8th February, 1581, was ancestor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT I. Ann, b. 1627; d. 17th May, 1709; m. John GREEN, b. 1620, d. 27th November, 1708. 
2 CONT
2 CONT II. CHRISTOPHER, b. 1632, of whom later. 
2 CONT
2 CONT III. John, d. 1st October, 1676; 24th July, 1667, Lieutenant of Troop of Horse; served as Captain in King Philip's War, 1676; m. Mary COLE, daughter of James and Mary COLE, she m. (secondly) 1677, John POCOCKE. 
2 CONT
2 CONT IV. Job, d. 1684; Deputy, 1670; Committee to treat with Indians, 7th May, 1673; Assistant, 1673-1675; m. Mary UNTHANK, d. 1724, daughter of Christopher and Susanna UNTHANK. 
2 CONT
2 CONT V. Catherine, m. Bartholomew WEST. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Discussion as to whether there were one or two William Almy’s in New England in the 1630s is covered in The Great Migration Begins
2 CONT
2 CONT ———————————————————————————————————————
2 CONT Plantagenet Ancestry: A Study In Colonial And Medieval Families, 2nd Edition, 2011 by: Douglas Richardson pg 227
2 CONT
2 CONT 18. AUDREY BARLOW, born about 1600-3 (aged 26 in 1626, aged 32 in 1635). She married by license dated 17 July 1626 WILLIAM ALMY (or ALMEY), Gent., of South Kilworth, Leicestershire, son and heir of Christopher Almy, of South Kilwo
2 CONC rth, Leicestershire, by ________, daughter of _______ Clarke, of Luttenworth, Leicestershire. He was born about 1600-1 (aged 26 in 1626, aged 34 in 1635). They had three sons, Christopher, John, and Job, and two daughters, Katheri
2 CONC ne (wife of Bartholomew West and Nicholas Brown) and Annis (wife of John Greene). In 1625 Edward Clement, clerk, sued William Almey, yeoman, son and executor of Christopher Almey, deceased, in the Court of Requests by regarding th
2 CONC e parsonage of Luttenworth, Leicestershire and a bond connected therewith. He and his wife, Audrey, and their two children, Annis and Christopher, immigrated to New England in 1635 on the ship Abigail. The settled initially at Lyn
2 CONC n, Massachusetts, but in 1637, they removed to Sandwich, Massachsetts. In 1638 he was one of several Sandwich men who were fined for keeping swine unringed. He took the oath of fidelity at Sandwich in 1639. In 1640 he received 8-1
2 CONC /2 acresin the division of meadow at Sandwich. in 1642 he sold his dwelling house in Sandwich, Massachusetts to Edmond Freeman the younger and removed to Portsmouth, Rhode Island. In 1643 he received a grant of eight acres of plan
2 CONC ting ground in Portsmouth. In 1644 he was one of three men to have land at the wading river at Portsmouth. In 1650 the Portsmouth town council granted that he should have the land that lies at the head of his farm to come to the s
2 CONC ame height that Philip Shearman his land now runneth. He served as Deputy for Portsmouth to the Rhode Island General Court, 1648, 1650, 1656-7, and 1663; Assessor for Portsmouth, 1659, 1663; and Portsmouth town meeting moderator, 
2 CONC 1660. In 1652 he sued John Smith, of Warwick, merchant, claiming that Smith and his partner william Field had detained the quantity of five anchors and a half of liquors worth ???40; the town of Providence first dismissed the case
2 CONC , but reexamined the evidence and found for Almy. In 1656 he sold to Richard Bulgar of Portsmouth a grant of eight acres of land within the common fence. In 1659, as William Almy of Portsmouth, planter, he deeded to his son, John 
2 CONC Almym about 50 acres of land, it being part of his farm in Portsmouth, Rhode Island whereon I now dwell. In 1669 the town of Portsmouth voted to institute as a suit against him for throwing a fence across a highway which doth lead
2 CONC  to one of the most principal watering places for cattle in this town. The town subsequently won the suit in the court of trials. WILLIAM ALMY left a will dated 28 Feb. 1676/7, proved 23 April 1677. His wife, Audrey, was living 28
2 CONC  Feb. 1676/7. Source Link: https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000175881871822label=@S25@ Source Link: https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000175875792829label=@S156@
2 CONT
