2 CONT == Disputed Parentage ==Lettice-37|Thomas Lettice alleged parent of this Thomas is unproven and has been disconnected until proof can be found
2 CONT
2 CONT == Thomas Lettice, Immigrant to Plymouth, Massachusetts ==
2 CONT '''Birth:''' It has been suggested and many times repeated that Thomaswas born about 1604 or 1612 in West Riding or Ecclesford, England. These are at the moment only unproven suggestions, as is the suggested pedigree that is attac
2 CONC hed to him. Is there a source for this information? B-404|B-404 09:27, 14 May 2014 (EDT)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriage:''' His wife's name was Anne. We know this from his will.
2 CONT '''Death:''' Thomas died between Oct 25th 1681 (date of his will) and Oct 1682 (will presented at the court.)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Last Will & Testament'''''<br/>
2 CONT "I Thomas Lettice of Plymouth... bequeath unto my three Daughters viz Anne the wife of Samuell Jenney[,] Elizabeth Cooke widdow; and Dorethythe wife of Edward Gray five shillings apeece" The remainder of the real and personal esta
2 CONC te, after the payment of debts and legacies, was bequeathed to wife Anne, who was made executrix.The will was dated 1678 but the month and day were omitted. It was signed by a mark, and witnessed by Jonathan Barnes and Joseph Howl
2 CONC and. "October the 25th 1681 Thomas Lettice above Named Did Declare this abovewritten Instrument To be his Last Will and Testament in prsence of Nathaniel Thomas;" "Capt. Thomas made oath to this abovewritten Will att the Court hel
2 CONC d att Plymouth in October 1682." and  "Leift: Joseph howland made oath tothis Will Att the said Court;" The will was recorded in Plymouth Colony Wills, Volume IV, Part II, page 11.<ref>“Notes By the Editor.”  ''The Mayflower Desce
2 CONC ndant: A Magazine of Pilgrim Genealogy and History.'' Vol. 14 p. 64. Boston, MA:Massachusetts Society of Mayflower Descendants,, 1912</ref>
2 CONT {{Image|file=B-404-8.jpg |size=150 }}'''Arrival:''' Thomas was in Plymouth in 1636. In March 1636 Frauncis Cooke complained against Thomas Lettice and three others, that they abused his cow so she lost her calf, subsequently her m
2 CONC ilk, and was in danger of being lost herself. The jury found for the plaintiff, Cooke, against John Browne the elder, the employer of Thomas and the others [v12:p5]<ref name="PCR">''Records of the Colony of New Plymouth, in NewEng
2 CONC land'' Vols. 1-8, edited by N.B. Shurtleff; v. 9-12, by David Pulsifer. Various dates of publication. [information in [ ] is volume and page #] Available online at [http://archive.org/search.php?query=Plymouth%20colony%20records%2
2 CONC 0shurtleff%20AND%20mediatype%3Atexts Archive.org]</ref>.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life in Plymouth''' Thomas owned land by 2 Dec 1639 when he was assigned a garden place [v1:p136]. He was granted 6 acres of upland 5 May 1640 [V1:p152] and purchased a house, garden and seven acres from Thomas Cushman in March
2 CONC  1641 [v12:p77]<ref name="PCR"/>. Thomas Lettis, was on a list of those able to bear armes, men age 21 to 60, in 1643.<ref>"List of those Able to Bear Arms in the Colony of New Plymouth in 1643" NEHGR 4:255.</ref> Thomas was admit
2 CONC ted and sworn a freeman 6 June 1654 [V3:p48]<ref name="PCR"/>.  He performed other civic duties: Served on juries in 1654 [v7:p72] and1660 [v3:p196]; was surveyor of highways in 1662 [v4:p15] and 1666 [v4:p123]; witnessed inventor
2 CONC ies and served on committees.<ref name="PCR"/>. He was a carpenter in 1666 when he signed (x'd) a bond with his daughter Elizabeth<ref>“Notes by the Editor.” The Mayflower Descendant. vol 15 p 63</ref>.
2 CONT
2 CONT {{Image|file=Royce-35-1.jpg |align=l |size=96 }}In 1669 the court record indicates he kept an ordinary. "July 6th 1669...none shall sell wine liquors Cyder or beer by retaile in the Collonie except they have a Lycence: and to pay 
2 CONC for theire lycence according to the Capasitie of the place where they live;"
2 CONT
2 CONT The list following includes Thomas Lettice at £02.00.00 "Moreover it is enacted by the Court that all such Lycenced ordinariesshall not suffer prophane singing daunceing or revelling in theire houses on the penaltie of ten shillin
2 CONC gs for every default and that all ordinary keepers be ordeded to keep good beer in theire houses to sell by retaile and that some one in every towne be appointed to see that the beer they sell be suitable to the prise they sell it
2 CONC  for."[v11:p222]<ref name="PCR"/>.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''CHILDREN''' [v8:p239]<ref name="PCR"/>.* Elizabeth Lettice married William Shirtley; Plymouth, 18 October 1655.
2 CONT * Dorothy Lettice married Edward Gray, Plymouth, 12 December 1665.
2 CONT * Thomas Lettice, son of Thomas, Plymouth died 3 November 1650.* Anne Lettice married Samuel Jenney. She is not in the town record but is named in Thomas' will. 
2 CONT
