1 NOTE Source 2
2 CONT Title: v15t3582.ftw
1 NOTE John Jenney and Mary Carey were born in England and were married there. As there was considerable religious persecution in the early 1600's in England, where the official religion was against any from of the Protestant religions, 
2 CONC the Puritans were given permission to settle in the New England colonies in America where the first of them landed from the Mayflower in 1621.
2 CONT John Jenney was said to be a man of some means and being a Puritan, he and Mary embarked from England on the ship 'Little James' probably in the spring of 1623. The 'Little James' was a small sailing ship of the type in use at tha
2 CONC t time. Due to the smallness of the ship, and the number of passengers on board, there were very crowded conditions.
2 CONT The North Atlantic is usually very rough in the spring of the year, so the crowding of the passengers was intensified by their being shut up between decks during the rough, stormy weather encountered, and the crossing was, no doub
2 CONC t, a very miserable one. They were in somewhat poor spirits and very thankful when the made landfall and disembarked at the Plymouth Colony later in the summer of 1623.
2 CONT It is said of John Jenney that he was a very stern and religious man. He was very active in the affairs of the colony and contributed much toward the welfare and the building of the early New England settlements.
