1 NOTE Sarah Carey was christened in 1590 in Suffolk, England. John Jenney, her future husband, was born in 1585 in Norwich, Norfolk, England. Both of them had moved to Leiden in Holland as members of the Separatist Pilgrims by 1614. The
2 CONC y were married there on November 1, 1614.
2 CONT
2 CONT John and Sarah were devout Christians and members of the Leiden congregation. They and their 2 living children traveled to Plymouth Colony on the Little James in 1623. Young Sarah was born on board the Little James, enroute to the
2 CONC  New World in July 1623.
2 CONT
2 CONT John was a brewer and miller by trade. When he arrived in Plimouth, a small mill had already been set up just north of the plantation. The mill processed raw corn and ground it into a corn meal, one of the main sources of food for
2 CONC  the colonists.
2 CONT
2 CONT As related by Nathaniel Morton in New England’s Memorial, published in 1669:
2 CONT
2 CONT "One of the principal passengers that came in her was Mr. John Jenny, who was a godly, though otherwise a plain man, yet singular for publicness of spirit, setting himself to seek and promote the common good of the plantation of N
2 CONC ew Plymouth; who spent not only his part of his ship (being part owner thereof) in the general concernment of the plantation, but also afterwards was always a leading man in promoting the general interest of this colony. He lived 
2 CONC many years in new England, and fell asleep in the Lord, anno 1644."
2 CONT
2 CONT John's first endeavor at Plymouth was not the mill, but the establishment of a malting floor and brew house. Some speculate that arrangements had been made for John to set up a New England brewery to provide the southern colonies 
2 CONC with beer. It has been speculated that Plymouth's survival was at least partially because they drank beer, instead of relying on local water supplies. It was common in England for people to drink beer, even children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beer was produced by soaking malt grains in water and spreading them onto a cool stone floor. The grains would then sprout or "malt." They were dried in a kiln, ground to a fine texture, then brewed to make beer. The cool New Engl
2 CONC and climate was ideal for this process and allowed John Jenney to produce beer for ten months out of the year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jenney was involved in refinancing the Plantation in 1626 and served several terms as an Assistant to the Governor as well as in other positions of responsibility within the Colony.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1636, the original corn mill owners had moved or died, and it was decided that John would be given the mill and the corn grinding business. Jenney's holdings then included a mill, a counting house, a corn exchange, a malting fl
2 CONC oor, a bake house, and a brewery.
2 CONT
2 CONT The counting house loaned seed to planters in the spring. When the crop came in, the seed was paid back—with interest. The corn exchange was an establishment that bought and sold grains. Excess crops could be deposited, traded, an
2 CONC d drawn upon much like our banking system today.
2 CONT
2 CONT John operated the mill until his death in 1644. After his death, Sarah operated the corn mill. Her son Samuel had become a talented ship's joiner—a boat builder and carpenter—and moved to Portsmouth Rhode Island in 1643. Sarah Jen
2 CONC ney died on April 4, 1656 in Plymouth. Samuel then returned with his family to run the family business.
