1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John Hardy was born about 1591 in England, and died 1651 in Salem, MA; his parents are unknown.  He married Elizabeth ? on Bef. 1623 in England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===family===
2 CONT
2 CONT More About John Hardy and Elizabeth ?: Marriage: Bef. 1623, England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children born in England: 
2 CONT * 2. Elizabeth (2) Hardy, b. Abt. 1623, d. Aft. 1667, Marblehead, MA. See Elizabeth Hardy wife of Roger Haskell note [RMS] 
2 CONT * 3. ii. Joseph(2), b. ______; m. Martha, whose surname is unknown; d. late in 1688. 
2 CONT
2 CONT === origins===
2 CONT
2 CONT John Hardy of Salem has sometimes been confused with another https://www.geni.com/people/John-Hardy-of-Isle-of-Wight/6000000003524042563 who died at Isle of Wright, Virginia, son of https://www.geni.com/people/Richard-Hardy/600000
2 CONC 0037317302407?through=6000000011058492277 and  https://www.geni.com/people/Alice-Hardy/6000000037317190580?through=6000000011058492277 [SIC - don't think that ancestry is proven for that John Hardy]. His wife was not [https://www.
2 CONC geni.com/people/Olive-Hardy/6000000003523926139?through=6000000003524042563 Olive] perhaps Council who was the wife of John Hardy of Isle of Wight.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Anderson's Great Migration Project,  '''John Hardy's origin is unknown'''. Furthermore, "no connections are seen between the family of Thomas Hardy of Haverhill and that of John Hardy of Salem."
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Charles Anderson estimates, based on the estimated date of marriage, that John Hardy was born in about 1591. He is shown on the list of passengers from Weymouth, Dorsetshire, sailing to Massachusetts aboard the "Recovery" i
2 CONC n 1634. He married Elizabeth (Unknown). He had three children, and assuming that all were by Elizabeth, he would have been married by 1616.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===biography===
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for John Hardy: HARDY and HARDIE; Past and Present by H. Claude Hardy & Rev. Edwin N. Hardy 1935 pages 991 - 994 CHAPTER XVI 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Hardy Family of Salem, Mass. 1. John(1) Hardy, born in England about 1600; died during the latter part of the year 1651 or very early in 1652; married in England to Elizabeth, whose last name is unknown. She died in 1654. Whe
2 CONC n John Hardy was made a freeman, 3 Sept. 1634, it is recorded that "he was a middle-aged man."(See History of Salem, Vol. I, page 286, by Sidney Perley.) Mention has already been made of the assumed fact that he was a brother of T
2 CONC homas(1) of Salem, Boston, Ipswich, Rowley and Bradford, Mass.(See Thomas Hardy Family of Bradford, Mass., page 1, by D. W. Hoyt.) In certain branches of the Hardy Family there is a tradition which has been passed from one generat
2 CONC ion to another that these brothers come over from England to this country at or about the same time. If this tradition is true, as has already been pointed out, then it is logical to assume that John(1) Hardy of Salem, Mass., Thom
2 CONC as(1) Hardy of Ipswich, Mass., and Richard(1) Hardy of Concord, Mass., and of Stamford, Conn. were brothers. That they were closely related thaere can be no doubt, and the records show that they were in close association during th
2 CONC ose early colonial years. That the above mentioned were brothers has been assumed by practially all historians and genealogists, such as David W. Hoyt, Charles Henry Pope, Joseph B. Felt, Savage and many others. See Thomas Hardy F
2 CONC amily of Bradford, by David W. Hoyt; Pioneers of Massachusetts, by Charles Henry Pope; History of Ipswich, Essex and Hamilton, by Joesph Felt; Genealogical Dictionary of New England, by Savage.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT It is evident that John(1) Hardy was married before he left England and that his children were born there. There is somewhat of a doubtful reference (See Savage's Genealogical Dictionary of New England, page 335.) that he returned
2 CONC  to England about 1643 or 1644 for his son Joesph, and that on his return secured a grant of land for him. It is evident that John(1) Hardy came to America ahead of his brothers Thomas(1) and Richard(1). Banks in his "Planters of 
2 CONC the Commonwealth", (see Bank's Planters of the Commonwealth, page 207.) mentions him as a possible passenger on the ship Lyon, which arrived in Massachusetts in 1630 prior to the arrival of Governor Winthrop's fleet of eleven ship
2 CONC s of the same year. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John(1) Hardy, it may be said without hesitation, was one of the most prominent citizens of Salem, Mass., up to the time of his death in 1654. He served the town officially in many ways, (Ibid page 207.) was selectman in 1647, was
2 CONC  Juryman again and again, was entrusted frequently to execute important commissions, and had valuable grants of land. He and his wife were members of the church and he was one of the signers of a remarkable church covenant prepare
2 CONC d by the Rev. John Fiske, who was pastor there 16637s-40. John Hardy was a man of wealth, the owner of much real estate, giving to his daughter and son-in-law a valuable tract of land at their marriage. He was well known because o
2 CONC f his shipping interests, and was mentioned as a "fisherman"; reference is made to the shipwreck of one of his vessels, but Perley, in his History of Salem, has this to say, "Among the vessels owned in Salem about 1650 the fleet o
2 CONC f John Hardy was perhaps the largest", and then a number of these vessels were named. It is clearly demonstrated that he laid the foundation for the family's remarkable achievements in later generations as mariners. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He made a will dated 30 Jan. 1651-2, and his signature, which "John Hardy" is here presented, is the only one known. His will was proved 30 June 1652, and in it he definitely mentions the following persons; 
2 CONT
2 CONT >His wife Elizabeth, his son Joesph, his daughter Elizabeth and her husband Roger Haskell and their four children, John, William, Mark and Elizabeth. His wife's will mentions the same persons. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Special attention is called to these facts because there has been some question about the number and names of the children of John Hardy. In his will only two are mentioned, and it may be safely assumed that if there were other ch
2 CONC lidren they would surely have been named in the will. Futher, Perley, the Salem historian, and genealogists of branches of the John Hardy family are all agreed, so far as this research has extended, in affirming that John Hardy ha
2 CONC d but two children, Elizabeth and Joesph. However, it is quite possible and somewhat probable that there may have been a son John who died before his father's death. References to another son who survived the father when examined 
2 CONC indicate some error in recording. At least this may be said, that not once do we find John Hardy mentioned as senior. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hardy name is perpetuated in Salem, Mass., in many ways. There is Hardy Street, the Hardy Rock, Hardy Wharf, and in one or another way many of the historical shrines of Salem are closely associated with the Hardy name. This is
2 CONC  notably the case with the Hardy House, the birthplace of Nathaniel Hawthorne, and Ropes Memorial, the Pickering House, the Witch House, etc. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===sources cited===
