2 CONC , about ten miles northeast of Boston. (They are believed to have arrived on the ship 'The Blessing of the Bay'.) A section of land was assigned to him, 3 July 1637, in Duxbury, 30 miles south of Boston; but in the same year there
2 CONC  appeared on the map, 64 miles south of Boston, the township of Sandwich, in the colony of Plymouth, to which he moved the following year, making his permanent abode in that section of the township which has come to be known as Sa
2 CONC gamore... He was one of the original eleven male members of the first Congregational Church in Sandwich and was known among his contemporaries as "Goodman Burgess." Abridged Compendium by Frederick Virkus, pg 970: BURGESS, Thomas 
2 CONC (1603-85), from Eng. to Salem, Mass., ca. 1630; settled at Sandwich, 1637; was an original mem. of the church there, and dep. Gen. Ct., 1646, et seq.; m Dorothy -----. More About THOMAS BURGESS: Anc. of President: Hayes & FDR Buri
2 CONC al: February 23, 1684/85, Old Burying Ground, Grove ST, Sandwich, MA Emigration: ca. 1630, arrived at Salem, Massachusetts Issue: 5 children Misc: 1643, List of Men able to bear arms, Sandwich Source 1: Burg., by K.W.Hiam, 1997, N
2 CONC EHGS pg1-3 Source 2: Burg. By Eben. Burgess, 1865 pgs 9-12 Source 3: Rchrdson&Elsworth by R.E.Richardson Source 4: FTM CD113, Virkus, pg 970 Source 5: 1961, Ezra Perry, NEHGR 115:86 Source 6: Anc.of American Pres. by Roberts, pg27
2 CONC 5 Source 7: April 27, 2003, rootsweb.com-Burgess-Bill Burgess Will: April 4, 1684, signed Notes for DOROTHY WAYNES: Burgess Gen., by K. W. Hiam,1997, NEHGS pg 2: I am not certain that 'Waynes' was actually Dorothy Burgess's maiden
2 CONC  name.A submission by Benjamin F. Wilbour of Little Compton, Rhode Island, to 'Notes' in the NEHGR (118 [1964]:323) reads thus:'The following record from 'Paver's Marriage Licences (in Yorkshire Archaeological Journal, Vol. 20 p. 
2 CONC 74, 1909) may pertain to him and suggest the place of his birth 'Thomas Burgess and Dorothy Waynes [married at] Tanfield [co. York, England] in 1628 .'(See also The American Genealogist 61[1985]:69.) More About DOROTHY WAYNES: Anc
2 CONC . of President: R.B.Hayes,FDR Burial: March 1686/87, Old Burying Ground, Grove ST, Sandwich, MA Misc: also called Dorothy Waynes/Phippen Source 1: Burg., by K.W.Hiam, 1997, NEHGS pg 1-3 Source 2: Burg, By E. Burgess, 1865, pg 9-12
2 CONC  Source 3: Anc.of American Pres. by Roberts, pg275 Source 4: April 27, 2003, rootsweb.com-Burgess-Bill Burgess
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of THOMAS BURGESS and DOROTHY WAYNES are: 2.	i.	 	ELIZABETH2 BURGESS, b. ca. 1629, prob near Truro, Cornwall, England; d. September 26, 1717, Sandwich, Massachusetts. 3.	ii.	 	JOHN BURGESS, b. ca. 1631, prob. Sandwich, Ma
2 CONC ssachusetts; d. 1701, Yarmouth, Massachusetts. 4.	iii.	 	JACOB BURGESS, b. ca. 1633; d. March 17, 1718/19, Sandwich, Massachusetts.
2 CONT
2 CONT _____________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Disputed Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT A christening of 16 Aug 1601[1] in Truro, Cornwall, England has been assigned to Thomas Burgess, the emigrant, but this infant seems to have died the following month.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Supposedly a will of Thomas Burgess, merchant of Cornwall, dated 20 Sep 1619, probated 12 Dec 1622 (PCC 127 Swann = Waters, Gleanings 992) mentions a son Thomas.[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT But if the emigrant was he who married Dorothy Wayne way across England in York, it's not likely these were the same men.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bottom line: The origins of Thomas Burgess remain unknown. Distinguishing him from another Thomas Burgess
2 CONT
2 CONT An examination of early New England colonists at sea identifies one Thomas Burgess who may have been a contemporary of Thomas of Sandwich:
2 CONT
2 CONT     "Borges R[iver] shows up for Thomas Burges (d. 1683) of Charlestown and later Concord. About 1645, Burges had been back to Northamptonshire as attorney in land matters for Thomas James (d. 1666) of Salem, whose own estate was 
2 CONC finally settled in Salem although he had died in Albermarle, Carolina. Thomas Burges made a will on 9 August 1662 'being Now Bound A voiage to Sae," and five days later the exploratory expedition sailed southward. Just before the 
2 CONC migratory expedition, on 20 January 1662/3, Buges sold 20 acres in Woburn, a git from Mr. George Bunker."[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Disputed Wives
2 CONT
2 CONT That his wife's name was Dorothy is supported by a 1654 land deed:
2 CONT
2 CONT     This Bargane and sale acknowlidged by Thomas Burgis senir and Dorathy his wife this 5t of Aprill 1654 before mee Myles Standish The Marke of Tho T Burgis.[4] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Winifred Lovering Holman's "Burgess Lineage, 1957-1958," a typescript in the New England Historic-Genealogical Society library, gives Dorothy Phippen as being the wife of this Thomas Burgess.
2 CONT
2 CONT It has been suggested that he was the Thomas Burgess who married a Dorothy Wayne in Tanfield, York, England, in 1628.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT     A marriage license was found for Thomas Burgess and Dorothy Waynes for 1628 at Tanfield, England, parish of Chester-Le-Street, Durham County. The ancestry of Thomas Burgess is not certain. Research indicates several possible r
2 CONC oots. On January 12, 1995, Paul F. Burgess, author of "The Burgess History Tree", wrote a letter to "The Burgess Bulletin" and stated that he had hired a researcher in England. The researcher indicated several possible connections
2 CONC  but did state that he was not the Thomas born in 1601 in Truro, which is the one with the Pye/Phippen connections. Other possible connections include: Thomas Burgess baptized 2 Oct 1603, son of Thomas Burgess and Elizabeth Seddon
2 CONC  of Lancashire; Another Thomas Burgess was baptized 4 Nov 1603 and his wife, Dorothy Goodman, baptized in 1613 at Coffinswell, Devonshire, England. This leads to speculation since Thomas was known as "The Goodman Burgess" in early
2 CONC  New England records. 
2 CONT
2 CONT An additional name, Goodman, has also been suggested, evidence unknown. Note that "goodman" was a title frequently used in colonial times. Disputed Children
2 CONT
2 CONT     Ruth (who supposedly married Richard Taylor, of the Rock, of Yarmouth): "Various records exist about these family members, yet none refer to an additional daughter named Ruth nor a son-in-law Richard Taylor. No other Burgess o
2 CONC f appropriate age to be father of a Ruth is found in the records. Further working against [the theory that Ruth (______) Taylor was daughter of Thomas Burgess] is that none of [her] children... had Burgess family given names."[6]
2 CONT     Deborah (who supposedly married Nathan Fish), but subsequent research has associated her as likely daughter of this Thomas' son Jacob by the latter's wife Mary Nye.[7] 
2 CONT
2 CONT The will of Thomas Burgess named four sons -- Thomas, Jacob, John and Joseph -- and one son-in-law, Ezra Perry (who is also named in the above land deed).[8],[9] Ezra Perry married Elizabeth Burgess 12 Feb 1651/2.[10] Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Burgess and his family immigrated to Salem, MA, about 1630 and settled in Lynn.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT     That he is not included in Robert C. Anderson's Great Migration series (which covers immigrants from 1620-1635) suggests he did not emigrate this early (1630). 
2 CONT
2 CONT On July 3, 1637 a section of land was assigned to him in Duxbury. In 1638 he forfeited his land in Duxbury and moved to Sandwich in the section called Sagamore. In 1640 he served as Grand Juryman. In 1642, 1645, 1648, 1654, 1660, 
2 CONC 1662, and 1668 he served as Deputy to Plymouth General Court. In August 1643 he was on the list of men "able to bear arms". In 1645, 1662, 1663, and 1678 he served as Surveyor of Highways. In 1667 and 1672 he served as Selectman. 
2 CONC On August 18, 1645 Thomas and 4 other men from Sandwich began 13 days of service in hostilities with the Narrangansett Indians. On March 3, 1654 Thomas received a grant of land in Manomot as reward for his service.[citation needed
2 CONC ]
2 CONT
2 CONT He is mentioned in many of the early records of Sandwich.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Burgess died 13 February 1685 and is buried at Old Town Cemetery in Sandwich, Massachusetts.[11] Property
2 CONT
2 CONT He was granted a tract of land purchased for him by Miles Standish of Indian Josiah Dwelling.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT     THOMAS BURGE, SR., TO EZRA PERRY 1663 Prence Govr: The 10th of July 1663 Memorand: That Thomas Burge senr of the Towne of Sandwich in in the Jurisdiction of Plymouth in New England plantor Doth acknowlidg that for and in Consi
2 CONC deration of a valluable sume; to him already payed by his son in law Named Esra Perrey of the Towne aforsaid in the Jurisdiction aforsaid plantor; hee hath bargained and sold enfeofed and Confeilmed and by these prsents Doth barga
2 CONC ine allianate sell enfeof and Confeirme unto the said Esra Perrey the one halfe of a Certaine tract of land lying and being att a place Called Mannomett in the Jurisdiction aforsaid; which said Tract of land was purchased by Capta
2 CONC ine Standish by the appointment of the Court of Josias of Nausett an Indian Sachem; in the behalfe of the said Thomas Burge as appeers by a Deed bearing Date the third Day of march Anno Dom 1652 and is That Thomas Burgis of Sandwi
2 CONC dge hath absolutly barganed and sold to ffrancis Allen of Sandwidge to him and heires for ever a pcell or tract of land being within a ffence which said upland I the said Thomas Burgis senr bought of Thomas Boardman. as also a pce
2 CONC ll of meadow bought of the said Thomas Boardman as aforsaid this meadow bounded with 2 Creekes lying before or Joyning to the aforsaid upland to the said ffrancis Allen to have and to hold to him and his heires forever;
2 CONT
2 CONT         This Bargane and sale acknowlidged by Thomas Burgis senir and Dorathy his wife this 5t of Aprill 1654 before mee Myles Standish The Marke of Tho T Burgis.[13] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT     Thomas Burgess Jr., the eldest, of Rhode Island
2 CONT     John
2 CONT     Jacob
2 CONT     Joseph[14]
2 CONT     Daughter who married Ezra Perry 
2 CONT
2 CONT NO DAUGHTER RUTH. Last Will & Testament
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of Thomas Burgess "of Sandwich", dated 4 Apr 1684.[8] In his Will, he names:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Wife 
2 CONT     Sons: 
2 CONT
2 CONT     Thomas the eldest, of Rhode Island
2 CONT     John
2 CONT     Jacob
2 CONT     Joseph
2 CONT     "my son" Ezra Perry 
2 CONT
2 CONT     Grandsons: 
2 CONT
2 CONT     Thomas, son of Jacob
2 CONT     Thomas, son of John 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Executors were Ezra Perry and Jacob Burgess, and the witnesses were Thomas Tupper and Martha Tupper. Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT     ↑ LDS International Genealogical Index
2 CONT     ↑ "Ancestry of President Ruther B. Hayes," in The American Genealogist, 56(1980):234
2 CONT     ↑ "New Englanders at Sea," in NEHGR, 124(1970):93
2 CONT     ↑ "Plymouth Colony Deeds," in The Mayflower Descendant, 8(1906):73, citing page 122
2 CONT     ↑ "Paver's Marriage Licenses," in Yorkshire Archaeological Journal, 20 (1909):74
2 CONT     ↑ Jillaine S. Smith, "Two Richard Taylor Families of Yarmouth," in Register, 165(2011):191
2 CONT     ↑ "The Wife of Nathan Fish of Falmouth," in NEHGR 138(1984):132
2 CONT     ↑ 8.0 8.1 8.2 Ebenezer Burgess. Burgess genealogy. Boston: Press of T. R. Marvin & son, 1865. Open Library Page 9-12
2 CONT     ↑ Lydia B. (Phinney) Brownson and Maclean W. McLean, "Ezra Perry of Sandwich, Mass. (c. 1625-1689)," Register, 115(1961):86-99
2 CONT     ↑ Kardell and Lovell, Vital Records of Sandwich, 1:12
2 CONT     ↑ Thomas Burgess's memorial page at findagrave.com, with photos of his gravestone.
2 CONT     ↑ "Plymouth Colony Deeds," in Mayflower Descendant, 2(1900):29, citing 37 (1652)
2 CONT     ↑ "Plymouth Colony Deeds," in The Mayflower Descendant, 8(1906):73, citing page 122
2 CONT     ↑ "Plymouth Colony Deeds," in The Mayflower Descendant, 18(1916):89, citing p. 125: a 1663 land deed made by Thomas Burgess Sr. to his son Joseph Burge.
2 CONT
2 CONT See also:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Immigrant England Pilgrims (accessed 17 Nov 2013)
2 CONT     Vital Records of Bellingham Massachusetts to the Year 1850, Boston: New England Historic Genealogical Society, 1904. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Burgess, Thomas (~1603 - 1685) b. ABT 1603 in England d. 13 FEB 1685 in Sandwich, Barnstable County, Massachusetts
2 CONT
2 CONT     Possibly another child Ruth who married Richard Taylor of the Rock (He built his house near a large rock near the boundary between Hockanom and Nobscussetin the northeastern part of town, and called that to distinguish him fro
2 CONC m the Richard Taylor who was a tailor) This possible child of Thomas and Dorothy is not mentioned in his will or codicil.
2 CONT
2 CONT     It is generally believed that Thomas migrated from England not far from 1630, and lodged for a time at Lynn, MA. A section of land was ssigned to him, in that part of Plymouth called Duxbury, July 3, 1637. This section of land
2 CONC , being forfeited by his removal to Sandwich, MA in the same year, was assigned to Nicholas Bobbins, November 5, 1638, who made to the former occupant some remuneration for fences and culture. In the settlement of Sandwich, Thomas
2 CONC  Burgess, became associated with Edmund Freeman, Henry Feake, Richard Chadwell, William Almy, Thomas Tupper, William Wood, Edward Dillingham, John Carman, George Knot and Thomas Dexter. Savage says of him, "He was a chief man amon
2 CONC g them." In the church, instituted in 1638, under the paastoral care of William Leverich, he was an original member. In process of time he became a large landholder, and with advancing age hewas called Goodman Burgess. He served t
2 CONC he town in every office, humble or honorable, from road-surveyor to deputy to the Court at Plymouth, for several successive years.
2 CONT
2 CONT The ancestry of Thomas Burgess is not certain. Research indicates several possible roots. On January 12, 1995, Paul F. Burgess, author of 'The Burgess History Tree', wrote a letter to 'The Burgess Bulletin' and stated that he had 
2 CONC hired a researcher in England. The researcher indicated several possible connections but did state that he was not the Thomas born in 1601 in Truro, which is the one with the Pye/Phippen connections. Other possible connections inc
2 CONC lude: Thomas Burgess baptized 2 Oct 1603, son of Thomas Burgess and Elizabeth Seddon of Lancashire; Another Thomas Burgess was baptized 4 Nov 1603 and his wife, Dorothy Goodman, baptized in 1613 at Coffinswell, Devonshire, England
2 CONC . This leads to speculation since Thomas was known as 'The Goodman Burgess' in early New England records. 
2 CONT
2 CONT spouse: ?, Dorothy (~1605 - 1687) - m. 1628 in Chester-le-Stree, Tanfield, England
