1 NAME John /Lake/ of Great Fanton
2 GIVN John
2 NSFX of Great Fanton
2 _MARNM Lake
2 NICK John Richard Lake
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 26 SEP 1590
2 PLAC Great Fanton
2 ADDR
3 CITY North Benfleet
3 STAE England
1 BURI
2 DATE 23 AUG 1657
2 ADDR
3 CITY St Dunstan and All Saints, Stepney,  Stepney, London
3 STAE England
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 DEAT
2 DATE 23 AUG 1657
2 ADDR
3 CITY North Benfleet
3 STAE England
1 OCCU DIRECT DESCENDANT OF LOUIS IV, KING OF FRANCE, Isabelle (de) VERMANDOIS, (FROM CHARLEMAGNE), Harold II (King) of NORWAY, ALSO FROM ALFRED THE GREAT, KING ENG, AND HENRY, EMPEROR OF GERMANY., Alexander I King of SCOTS and Henry I B
2 CONC EAUCLERC King of England
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John Lake married in England to Margaret Read, daughter of Edmund Read. John died in England and his widow Margaret with her sister Elizabeth (Read) Winthrup, wife of Gov. John Winthroup, and her two daughters came
2 CONC  to New England. Margaret lived in Ipswich, MA and died there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: A Gen. of the Lake Family, by Adams 1915, and NEHGS July 1930
2 CONT
2 CONT ==============================
2 CONT
2 CONT Great Fanton Hall, North Benfleet, Essex, England
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. John Lake I was born in 1511 in Irby, Lincolnshire, England. He married Elizabeth Lone. She was born in 1515 in North Benfleet, Essex Co., England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. John Lake II was born circa 1535. He married first Agnes Tharrot circa 1560. She was born circa 1540 and died in 1562. He married second Margaret Osgodby circa 1570. He died leaving a will dated April 26, 1575 and was buried in
2 CONC  the parish of Irby-upon-Humher, Lincolnshire, England. John was "a little tailor in a little village." 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Richard Lake I was born in 1570 and baptized on July 16, 1570 in Tetney, Lincolnshire, England and died on December 17, 1626 in Tetney, Lincolnshire, England. He married first Anne Wardell before 1600. She was a daughter of Edw
2 CONC ard Wardell III of Keeby. He was a Taylor. The will of "Richard Lakes of Tetney, Taylor" was dated September 2, 1626 and mentions his seven children by name. Baptismal records and the wills of the various family members establish 
2 CONC the Lake line in England. Richard is also listed as being from Irby, Lincolnshire, England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Sir Edward Lake was born in 1600 or 1601 and was baptized on February 22, 1600 or 1601 in Tetney, Lincolnshire, England and died on July 18, 1674 and was buried in Lincoln Cathedral. He married Anne Bibye a daughter of Simon Bi
2 CONC bye (or Biby or Bybye). He received the degree of B. A. at Oxford in 1627. He was an eminent lawyer, becoming advocate general for Ireland. On the outbreak of the Civil Wars, he both fought and wrote on the King’s side. At the bat
2 CONC tle of Edgehill he received sixteen wounds, and having lost the use of his left hand by a shot, he placed his horses bridle between his teeth and fought with his sword in his right hand. He was promised as his reward a barony and 
2 CONC an augmentation to his arms by King Charles I, in 1643. But since the King was beheaded and the period of the Commonwealth intervened, nothing was done until 1661. King Charles II then made him Chancellor of the Diocese of Lincoln
2 CONC , and the augmented coat of arms promised by King Charles I was in 1661 formally authorized by the College of Arms. From Burke’s Peerage and Baronetage (1905) comes the following: "Edward Lake, L.L.D. Chancellor of the diocese of 
2 CONC Lincoln, was a devoted adherent of Charles I, for whom he fought at the battle of Naseby and received no less than sixteen wounds. He was by Charles I, in consideration of his zeal and loyalty, given a coat of augmentation, and an
2 CONC  additional coat, and was named a baronet, with the privilege of nominating his successor to the title, but no patent was taken out at the time. Sir Edward, dying without issue in 1674, was succeeded by his grandnephew." The will 
2 CONC of Sir Edward Lake is dated April 8, 1665; he added codicils in 1671 and in 1674, they being rendered necessary by the death of the executors successively named. This will is an interesting document, since it gives us much informa
2 CONC tion in regard to the antecedents and connections of Sir Edward. He leaves a bequest to the Church at Normanton near Pontefract in Yorkshire, because it was the home of his paternal ancestors. He mentions his kinsman and servant C
2 CONC hristopher Lake, his brother John Lake, his nephew Francis Lake of Hatcliffe, the eldest son of his brother Luke Lake, and Stephen, eldest son of his brother Thomas Lake. He named his half-brother Thomas Lake, Jr. executor, and di
2 CONC rects that the male heirs forever may for their Christian name have the name Biby or Seaman, his wife’s mother’s surname, or Caly, an ancestor of the time of Edward Wardell III, in honor of his mother, a daughter of Edward Wardell
2 CONC . Thomas Lake, the brother in New England, died before Sir Edward, and in a codicil he names Thomas’ son Stephen executor in his place; then Stephen died, and Thomas, Stephen’s brother, is named. This son of his brother Thomas, Si
2 CONC r Bibye Lake, was his succeeding heir as shown below. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. A Son who died as an infant before 1666. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Lake I married second Anne Morraly about 1614. She was a daughter of Edward Morraly of Claxby-by-Normandy. The will of Anne Morraly-Lake was dated May 16, 1627 and names her five then living children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Richard Lake II was born in 1614 and died in 1646 or 1647. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Thomas Lake, Sr., Capt. was born in 1615 and was baptized on August 11, 1615 in Tetney, Co. Lincoln, England and died in Maine in August 1676. [According to the will of his brother, Edward, Thomas died before Edward and Edward 
2 CONC died July 18, 1674.] He first settled in New Haven, Conn., where he married Mary Goodyear. She was a daughter of Stephen Goodyear, a prominent merchant and Deputy Governor. (Wotton’s "The Baronetage of England", Vol. 3 states Thom
2 CONC as Lake "married Mary, daughter of Stephen Goodyer of London, goldfmith.") Several children of Thomas and Mary are mentioned in the Boston records, but only Stephen, Ann and Thomas grew up. Thomas was one of the leading men of his
2 CONC  time in Boston and an influential merchant. He became Captain of the Ancient and Honorable Artillery Company in Massachusetts in 1653. He was a ship owner in 1654 and that same year purchased half of Arrowsic Island in Kennebec, 
2 CONC Maine. For many years he had a trading post there with large transactions with the Indians. He was made a freeman and then selectman in 1671. He was killed by Indians leaving a large estate for his family. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Stephen Lake who died young before 1674. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Eight children who did not leave issue. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Thomas Lake, Jr. was born on February 9, 1656 in Boston, Mass. and died on May 22, 1711. He married Elizabeth Story a daughter of John Story of Derbyshire, England. Thomas returned to England to live at Bishop’s Norton, Lincoln
2 CONC shire. He was a barrister and a member of the Middle Temple. He lived at Bishop’s Norton, Lincolnshire, England. Sir Edward Lake named Thomas as executor of his will after Thomas’ father and older brother, Stephen, died. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Mary Lake 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Sir Bibye Lake. He was a sub-governor of the African Company, and in 1711 received from Queen Ann a confirmation of the title, along with the Coat-of-Arms, granted to Sir Edward Lake. It is through this son that the Baronet lin
2 CONC e has continued in England. The descendants of Sir Bibye have held honorable places in English affairs down to the present [at least 1954 or 1956 when this book was written]. Counting from Sir Bibey, the seventh Baronet was Sir Vi
2 CONC ncent Atwell. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Ann Lake was born on October 12, 1663 and died in 1737. She married first the Rev. John Cotton. He was born in 1658 and died in 1710. She married second Dr. Increase Mather, Rev. by whom she "left numerous descendants." It is o
2 CONC nly through this daughter Ann that American descendants may trace this Lake ancestry. The lineage of Ann (Lake) Cotton Mather has been traced for several generations in England after many years of research. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Ambrose Lake was born in 1617. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. John Lake was born in 1618 and baptized on November 19, 1618 in Tetney, Lincolnshire, England and died on August 6, 1677. He married Mary Coy. He was a taylor as was his father and grandfather before him. He was in Boston, Mass
2 CONC . by 1643 and made freeman in 1644. John and Mary left no issue. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Luke Lake was born in 1620. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Francis Lake of Hatcliffe. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Anne Lake was born in 1626. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Robert Lake was born in 1537 in North Benfleet, Essex Co., England and died in 1574. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Richard Lake was born in 1539 in Rayleigh, Essex Co., England and died on September 24, 1599 in North Benfleet, Essex Co., England and was buried in North Benfleet, Essex Co., England.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. John Lake, Sr. was born in 1565 in North Benfleet, Essex Co., England and died on December 10, 1612 in North Benfleet, Essex Co., England. He married Elizabeth Sandell in 1590 in North Benfleet, Essex Co., England. She was born
2 CONC  in 1570 in Nevedon, Essex Co., England and died on May 4, 1616 in North Benfleet, Essex Co., England. She was a daughter of John Sandell who was born in 1525 in Basildon, England and married Thomasine ???? who was born in 1525. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Anne Lake 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Margaret Lake 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. John Lake, Jr. was born on September 26, 1590 in Normantown, Yorkshire, England and died after 1657 in Ipswich, Essex Co., England. He married Margaret Reade about 1616 in England. She was born on July 11, 1598 in North Benflee
2 CONC t, Essex Co., England and died on September 24, 1672 in Ipswich, Essex Co., Mass. "The Ancestry of Bethia Harris" written in 1934 by Walter Goodwin Davis says "these Lakes are not of Visitation rank and no arms can be claimed for 
2 CONC them. The ancestry of Margaret Reade-Lake long baffled genealogists, but it is now established that she was in fact the wife of this John Lake of Great Fanton Hall. Mistress Margaret Lake came to America in 1635, with her sister E
2 CONC lizabeth (Reade) Winthrop, wife of Governor John Winthrop, Jr., and two of her daughters, one of whom, Hannah, married John Gallup; the other named Martha married Thomas Harris of Ipswich, Esex Co., Mass. She also had a son John, 
2 CONC who is said not to have come to this country but was actually disowned for abandoning his education. Her will is dated August 30, 1672, and was proved September 24, 1672. She mentions only her two daughters and their children. She
2 CONC  was the daughter of Edmund Reade, of Wickford, Essex, England, and widow of John Lake. John Lake claimed descent from the same family to which Sir Edward Lake and Captain Thomas Lake, of Boston, belonged. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. John Lake III was born on July 6, 1617 in Wickford, Essex Co., England and died before August 4, 1696 in Gravesend, Long Island, Kings Co., N.Y. He married Anne Spicer before March 14, 1649 in Gravesend, Long Island, Kings Co.,
2 CONC  N.Y. She was born in 1632 in Brooklyn, Kings Co., N.Y. and died after 1709 in Gravesend, Long Island, Kings Co, N.Y. She was a daughter of Thomas Spicer and Micah. He came over on Capt. Thomas Lake's Ship to Boston then took up w
2 CONC ith Lady Deborah Moody and Sir Thomas Spicer and Moved to Rhode Island then in 1643 to New Amsterdam, back to RI after Gov. Kieft  massacre then returned again in 1645 to rebuild. Freedom, of religion, of government, a new world, 
2 CONC a new beginning
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Daniel Lake died in 1643 @ 24. In his will dated December 1642 when he was living in Leighton Buzzard, Bedfordshire, England, he left 60 pounds to his brother John to pay John’s way to America as John was disowned by his family
2 CONC  for not continuing his education thus losing his inheritance. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Thomas Lake I was born in Wickford, Essex Co., England and was baptized on January 18, 1619 in Wickford, Essex Co., England. He married Sarah Peate in 1685. She was born on October 13, 1665 in Stratford, Fairfield Co., Conn. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Richard Lake was born in Wickford, Essex Co., England and baptized on May 21, 1620 in Wickford, Essex Co., England and died on May 22, 1620 in Wickford, Essex Co., England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Hannah Anna Lake was born on July 3, 1621 in North Benfleet, Essex Co., England and died on December 19, 1675 in Stonington, Conn. She married John Gallop in 1643 in Boston, Mass. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Elizabeth Lake was baptized on June 5, 1623 in North Benfleet, Essex Co., England and died on August 31, 1623 in North Benfleet, Essex Co., England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Martha Lake was born and baptized on July 20, 1624 in North Benfleet, Essex Co., England and died on April 5, 1700 in Ipswich, Essex Co., Mass. She married first Arthur Harris in 1640 in Bridgewater, Mass. She married second Th
2 CONC omas Harris on November 15, 1647 in Ipswich, Exxes Co., Mass. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Samuel Lake bap. Mar. 29, 1629, North Benfleet, Essex, died on March 19, 1630. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Elizabeth Lake was baptized on February 17, 1631. Died infant.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.pcez.com/~bigshoe/dt/Vsik/smit.html
2 CONT
2 CONT  John Lake was born 1 in "of Great Fanton Hall,North Benfleet, Essexshire, England. He was christened 2 on 26 Sep 1590 in North Benfleet,Essexshire,England. He died 3 on 18 Jan 1661/1662 in North Benfleet,Essexshire,England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents:  John Lake (c. 1565-1612) and Elizabeth Sandell (bet. 1565-1570-1616)
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 1: Margaret Reade (1598-1672) about 1615 in No. Benfleet,Essexshire,England.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT M 	i 	John Lake was christened on 6 Jul 1617 in Wickford,Essexshire,England. He died about 1658.
2 CONT
2 CONT M 	ii 	Thomas Lake was christened on 18 Jan 1618/1619 in Wickford,Essexshire,England.
2 CONT
2 CONT M 	iii 	Richard Lake was christened on 21 May 1620 in Wickford,Essexshire,England.
2 CONT
2 CONT F 	iv 	Hannah Anna Lake was christened on 3 Jul 1621. She died after 28 Feb 1680/1681.
2 CONT
2 CONT F 	v 	Elizabeth Lake (infant) was christened on 5 Jun 1623 in North Benfleet,County Essex,England. She died in Aug 1623 in North Benfleet,County Essex,England. She was buried on 31 Aug 1623 in North Benfleet,County Essex,England.
2 CONT
2 CONT F 	vi 	Martha Lake was christened on 20 Jul 1624. She died on 5 Apr 1700.
2 CONT
2 CONT M 	vii 	Samuel Lake was christened on 29 Mar 1629 in North Benfleet,County Essex,England. He died in Mar 1630 in North Benfleet,County Essex,England. He was buried on 19 Mar 1630 in North Benfleet,County Essex,England.
2 CONT
2 CONT F 	viii 	Elizabeth Lake was christened on 17 Feb 1630/1631 in No. Benfleet,Essexshire,England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Weblinks:
2 CONT http://www.lynngallup.org/genfam/pafg04.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT John was of Great Fanton Hall in North Benfleet and the third generation of Lakes to occupy the manor house having inherited it from his father John, who was born there in 1565. Sometime between 1631 and 1635 (1635 on the "Abigale
2 CONC ") John's wife, Margaret (Reade) Lake, left him and emigrated with her sisters and their families to New England because of fear of the plague after losing 2/3's of the children from the Lake, Reade and Cooke families, taking with
2 CONC  her, her two daughters Ann and Martha Lake. Margaret lived, for a time with the the family of her brother-in-law Governor John Winthrop, in New London, Conn. The Cooke's also came over. John Lake's date of death is determined fro
2 CONC m Margaret's correspondence with England over the years, wherein it is obvious from a letter in January 1662 that John was deceased.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Lake is claimed to be a descendent on his father's side in the thirtieth generation of Charlemagne, Frankish king and Roman emperor, by his wife Hildegarde, daughter of the Swabian duke Godfrey; in the thirty-first of Pepin t
2 CONC he Short, the first Carlovingian king of the Franks, and in the thirty-third of Pepin of Heristal, mayor of the palace under the last Merovingian kings, who died A.D. 714.
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations:
2 CONT
2 CONT     1(various contributing members), New England Historical and Genealogical Society Register (Published by the Society at 9 Ashburton Place, Boston), Vol. LXXXIV/Pages 304 - 317.
2 CONT     by Walter Goodwin Davis.
2 CONT
2 CONT     2(various contributing members), New England Historical and Genealogical Society Register, Vol. LXXXIV/Pages 304 - 317, Jul 1930.
2 CONT     by Walter Goodwin Davis.
2 CONT
2 CONT     3(various contributing members), New England Historical and Genealogical Society Register, 1930/LXXXIV/304-317, Jul 1930.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is still a controversy regarding which John Lake was born from Ann Wardell, if our John was descent of Ann, then the Lake Baronetcy can be debated, an mtDNA test would clear it up.  http://www.genealogy.com/users/f/a/i/Cathe
2 CONC rine-Thelma-Fairbairn/TREE/0001tree.html
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Category: North Benfleet, Essex
2 CONT ==Biography==John Lake of Great Fanton Hall, North Benfleet, county Essex, was christened on 26 September 1590 at North Benfleet, Essex, England.<ref>Davis, page 315.</ref> He was a son of John Lake of Great Fanton Hall (buried 10
2 CONC  December 1612).<ref>Davis, pages 313 and 315.</ref> 
2 CONT John Lake was married in England in about 1616 to Margaret Reade, daughter of Edmund Read.<ref>Davis, page 315.</ref> Children of John Lake and Margaret (Reade) Lake were:<ref>Davis, pages 316-317.</ref>
2 CONT # John, baptized on 6 July 1617 at Wickford, Essex
2 CONT # Thomas, baptized on 18 January 1618/9 at Wickford, Essex# Richard, baptized on 21 May 1620 and buried on 22 May 1620, at Wickford, Essex# Ann (later called Hannah), baptized on 3 July 1621 at North Benfleet, Essex; emigrated to 
2 CONC New England with her mother and married John Gallop# Elizabeth, baptized on 5 June 1623 and buried on 31 August 1623 at North Benfleet, Essex# Martha, baptized on 20 July 1624 at North Benfleet, Essex; emigratedto New England with
2 CONC  her mother and married Thomas Harris. # Samuel, baptized on 29 March 1629 and buried on 19 March 1629/30 at North Benfleet, Essex# Ellizabeth, baptized on 17 February 1630/1 at North Benfleet, Essex;no further record. Margaret (R
2 CONC eade) Lake left England without her husband some time between 1631 and 1635, emigrating to New England with her sisters, including Elizabeth (Read) Winthrop, wife of Gov. John Winthrop, and her two daughters, Ann (Hannah) and Mart
2 CONC ha. Margaret lived in Ipswich, Massachusetts, and died there.<ref>A Gen. of the Lake Family, by Adams 1915, and Davis, pages 315-316.</ref> ''The Great Migration Directory'' states Margaret Lake's date of arrival in New England as
2 CONC  1638.<ref>Anderson, Robert Charles. ''The Great Migration Directory,'' New England History Genealogical Society, 2015.</ref> John Lake was recorded as living in 1657, and died in England some time before 18 January 1661/2. <ref>D
2 CONC avis, page 316.</ref>
2 CONT
