2 TITL Mayflower Deeds and Probates, 1600-1850
1 NOTE John married Elizabeth Tilley in 1622.  Another baptism date of 17 May 1881 has been given.
1 NOTE  "John Howland came to America on the Mayflower when he was 28 years old.
2 CONT He signed the Mayflower Compact, lived to be more than 80 years old and was
2 CONT the last survivor of the Mayflower fathers. There were 101 pilgrims on the last
2 CONT voyage of which 50 died the first winter."
2 CONT __________________________________________________
2 CONT Source:`The Life Story of Hyrum Oscar Crandall"
2 CONT Compiled and published by Henry E. Miller 1994 page 264
2 CONT
2 CONT The Mayflower was a 90 foot sea "tramp" that usually carried any cargo they
2 CONT could find. The load just prior to the famous voyage was load of wine from
2 CONT Franca to England. They left Plymouth, England on September 20, 1620 and
2 CONT traveled for 68 days in the worst weather of the year in the North Atlantic.
2 CONT Their food during the two month trip was sea biscuits, salted meat, dried fish
2 CONT and washed down with beer. They stopped first at Provincetown,
2 CONT Massachusetts, then proceded to Plymouth, across Cape Cod Bay. They almost
2 CONT had a mutuny after landing so they prepared and signed the Mayflower compact,
2 CONT stating that everyone would have fair treatment from the government that would
2 CONT be set up.
2 CONT ______________________________________________
2 CONT John Howland b. 1599, Huntingdon, England. d. Feb 24, 1671 in Rocky Nook
2 CONT MA. John came over on the Mayflower as a servant to John Carver. There is
2 CONT an interesting story about how he fell overbroad.
2 CONT ______________________________________________
2 CONT Copied from
2 CONT http://members.aol.com/calebj/passenger.html
2 CONT BORN:   About 1599*, Fenstanton, Huntingdon, England, son of
2 CONT Henry and Margaret (---) Howland
2 CONT DIED:   23 or 24 February 1672/3, Rocky Nook, Kingston,
2 CONT MA.  (the Plymouth Colony Court Records say he died on 23
2 CONT February, the Plymouth Church Records say he died 24
2 CONT February).
2 CONT MARRIED:   Elizabeth Tilley, daughter of John and Joan
2 CONT (Hurst)(Rogers) Tilley of the Mayflower, about 1624.
2 CONT
2 CONT * The traditional date that has been ascribed to John Howland's
2 CONT birth is "about 1592", and this has never really been questioned.
2 CONT  However, a birth about 1599 is clearly a better estimate for the
2 CONT following reasons:
2 CONT
2 CONT      John Howland is called a "manservant" in William
2 CONT      Bradford's passenger list.  Servants were contracted out
2 CONT      until the age of 25.  Thus, Howland must have been under
2 CONT      25 in 1620, meaning he had to have been born after
2 CONT      1595.  Since Howland signed the Mayflower Compact, he
2 CONT      must have been born sometime before 1600 to have been
2 CONT      legally old enough to sign.
2 CONT      John Howland's wife was born in 1607, and it is most
2 CONT      unlikely that he, at the age of 32, married a 17 year old girl
2 CONT      as his first wife.  Most men married about age 25, and
2 CONT      since his marriage occurred about 1624, this would place
2 CONT      his likely birth at 1599.
2 CONT      John Howland's last child was born in 1649.  If the 1592
2 CONT      date were accepted, he would have fathered a child at the
2 CONT      age of 57, a most unlikely circumstance.
2 CONT      William Bradford writes in that John Howland was a "lusty
2 CONT      young man" in 1620.  It is unlikely that Bradford would call
2 CONT      a 28-year old a "young man".  The only other person
2 CONT      Bradford called a "young man" in 1620 was John Alden,
2 CONT      who was born in 1599.
2 CONT
2 CONT CHILDREN:
2 CONT  NAME         BIRTH                               DEATH                                             MARRIAGE
2 CONT  Desire      c1625, Plymouth         13 October 1683,  Barnstable, MA            John Gorham,  c1643
2 CONT  John         24 April 1627, Plymouth      after 1699                                               Mary Lee,
2 CONT 26  October 1651, Plymouth
2 CONT  Hope        30 August 1629, Plymouth   8 January 1683/4, Barnstable          John Chipman, c1647
2 CONT  Elizabeth    c1631,possibly in Maine    1691, Oyster Bay, NY                 (1). Ephraim Hicks,
2 CONT 13 September 1649, Plymouth
2 CONT (2). John Dickinson,
2 CONT 10 July 1651, Plymouth
2 CONT  Lydia       c1633, possibly in Maine       aft. 11 January 1710/11                 James Brown, c1654
2 CONT  Hannah    c1637, possibly in Maine       unknown                                        Jonathan Bosworth,
2 CONT probably on 6 July 1661
2 CONT  Joseph     c1640, Kingston, MA           January 1703/4, Plymouth              Elizabeth Southworth,
2 CONT 7 December 1664, Plymouth
2 CONT  Jabez        c1644, Kingston, MA           between 1708 and 1712                 Bethiah Thacher, c1668
2 CONT  Ruth        c1646,  Kingston, MA    between 1672 and 16 October 1679          Thomas Cushman,
2 CONT 17 November 1664, Plymouth
2 CONT  Isaac  15 November 1649, Kingston,     MA  9 March 1723/4,                      Elizabeth Vaughan,
2 CONT                                                                  Middleboro, MA                c1676, probably at Marshfield, MA
2 CONT  ANCESTRAL SUMMARY:
2 CONT
2 CONT The ancestry of John Howland is discussed in John Howland of
2 CONT the Mayflower through Desire Howland for Five
2 CONT Generations", Vol. 1, by Elizabeth Pearson White, available
2 CONT from the Mayflower Web Page bookstore.  John Howland is the
2 CONT son of Henry and Margaret Howland of Fenstanton, Huntingdon,
2 CONT England.  Henry died on 17 May 1635 in Fenstanton, and
2 CONT Margaret was buried on 31 July 1629. Besides son John, who
2 CONT came on the Mayflower, they also had Humphrey, Arthur,
2 CONT Henry, George, and Margaret. Henry came to Plymouth
2 CONT sometime before 1633, and Arthur came sometime before 1640.
2 CONT For information on Arthur Howland, see the National
2 CONT Genealogical Society Quarterly 71:84+, and for information on
2 CONT Henry Howland see NGSQ 75:105-116, 216-225.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Howland is an ancestor to President George Bush, and to
2 CONT First Lady Edith (Carrow) Roosevelt (Mrs. Theodore
2 CONT Roosevelt).  Presidents Richard Nixon and Gerald Ford are
2 CONT descendants of John Howland's brother Henry.  Winston
2 CONT Churchill is descended from John Howland's brother Arthur.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Howland's Will
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Last Will and Testament of mr John howland of Plymouth
2 CONT late Deceased, exhibited to the Court held att Plymouth the fift
2 CONT Day of March Anno Dom 1672 on the oathes of mr Samuell
2 CONT ffuller and mr William Crow as followeth
2 CONT
2 CONT Know all men to whom these prsents shall Come That I John
2 CONT howland senir of the Towne of New Plymouth in the Collonie of
2 CONT New Plymouth in New England in America, this twenty ninth Day
2 CONT of May one thousand six hundred seaventy and two being of
2 CONT whole mind, and in Good and prfect memory and Remembrance
2 CONT praised be God; being now Grown aged; haveing many
2 CONT Infeirmities of body upon mee; and not Knowing how soon God
2 CONT will call mee out of this world, Doe make and ordaine these
2 CONT prsents to be my Testament Containing herein my last Will in
2 CONT manor and forme following;
2 CONT
2 CONT Imp I Will and bequeath my body to the Dust and my soule to
2 CONT God that Gave it in hopes of a Joyfull Resurrection unto Glory;
2 CONT and as Concerning my temporall estate, I Dispose thereof as
2 CONT followeth;
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I Doe give and bequeath unto John howland my eldest
2 CONT sonne besides what lands I have alreddy given him, all my Right
2 CONT and Interest To that one hundred acres of land graunted mee by
2 CONT the Court lying on the eastern side of Tauton River; between
2 CONT Teticutt and Taunton bounds and all the appurtenances and
2 CONT privilidges Therunto belonging, T belonge to him and his heirs and
2 CONT assignes for ever; and if that Tract should faile, then to have all
2 CONT my Right title and Interest by and in that Last Court graunt to mee
2 CONT in any other place, To belonge to him his heires and assignes for
2 CONT ever;
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my son Jabez howland all those
2 CONT my upland and Meadow That I now posesse at Satuckett and
2 CONT Paomett, and places adjacent, with all the appurtenances and
2 CONT privilidges, belonging therunto, and all my right title and Interest
2 CONT therin, To belonge to him his heires and assignes for ever,
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I Give and bequeath unto my son Jabez howland all that my
2 CONT one peece of land that I have lying on the southsyde of the Mill
2 CONT brooke, in the Towne of Plymouth aforsaid; be it more or lesse;
2 CONT and is on the Northsyde of a feild that is now Gyles Rickards
2 CONT senir To belonge to the said Jabez his heirs and assignes for ever;
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto Isacke howland my youngest
2 CONT sonne all those my uplands and meddows Devided and undivided
2 CONT with all the appurtenances and priviliges unto them belonging,
2 CONT lying and being in the Towne of Middlebery, and in a tract of
2 CONT Land Called the Majors Purchase near Namassakett Ponds;
2 CONT which I have bought and purchased of William White of
2 CONT Marshfeild in the Collonie of New Plymouth; which may or shall
2 CONT appeer by any Deed or writinges Together with the
2 CONT aformentioned prticulares To belonge to the said Isacke his heirs
2 CONT and assignes for ever;
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my said son Isacke howland the
2 CONT one halfe of my twelve acree lott of Meddow That I now have att
2 CONT Winnatucsett River within the Towne of Plymouth aforsaid To
2 CONT belonge to him and said Isacke howland his heires and assignes
2 CONT for ever,
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I Will and bequeath unto my Deare and loveing wife
2 CONT Elizabeth howland the use and benifitt of my now Dwelling house
2 CONT in Rockey nooke in the Township of Plymouth aforsaid, with the
2 CONT outhousing lands, That is uplands uplands [sic] and meddow
2 CONT lands and all appurtenances and privilidges therunto belonging in
2 CONT the Towne of Plymouth and all other Lands housing and
2 CONT meddowes that I have in the said Towne of Plymouth excepting
2 CONT what meddow and upland I have before given To my sonnes
2 CONT Jabez and Isacke howland During her naturall life to Injoy make
2 CONT use of and Improve for her benifitt and Comfort;
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my son Joseph howland after the
2 CONT Decease of my loveing wife Elizabeth howland my aforsaid
2 CONT Dwelling house att Rockey nooke together with all the outhousing
2 CONT uplands and Medowes appurtenances and privilidges belonging
2 CONT therunto; and all other housing uplands and meddowes
2 CONT appurtenances and privilidges That I have within the aforsaid
2 CONT Towne of New Plymouth excepting what lands and meadowes I
2 CONT have before Given To my two sonnes Jabez and Isacke; To
2 CONT belong to him the said Joseph howland To him and his heires and
2 CONT assignes for ever;
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my Daughter Desire Gorum twenty shillings
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give and bequeath To my Daughter hope Chipman twenty shillings
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my Daughter Elizabeth Dickenson twenty shillings
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my Daughter Lydia Browne twenty shillings
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give & bequeath to my Daughter hannah Bosworth twenty shillings
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my Daughter Ruth Cushman twenty shillings
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give to my Grandchild Elizabeth howland The Daughter of
2 CONT my son John howland twenty shillings
2 CONT
2 CONT Item my will is That these legacyes Given to my Daughters, be
2 CONT payed by my exequitrix in such species as shee thinketh meet;
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I will and bequeath unto my loveing wife Elizabeth howland,
2 CONT my Debts and legacyes being first payed my whole estate: vis:
2 CONT lands houses goods Chattles; or any thing else that belongeth or
2 CONT appertaineth unto mee, undisposed of be it either in Plymouth
2 CONT Duxburrow or Middlbery or any other place whatsoever; I Doe
2 CONT freely and absolutly give and bequeath it all to my Deare and
2 CONT loveing wife Elizabeth howland whom I Doe by these prsents,
2 CONT make ordaine and Constitute to be the sole exequitrix of this my
2 CONT Last will and Testament to see the same truely and faithfully
2 CONT prformed according to the tenour therof; In witness whereof I the
2 CONT said John howland senir have heerunto sett my hand and seale the
2 CONT aforsaid twenty ninth Day of May, one thousand six hundred
2 CONT seaventy and two 1672
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed and sealed in the
2 CONT
2 CONT prsence of Samuel ffuller John Howland
2 CONT
2 CONT William Crow And a seale
