2 CONT In his list of passengers on the Mayflower, Bradford tells us that John Howland
2 CONT was one of the "manservants" of John Carver [Bradford 441]. During a particularly
2 CONT bad storm on the crossing John Howland (characterized by Bradford as "a lusty
2 CONT young man") went above deck and was swept overboard, but it pleased God that
2 CONT he caught hold of the topsail halyards which hung overboard and ran out at length.
2 CONT Yet he held his hold (though he was sundry fathoms under water) till he was hauled
2 CONT up by the same rope to the brim of the water, and then with a boat hook and other
2 CONT means got into the  ship again and his life saved. And though he was something ill
2 CONT with it, yet he lived many years after and became a profitable member both in
2 CONT church + commonwealth [Bradford 59].
2 CONT
2 CONT      Emigration - 1620; Fenstanton, Huntingdonshire, England;
2 CONT
2 CONT           Mayflower.
2 CONT
