1 NOTE HISTORY CHART FROM MAYFLOWER ANCESTOR TO MARY McCLELLAN BAGLEY
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Almeda McClellan had two ancestors who were signers of the famous"Mayflower Compact," John Howland and John Tilley. We can point withpride to two others who were with the first landing of the Mayflower.John Tilley's wife and 
2 CONC daughter, Elizabeth, who later married JohnHowland. Following is the line of descent from our Mayflower ancestors,starting with John Howland, step by step down to Mary Almeda McClellanBagley.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. John Howland of the Mayflower. His daughter was Hope
2 CONT 2. Howland who married John Chipman and their son
2 CONT 3. Samuel Chipman who married Sarah Cobb who had son
2 CONT 4. Thomas Chipman and he married Abigail Lothrop (Lathrop) their son
2 CONT 5. Amos Chipman married Sarah Daggett (Dogget) whose son
2 CONT 6. Barnabas Lothrop Chipman md Beulah Evart and their daughter
2 CONT 7. Susannah Chipman married Sheldon Nichols and their daughter
2 CONT 8. Rhoda Ann Nichols married Hugh Day and their daughter
2 CONT 9. Almeda Day married William Carroll McClellan, their daughter
2 CONT 10. Mary Almeda McClellan married Edward Alma Bagley. From this you cantrace your descendants back to the Mayflower.
2 CONT
2 CONT * * * * * * * * * * * *
2 CONT
2 CONT THE HOWLANDS
2 CONT
2 CONT Governor Bradford's history of Plymouth Colony records an interestingstory concerning John Howland.
2 CONT
2 CONT "In a mighty storm, a lustie young man (called John Howland) coming uponsome occasion above ye gratings, was with a seele of ye shipe thrown intoye sea, but it pleased God that he caught hould (although he was sundriefathoms under
2 CONC  water) till he was hold up by ye same rope to ye brine ofye water and then with a boat hoope and other means got into shipe again,and his life saved; and tho' he was something ill with it, yet he livedmany years after and became 
2 CONC a profitable member, both in church andCommonwealthe."
2 CONT
2 CONT This was indeed a providential escape from death and fortunate it was forthe colony and for the country in the years to follow, because JohnHowland's descendants now number by the tens of thousands. They haveincluded great leaders
2 CONC  in many fields.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Mayflower was eventually anchored in what is now known asProvincetown Harbor where forty of the passengers drew up and signed thenow famous "Mayflower Compact."
2 CONT
2 CONT John Howland, then twenty-seven and unmarried, was one of the signers ofthe "Mayflower Compact", as was John Tilley who was accompanied by hiswife and their daughter, Elizabeth, then a girl of fourteen years.Shortly after arriving
2 CONC  in the "New World" John Tilley and wife died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Having arrived at Plymouth Harbor in 1620 and once ashore at the start ofa severe New England winter, they began construction of such shelter asthey could speedily erect. As time allowed, they began their morepermanent houses, the
2 CONC  pallisado type, which can be seen in the presentreplica of the original Plymouth Plantation, only a few miles distantfrom its original site near where the present Plymouth Rock rests underits canopy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The men from the Mayflower were sent out to find a place for the buildingof their homes. John Howland had an experience shortly after arrivingwhich might also have sent him to a watery grave. Both Howland and his(to be) father-in-
2 CONC law, John Tilley were members of the Mayflower thirdexploring expedition. It was very cold weather and the ocean spray frozeon to their clothes "like coats of iron". One morning at dawn they wereattacked by Indians on the shore at
2 CONC  Easham, which became known by thename of "First Encounter".
2 CONT
2 CONT During the day the ship lost its mast and rudder due to heavy rain andwinds and that night was nearly wrecked in the breakers. Luckily the shipdrifted to a sheltered position at an island later known as "Clark'sIsland", named afte
2 CONC r the Mr. Clark who was the mate of the Mayflower.After other harrowing experiences, the expedition returned, having spenta week in exploring efforts. It is believed that John Tilley's death mayhave been caused by this rough ordea
2 CONC l.
2 CONT
2 CONT About 1624 John Howland married Elizabeth Tilley, a girl of eighteen. TheHowlands' first home was on Summer Street, where presumably Hope was born30 August, 1629. The family moved to the finer house which John Howlandhad built at 
2 CONC Rocky Nook and first occupied in 1638. Here he made his homeuntil 23 February, 1672 when he died. This site is landscaped andsuitably marked with a monument. Its head bears an intaglio illustrationof the Mayflower and the inscript
2 CONC ion reads as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Here stood the Home of John Howland and his wife
2 CONT Elizabeth Tilley Howland
2 CONT from 1638 until his death, Feb. 23, 1672.
2 CONT Both were passengers in the ship "Mayflower"
2 CONT In grateful remembrance this land has been bought
2 CONT By their descendants and this memorial erected
2 CONT upon the 300 Anniversary of their landing
2 CONT at Plymouth, Mass. 1620-1920
2 CONT
2 CONT * * * * * * * * * * *
2 CONT
2 CONT MAN OVERBOARD
2 CONT
2 CONT In the darkness of the 'tween deck, Will sat with a group of men and boyslistening with frightened ears to the gale howling outside. And they atetheir cold supper of boiled salt beef and hard dry biscuit.
2 CONT
2 CONT Will could scarcely remember his last hot meal. The storm had startedwhen they were about halfway across the ocean. Since then, they had livedin the belly of the pitching ship.
2 CONT
2 CONT Will smelled the stench of unwashed bodies, rotting food and theMayflower's former cargoes of fish, turpentine and tar. It wasoverpowering.
2 CONT
2 CONT "I can't breathe in here," John Howland, Mr. Carver's secretary, saidsuddenly.
2 CONT
2 CONT One lurch, more violent than the other, suddenly threw Will to the floor.His head throbbed from a bang against a stanchion. Icy water dripped downon him. He was paralyzed with fear.
2 CONT
2 CONT Will heard a sharp gasp of pain near him. And John Howland said loudly,"We must have a light to see to lash things down. It's dangerous to havethem crashing about. I've already hurt my leg."
2 CONT
2 CONT Will found a dry candle in his sea bag. Howland struck a spark andlighted the candle.
2 CONT
2 CONT "We'd best get some lanterns," one of the men said.
2 CONT
2 CONT Will strained at ropes to secure the vinegar keg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beside him he could hear Howland's labored breathing.
2 CONT
2 CONT "You had better rest," Will told him.
2 CONT
2 CONT "I'll be all right," Howland answered. "I can't catch my breath. I mustget some air." In another moment he staggered off.
2 CONT
2 CONT Frightened, Will went after him. He lost Howland for a time in theshadows. Then Will saw him climbing the ladder. Howland pushed the hatchopen. Water cascaded down as he stepped to the deck.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Mr. Howland! Mr. Howland! Come back!" Will shouted. The man must havetaken leave of his senses to go out on the open deck in the storm.
2 CONT
2 CONT Will scurried up the ladder. Just as he raised his head above theopening, a great wave swept past. To his horror, he heard Howland cry,"Help! Help! Somebody help me!"
2 CONT
2 CONT "Man overboard!" Will screamed. "Mr. Howland washed overboard!"
2 CONT
2 CONT Sailors came running. They brought ropes and tied them about theirwaists.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Howland! Howland!" a sailor called out. "Listen ,Listen, I thought Iheard something."
2 CONT
2 CONT "The water's icy. No one can stay alive in it." another said.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then Captain Jones roared, "Quiet! Listen every body. Men," he called tohis crew, "swing your lanterns over the side. Search the water."
2 CONT
2 CONT For a moment there was nothing to be heard but the storm's roar. ThenWill caught a thin, wild cry
2 CONT
2 CONT "I heard him! I heard Howland!" Will screamed "He's down this way!"
2 CONT
2 CONT Two sailors swung their lanterns in wide arcs.
2 CONT
2 CONT "There he is!" one of them cried. "He's hanging on to one of thehalyards."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Bear a hand, men!" Captain Jones bellowed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hands grasped the halyard. Men hauled it in.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Use your boathook," Jones commanded a sailor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Will watched them catch Howland's jerkin with the hook and pull him out,gasping and shivering.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Thank God," said Carver. "Thank God, he's saved!"
