2 CONT BORN: About 1599*, Fenstanton, Huntingdon, England, son of Henry and 
2 CONT Margaret (---) Howland
2 CONT DIED: 23 or 24 February 1672/3, Rocky Nook, Kingston, MA. (the 
2 CONT Plymouth Colony Court Records say he died on 23 February, the Plymouth 
2 CONT Church Records say he died 24 February).
2 CONT MARRIED: Elizabeth Tilley, daughter of John and Joan (Hurst)(Rogers) 
2 CONT Tilley of the Mayflower, about 1624. 
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT ------
2 CONT * The traditional date that has been ascribed to John Howland's birth is 
2 CONT "about 1592", and this has never really been questioned. However, a 
2 CONT birth about 1599 is clearly a better estimate for the following reasons: 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Howland is called a "manservant" in William Bradford's passenger 
2 CONT list. Servants were contracted out until the age of 25. Thus, Howland 
2 CONT must have been under 25 in 1620, meaning he had to have been born after 
2 CONT 1595. Since Howland signed the Mayflower Compact, he must have been born 
2 CONT sometime before 1600 to have been legally old enough to sign. 
2 CONT John Howland's wife was born in 1607, and it is most unlikely that he, at 
2 CONT the age of 32, married a 17 year old girl as his first wife. Most men 
2 CONT married about age 25, and since his marriage occurred about 1624, this 
2 CONT would place his likely birth at 1599. 
2 CONT John Howland's last child was born in 1649. If the 1592 date were 
2 CONT accepted, he would have fathered a child at the age of 57, a most 
2 CONT unlikely circumstance. 
2 CONT William Bradford writes in that John Howland was a "lusty young man" in 
2 CONT 1620. It is unlikely that Bradford would call a 28-year old a "young 
2 CONT man". The only other person Bradford called a "young man" in 1620 was 
2 CONT John Alden, who was born in 1599. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT ------
