2 CONT Chapter 5: Quaker Ranters, Baptist Schismatics, and Indians with Tongues 
2 CONT Running Out (1657-1675)
2 CONT Middleborough
2 CONT
2 CONT Plymouth records as kept by Secretary Nathaniel Morton show two 
2 CONT consecutive items of interest:
2 CONT xxx The 23th of February, 1672[/73], Mr John Howland, Senir, of the towne 
2 CONT of Plymouth, deceased. Hee was a godly man and an ancient professor in 
2 CONT the wayes of Christ; hee lived untill hee attained above eighty yeares in 
2 CONT the world. Hee was one of the first comers into this land, and proved a 
2 CONT usefull instrument of good in his place, & was the last man that was left 
2 CONT of those that came over in the shipp called the May Flower, that lived in 
2 CONT Plymouth; hee was with honor intered att the towne of Plymouth on the 25 
2 CONT of February, 1672.
2 CONT Thomas Prence, Esquire, Govr of the jurisdiction of New Plymouth, died 
2 CONT the 29th of March 1673, and was interred the 8th of Aprill following. 
2 CONT After hee had served God in the office of Govr sixteen years, or neare 
2 CONT therunto, hee finished his course in the 73 yeare of his life. Hee was a 
2 CONT worthy gentleman, very pious, and very able for his office, and faithful 
2 CONT in the discharge therof, studious of peace, a welwiller to all that 
2 CONT feared God, and a terrour to the wicked. His death was much lamented, and 
2 CONT his body honorably buryed att Plymouth the day and yeare above mensioned
2 CONT Plymouth Colony: Its History and People 1620-1691
2 CONT Part One: Chronological Histories
2 CONT Chapter 6: King Philip's War (1675-1676)
2 CONT
2 CONT xxx John Howland had been part of Plymouth above fifty years. Though 
2 CONT there were still a few Mayflower passengers surviving outside Plymouth, 
2 CONT virtually all the old guard were gone now. Governor Prence, too, having 
2 CONT arrived in 1621 on the Fortune, had spent more than fifty years 
2 CONT intertwining his life with that of the colony. Though reputed to be a 
2 CONT strict man, a severe man, he must have been in some way representative of 
2 CONT at least the freemen of the colony, for after the death of Bradford they 
2 CONT continually reelected him governor until the day he died. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Plymouth Colony: Its History and People 1620-1691
2 CONT Part Three: Biographical Sketches
2 CONT Biographical Sketches
2 CONT Howland, John
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT John Howland married, probably ca. 1626, Elizabeth Tilley, q.v. In his 
2 CONT will, dated 29 May 1672, inventory 3 March 1672/73, he mentioned his wife 
2 CONT Elizabeth; oldest son John Howland; sons Jabez and Joseph; youngest son 
2 CONT Isaac; daughters Desire Gorham, Hope Chipman, Elizabeth Dickenson, Lydia 
2 CONT Browne, Hannah Bosworth, and Ruth Cushman; and granddaughter Elizabeth 
2 CONT Howland, daughter of his son John (MD 2:70). His widow [p.312] Elizabeth 
2 CONT died at the home of her daughter Lydia Browne, wife of James, at Swansea 
2 CONT on 21 December 1687, and in her will, dated 17 December 1686, proved 10 
2 CONT January 1687/88, she said she was seventy-nine years old, and mentioned 
2 CONT her sons John, Joseph, Jabez, and Isaac; daughters Lydia Browne, 
2 CONT Elizabeth Dickenson, and Hannah Bosworth; son-in-law Mr. James Browne; 
2 CONT and grandchildren James Browne, Jabez Browne, Dorothy Browne, Desire 
2 CONT Cushman, Elizabeth Bursley, and Nathaniel the son of Joseph Howland (MD 
2 CONT 3:54). Franklyn Howland, A Brief Genealogical and Biographical History of 
2 CONT Arthur, Henry, and John Howland and their Descendants… (New Bedford, 
2 CONT Mass., 1885), contains many errors. It is debatable whether John Howland 
2 CONT or John Alden has the greatest number of descendants living today, but 
2 CONT certainly the number of both is high. Elizabeth Pearson White, former 
2 CONT editor of the Mayflower Quarterly, is compiling a comprehensive family 
2 CONT history of the first five generations of John Howland's family
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
