2 CONT ===Summary===
2 CONT
2 CONT Deacon '''William Crocker''', born say 1615 in England; died Sep 1692, Barnstable, Massachusetts. He was one of the founders of Barnstable, Massachusetts in 1639. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Crocker, 11 February 1614 – 6 September 1692
2 CONT * Birth: 11 February 1614, Devon, England, United Kingdom
2 CONT * Death: 6 September 1692, Barnstable, Barnstable, Massachusetts, United States
2 CONT * Father: Hugh Crocker, 1598-1662 [unproven, see warning above]
2 CONT * Marriage: 1636, Alice  -1684, Children (7):
2 CONT # John Crocker, 1637-1711
2 CONT # Elizabeth Crocker, 1639-1658
2 CONT # Samuel Crocker, 1642-1681
2 CONT # Job Crocker, 1644-1719
2 CONT # Josiah Crocker, 1647-1698
2 CONT # Eleazer Crocker, 1650-1723
2 CONT # Joseph Crocker, 1654-1742
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://ancestors.familysearch.org/en/MFZK-WCK/william-crocker-1614-1692
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Alternate summary===
2 CONT
2 CONT Deacon William Crocker
2 CONT * Born about 11 Feb 1614 in Modbury, Devon, England
2 CONT * Son of Hugo Crocker and Thomasine (Mitchell) Crocker
2 CONT * Brother of John Crocker, Hugh Crocker, Joane Crocker and Samuel Crocker
2 CONT * Husband of Alice (Unknown) Crocker — married 1 May 1637 (to 4 May 1684) [location unknown]
2 CONT * Husband of Patience (Cobb) Crocker — married 1686 [location unknown]
2 CONT * Father of John Crocker, Elizabeth Crocker, Samuel Crocker, Job Crocker, Josiah Crocker, Eleazer Crocker and Joseph Crocker
2 CONT * Died 22 Oct 1692 in Barnstable, Barnstable, Massachusetts Bay Colony
2 CONT
2 CONT William Crocker migrated to New England during the Puritan Great Migration (1620-1640). William is said to have come with his brother John in 1634, either with Rev. Mr. Lathrop or near the same time. They stopped in Roxbury before
2 CONC  settling in Scituate[9].
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Crocker-7
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography===
2 CONT
2 CONT Deacon William Crocker, b. 11 Feb 1615/6 Modbury, Devon, England; d. Sep 1692, Barnstable, Massachusetts. He was one of the founders of Barnstable, Massachusetts (1639). He met Alice Foster (AKA: Hoyt) before emigrating to the Col
2 CONC onies, and they were married in Roxbury, England. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In Barnstable he became Deacon of the Church, and its Deputy to the General Court 1670, 1671, 1674.  In 1643 he moved to West Barnstable, where he died in 1692.  Alice Foster Hoyt, was born before 1636, and died in 1684. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William and Alice's children are:
2 CONT # John, b. 1 May 1637                                       
2 CONT # Elizabeth b. 22 Sep 1639                                       
2 CONT # Samuel b. 3 July 1642                                       
2 CONT # Job b. 19 Sep 1644                                       
2 CONT # Josiah b. 19 Sep 1647                                       
2 CONT # Eleazer b. 21 July 1650                                       
2 CONT # Joseph  (Sgt) b. 165
2 CONT
2 CONT Deacon William Crocker, a native of Lyneham, Devonshire, emigrated to New England in 1634 or 1635. Lived first Roxbury or Scituate. Removed to Barnstable in 1639, year in which the town was settled. (p. 414)  He was a deacon in th
2 CONC e church, and was an influential citizen and the owner of a large landed estate. (p. 1122)
2 CONT
2 CONT He first settled in the easterly part of Barnstable, but in 1643, removed to the westerly part where in 1655, he owned one hundred and twenty six acres of upland and twenty two acres of meadow. (p. 1122) [3] He was often employed 
2 CONC in the business of the town and in settling estates. He acquired a large landed estate and for many years was the richest man in the town. (p. 876)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Accomplishments & Occupations===
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1636: Built a frame house in Scituate, the fourty forth in that town.
2 CONT * 1636: Dec. 25: Deacon William Crocker united with Rev. Mr. Lothrop's church in Scituate.
2 CONT * 1639: October: Removed to Barnstable in October 1639, and was among the first settlers.
2 CONT * 1644: Constable.
2 CONT * 1654: 1655: 1657: 1661: 1667:1675: Grand Juror.
2 CONT * 1668: Salesman.
2 CONT * 1670: 1671: 1674: Deputy to the General Court at Plymouth. (p. 876) [4]
2 CONT * 1643: The three deacons of the church Dimmock, Cobb, and Crocker each complied with the order of the Colony Court and built fortification houses, aided by their neighbors, in case of a sudden assault by the Indians. The building
2 CONC s were to be a common place for refuge (Barnstable Families, p. 211) [5]. Deacon Crocker married his first wife Alice Foster [1] in 1636 at Scituate. She was the mother of all his children and died soon after 1683. (p. 1122) [3] H
2 CONC e married second, Patience (Cobb) Parker, widow of Robert Parker and daughter of Elder Henry Cobb (p. 876) [4] (p. 1122) [3] (p. 192).[6].
2 CONT
2 CONT His sons assisted their father on the farm, and at seventeen were able to do the work of a man. The girls were also brought up to more than earn their own living and assisted their mother spinning and weaving the flax and wool, an
2 CONC d making their own and their brother's garments; and in hay time and at harvest, assisted their brothers (Barnstable Families, p. 208). [5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Deacon William died in September, 1692, aged about eighty (p. 414). [2] (p. 876)[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Last Will & Testament===
2 CONT
2 CONT Deacon William Crocker's Last Will and Testament was dated 6 Sept 1692, and mentions:
2 CONT
2 CONT To Patience my loving wife.
2 CONT Sons: John, Job, Josiah, Eliazer, Joseph Grandsons: Nathaniel, son of John; Samuel, son of Job; Thomas, son of Josiah (Barnstable Families, p. 205-8).[5] Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # John, born in Scituate, May 1, 1637. (p. 1122) [3] (p. 99) [7]; married Mary Bodfish in 1659. ( Vol. 3; p. 150)[8] He married second Mary Bursley in 1663 (Barnstable Families, p. 211).[5]
2 CONT # Elizabeth, September 22, 1639. (p. 1122) [3]; died May 1658. (Vol. 3; p. 150)[8]
2 CONT # Samuel, born in Barnstable, June 3, 1642 (p. 1122) [3](Vol. 3; p. 150)[8] ; died Dec., 1681 from injuries in an assault by Indian James in November or that year. (p. 209; p. 213)[5]
2 CONT # Job, born March 9, 1644. (p. 1122) [3](Vol. 3; p. 150)[8]; died March, 1718-19. Like his father, a deacon, married first Mary Walley in Nov. 1668; married second, Hannah Taylor/Tailor July 19, 1680. (p. 214; p. 215) [5]
2 CONT # Josiah, born September 19, 1647 (p. 1122) [3](Vol. 3; p. 150)[8]; died Feb. 2, 1689-9; married Melatiah Hinckley, daughter of Gov. Thomas Hinckley Oct. 23, 1668. (p. 216) [5]
2 CONT # Eleazer, born July 21, 1650; (p. 1122) [3](Vol. 3; p. 150)[8] ; married first Ruth Chipman on April 7, 1682; married second on Jan. 25, 1716-17, Mercy Phinney. (p. 217) [5]
2 CONT # Joseph, born 1654 (p. 1122) [3](Vol. 3; p. 150)[8] ; married Temperance Bursley in Dec., 1677. (p. 218-19) [5]
2 CONT
