1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Elizabeth COOKE  1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 was born 1573 in Pebmarsh, Essex, England. She died 1637 in Salem, Essex, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Parents:  Thomas COOKE,   b. 1541, Wickford, Essex, England  &  Susan BRAND
2 CONT
2 CONT Married
2 CONT # Edmund READE on 1594 in Wickford, Essex, England as his 2nd wife.  he died 1623
2 CONT # Hugh Peters  1625 - 1st wife for peters, 2nd   marr  Elizabeth
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Edmund Reade & Elizabeth Cooke:
2 CONT #  Edmund READE 1, 2 was christened 6 Nov 1595 in Wickford, Essex, England. He died Mar 1600 in North Benfleet, Essex, England and was buried 3 30 Mar 1600 in North Benfleet, Essex, England.
2 CONT #  Mary READE 1 was christened 2 26 Jun 1597 in North Benfleet, Essex, England. She died Apr 1602 in North Benfleet, Essex, England and was buried 3 13 Apr 1602 in North Benfleet, Essex, England.
2 CONT # Margaret READE was christened 16 Jul 1598 and died Sep 1672.
2 CONT #  William READE 1, 2 was christened 3 1 Jan 1600 in North Benfleet, Essex, England. He died 3 Apr 1659 in North Benfleet, Essex, England.
2 CONT # Martha READE was christened 22 Jul 1602 and died 1661.
2 CONT # Edmund READE 1, 2 was christened 22 Jul 1604 in North Benfleet, Essex, England. He died Nov 1613 in Wickford, Essex, England and was buried 9 Nov 1613 in Wickford, Essex, England.
2 CONT # Thomas READE 1 was christened 2 7 Jan 1607 in North Benfleet, Essex, England. He died 3 15 Sep 1607 in North Benfleet, Essex, England.
2 CONT # Samuel READE 1, 2 was christened 3 31 Jul 1609 in North Benfleet, Essex, England.
2 CONT #  Colonel Thomas READE was christened 15 Aug 1612 and died Nov 1662.
2 CONT # Elizabeth READE was christened 27 Nov 1614 and died 24 Nov 1672
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Puritan Great Migration
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Cooke-2187
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Cooke
2 CONT
2 CONT Origin
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth was born July 2, 1568 at Pemmersley, Essex, England.[citation needed] She was a daughter of Thomas Cooke of Pebmarsh, co. Essex, England.[1] Her mother was Susan or Elizabeth Brand.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth married c. 1594 to Edmund Reade, son of William Reade and Martha Church.[1] The first child was born at Wickford, which was Edmund's home.[1] They then lived at the adjoining parish of North Benfleet from 1597 to 1612, p
2 CONC ossibly in the small manor adjoining the church.[1] They had ten children together.[1] By 1614, they had returned to Wickford, co. Essex.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Edmund and Elizabeth:[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT # Edmund bpt Nov 6, 1595 at Wickford; mar
2 CONT # Mary b June 18, 1597, bpt June 26, 1597 at North Benfleet; buried April 13, 1602
2 CONT # Margaret b July 11, bpt July 15, 1598; mar John Lake
2 CONT # William b Oct 28, 1599, bpt Jan 1, 1599/1600; mar Anne Alleyn, daughter and heiress of Thomas Alleyn
2 CONT # Martha b July 13, bpt July 22, 1602; mar 1) Daniel Eppes, 2) Samuel Symonds who became Dept. Gov. Massachusetts Bay Colony
2 CONT # Edmund (2) bpt July 22, 1604, d Nov 9, 1613 at Wickford
2 CONT # Thomas b Jan 2, bpt Jan 7, 1605/6 at North Benfleet, d Sept 15, 1607
2 CONT # Samuel b July 23, bpt July 31, 1609 at North Benfleet; physician at Bishop's Stortford, co. Herts.; unmarried
2 CONT # Thomas (2) bpt Aug 15, 1612 at North Benfleet; mar Priscilla Banks, emigrated to Salem
2 CONT # Elizabeth bpt Nov 27, 1614 at Wickford; mar John Winthrop, Jr. who became Governor of Connecticut
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 20, 1623, Edmund made his will at Wickford, and he was buried there on December 1, 1623.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Reverend Hugh Peter
2 CONT
2 CONT After Edmund died, Elizabeth married, as her 2nd, to Rev. Hugh Peter, son of Thomas and Martha (Treffry) Dyckwood AKA Peter, of Fowey, co. Cornwall.[1] He graduated from University of Cambridge in 1617/18, so he must have been qui
2 CONC te a bit younger than she. Hugh was a non-conformist minister; his beliefs were so unpopular with the English authorities that he fled to Holland in 1629.[1] Some of Edmund and Elizabeth's children were living in the New England c
2 CONC olonies by this time. In 1635, Hugh emigrated to Massachusetts Bay. He became the minister of the Salem church in December 1636, and his ideas were again controversial.[1] In 1641, he was sent as an agent of the colonies to Englan
2 CONC d, where he served with the Parliamentary forces during the English Civil War. When Charles II was re-instated, Hugh was arrested for complicity, held in the Tower, and eventually executed on October 16, 1660.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth died about 1637, and Hugh Peter married, as his 2nd, to Deliverance Sheffield.[1] They had one daughter together, Elizabeth.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Research Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT from GM:
2 CONT
2 CONT marriage to Hugh Peter about 1625, Elizabeth (Cooke) Reade, daughter of Thomas Cooke and widow of Edmund Reade.
2 CONT
2 CONT Migrated from Rotterdam in 1635; resided at Salem. He returned to England permanently in August 1641.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh Peters first mbr of Salem church after reorganization in 1636.
2 CONT Deliverance admitted to church on Jan. 12, 1639/40 (later excommunicated.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth (Cooke) Reade Peters died at Salem in March or April 1638.
2 CONT Her widower married Deliverance (_____) Sheffield by 1639. She was dismissed from Salem church on January 2, 1639/40. Deliverance living as late as 1677 in England. They had 1 child: Elizabeth Peter bap Salem Mar. 8, 1639/40. m. A
2 CONC pr 23, 1665 to Robert Barker.
2 CONT
