1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Joan (Hurst) Tilley, &quot;Mayflower&quot; Passenger''' was born in [https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henlow Henlow], Central Bedfordshire, England and baptized at the parish church there on March 13, 1567/8.  Her parents were [
2 CONC https://www.geni.com/profile-21331712 William Hurst] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-4114080 Rose Marshe].  She came on the [https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mayflower Mayflower] (1620) with her second husband, [https://www.geni.
2 CONC com/people/John-Tilley-Mayflower-Passenger/6000000000453526520?through=6000000001170118753 John Tilley], and their 13 year old  daughter [https://www.geni.com/people/Elizabeth-Howland-Mayflower-Passenger/6000000001170101143?throug
2 CONC h=6000000001170118753 Elizabeth], as well as John's brother Edward Tilley and his wife Ann or Agnes Cooper.  Edward and Ann brought along Ann's relatives Henry Sampson and Humility Cooper. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan and her husband died the first winter at Plymouth, (1621),  and were buried in the Coles Hill Burial Ground at Plymouth, Plymouth County, Massachusetts. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan Hurst was baptized 13 Mar 1568 at Henlow, Bedfordshire, England. Joan was the daughter of [[Hurst-27|William Hurst]] and [[Marshe-3|Rose Marshe]]
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Henlow Parish Registers & Bishop's Transcripts; Baptisms: 1567/8  Mar 13 Joan, d. William Hurst.<''English Ancestry of Seven Mayflower Passengers: Tilley, Sampson and Cooper'', by Robert Leigh Ward,database online at The Ame
2 CONC rican Genealogist, Vol. 52 #4; p. 204
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan married first [[Rogers-214|Thomas Rogers]] 18 Jun 1593 at Henlow,Bedfordshire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Thomas Rogers was not the Mayflower passenger. Joan was mentioned as his widow when she married John Tilley in 1596.  ''English Ancestry of Seven Mayflower Passengers: Tilley, Sampson and Cooper'', by Robert Leigh Ward, data
2 CONC base online at The American Genealogist, Vol.52 #4; p. 203/4
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan married secondly, [[Tilley-84|John Tilley]] 20 Sep 1596 at Henlow, Bedfordshire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Henlow Parish Register & Bishop's Transcripts; Marriages: Tilley; 1596, Sep. 20; John Tyly & Joan Rogers ''English Ancestry of Seven Mayflower Passengers: Tilley, Sampson and Cooper'', by Robert Leigh Ward, database online a
2 CONC t The American Genealogist, Vol. 52 #4; p. 199
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tilley was baptized 19 Dec 1571 at Henlow, Bedfordshire,England, the son of [[Tilley-562|Robert Tilley]] and [[Ellborne-1|Elizabeth Ellborne]].
2 CONT
2 CONT John, Joan, their daughter Elizabeth, John’s brother, Edward, and Edward’s wife, Ann, were passengers on the Mayflower in 1620. John andEdward both signed the Mayflower Compact. John, Joan, Edward, and Ann died shortly after their
2 CONC  arrival in Plymouth. Of the Tilley family who came to the New World, only daughter Elizabeth survived.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Children of Joan Hurst and Thomas Rogers ===
2 CONT # [[Rogers-3683|Joan Rogers]], baptized 26 May 1594 at Henlow, Bedfordshire, England; married [[Hawkins-8452|Edward Hawkins]] 27 Jun 1620 atSt. Paul’s Church, Bedford, Bedfordshire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Children of Joan Hurst and John Tilley ===
2 CONT # [[Tilley-124|Rose Tilley]], baptized 23 Oct 1597 at Henlow, Bedfordshire, England; Rose Tilly, daughter of John Tilly : England Births and Christenings, 1538-1975," database, FamilySearch(https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:
2 CONC V5GY-8JB : 11 February 2018, Rose Tilly, 23 Oct 1597); citing HENLOW,BEDFORD,ENGLAND, index based upon data collected by the Genealogical Society of Utah, Salt Lake City; FHL microfilm Q942.565 B4E V. 26. buried 28 Feb 1601 at Hen
2 CONC low. Rose Tilley, buried 28 Feb 1601 at Henlow, Bedford : Maine, Nathan Hale CemeteryCollection, ca. 1780-1980," database with images, FamilySearch(https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:QVJ5-MXF8 : 16 March 2018), Rose Tilley, ;
2 CONC  citing Henlow, , Bedford, England, Cemetery, Maine State Library, Augusta; FHL microfilm 1,316,315.
2 CONT # [[Tilley-125|John Tilley]], baptized 26 Aug 1599 at Henlow, Bedfordshire, England
2 CONT # Rose Tilley, baptized 28 Feb 1602 at Henlow, Bedfordshire, England.  Rose Tyllye, daughter of John Tyllye : "England Births and Christenings, 1538-1975," database, FamilySearch(https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:V5GY-8VM : 
2 CONC 11 February 2018, Rose Tyllye, 28 Feb 1602); citing HENLOW,BEDFORD,ENGLAND, index based upon data collected by the Genealogical Society of Utah, Salt Lake City; FHL microfilm Q942.565 B4E V. 26.</ref># [[Tilley-127|Robert Tilley]]
2 CONC , baptized 25 Nov 1604 at Henlow, Bedfordshire, England; served as an apprentice to a tailor; married Mary Hawkins 1 Nov 1632 at St Paul’s Church, Bedford, Bedfordshire, England
2 CONT # [[Tilley-73|Elizabeth Tilley]], baptized 30 Aug 1607 at Henlow, Bedfordshire, England; sailed on the Mayflower in 1620;  married [[Howland-21|John Howland]] 14 Aug 1623 at Plymouth, Plymouth, Massachusetts; died 21 Dec 1687 at S
2 CONC wansea, Bristol, Massachusetts; will written 17 Dec 1686 and proved 10 Jan 1687/8
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Identity
2 CONT
2 CONT From MAYFLOWER INCREASINGS by Susan Roser 
2 CONT
2 CONT >There has been some controversy about the identity of the wife of John Tilley, who came over on the Mayflower. In "Saints and Strangers" by George F. Willison, she was identified as "Mrs. Elizabeth (Comyngs) 1585-1621 of St. Andr
2 CONC ew Undershaft, London. Married London 1605". In "The Women who came in the Mayflower" by Alice Russell Marble, she is identified stating "the mother of Elizabeth Tilley bore a Dutch name, Bridget Van De Veldt".
2 CONT
2 CONT >Only in 1976 were the Tilley records discovered in the Henlow parish church proving that Elizabeth Tilley, the bride of John Howland, was the daughter of Joan Hurst. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "This letter is in reference to the query from Harold H. Everett of Flagler Beach, FL, who wrote about Joan Hurst Rogers being the step-mother of Mayflower Pilgrim rather than her mother (MQ, Aug, 1991, p. 232). 
2 CONT
2 CONT >Joan Hurst Rogers did not 'come to New England" to marry John Tilley. John Tilley and Joan Rogers were m. in Henlow Co., Bedford, ENG on 20 Sep 1596, in St. Mary the Virgin Church, where John Tilley and his ancestors had been mem
2 CONC bers for generations. Joan Rogers was bapt. in the same church on 13 Mar. 1567/8 as Joan Hurst, dau. of William Hurst. Records also show that Joan (Hurst) Rogers Tilley and husband, John, had the following children baptized there 
2 CONC (source: Redding Family and its Relatives, by Billie Redding Lewis, 1984, 3rd edition): . . . 
2 CONT
2 CONT >Elizabeth, bapt. 30 Aug. 1607; this date agrees with the death record of 21 Dec. 1687, which states that at the time of death, she was 80 years of age." 
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Their mother Rose remarried after the death of Joan's father to John Rushe in 1571 and had a second family of two sons and two daughters so Joan would have grown up with her step-family as well as her sister and her brother Henr
2 CONC y Hurst. In his will of 1603 John Rushe left generous legacies of forty shillings to Joan and each of her children."
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT From MFQ, vol 62, no 1, Feb 1996, p 56-60. MFQ, vol 62, no 1, Feb 1996, p 56-60. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >Both she and her husband, John TILLEY were probably "secretly buried ...at night on Cole's Hill, overlooking Plymouth Rock" so that the Indians wouldn't know of their great losses that first few months. They leveled the graves an
2 CONC d planted the plot with corn the next spring. Wnen water pipes were being installed along the Hill in 1859, some bones were uncovered, which Dr. Oliver Wendell HOLMES confirmed to be the bones of those who died in 1620-1621.  The 
2 CONC Pilgrin Society had them mortared into the ceiling of the canopy directly above Plymouth Rock.  In 1921, during the celebration of the 300th anniversary of the landing of the Pilgrims, a giant sarcophagus was unveiled on Cole's Hi
2 CONC ll, after a procession through the streets, and in it were placed the small box of Pilgrin bones unearthed 50 years before. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From MFQ, vol 61, no 3, Aug 1995, p 177: vol 62 no 1, Feb 1996, p 56-57: vol 63 no 1, Feb 1997, p 43. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >Married 1st ROGERS, Thomas, 18 Jun 1593, Henlow, Bedfordhire, Eng. They had 1 child, Joan ROGERS, who would have been 26 when the Mayflower sailed in 1620. 2nd TILLEY, John, 20 Sep 1596, St. Mary the Virgin's Church, Henlow, Engl
2 CONC and. They had 5 children christened in Henlow, Bedfordshire, England. The children, except for Elizabeth, may also have been married but preferred to stay in England.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Photo of monument Added by:  Caryn on 3 Jan 2017 at FindAGrave
2 CONT {{//s3.amazonaws.com/photos.geni.com/p13/be/8f/f2/aa/534448482f65d37d/c8acd7d3-d6cf-4b1f-90cf-4fc107e3f544_original.jpg}}
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan’s daughter Elizabeth Tilley went on to marry fellow Mayflower passenger John Howland, with whom she had ten children and 88 grandchildren. Because of their great progeny, she and her husband have millions of living descendant
2 CONC s today.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
