2 CONT Margaret's maiden name and parentage before her burial on July 31, 1629, in Fenstanton are not documented in historical records. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A last name at birth or parentage is not yet found for Margaret, the wife of Henry Howland. Despite extensive research and genealogical records, Margaret's origins remain a mystery. It is possible that further discoveries may shed
2 CONC  light on her family background, but for now, her maiden name and parentage will remain unknown. The lack of information surrounding Margaret adds an air of intrigue to her story and leaves researchers wondering about her past.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Birth ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Howland was likely born around 1567 in Huntingdonshire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Name ===
2 CONT
2 CONT George E. McCracken, a genealogist, wrote an article in 1950, and he said, "Arthur Howland, Sr., early an immigrant to Plymouth Colony and later a resident of Marshfield, Mass., was a son, probably the eldest, of Henry Howland, Sr
2 CONC ., buried in Fen Stanton, Huntingdonshire, England, on 19 May 1635, and of his wife whose first name was almost certainly Anne." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Various authorities name Henry Howland, Senior's wife, as Anne or Margaret. She is sometimes mistakenly identified as Alice Ayres. However, Alice Ayres belonged to a different Howland family in Ely, not related to the correct Howl
2 CONC ands of Fenstanton. The Ely marriage was in 1600, much too late for the accepted ages of John and his brothers. These errors may have originated in 1949 when the well-known genealogist Leon Clark Hills discovered Howland marriage 
2 CONC records in Ely. Francis Stoddard reported this discovery in "The Truth About the Pilgrims." The Pilgrim John Howland Society re-examined the records and determined that the Ely and Fenstanton Howland families were distinct.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Anthony Wagner's work was published in ''The Howland Quarterly'', specifically in volume 28, issues 2 and 3, during January–April 1964. The main purpose of this article is to distinguish between the Ely and Fenstanton Howland 
2 CONC families.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is evident that the name Anne is incorrect; however, only one historical document shows her name was Margaret.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Children ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry and Margaret Howland had six known sons and one daughter. All six sons became apprentices in London. Genealogists estimated the birth dates using marriage records, apprenticeships, and the presumed birth order from wills. A 
2 CONC noted Mayflower researcher Caleb Johnson used the dates in his analysis of the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT #Arthur Howland's estimated birth year is around 1591. This birth date is a very rough estimate, considering he had a child by 1616. His brother, Humphrey Howland, mentioned him in his will. He was in London in May 1639 and later 
2 CONC appeared in Duxbury, Massachusetts, by August 6, 1640. Arthur Howland was laid to rest in Marshfield, Massachusetts, on October 30, 1675. He married Grace, who was buried on December 4, 1635, at St. Giles Cripplegate, London. He m
2 CONC arried second, Margaret. She died on January 22, 1882/83, at Marshfield.
2 CONT #George Howland's estimated birth year is around 1593. The birth date is a rough estimate. He was buried on February 10, 1643/44, at St. Dunstan in the East, London, a merchant and singleman.
2 CONT #Humphrey Howland was estimated to be born around 1595, determined from his apprenticeship in 1613. He married Margaret Calvert at St. Mary Matfellon, Whitechapel, on November 21, 1622. He married his second wife, Anne _____, on O
2 CONC ctober 16, 1636. His will was written on May 28, 1646, and proved on July 10, 1646. Anne was buried on December 20, 1653, at Barking, Essex, England.
2 CONT #John Howland was estimated to be born around 1598 based on historical records, including signing the Mayflower Compact, apprenticing in 1620 to John Carver, and marrying around 1623. He died on February 23, 1772, at Rocky Nook, P
2 CONC lymouth, Massachusetts. He married Elizabeth, daughter of John and Joan (Hurst) (Rogers) Tilley, in Plymouth around 1623.
2 CONT #Henry Howland was likely born around 1600, deduced from his probable marriage around 1624. He passed away on January 1, 1670/71, in Duxbury, Massachusetts.
2 CONT #Margaret Howland was estimated to be born around 1602. She married Richard Phillips, a shoemarker, at Fenstanton on April 26, 1623.
2 CONT #Simon Howland was baptized on August 19, 1604, in Fenstanton, Huntingdonshire, England, as recorded in church documents. He was the only child whose baptism record existed. Simon Howland was buried on October 3, 1636, at St. Mary
2 CONC  Magdelen, Bermondsey in the London Borough of Southwark. He married Anne Weber on April 1, 1632, at St. Mary Magdelen Bermondsey.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Life ===
2 CONT The baptismal record of Henry Howland's son suggests her family resided in Fenstanton from 1604 until her death in 1629. The location of their residence before 1604 remains unknown. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry and Margaret Howland had six sons who participated in apprenticeships at one of the trade guilds in London starting in 1616. This indicates their direct involvement in the textile trade through trade guilds; for instance, Hu
2 CONC mphrey, Henry, and Simon Howland worked with drapers, while Arthur specialized in leather dressing or glovemaking. Additionally, George Howland was involved in general merchandise, and John Howland was pursuing a fur trading busin
2 CONC ess in Plymouth Colony.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Death ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Unfortunately, the original parish registers of Fenstanton never recorded the death dates but only recorded all the christening, marriage, and burial dates.
2 CONT
2 CONT The exact date of Margaret Howland's death remains unknown. She died sometime before July 31, 1629, so that is the burial date, but not the death date.
2 CONT
2 CONT July 30, 1629, is an estimated death date because it is commonly assumed she died the day before her burial on July 31, 1629, but no source confirms the death date.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Burial ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Howland was buried on July 31, 1629, at St. Peter and St. Paul Churchyard in Fenstanton, Huntingdonshire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT The original burial record of Fenstanton reveals that Margaret Howland, wife of Henrie Howland, was buried on July 31, 1629. The entry in the burial record says, "Burialls Anno 1629... Margarett Howland wife of henrie Julye 31", a
2 CONC ligning with Howland Quarterly, The Pilgrim John Howland Society, 28 (Jan-April 1964): 6, which says, "1629 July 31 — Margaret Howland wife of Henry Buried."
2 CONT
2 CONT Also, in 1949, Leon C. Hills of Washington, D.C., a well-known genealogist, wrote: "Henry Howland, buried Fern Stanton, Huntingshire, Eng., May 17, 1635... married 2nd Margaret. She was buried at Fen Stanton July 31, 1629." Howlan
2 CONC d Quarterly noted that Leon C. Hills referenced these records, sourced from copies of England Parishes in the Congressional Library, Washington, D.C.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Research Notes ===
2 CONT ==== Common Errors to Avoid ====
2 CONT '''Incorrect conflation:''' Margaret of Fenstanton is not identical with Alice Ayers of Ely, as is sometimes said. This Alicia Ayers married Henricus (Henrie) Howland on 26 April 1600 in Ely, Cambridgeshire. They had two known chi
2 CONC ldren, John Howland, who was baptized in 1603, and Henry Howland, who was baptized in 1604. The names and dates made them good candidates to be the parents of the New England immigrants for many years. However, it has been shown t
2 CONC hat this was an entirely different Alicia Howland lived in Ely.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Incorrect marriage date:''' The marriage date of 26 April 1600 previously on this profile comes from the mistaken belief that Margaret Howland of Fenstanton was the same person as Alicia Ayers of Ely, who married Henry Howland 
2 CONC of Ely in 1600.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Incorrect name:''' The name of Henry Howland of Fenstaton's wife as Alice Ayers comes from the mistaken belief that Margaret Howland of Fenstanton was the same person as Alicia Ayers of Ely, who married Henry Howland of Ely in 
2 CONC 1600.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Incorrect name:''' The belief that Henry Howland of Fenstanton's wife was named Anne arose from a misunderstanding of Humphrey Howland's will, which initially used A:H to represent his daughter, Anne Howland. Humphrey Howland m
2 CONC arked all pewters with A:H to ensure they were designated for his daughter, Anne Howland. Furthermore, he used the term "her" as a gender possessive adjective before "grandmother" in his will, clarifying that it referred to Anne H
2 CONC owland's maternal grandmother rather than to his own mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Recent advancements in genealogical research have shed light on the lineage of Anne Howland, particularly concerning her grandmothers. A pivotal discovery involves the 1646 will of Humphrey Howland, which has clarified the distinc
2 CONC tions between Anne's paternal and maternal grandmothers. Through meticulous examination of probate records in London, researchers have identified Christopher Colvert, a prominent Citizen and Merchant Taylor, as Anne's maternal gra
2 CONC ndfather. This connection is further substantiated by Humphrey Howland's management of Colvert's estate, as outlined in Colvert's nuncupative will from 1640, which specifically addressed the welfare of his grandchildren, Ann and N
2 CONC athaniel Howland. The mention of Humphrey Howland as the executor during the grandchildren's minority emphasizes the familial connections and the importance of this discovery in understanding his wife's heritage. This finding is c
2 CONC onsistent with the baptismal record of Humphrey Howland's son Nathaniel and his will from 1646, which explicitly mentions his daughter Ann. Additionally, burial records from Saint Pancras, Soper Lane, London, confirm the burial of
2 CONC  Christopher Colvert's wife, Elizabeth Colvert, on September 13, 1604, suggesting that Margaret Calvert was quite young when her mother died. This breakthrough not only enriches our understanding of Anne Howland's family history b
2 CONC ut also highlights the importance of historical documents in genealogical research.
2 CONT
2 CONT A closer examination of Humphrey Howland’s will reveals that the initials "A.H." likely refer to his daughter, Anne Howland, rather than suggesting the initials of her grandmother. It seems that Humphrey affixed these labels to th
2 CONC e pewter items to designate them as belonging to Anne, indicating that they were intended as a wedding gift for her. Furthermore, historical records indicate that these pewter pieces were previously owned by her maternal grandmoth
2 CONC er, Elizabeth Colvert. However, it is important to note that the name "Anne" attributed to Henry Howland's wife is incorrect, as only one historical document identifies her as Margaret.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Incorrect son:''' Since 2014, the contributor of ''Find a Grave'' has claimed that [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/132611449/jonathan_aires-howland Jonathan Aires Howland (1586–1590)] was believed to be the son of Henry Ho
2 CONC wland and Ann Margaret Aires. The claim alleged that he was buried at St. Peter and St. Paul's Graveyard in Fenstanton, Cambridgeshire. The contributor's purpose was to insert her maiden name Aires to Jonathan's middle name, as if
2 CONC  Jonathan were their son. This information was obtained from online public and private family trees, which included inaccurate and misattributed sources. No historical records exist to verify the existence of Jonathan Aires Howlan
2 CONC d. The burial records in Fenstanton or Ely did not mention his burial in 1590. Two parishes in Ely started recording baptisms, marriages, and burials in 1599, and one parish in Fenstanton began recording events in 1612. Moreover, 
2 CONC Bishop’s Transcripts for 1604–1605, 1608, and 1610 provide additional information not found in Fenstanton's original registers. Some genealogists on Ancestry.com have incorrectly identified Jonathan Aires Howland as the son of Hen
2 CONC ry Howland and Ann Margaret Aires or Alicia Ayers from Ely. Since he was born in 1586 and died in 1590 before the marriage of Henry Howland and Alicia Ayers, both of Ely in 1600, it is clear he could not have been their son. This 
2 CONC misinformation resulted from the careless creation of a non-existent person.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==== Recommendation ====
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. One potential avenue for further research could be to explore local church records or parish registers in Fenstanton during the time period leading up to Margaret's burial in 1629.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Another possibility is to investigate any known family connections or relationships that may provide clues about Margaret's maiden name and parentage.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. It may also be worth looking into other historical documents from the same time period, such as wills, land deeds, or tax records, which could shed light on Margaret's background.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Researchers could consider reaching out to local historians or genealogical societies in Fenstanton for assistance in uncovering more information about Margaret and her family history.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Examining neighboring communities and their historical records might also yield valuable insights into Margaret's origins prior to her burial in Fenstanton in 1629.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Study a history of Fenstanton, Huntingdonshire, or if there is no history of that parish, then a history of the county, looking for evidence of separatist views in the parish or county that would lead to the Howlands’ immigrati
2 CONC on.  
2 CONT
2 CONT === Sources ===
2 CONT *Gilbert, Hilah Scott. ''Ancestors of Heather J. Gilbert, Susan Scott Gilbert and Allyn Ellsworth Gilbert, Jr.''. United States: [Typescript Print], 1963, p. 31. [https://www.ancestry.com/imageviewer/collections/28456/images/dvm_G
2 CONC enMono007911-00017-1?usePUB=true&pId=30 Ancestry image]. Text: "Henry Howland of Fenny Stanton, co Huntington, England was born in latter half of the sixteenth century. He was buried in Fenny Stanton 19 May 1635. Possibably his wi
2 CONC fe's name was Anne as his son Humphrey Howland bequeathed to his daughter Anne all the petter marked A. H. which had belonged to. her grandmother." The name Anne is incorrect. Only one historical document only identifies her name 
2 CONC as Margaret, not Anne.
2 CONT
2 CONT * "The Will of Arthur Howland, Senior, of Marshfield." George E. McCracken, ''NEHGR'', vol. 104, pp. 221-225 (Jul 1950). [https://www.americanancestors.org/databases/new-england-historical-and-genealogical-register/RecordDisplay?v
2 CONC olumeId=11706&pageName=221&rId=236364470 American Ancestors image]. Text: "Arthur Howland, Sr., early an immigrant to Plymouth Colony and later a resident of Marshfield, Mass., was a son, probably the eldest, of Henry Howland, Sr.
2 CONC , buried in Fen Stanton, Huntingtonshire, England, on 19 May 1635, and of his wife whose first name was almost certainly Ann." The name Ann is incorrect. Only one historical document only identifies her name as Margaret, not Ann.
2 CONT
2 CONT * "English Research." ''The Howland Quarterly'', vol. 28, nos. 2 and 3, p. 6 (Jan – Apr 1964). [https://www.familysearch.org/library/books/idviewer/167388/6 FamilySearch image]. (This article disentangles the Ely and Fenstanton Ho
2 CONC wlands.) Text: "Henry Howland, of the Parish of Fenny Stanton, Huntingdonshire, yeoman, died at Fen Stanton, 17 May 1635. His wife, Margaret, buried at Fen Stanton, 31 July 1629."
2 CONT
2 CONT * "Fen Stanton Records" by Sir Anthony Wagner. ''The Howland Quarterly'', vol. 28, nos. 2 and 3, p. 7 (Jan & Apr 1964). [https://www.familysearch.org/library/books/idviewer/167388/7 FamilySearch image]. (Sir Anthony was Garter Pri
2 CONC ncipal King of Arms and was hired to examine the above mentioned Ely and Fen Stanton records in 1963.)
2 CONT
2 CONT * Fitch, Marc, editor. ''Index to Testamentary Records in the Archdeaconry Court of London: Now Preserved in Guildhall Library, London''. Vol. 1, British Record Society, 1979, p. 66. [https://archive.org/details/indextotestament00
2 CONC 01chur/page/66/mode/1up Internet Archive Book]. Text: ''CALVERT, Colvert, Chris, cit & mcht tailor, St Mary Som., adm/nuncup W to Humphrey HOWLAND, f of Nath & Ann H. grdchn ex da. dur. their min. Year 1640 Register 8 Folio 350v. 
2 CONC <O.W.> [Register 7 Folio 59v].''
2 CONT
2 CONT * ''The Registers of St. Mary le Bowe, Cheapside, All Hallows, Honey Lane, and of St. Pancras, Soper Lane, London''. London, 1914, p. 294. [https://archive.org/details/registersofstmar44stma/page/294/mode/1up Internet Archive Book
2 CONC ]. Text: ''1603 Sep. 13 Elizabeth Coluert wife of Christopher Coluert, buried in the church neere to the font— Shee and Gillet in one graue''. There is an abbreviation of "P" in the margin next to the burial entry, indicating that
2 CONC  they died of the plague.
