2 CONT ===LACHLAN MACLEAN OF DUART===
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Genealogy===
2 CONT
2 CONT # [https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/mac/maclean02.php#linklo Stirnet: MacLean 02]
2 CONT # [https://www.stirnet.com/genie/data/british/mac/macleod06.php Stirnet: MacLeod 06] 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Lachlan Mòr Maclean
2 CONT 14th Chief
2 CONT
2 CONT This article needs additional research, citations, and verification. Please help improve this article by adding citations and references. Below is the material that has been gathered, unsourced material may be challenged and remov
2 CONC ed.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Fourteenth Chief
2 CONT     Born 1558
2 CONT     the "Great Maclean"
2 CONT     Lachlan's chiefship marks a turning point in the perspective of what a chief should be. He shifted away from groverning through harshly exercising the authority of chiefship to demonstrating the leaderhip of the chiefship.
2 CONT     In 1588 a Spanish galleon, part of the dispersed Armada, sought shelter in Tobermory Bay . Lachlan Mor, the 12 th chief, was quick to enlist the soldiers aboard for an expeditionary force against the MacDonalds, in return for 
2 CONC provisions. The clans were feuding over control of the sea routes to Ulster , with support for the Macleans coming from Elizabeth I of England . As security, officers from the ship were held hostage in Duart Castle , while some Ma
2 CONC cleans were held aboard the ship, including Maclean of Morven's son and heir, Donald. All those on board the ship died when it mysteriously blew up. Blame was laid at the door of the English or the MacDonalds, but legend speaks of
2 CONC  Donald MacLean carrying out the orders of Lachlan Mor.
2 CONT     Sir Lachlan Mor Maclean harried the Macdonalds of Islay, causing such carnage that, in 1594, he and the Macdonald chief were declared outlaws by the Privy Council. Lachlan redeemed himself when he fought for the king at the Ba
2 CONC ttle of Glenlivet in 1594.
2 CONT     "On advancing to Aberdeen, Argyle ordered Red Lion, the herald, to proclaim the royal commission by sound of trumpet in the market-place, and appointed Sir Lauchlan Maclean of Duart to the chief command under himself."1
2 CONT     "Errol was severely wounded with a bullet in the arm, and by one of the sharp-barbed arrows of the Highland bowmen, which pierced deep into the thigh. He lost his pennon, or guidon, also ; which was won by Maclean."1
2 CONT     "Seeing the day lost, Maclean, who had done most, and suffered least in this cruel fight, withdrew his men from the wood, and retired in good order but seven hundred Highlanders were slain in the chase, which was continued til
2 CONC l the steepness of the mountains rendered further pursuit impossible."1
2 CONT     "the superior armour and discipline of the Lowland knights proved too strong for the ferocious but irregular efforts of a much larger force of Highlanders."1
2 CONT     Lachlan Mor is also remembered for murdering eighteen of the guests at his widowed mothers second wedding, he then imprisoned the groom and made him suffer 'dailie tortour and panis'.
2 CONT     When King James VI learned of his dealings with Queen Elizabeth the punishment was sequestration of Duart to the King's Commissioners in 1604.
2 CONT     He was killed fighting in the Battle of Traigh Ghruinneart on the Island of Islay in 1598, whereupon his sons took revenge in the form of a massacre which is said to have lasted for 3 days.
2 CONT     Dubh Sith which translates as Black Elf killed Sir Lachlan Mor.
2 CONT     Died 05 August 1598
2 CONT
2 CONT 1Tytler, Patrick Fraser. History of Scotland. Vol. IX. Edinburgh: Tait, 1828. N. p146-150. Print.
2 CONT
