1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernhard_von_der_Borch ( Bernd von der Borch (auch Bernhard von der Borch; auch von der Borg) ( 15. Jahrhundert; † 15. Jahrhundert) war von 1471 bis 1483 Landmeister des Deutschen Ordens in Livland. Er
2 CONC  kam durch einen Putsch gegen seinen Vorgänger an die Macht. Er versuchte die weltliche Macht des Erzbischofs von Riga zu begrenzen. Der Konflikt mit dem Erzbischof Silvester Stodewescher dauerte Jahre an. Von der Borch setzte sic
2 CONC h zunächst durch und wurde von Kaiser Friedrich III. auch mit Stadt und Erzstift Riga belehnt. Im Kampf gegen die Russen erlitt er eine schwere Niederlage und musste 1481 einen langen Waffenstillstand akzeptieren. Nach der Einsetz
2 CONC ung eines neuen Erzbischofs durch den Papst kam es zum Aufstand gegen ihn. Er musste 1483 abdanken.)
2 CONT
2 CONT English:
2 CONT ..."Bernd von der Borch (also Bernhard von der Borch ; also from the Borg) (* 15. century; 15th century; 15th century from 1471 to 1483 was the landmaster of the Teutonic Order in Livonia. He came to power through a coup against h
2 CONC is predecessor. He tried to limit the secular power of the Archbishop of Riga. The conflict with Archbishop Silvester Stodevescher continued for years. Von der Borch initially prevailed and was also enough by Emperor Frederick III
2 CONC  with the city and Archbishopric of Riga. He suffered a heavy defeat in the fight against the Russians and had to accept a long ceasefire in 1481. After the appointment of a new archbishop by the Pope, there was an uprising agains
2 CONC t him. He had to abdicate in 1483.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT He came from the family from Borch, who comes from Westphalia. The Bishop of Reval Simon of Borch was a relative. In most cases, however, he is usually referred to as the uncle as Bernd's cousin. Simon later worked for Bernd as a 
2 CONC mediator and travelled for him to Pope and Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Put against the predecessor
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernd von der Borch was possibly Komtur in Marienburg.[2] In 1471, he appears as a marshal in connection with the revolt of members against Landmeister Johann Wolthus von Herse, who was accused of mismanagement, among other things
2 CONC . In fact, the revolt was probably directed in particular against the plans of an internal transformation of the Order in Livonia. He was also accused of being unwilling to act offensively against the Muscovites and the Russians o
2 CONC f Pskov. Herse was deposed and instead Bernd was elected by the Borch as land champion. Immediately afterwards, the brother of his predecessor, Ernst Wolthuß, began to reverse the decision in vain. The reforms of his predecessor i
2 CONC mmediately removed from the Borch.[3]
2 CONT Conflict around Riga City and Stift
2 CONT
2 CONT Very difficult was the conflict between the Order and the City and Riga Abbey. After a compromise of 1453, the city of Riga was under the lawsuit to the archbishop and the country master. Archbishop Silvester Stodewescher now trie
2 CONC d to expand his position. There was a dispute with the Borch, which was only ended by the mediation of the knighthood by a six-year decency. The Archbishop used the time to represent his cause to the Pope and to win allies in the 
2 CONC region. There were support from Poland and Sweden, while the remaining Bishop Livonia remained neutral. An alliance existed with the Archbishop of Uppsala and with other Swedish bishops. In Poland, Stodewescher began to recruit tr
2 CONC oops. The Pope also stood on his side and confirmed the sovereign rights over Riga. The Borch was banned by the Archbishop.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the state parliament of Valmiera in 1476, Bernd von der Borch announced that he wanted to take action against the archbishop by military means. He sent Simon from Borch to Emperor Frederick III to ask him to give him the regali
2 CONC a for Riga. On the state parliament, there was another compromise for ten years under the mediation of the knighthood. However, both sides continued to equip.
2 CONT
2 CONT The situation became even more difficult for the country champion because he received news of Russian armour. He once again sent Simon from the Borch to Rome, where he succeeded in persevering the Pope in favour of the Landmeister
2 CONC . Another attempt to settle the thing on the beeland parliament of Walk failed. Simon of the Borch came back in 1477 with the papal order that both parties to the dispute should bow to the judgment of Cardinal Stephan. While the c
2 CONC ountry master appeared before the Cardinal, Archbishop Silvester abstained. In Riga there were unrest against the Archbishop because he had banned the city because of their support of the Landmeister. After complaints, the Pope in
2 CONC jected the Archbishop to repeal the ban. To support New Year's Eve, two hundred Swedes landed, but were caught by the Borchs and sent back to Sweden on the orders.
2 CONT Fixation of power and defeat
2 CONT
2 CONT The Russians conquered Novgorod in 1478 and destroyed the Peterhof, among other things. They then marched into the bisolary of Dorpat and devastated the area. Landmaster of the Borch moved the Russians to meet the order. The intru
2 CONC ders then withdrew. The threat from outside meant that the internal dispute in Livonia was posted and a general national contingent was decided. The country champion took the opportunity and occupied the archbishop's castles. New 
2 CONC Year's Eve was captured and the stalls of Riga Abbey were worthy of the country master. Against former opponents, the Borch felt harsh.
2 CONT
2 CONT The archbishop died in custody. The country champion put pressure on the canons, some of whom were captured, so that they chose Simon from the Borch as the new archbishop. Bernd von der Borch intended a fundamental change. The new
2 CONC  archbishop should no longer be secular sovereign and should only carry out spiritual tasks. However, the Pope did not confirm this election, but made Stephan Grube the archbishop.[4] The Landmeister contacted the Swedes other tha
2 CONC n the danger from Russia. The request for support from the Teutonic Order in the actual state was rejected by the Grand Master.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the winter of 1480, Bernd von der Borch undertook a futile campaign against Pskov to strengthen his position. Then the Russian-Livland war began, in which the army suffered a heavy defeat against the troops of Ivan III. On the 
2 CONC 1st A peace was concluded in September 1481.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernd von der Borch turned to Frederick III again in 1481 to be voiced with the Archbishopric of Riga. This time the emperor followed the request, because a unified defense against the Russians seemed necessary.[6]
2 CONT Resistance from Riga
2 CONT
2 CONT Because there were again conflicts over Riga and preceded by the Borch against opposing clergy, he and Simon was banned by the Pope from the Borch and Stephan Grube was declared the sole over Riga. At first, this had no effect and
2 CONC  the city and knighthood of Riga supported by the Borch in a campaign against the invading Russians from Pskov. Von der Borch acted defensively, which cost him the trust of the Rigar residents. They now recognized pit as overlord.
2 CONC  Simon of the Borch, who still understood himself as a legitimate archbishop, expressed a spell in vain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernd von der Borch responded by force. During a bombardment of the town, the tower of the Jacobean Church was destroyed. The citizens of Riga, who did not trust in the still lack of pit, concluded a two-year ceasefire with the Bo
2 CONC rch in March 1483. When mine arrived in Riga, the city and knights passed to his side. The opponents of the Borchs went on the offensive. Even the Knights of the Order could no longer rely on the Borch, so that many castles and to
2 CONC wns fell to the opponents. Against this background, the master of the Livonian branch of the Livonian branch coined in Wenden. On the 14th November 1483 von Borch offered his resignation. In his place, Johann Freitag was elected l
2 CONC andlord . Nothing is known about the further life of the Borchs."...
2 CONT
2 CONT https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernd_von_der_Borch
