2 CONT
2 CONT == The Macdowall Family in Sweden ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''lbrecht MacDougall of Mackerston, born in Scotland 1541, came to Sweden 1594, became collector of taxes to Queen Christina 1626, died 1646. His son Mauritz became colonel and was naturalized by the name of Duvall 1638, and his 
2 CONC son James fell as lieutenant at Czestochova in Poland 1705. James, the second son of Albrecht MacDougall, born 1589, entered the Swedish service 1607, was in 1630 colonel of two regiments of German foot-soldiers, then general of a
2 CONC  Swedish army in Germany and was shot at Grossglogau 1634. The title of baron promised to him by Gustavus Adolphus was afterwards bestoved [sic] upon his two sons: Jacob, born 1625, lieutenant-colonel and afterwards governor of Ös
2 CONC terbotten 1669 (whose son Jacob served under Carl XII as major in the Wiborg, and afterwards as lieutenant-colonel in the Åbo cavalry), and Gustaf, born 1630, lord of the bedchamber to Queen Christine and Karl X, minister in Denma
2 CONC rk 1656, speaker of the nobility at the diet at Upsala 1675. He was sent in 1658 to England to offer condolence upon the death of Cromwell. His son Axel, who took again the name of MacDougall, born 1667, was body-guard to Karl XII
2 CONC , was present at the battle of Bender, and became major-general 1722. Numerous members of the family have since been officers, some in the Swedish, others in the Finnish armies. John Didrik Duvall, born 1723, was speaker for the n
2 CONC obility at the diet of 1786, and was made lieutenant general the same year. The family is still extant in Sweden. 
2 CONT
2 CONT genus forum: 
2 CONT http://suku.genealogia.fi/archive/index.php/t-11807.html. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Makerstoun Description of Makerston] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT "Makerstoun was a rural parish on the North border of Roxburghshire, whose church stands 5 miles SW of Kelso. The name is supposed to have been derived from the original proprietor, Machar, or Machir. . . . In the 1840s, nearly al
2 CONC l the land was the property of Sir Thomas and Lady Makdougal Brisbane; the remainder belongs to the Duke of Roxburghe. The MakDougall residence, today known as "Makerstoun House," is a square three-storied building, situated on th
2 CONC e N bank high on a hill overlooking the river Tweed, and standing in grounds that are extensive and well-wooded. The park contains about 100 acres." [http://haygenealogy.com/hay/scotland/makerstoun.html Makerstoun, Roxburghshire, 
2 CONC Scotland]
2 CONT
