0 @N6000000000337361682@ NOTE Source #1:  George E. McCracken, "Nathaniel Foote's English Relatives" in "The American Genealogist" (TAG) Whole Number 212, Vol. 53, No. 4, October, 1977, pp. 193-206.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #2:  Janice Greene Valore, "More on Nathaniel Foote's Ancestors" in "The American Genealogist" (TAG) Whole Number 214, Vol. 54, No. 2, April, 1978, pp. 99-101.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #3:  Janice Greene Valore, Myrtle Stevens Hyde & Glade Ian Nelson, "The Brookes of London:  Maternal Ancestors of Nathaniel and Joshua Foote"  in "The American Genealogist" (TAG) Whole Number 220, Vol. 55, No. 4, October, 1
1 CONC 979, pp. 193-206.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #4:   Myrtle Stevens Hyde, "Ancestors of Nathaniel Foote:  Warren Line" in "The American Genealogist" (TAG) Whole Number 231, Vol. 58, No. 3, July, 1982, pp. 165-167.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #5:  Goodwin, Nathaniel. The Foote Family. Case, Tiffany and Company. Harftord. 1849. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Nathaniel Foote. Born on 21 Sep 1592 in Colchester, Essex, Eng. Nathaniel died in Wethersfield, CT on 20 Nov 1644; he was 52.
1 CONT
1 CONT "This most reputable Conn. family, commenced its American history in Weth., yet, owing to an apparently inconquerable migratory tendency in its earlier representatives, the name had entirely disappeared form the town by the end of
1 CONC  the third generation. Through its various early intermarriages with other Weth. families, however, the history of these early generations possess Colchester, Conn., and Hadley, Hatfield, Deerfield and other old towns in Western M
1 CONC ass. The family has also been fortunate in having had its history wirtten by a competent had, half a century ago."6 [The Foote Family, by Nathaniel Goodwin, 1849.] 
1 CONT
1 CONT "A conspicuous feature in the history of the first generations of the Foote family, is the deaths, sufferings and captivities of its members, and of those connected with them by marriage, at the hands of the Indians."6
1 CONT
1 CONT "It is by no means certain that Mr. Foote, as some have asserted, was the first settler at Weth., but it is probably true that he was one of the first ten men, known as 'adventurers,' who absolutely first settled here; and that he
1 CONC  was the largest holder of so-called 'Adventurer's lands.' In the original lay-out of the town, 1640, he received a home-lot of ten acres, at South End of Broad St., East side, and gradually became the owner of other pieces of ld.
1 CONC , partly in the Great Meadow, east of his home-lot, amounting in all to over 400 acres. ... In 1641-2-4, he represented the town in the General Court, an evidence of the respect and confidence in which he seems to have been held b
1 CONC y his fellow-townsmen. In May 1637, when the little army under Capt. John Mason was being provisioned for the memorable Pequot campaign, it was 'ordered yt that there shalbe 1 hogg prvided att Wythersfeild for the design in hand, 
1 CONC weh. [i.e. the hogg, not the expedition] is conceived to be Nathaniell Footes'-a compliment, certainly form the Col. authorities, to Mr. Foote's ability in raising good pork!"6
1 CONT
1 CONT When Nathaniel was 15 he was an apprentice to Samuel Croyle, grocer of Colchester. He arrived at Watertown, MA, in 1630, a freeman there in 1634. He removed to Wethersfield, CT, in 1636.1
1 CONT
1 CONT Nathaniel brought from England his wife Elizabeth and children Nathaniel, Elizabeth, Mary ,Robert, Frances, and Sarah; had Rebecca, born probably at Watertown. Freeman 3 Sep 1634, then removed to Wethersfield 1636, where he was re
1 CONC p. 1641-44.7
1 CONT
1 CONT Check these references in TAG for Nathaniel's ancestry: 53:193-206, 54:99-101, 55:193-206, and 58:165-167.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Jan 1615 Nathaniel married Elizabeth Deming, daughter of Jonathan Deming (ca 1574-) & Elizabeth Gilbert (ca 1578-), in Colchester, Essex, Eng. Born in Oct 1595 in Shalford, Essex, England. Elizabeth died on 28 Jul 1683 in Wethe
1 CONC rsfield, CT.6
1 CONT
1 CONT That Elizabeth "was a woman of character and a good wife, is evidenced by the fact that her first husband (Foote) dying intestate, she was by the Particular Court to whom the inventory of hsi estate was presented, 'admitted to adm
1 CONC inister the estate;' and by the will of her second husband (Welles) 'she was to enjoy and improve' his whole estate, so long as she remained a widow, ... 'that she may keep the better hospitality.'"6
1 CONT
1 CONT They had the following children:
1 CONT  54 i. Elizabeth (1617-1700) 
1 CONT  55 ii. Nathaniel (ca1620-1655) 
1 CONT  56 iii. Mary (1623-1721) 
1 CONT  57 iv. Robert (1627-1681) 
1 CONT  58 v. Frances (1629-ca1681) 
1 CONT  59 vi. Sarah (1632-1673) 
1 CONT  60 vii. Rebecca (ca1634-1701) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #6:  Sherman W. Adams and Henry R. Stiles, "The History of Ancient Wethersfield, Connecticut, Volume II:  Genealogies and Biographies" (Camden, Maine:  Picton Press, 1996 reprint of 1904 edition), pp. 219-220.
0 @N6000000001036898579@ NOTE http://www.footefamily.org/nathno1a.htm
1 CONT
1 CONT (10) Nathaniel Foote The Settler
1 CONT Son of Robert Foote Of Shalford  03:24:04 PM July 29, 2003
1 CONT (Third Direct Ancestor In England)
1 CONT (First Direct Ancestor In America)
1 CONT
1 CONT Birth Date: September 21, 1592.
1 CONT Birth Place: Shalford,Colchester, Essex, Eng,
1 CONT Christening: Unknown.
1 CONT Death: Wethersfield, Hartford, Connecticut
1 CONT Burial: November 20, 1644 In Wethersfield, Hartford, Connecticut
1 CONT Children: Seven Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Generation: First Generation In America
1 CONT
1 CONT Father: Robert Foote of Shalford
1 CONT
1 CONT Mother: Joan Brooke
1 CONT
1 CONT Our No. 1 Ancestor in America, was born September 21, 1592 in Shalford Colchester Esses, England. He was the son of Robert Foote of Shalford, County of Essex, A Yoeman and grandson of John Foote of Royston. Royston is located near
1 CONC  Colchester England. Nathaniel s mother's name was Joan (Brooks). She was the daughter of John and Elizabeth Brooks of London. He resided in Shalford Parish, county Essex and St Mary Bothaw Parish, London.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage: A Short time after he finished his apprenticeship training, he was married to Elizabeth Deming (In January of the year 1616) in Colchester, Essex, England. She was the sister of John Deeming, who was one of the first set
1 CONC tlers of Wethersfield Conn. in the year of 1615. John Deeming was for many years the one of the magistrates of the "Colony of the Connecticut" and one of the Patenees named in it's charter.
1 CONT
1 CONT Children of Nathaniel and Elizabeth Foote
1 CONT No. Ancestor's Name  Parent  Birth Death Wife
1 CONT (22) Elizabeth Foote  (10)  1616 1700 Josiah Churchill
1 CONT (23) Nathaniel Foote  (10)  1618 1655 Elizabeth Smith
1 CONT (24) Mary Foote  (10)  1623 1687 John Stoddard
1 CONT (25) Robert Foote  (10)  1627 1681 Sarah Potter
1 CONT (26) Frances Foote  (10)  1629 1673 John Ickinson
1 CONT (27) Sara Foote (10)  1632 1672 ---
1 CONT (28) Rebecca Foote  (10)  1634 1701 Phillip Smith
1 CONT
1 CONT Nathaniel's Apprenticeship
1 CONT Nathaniel Foote The Settler resided in Shalford Parish, county Essex and St Mary Bothaw Parish, London. at the age of sixteen was apprenticed on 21 September, 1608 to Samuel Croyle of Colchester, a "Grocer" and "Free Burgess", for
1 CONC  a period of 8 years or until his 24th birthday in the year 1616. when he brought a message from Beatrice Barker, Esq., located on East Street, St James Parish, Colchester. He was a grocer himself as of 18 Oct 1619.
1 CONT
1 CONT A short time after he finished his apprenticeship training, he was married to Elizabeth Deeming (In January of the year 1616) in Colchester, Essex, England. She was the sister of John Deeming, who was one of the first settlers of 
1 CONC Wethersfield Conn. in the year of 1615. John Deeming was for many years the one of the magistrates of the "Colony of the Connecticut" and one of the Patenees named in it's charter.
1 CONT The Movement to America
1 CONT Sometime before the year 1633 the family moved from London England to Boston, Massachusetts. He settled first, upon arrival in the New World, at Watertown, Mass.; where he took the freeman's oath 3 Sep 1633. According to the recor
1 CONC ds of "The Original Distribution of Lands Around Wethersfield" recorded in 1640, a short time after arriving in Wethersfield in 1635, Nathaniel received a ten acre house lot on the east side of Broad Street. This land was near the
1 CONC  south end of the street. This land was purchased directly from the Wongunk Indians at a place located along the Connecticut River that was called Pyguaq, later renamed Wethersfield. Additionally, he became the owner of several ot
1 CONC her tracts laying in part in the great meadow east of his house and containing close to 400 acres of land. In 1641, he was appointed a delegate to the General Court. He was a juror in 1643 and 1644.
1 CONT
1 CONT Note: Part of his land is now a public park at the foot of Broad Street.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources:
1 CONT "NEHGR", Vol. IX, 1855, p.272, "Pedigree of Foote", compiled from Goodwin's Genealogy of the Foote Family.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Foote Family, comprising the Genealogy and History of Nathaniel Foote of Wethersfield, Conn.", Vol. I, Abram W. Foote, 1907.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Footenotes", newsletter of The Foote Family Association of North America, Vol. XI, p.9, October, 1992.
1 CONT
1 CONT "New England Families", Vol. I, pp.279-281, William Richard Cutter, Woburn, MA., 1913.
1 CONT
1 CONT World Family Tree, Vol. 2, chart 861.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nathaniel's Profession
1 CONT Even though Nathaniel's main profession in England was that of a Crochet, he became a farmer. He was also active in the public trusts of the town. He was appointed as a delegate to the "General Court" in the year 1644.
1 CONT Nathaniel Foote was the first cousin of Sir Thomas Foote, Sheriff of London in 1649 and Lord Mayor of London in 1650. Sir Thomas Foote was the son of Robert Foote of Royston and the brother of Robert Foote of Shalford, who was Nat
1 CONC haniel s Father.
1 CONT Records show that Nathaniel Foote died in Wethersfield, Mass in November, 1644 at the age of 51. He was buried in the burying ground in the rear of the town meeting house, where nine generations are buried. Nathaniel was survived 
1 CONC by his wife, two sons, and five daughters.
1 CONT Nathaniel's Will
1 CONT
