1 CONC th of allegiance with others in 1644.In 1645, Brother Potter (William) made an offter, "to carry every man's grist from their howes to the mill & bring it back againe to their howes for 2d pr. bushell."His names appears in the sea
1 CONC ting of the meeting house in 1646, and in 1647 he was one of the Fence viewers for the farms this side of the East River.In Oct. 5, 1647, it is recorded, that Mr. Evance hath sold to Wm. Potter 27 and a half acres of meadow as it 
1 CONC cometh to him in the towne books, and lyeth in the east meadows between M. Cryane and Br. Punderson and 32 acres of upland. He was one of the appraisers on the estate of Richard Mansfield in 1656. His home lott was on the est side
1 CONC  of the Quinipiac River, near the present Cedar Hill railroad station.
1 CONT "William Potter disposes of his eastate of outward things as followeth. After all Debts dischaged my will is that my wife should have her living out of the farm till my Sonne Nathanll, come to ye age of 21 years, then he sd. Natha
1 CONC niell is to possess ye sd. Farms and all yt is upon it, if my wife continue a widdow my will is yt my Sonne Nathanll allow her a confomrtable Living out of the same and if shee see cause to Dwell elsewhere my will is yt my sonne N
1 CONC athaniell allow her 12th a year.It; my will is yt my sonne Joseph should have 30th, payed him within yt term of six years after ye date hereof.It; my will is yt my daughter Hope and my daughter Rebeckah shall have 20th apiece payd
1 CONC  ym them their mother sees good to pay it them. My will is that those Legasyes be payd out of the farms before it come into my sonnes Nathanll's hands. 19. 3. 62.
