0 @N6000000003075958209@ NOTE [Brøderbund WFT European Origins Vol. E1, Ed. 1, Tree #0003, Date of Import: Dec 25, 1999]
1 CONT
1 CONT Tristram came from England in 1642, to Salisbury and Haverhill.  Then to Newbury about 1648 and beck to Salisbury 1654/55.  A founder of Nantucket 1660, chief magistrate there 1671, comissioner 1655.  Source: Savage Gen Dic.
1 CONT
1 CONT see NEH & GR vol 2 p 236 for more info
1 CONT From Coffin Family p. 34 and 47:  Tristram lift England in 1642 (beginning of Civil War), at age 37 with his wife, 5 small children, a widowed mother and unmarried sisters.  Ships arriving that year from England were Hector, Griff
1 CONC in, Job Clement and Margaret Clement - owned by Robert Clement, who also emigrated the same year.  He went first to Salisbury, then to Haverhill, to Newberry and finally to Natucket.  In 1659, Tristram, representing a company, wen
1 CONC t to explore the possibilities of settling Martha's Vineyard or Nantucket, taking with him an Indian Guide, named Peter Folger (later to become grandfather of Benjamin Franklin).  nantucket was purchased for 30 English pounds and 
1 CONC two beaver hats.  Tristram is considered to be the founder of Nantucket (1660), where he was chief magistrate 1671, commissioner 1665.  nantucket was first settled in 1659, under New Youk juisdiction and ceded to Massachusetts in 
1 CONC 1692.
0 @N6000000005584475096@ NOTE
1 CONT Early settlers in New England are frequently described as outsized men doing outsized things. For most of these, the description does not bear up well under scrutiny .  Tristram Coffin, however, was truly a man who set the shadow 
1 CONC of history, like a giant pine over the low scrub brush. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born in Western England in 1604.  Shakespeare was writing some of his best plays, Elizabeth the first had just died, and her Scottish cousin, James had assumed the throne. The ensuing years in England were as turbulent as a
1 CONC ny, probably pushing Coffin to leave his large family estate in Brixton and sail to America with his wife, their five kids, his mother and two of his sisters. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Once in Massachusetts, Coffin bounced around the settlements.  They started in Salisbury, then Haverhill, then Newbury, and finally back to Salisbury.  In Newbury, he was involved in a crime that exemplifies the problems Coffin ha
1 CONC d with authorities.  He and his wife, Dionis, were running a tavern and making beer.  The Puritans, being somewhat meddlesome , had decreed that you could only use four bushels of malt to make beer.  Coffin used six bushels and ch
1 CONC arged more for it.  While he got in trouble with the authorities, he did a brisk business. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Tristram Coffin was a very successful businessman for the time.  He ran a busy inn, a ferry service up in Newburyport and helped his son, Peter run a thriving sawmill up in Dover, New Hampshire. 
1 CONT
1 CONT So, when the Coffins came to the island, they came with a plan.  Tristram not only wanted to come to Nantucket in order to get away from the meddlesome Puritans, but also to consolidate his gains.  When the original 20 “full-share
1 CONC ” purchasers got the land, five of those twenty were either sons or sons-in-law of Tristram.  Many of the others lived off-island and Coffin controlled their votes.  As a result of this dominance and of the scarcity of wood on-isl
1 CONC and, the new town was able to install Peter Coffin as the on-island wood monopoly .  To a modern eye, this may seem silly considering the distance between Dover and Nantucket.  However, in the seventeenth century, boats traveled m
1 CONC uch faster than anything did on land .  Nantucket was far less of an island than it is now. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The original settlers put their roots down on land that starts at the base of the harbor (Wherfore Creek, near Island Home) to Waqutaquaib Pond (Sanford Farm) and points north.  The center of the island became Capaum Pond (or Capp
1 CONC amet Harbor).  The Harbor was protected by rolling hill, was relatively deep for boats, but had neither marshes nor beaches. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Coffin further strengthened his financial hand by building his house on the western side of the harbor.  All of the other houses ran in two rows; one towards Long Pond, one towards the harbor.  All roads led to his land.  Coffin’s
1 CONC  house was the largest in the village and frequently was the site of town meetings and trials.  He named the beach to the east of his house “Northam” after an area in England.  The beach to the west he named “Dionis” after his wif
1 CONC e. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The Coffins did well out here and reproduced prodigiously .  By Tristram’s death in 1681, he had seven children and sixty grandchildren.  By 1728, 1582 descendents had been born, of which 1128 lived in New England.  There were mor
1 CONC e Coffins alive than there were settlers on the island. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In order for the island to remain viable, they needed a moneymaker.  Sheep seemed to be the right answer.  Sheep were often raised on islands because there were relatively few predators and there weren’t too many tricky places for
1 CONC  the notoriously dumb animals to get lost.  Coffin got a deal with the Natives were they could graze over the entire island from October to May; away from the Natives growing season. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Born: 4 Mar 1608/9 Brixham Parish, Plymouth, Devonshire, England
1 CONT Died: 2 Oct 1681 Nantucket, MA
1 CONT
1 CONT Father: Peter Coffin (Coffyn)
1 CONT Mother: Joan Kember
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage: Dionis Stevens, b. 4 Mar 1609/10, dau of Robert Stevens Esq. and Dionis ____.
1 CONT Married: 1630, Brixton, Devonshire, England
1 CONT
1 CONT Note: This family came to MA from England in 1642, when Tristram was 37years old. He was a Judge. They were the parents of nine children. We are connected through 4 of them...James, Mary , John and Stephen. The male lines will be 
1 CONC carried on, and the female can be traced through her husbands line.
1 CONT
1 CONT Children:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1. Peter Coffin b. 1631 Eng. , d. 21 Mar 1715 at Exeter, NH. He m. ca 1656 Abigail Starbuck. He was a prosperous lumber merchant in NH, also a Judge. He was the family's weathiest member.
1 CONT
1 CONT 2. Tristram Coffin b. 1632 Eng., m. 2 Mar 1652 Judith Greenleaf, widow of Henry Somerby. Tristram was a successful hatter.
1 CONT
1 CONT 3. Elizabeth Coffin b. 1634 Eng. m. 13 Nov 1651 Capt. Stephen Greenleaf.
1 CONT
1 CONT 4. JAMES COFFIN b. 1640 Eng., d. 1720 Nantucket. (See next generation)
1 CONT
1 CONT 5. John Coffin b. Eng., died in infancy.
1 CONT
1 CONT 6. Deborah Coffin b. 15 Nov 1642 Haverhill. MA, d. 8 Dec 1642 there.
1 CONT
1 CONT 7. MARY COFFIN b. 20 Feb 1645 Haverhill, MA, m. 1662 to Nathaniel Starbuck. He b. 1635 and d. 6d 6m 1719. (My 9th g. grandparents, see next generation.)
1 CONT
1 CONT 8. JOHN COFFIN b. 1647, d. 1711 (See next generation)
1 CONT
1 CONT 9. STEPHEN COFFIN b. 1652 d 1734 (see next generation.)
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Note: Tritram Coffin (1610-1681) of Salisbury, Massachusetts. English home: Baptized 11 Mar. 1610, Brixton, Devon (25 miles south of Exeter), on the English Channel, son of Peter Tristram (will, 21 Dec. 1627, proved 13 Mar. 1627/8
1 CONC ) and Joanna Kember. His father had a tenement called "Silferhey" in Butlers (now Butlass) in Brixton parish. Tristram 's grandparents were Nicholas Coffin of Butlers, Brixton (will, 12 Sept. 1613, proved, 3 Nov. 1613) and Johan, 
1 CONC who had land in Plympton Erle, Joanna Kember (b.a. 1584) was daughter of Robert Kember of Brixton, Devon. His will proved, 23 Jan. 1612/13. Dionis Stevens, Baptized 4 Mar. 1609/10, Brixton was daughter of Robert Stevens, whose wil
1 CONC l was proved 4 Feb. 1627/8. He was son of John Stevens of Brixton whose will was proved, 15 Dec. 1611.
1 CONT
1 CONT Tristram came in 1642 to Haverhill, Massachusetts. With him were his wife, five children, his mother (age 58), and two unmarried sisters, Eunice and Mary. His wife was pregnant on the voyage and she gave birth on 15 Nov. 1642 to a
1 CONC  daughter who died a few weeks later.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT THE COFFIN LINE
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Several theories are advanced regarding the origin of the family surname of Coffin, or Coffyn as was the earlier spelling. Allen Coffin, Esq., says..... Coffin is a word of Hebrew origin, signifying a small basket. Whether the Isr
1 CONC aelitish hosts were sufficiently enlightened to be in the enjoyment of baskets before the Eqyptians, or whether the chosen people of God were especially favored with knowledge of basketmaking while the rest of the world plodded on
1 CONC  with less commodious means of transit, are matters which cannot at this remote period of time be satisfactorily answered.
1 CONT
1 CONT If any one man may be considered the patriarch of Nantucket, to whom more than to any other person, the descendants of the old Nantucket families may trace a common origin, that man is Tristram Coffin.
1 CONT
1 CONT Allen Coffin Esq. in his "Life of Tristram Coffyn" traces the family origin in England back to the days of Norman Conquest, the ancient seat of the name of England, now called Portledge, in the Parish of Ilwington, near Bideford, 
1 CONC County of Devon, havng been granted to Sir Richard Coffyn, Knight, for valuable services rendered to William the Conqueror. Peter Coffyn, father of Tristram was doubtless born aout 1580 and died in 1627/8. He married Joa Kember (T
1 CONC hember) and had two sons and four daughters. Tristram was the elder son, his brother John died in Plymouth Fort, England, after having been mortally wounded. Tristram was born at Brixton, in 1605, and married Dionis Stevens, daugh
1 CONC ter of Robert Stevens, Esq. of Brixton, about 1630. They came to America about 12 years later. Their first settlement was at Salisbury, and they subsequently removed to Haverhill. His name appears as a witness to an Indian deed in
1 CONC  Pentucket (Haverhill) in Nov 1642. He removed to Newbury in 1648/9 and thence to Salisbury in 1654/5. In 1644, he was licensed to keep an ordinary, sell wine and keep a ferry on the Newbury side of Carr's Island. In 1653, his wif
1 CONC e was presented to the General Court, charged with selling beer at three pence per quart, but as she proved by witness, that she made it much stronger and gave quid pro quo, she was discharged. (The law required four bushels of ma
1 CONC lt to the hogshead at a price not to exceed two-pence per ale quart. She proved that she used six bushels.)
1 CONT
1 CONT Tristram returned to Salisbry and there made his plans to be one of the purchasers of Nantucket Island. He died in Oct 1681. In other reports, he has been referred to as the famed Judge of Newbury and Nantucket
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Nicholas Coffyn, the grandfather of Tristram, was probably bora about the middle of the sixteenth century, in the reign of Edward VI. (1550). He lived to the end of the reign of the Tudors, and saw the reign of the Stuarts commenc
1 CONC ed in the person of James VI. of Scotland and I. of England. He died in the reign of James I. (1613). His eldest son, Peter, doubtless succeeded to his estates; and his youngest son, John, acquired some estate, as he made our Tris
1 CONC tram his executor. The other sons, Nicholas and Tristram, have not been accounted for, neither has his daughter Anne.
1 CONT
1 CONT Peter Coffyn, the father of Tristram, must have been born during the reign of Queen Elizabeth, about the year 1580. He died about the close of the year 1627 or early in the year 1628. At present we know of but little more of Trist
1 CONC ram's father than we do of his grandfather, save that he married Joan Thember (or Thumber) and had two sous and four daughters, of whom Tristram was the eldest, the other son, John, having died in Plymouth Fort, England, after rec
1 CONC eiving a mortal wound.
1 CONT
1 CONT Tristram Coffyn was born at Brixton, near Plymouth, county of Devon, England, during the reign of James I., in the year 1605, as previously stated. He married Dionis Stevens, daughter of Robert Stevens, Esq., of Brixton, about the
1 CONC  year 1630. The particular causes or circumstances which induced his emigration to America have been a subject of profound study and mature deliberation. If we look at the contemporaneous history of England we shall find that the 
1 CONC time which covers Tristram's mature life in England, about fifteen years, marks a most eventful period—the moment when intellectual freedom was claimed unconditionally by Englishmen as an inalienable right, and when ecclesiastical
1 CONC  forms were not spared by the revolutions of the time.
1 CONT
1 CONT James I., whose reign had been adorned by Shakespeare and Bacon, died in 1625, when Tristram was 20 years old. Charles I. had been upon the Throne but two years when Tristram's father died. The Petition of Right, in 1628, sought t
1 CONC o limit the powers of the Crown, and the King soon after abolished the Parliament and established the Star Chamber. Puritanism was making rapid strides, and large numbers of Puritans were leaving England. So great was the exodus t
1 CONC hat the King prohibited their departure, and Hampden, Pym, and Cromwell were prevented from leaving. About this time the Duke of Buckingham was assassinated. In 1638 the Scotts, to maintain their ecclesiastical rights, took up arm
1 CONC s against the King, having framed the celebrated Solemn League and Covenant, and sustained the Parliament in its opposition to Charles. The Earl of Strafford and the Archbishop of Canterbury, as chief advisers of the King, were im
1 CONC peached and beheaded (the former in 1611, and the latter in 1641). The Presbyterians, who were now a majority in the Commons, procured the exclusion of the Bishops from the House of Lords, in 1611, which was followed by an act, in
1 CONC  1613, entirely abolishing the Episcopacy, so that Charles began to realize that without Bishops there would be no King. Under these circumstances the Long Parliament convened.
1 CONT
1 CONT The irrepressible conflict between Charles I. and the Parliament came to a crisis in 1612, and in August of that year the royal standard was raised at Nottingham. The King was generally supported by the nobility, the landed gentry
1 CONC , the High Church party, and the Catholics; and the Parliament was sustained by the mercantile and middle classes and the lower order of the great towns.
1 CONT
1 CONT Tristram Cofiyn was of the landed gentry. Most probably he was a Churchman after the order of Elizabeth's time. Conformably to his father's will he was to be provided for " according to his degree and calling." He must, therefore,
1 CONC  have had a calling—a profession. He may have taken holy orders or practiced at Exeter nisi prius, (although there appears but little in his life at Nantncket to warrant the belief that he ever pretended to anything more than plai
1 CONC n Mister Tristram Coftyn). Yet it docs appear that the very year of the appeal to arms, 1612, after the conflict had been waged, Tristram Cofl'yn, at the age of 37, left all of his comfortable estates in Old England and embarked f
1 CONC or America, bringing with him his wife and five small children, his mother, then aged 58 years', and two unmarried sisters, and none of them ever returned.
1 CONT
1 CONT It will be asked How could Tristram CofFyn, a royalist, leave Brixton, at a time when Plymouth and the whole southern coast was coib- trolled by Parliament forces ? And, if there had not been a pacific feeling among neighbors and 
1 CONC an interruption of hostilities in Devon at this time, it could not have happened. But Lingard, in his History of England (v. 10, p. 120), states that there were four counties, " those of York, Chester, Devon, and Cornwall, in whic
1 CONC h the leaders had already learned to abhor the evils of civil dissention. They met on both sides and entered into engagements to suspend their political animosities, to aid each other in putting down the disturbers of the public p
1 CONC eace, and to oppose the introduction of any armed force, without the joint consent of both the King and the Parliament." This period of pacific intercourse which subsequently became associated in other counties, took place in Devo
1 CONC n, in 1642; and, under its operation, emigration to America offered .a proper solution of the problems that had perplexed the mature life of Tristram Coffyn in England. What to him were lands and tenements, rents and revenues,—und
1 CONC er the tyranny ol a King which in levying ship- money made all estates insecure, or under the sway of a Parliament which exacted a contribution of one-twentieth part of the e.state for the- support of an army, where human life was
1 CONC  always insecure, no matter which party succeeded, — to the unconditional liberty of body and soul which the wilderness of America offered. Saddened by the early decease of an only brother in a cause, which, if successful, offered
1 CONC  nothing but oppression; sickened of the continual revolution and anarchy which surrounded his early home, he quietly quitted his otherwise ample and comfortable estates in the Old World, and sought a home midst the hardy Pilgrim 
1 CONC settlers of the New. It was his utter want of faith in the institutions of England that sent him across the ocean with a wife and five small children, a widowed mother and two unmarried dependant sisters, to found a new home among
1 CONC  the barren hills of New England. While he could not bring his landed estates, he doubtless did not come penniless.
1 CONT
1 CONT Admiral Henry E. Coffin, R. N., in his correspondence with Mr. "William E. Coffin, of Richmond, Ind., states that Tristram Coffyn's estate at Brixton, upon the Restoration, was given by Charles II. to his bastard son who was calle
1 CONC d Batard, and that the property has been held in that family to this day. He also mentions the property at Butler's, as having recently been sold.
1 CONT
1 CONT Now, in what ship did Tristram Coffyn come to America? Of the Identity of the ship there is certainly much doubt. But it is generally conceded that he came in the ship with Robert Clement, who settled in Havcrhill, and that Tristr
1 CONC am first went to Salisbury. A descendant of Robert Clement, as I am informed by letter of Robert Coffin, of Hoboken, N. J., to WilliamE. Coffin, of Richmond, Ind., states that the ships which arrived in 1642, owned by Robert Cleme
1 CONC nt, in which he might have come, were the " Hector," " Griffin," " Job Clement," and " Margaret Clement." And so, if Robert Clement arrived in one of these ships, the same must be true of Tristram Coffyn.
1 CONT
1 CONT It appears that he did not affect a permanent settlement at Salisbury, tout removed the same year to the new settlement of Pentucket, soon afterward called Havcrhill. This settlement was commenced in 1640, Christopher Hussey being
1 CONC  among the first settlers, but no deed from the- Indians was obtained until 1642, when the name of Tristram Coffyn appears as one of the witnesses thereto. It was first recorded in the county records of Norfolk, (lib. 2, p. 209) ;
1 CONC  and, in 1832, the original deed was said to be in the possession of Charles White, Esq. As it is the first appearance of the name of Tristram Coffyn upon any document in America, I make a copy of it from the History of Haverhill 
1 CONC by B. L. Mirick. The marks made by the Indian sachems were representations of the bow and arrow :
1 CONT
1 CONT "Know all men by these presents, that wee Passaquoaud Sa
1 CONT consent of Passaconasvay; have sold unto ye inhabitants of 1'eiituckett 
1 CONT
1 CONT 11 ye lands wee have in Pentuckett; that is eyght myles in length from ye little Rivver In Pentuckett Westward: Six myles in length fromeye aforesaid Rivver northward: And six myles in length from ye foresaid Rivver Eastward, wth 
1 CONC ye Heand and ye rivver that ye ileand stand in as far in length as ye land lyes by as formerly expressed: that is, fourteeue niyle.s in length: And wee y,
1 CONT
1 CONT " Thomas Macy was a merchant, an enlightened mau, and much too wise to apprehend auy danger to his person or property from any person or persons, either legally or illegally. The utmost the law could do was to flue him £4, and thi
1 CONC s sum could be mitigated according to circumstances. This was actually the case with .ill those who were fined for 'entertaining Quakers,' at the time Thomas Macy was fined. He stands the lowest on the list, as you will find by ex
1 CONC amining the Colon} 'records. It is there stated, Thomas Macy is fined 10s. for, &c. The idea that his property was forfeited, is not correct. It will, perhaps, be new to some people, to know that Thomas Macy went back from Nantuck
1 CONC et and lived in Salisbury again, and sold his land, house, &c. The record says, 'Thomas Macy sold unto Anthony Colby, the house in which he, Thomas Macy, dwelleth at the present, together with barne and so much land as the garden 
1 CONC conteyneth on a straight Hoc to the eastermost corner of Koger Eastman's barne, &c.—See Registry of Deeds 1GG4, for in that year he lived in Salisbury. Thomas Macy never had 1000 acres of laud in Salisbury. The Salisbury records s
1 CONC tate that every man worth £50 or less, shall have 4 acres of planting ground; 8 acres for £100, and so on, 4 acres for every £50. With the planting laud, each settler had about double the quantity of Salt Marsh, &c. * * * * Macy w
1 CONC as certainly a man of fortitude, courage, good sense, and education.
1 CONT
1 CONT "James Coffin was one of the first settlers on the island in '59 or'GO, but he was in Dover in 1GG8, and was a member of that church in 1G71, and after that went back to Nantucket."
1 CONT
1 CONT These facts are introduced for the purpose of disabusing the minds of people familiar with the general history of Nantucket and its early settlers, because it has gone into history and been generally accepted as true that the earl
1 CONC y settlers came to Nantucket because of religious persecution. And as this impression is entirely erroneous, it is important that the descendants of Tristram Coffyn, as well as the descendants of each of the other early settlers o
1 CONC f Nantucket, should know that the first emigrations hither were purely and simply in the interest of improved homes, upon an agricultural and stock-raising basis. Neither was the island during its earliest years of white occupatio
1 CONC n that elysium which has been represented. The fathers of Nantucket did not all dwell together in unity and peace. There were local dissensions which increased with the increase of numbers, and disturbed the quietude of many homes
1 CONC . But, compared with the difficulties which afflicted many parts of New England, the oppressive spirit of the laws, the intolerance of religious views, the prohibitions of dress, and the Indian warfares, Nantucket was indeed bless
1 CONC ed with "plenty's golden smile," and "a refuge of the free."
1 CONT
1 CONT Early in 1659, according to Benjamin Franklin Folger, the most reliable genealogist of Nantucket, Tristram Coffyn proceeded upon a voyage of inquiry and observation, first to Martha's Vineyard, where he took Peter Folger, the gran
1 CONC dfather of Dr. Benjamin Franklin, as an interpreter of the Indian language, and thence to Nantucket, his object being to ascertain the temper and disposition of the Indians, and the capabilities of the island, that he might report
1 CONC  to the citizens of Salisbury what inducements for emigration thither were offered. I have discovered no evidence of the names of the others who accompanied him from Salisbury, but it is certain that he had others to assist in man
1 CONC aging the boat, and very probably some of his sons, as it is a tradition, if nothing better, that James Coffin, then about 19 years old, accompanied Thomas Macy and family, Edward Starbuck, and Isaac Coleman, later in the same yea
1 CONC r, when they took up their residences upon the island.
1 CONT Tristram's intercourse with the Indians was frank and kind, and they extended to him a warm welcome. His relations with the Indians ever thereafter inspired them with confidence in his dealings as chief magistrate, and very little
1 CONC  difficulty ever existed between the whites and the natives, a fact which was largely owing to the infusion of his liberal, high-minded and Christian character into the practical concerns of life among the Indians, for it is recor
1 CONC ded that there were some three thousand Indians upon the island when the white settlers made their abode among them, although I think that number an exaggeration.
1 CONT
1 CONT At Martha's Vineyard he entered into preliminary negotiations with Thomas Mayhew for the purchase of the island, before visiting it, and after his visit to the island, he made additional arrangements for its purchase, and returned
1 CONC  to Salisbury, where his report upon the condition of the island, the character of the Indians, and the advantages of a change of residence thither, was duly laid before his friends and associates. A company was organized for the 
1 CONC immediate purchase of the whole island, allowing Thomas Mayhew to retain a one-tenth portion thereof, with some other reservations. Several meetings of the purchasers appear to have been held at Salisbury, in the summer of 1059, a
1 CONC nd general rules and regulations, for the government of the island were adopted, as the following extracts from the records made at Salisbury will show:
1 CONT
1 CONT July 2d, 1659.—These people after mentioned did buy all right and enter- est of the Island of Nautucket that did belong to Sr Ferdinamlo George aud the Lord Sterling, Mr. Richard Vinet, Steward, Gentleman to Sir Ferdinando Oeorge,
1 CONC  and Mr. James Ferrctt, Steward to Lord Sterling, which was by them sold unto Mr. Thomas Mayhew, of Marthers Vineyard; these after mentioned did purchas of Mr. Thomas Mayhew these Kights: namely, the patteut Kight belonging to the
1 CONC  Gentleman aforesaid; and also the piece of Land which Mr. Mayhew did purchass of the Indians at the west end of the Island of Nautucket as by their grant or bill of sale, will largely appear with all the privileges aud appurtenan
1 CONC ces thereof; the aforementioned Purchasers are Tristram Coffin, Seuyr, Thomas Macy, Richard Swaiu, Thomas Barnard, Peter Coffin, Christopher Hussey, Stephen Greenleaf, John Swain, William Pile;., the Said Mr. Thomas Mayhew himself
1 CONC  also becom a Twentieth part purshaser so that they, vizt: Mr. Thomas Mayhew, Tristram Coffin, Siur., Thomas Macy, Kichard Swain, Thomas Barnard, Peter Coffin, Christopher Hussey, Stephen Greenleaf, John Swain, William Pile had th
1 CONC e whole and Sole Interest, Dispose!!, power, and privilege of said Island and appurtenances thereof.
1 CONT
1 CONT At Salysbury, February, 1659.—At a meeting of the purchasers or the major part of them appeared alowed by the rest together with some others that was owned for Ass'iates as will hereafter appear—it was agreed and Determined and ap
1 CONC provd as follows, vizt: that the ten owners will adinitt of Ten more partners who shall have equal! power aud Interest with themselves, and that either of the purchasers aforementioned shall have liberty to take a partner whome he
1 CONC  pleases not being mostly excepted against by the rest, At that meeting Robert Pike was owned partner with Christopher Hussey,"- Robert Barnard was owned partner with Thomas Barnard, Edwed Starbuck was oued to be Thomas Macy's par
1 CONC tner, aud Tristram Coffiu, jur., partner with Stephen Greeuleaf, James Coffin partner with Peter Coffin—at the same meeting it was mutually and unanimously agreed upon, determined and concluded, that no man whatsoever shall purcha
1 CONC se any land of any of the Indians upon the said ilaud for his own private or particular use; but whatsoever purchas shall be made, shall be for the general account of the Twenty owners or purchasers and whatsoever person shall pur
1 CONC chas any Land upon any other account, it shall be utterly void and null, except what is don by Leve from the said Owners or purchasers: at the same meeting it was ordered and Determined that there shall be ten other Inhabitants ad
1 CONC mitted into the Plantation who shall have such accomodation as the Owners or purchasers shall judge meet—as namely necessary tradesman and Seaman.
1 CONT
1 CONT At a meeting of these owners of the Island of Nantucket at Salisbury it was Debatted, and after debatted, determed and concluded, that as there had ben a former meeting in Salisbury at the House of Benjamin Cambell, in February, I
1 CONC G'ii), in which meeting orders was made for Prohibiting of any Pwsou from the purchasing any laud from any of the Indians upon the Island of Nantucket except for the use of the Twenty owners or purchasers, the Order shall stand In
1 CONC violable unalterable as that which also as that which is likely necessary to the continuance of the well being of the place and the Contnrary, that which tends to the confusion and Ruine of the whole and the Suverting of the rules
1 CONC  and orders allreacly agreed upon and the depriveing of the said owners of there Just rights and Interest. Also it was ordered at the same meeting that all the Laud that is fit for areable land convenient for House lot shall be fo
1 CONC rthwith measured, that the quantity thereof may be known, which being done, shall be divided byequel proportions, that is to say Four Fifths parts to the owners or purchasers; and the other Fifth unto the Ten other Inhabitants, wh
1 CONC ereof John Bishop shall have two parts or shares, that is to say of that Fifth part belonging to the Ten Inhabitant. Also at the same meeting it was ordered that Tristram Coffin, Thomas Macy, Edward Starbuck, Thomas Barnard, Peter
1 CONC  Folger of Mathers Vineyard, shall have power to measure and lay out said Land according to the above said awder, and whatsoever shall be done and concluded in the said Case by or any three of them, Peter Folger being one, shall b
1 CONC e accounted Legall and valid.
