1 CONT \Tristram Coffyn was thirty-seven years of age upon his removal to America, and fifty-live years of age at the time of his removal to Nan- tucket, having spent eighteen years in America previous to his coming to the island to resi
1 CONC depj It does not appear that his mother, Joan Coffyn, ever resided in Nantucket; and it does appear that she died in Boston, in May, 1661, at the age of 77 years; the Rev. Mr. Wilson, who preached the funeral sermon, spoke of her 
1 CONC as a woman of extraordinary character. Mirick's History of Haverhill, quoting from Sewall's Diary, which recorded the fact of Joan Cofiyn's death, says he " embalmed her memory." Of his two sisters who came with him to America, Eu
1 CONC nice married William Butler, and Mary married Alexander Adams, whose history cannot here be further followed up. And of his two sisters, Joan and Deborah, who were married in England before his departure for America, nothing has e
1 CONC ver been definitely ascertained.
1 CONT
1 CONT Three of his children, viz., Peter, Tristram, Jr., and Elizabeth, were married at the time of his removal to Naiitucket. Elizabeth married Stephen Greenleaf, November 13, 1651, while they were living at New- bury, the first grandc
1 CONC hild being Stephen Greenleaf, Jr., born August 15, 1652. This grandchild well remembered his great-grandmother, Joan Coflyn, and lived to see his own great-grandchildren. I do not find that Stephen Greenleaf or Tristram Coffin, Jr
1 CONC ., ever removed their families to or became residents of Nautucket, although they were of the original purchasers. Peter Coffin, however, became a resident of the island. Tristram, senior, did not remove his entire family until 16
1 CONC 6-2.
1 CONT
1 CONT From the early historical and traditional accounts Tristram Coffyn was the leading spirit among the islanders at the commencement of the settlement, and the interests which he and his sons and sons-in-law represented (for he frequ
1 CONC ently signed his name in matters of proprietorship for himself and five others), gave him power to control in a great degree the enterprises of the island. During the first years of his residence upon the island he was the richest
1 CONC  proprietor, except his son, Peter, who was reputed to be possessed of great estate. It is not to be found, however, that he ever exercised his power unduly for his own advantage. He sought to have his associates purchase jointly 
1 CONC with him the island of Tuckernuck. Failing to enlist them in his further enterprise, he, with his three eldest sons, made a purchase of it in their own right. When the man to whom certain rights had been granted upon condition tha
1 CONC t he maintain a mill for grinding the grain produced by the islanders failed to construct the same, it was Tristram Coffyn that assumed the contract and built and maintained the mill. In all the Indian troubles (for the aboriginee
1 CONC s were simply North American Indians), our common ancestor held them in subjection in such manner as commanded their respect. He employed large numbers of them in his farming operations, and built them improved wigwams upon his la
1 CONC nd. Our late most valued historian, Benjamin Franklin Folger, speaking of his relation to the Indians says: " The Christian character which he exhibited and which he practically illustrated in all the varied circumstances and cond
1 CONC itions of that infant colony, is analogous to that which subsequently distinguished the founder of Pennsylvania, so that the spirit of the one seemed to be but the counterpart of the other."
1 CONT
1 CONT The Indians were divided into bauds and sometimes had quarrels among themselves and sometimes were at variance with the whites. Whether the white man was always the aggressor, as has been asserted quite confidently by the philanth
1 CONC ropic friends of the Indian in later times, cannot be decided; but one thing may be stated without fear of contradiction, that the Indians became troublesome only after they had learned to drink rum, and rum was introduced among t
1 CONC hem by the whites. The early court records are mainly devoted to trials, convictions, and sentences of Indians to be whipped for getting drunk and for petty larcenies; and of fines imposed upon white men and women for selling rum 
1 CONC to Indians. Strong drink was the bane of the Nautucket Indians, and, so marked were its demoralizing influences, that the first General Court for Nantucket and Martha's Vineyard, composed of Tristram Coffyu, first chief magistrate
1 CONC  of Nantucket, and Thomas Mayhew, .first chief magistrate of Martha's Vineyard, and two associates from each island, enacted a law prohibiting the sale of intoxicating- drinks to Indians. It is probably the first prohibitory liquo
1 CONC r law on record. The evil of nun drinking among the Indians became a serious matter, and the prohibitory measures had a restraining influence; yet for the love of gain, white men then, as now, violated the law and sold rum to the 
1 CONC Indians. The law was occasionally enforced, however, and John Gardner, whose gravestone alone marks the spot where onr ancestors were first interred, complained to Governor Lovelace, at New York, under date of March 15, 1676, that
1 CONC  a half barrel of rum had been taken from him by Thomas Macy, then chief magistrate. Mr. Gardner also represented that the Indian Sachems said they would fight if the laws against them were enforced.
1 CONT
1 CONT Without entering into the consideration of all the causes which led to the personal and partisan difficulties between the white settlers at this time, it Is clear that rum-drmking among the Indians was one great cause; and the let
1 CONC ter of Thomas Macy to Governor Lovelace, bearing date of May 9, 1676, amply shows the fear he entertained of the Indians if strong drink was allowed to be sold them. And he asks the Governor to make an order prohibiting any vessel
1 CONC  that shall come into the harbor from selling strong drink to Indians, believing that an order from the Governor at New York would have more force than laws enacted by the magistrates. He uses this strong language: "Sir, concernin
1 CONC g the Peace we hitherto enjoy, I cannot imagine it could have bin if strong Liquor had bin among the Indians, as formerly: for my owne 3-* I have been to ye utmost an opposed of the Trade these 38 yeares, and I verily believe (res
1 CONC pecting the Indians) tis the only Ground of the miserable p'sent Ruine to both Nations; for tis that hath kept them from Civility, they have been the drunken Trade kept all the while like wild Beares and Wolves in the Wildernessc.
1 CONC "
1 CONT
1 CONT It also appears that the Court on one occasion took possession of all the liquor upon the island and disposed of it in small quantities as the owners and English neighbors had need of.
1 CONT
1 CONT The appointment of Tristram Coffyu as the first chief magistrate of Nantucket, was about the same time that Thomas Mayhew was appointed the first chief magistrate of Martha's Vineyard. The commission bears date 29th of June, 1671.
1 CONC  The two chiefs, together with two assistants from each island, were to constitute a General Court with appellate jurisdiction over both islands. It appears that Governor Lovelace desired the inhabitants to recommend two suitable 
1 CONC persons for chief magistrate, for him to make a selection from. They named Tristram Coffyn and Thomas Macy, and he made choice of the former.
1 CONT
1 CONT The town voted to have a harrow for the use of the inhabitants; and they further voted that Mr. Tristram Coffyn provide the harrow, and that he and Mr. Thomas Macy be empowered to see that every man sowed seed " according to order
1 CONC ."
1 CONT
