1 CONT Born: 4 Mar 1609/10 Brixton, England
1 CONT Died: 5 Dec 1705 probably at Nantucket, MA
1 CONT
1 CONT Parents: Robert Stevens Esq. - Dionis ______.
1 CONT
1 CONT Married: Tristram Coffin in 1630 at Brixton. They came to MA in 1642 from England, he being 37yrs old at the time. He was b. 1605 in Brixton, Devonshire, England, died Oct 1681 at Nantucket. They were the parents of nine children,
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Puritan women ran taverns, dame schools, and stores; examples of goodwives engaging in trade include Dionis Stevens Coffin and her daughter, Mary Coffin Starbuck, who notably prospered in different fields. They were influenced by 
1 CONC the culture, particularly the economy and religion, of their environments. These two women enjoyed unique opportunities that differed from place (the Massachusetts Bay Colony) to place (Nantucket Island, not then under the jurisdi
1 CONC ction of the Massachusetts General Court).
1 CONT
1 CONT What biographical information we have on Dionis comes chiefly from genealogical studies of the Coffin/Coffyn clan. Like her husband’s, Dionis’s family was landed gentry in Brixton (near Plymouth), in Devonshire. Born to Robert Ste
1 CONC vens and his wife Katherine ca. 1610, she married Tristram Coffin (born in 1605 to Peter and Joan [Thember] Coffyn) about 1630. Part of the Great Migration to New England, Tristram and Dionis arrived in the Massachusetts Bay Colon
1 CONC y in 1642[1] with their young family: Peter (b. 1631); Tristram, Jr. (b. 1632); Elizabeth (b. 1635); and James (b. 1640). First settling in Salisbury, the family later moved to Haverhill, where Dionis gave birth to three more chil
1 CONC dren: Deborah (born and died 1642); Mary (b. February 20, 1645); and John (b. 1647). The Coffins removed to Newbury in 1648, where their last child Stephen was born in 1652. In 1654, the family returned to Salisbury.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dionis’s business career probably began with an education in England that included rudimentary arithmetic (enough to keep account books) and domestic arts such as beer-making. Dark beer, or ale, was “the common table beverage” in 
1 CONC the diet of mid-seventeenth century Massachusetts men and women.[2] In 1644, Tristram received a license from the Essex County Court to run a ferry and an “ordinary” — or tavern — and to sell wine on the Newbury side of Carr’s Isl
1 CONC and on the Merrimack River. On December 26, 1647, Tristram received a renewal of his permit “to keep an ordinary and retayle wine” and maintain the ferry. The older Coffin sons ran the ferry while Dionis presumably operated the “o
1 CONC rdinary.” From the first years of settlement in America, public houses, as historian David W. Conroy notes, were controversial gathering places. Fearing unrest and upheaval, Puritan magistrates tried to restrict the number of tave
1 CONC rns in the Bay Colony.[3] Despite the reputation of early New England taverns, however, a high proportion of those granted licenses to run them were women. In the Coffin household, though, it was Tristram, not Dionis, who received
1 CONC  the license for a tavern.
1 CONT
1 CONT Coffin Family TreeTristram is mentioned more than twenty-five times from 1636 to 1661 in records of the Essex County Court. He was “made freeman of Haverhill” (November 4, 1645), sat “on the trial jury” at Ipswich Quarterly Court 
1 CONC (September 27, 1659), and served as an attorney, constable, and deputy marshal. Yet he was not above reproof, being cited for having “neglected the watch at Newbury” in 1654.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a woman in the Bay Colony, Dionis had little overt political or economic power. Yet it was Dionis who was called in to the Essex County Court of General Sessions of the Peace, Ipswich Quarterly Court, in September 1653. While i
1 CONC t was not unusual for a woman to appear in these records, this is the only time Dionis appears, and only as “wife,” not with her given name. The background of the case underlines the extent to which Puritan lawmakers regulated eve
1 CONC rything — down to a draught of beer. Under a Bay Colony law of 1645, “Every person licensed to keep an ordinary, shall always be provided with good wholesome beer of four bushels of malt to the hogshead, which he shall not sell ab
1 CONC ove two pence the ale quart, on penalty of forty shillings and for the second offense shall lose his license.” The court entry reads:
1 CONT
1 CONT Tristram Coffin’s wife presented for selling beer for three pence a quart. Proved, by testimony of Samuell Moore, that six bushels of malt were put into the hogshead. Discharged.[4]
1 CONT
1 CONT Thus in her only appearance in the Essex County court records, Goodwife Coffin was charged with selling beer at three pence per quart without the requisite amount of malt; she proved by evidence of witnesses that she not only used
1 CONC  the required amount of malt, but even more. Dionis was, in fact, entitled to charge three pence a quart, and the case against her was dropped. But the question remains: if the license was granted to Tristram, why wasn’t he summon
1 CONC ed? Apparently, Dionis had legal jurisdiction over the making of the beer. Could incidents like this charge, and the rigorous regulation of beer-making, have contributed to the Coffins’ decision to move from Essex County to Nantuc
1 CONC ket Island in 1661?
1 CONT
1 CONT The Massachusetts Bay Colony was overseen by a governor, a legislative body known as the General Court, and a system of magistrates, marshals, and officials operating on county and town levels. Using the Scriptures as the ultimate
1 CONC  source of all laws, the Colony was a “Holy Experiment” of governance. Nantucket was different. A proprietorship owned and ruled by ten partners and their deputies, it was physically and legally beyond the control of the Massachus
1 CONC etts Bay Colony, and was bought and settled with the primary objective of making money. Each landed partner was given a vote in town affairs; tradesmen, allowed on the island after its initial settlement, were only given half a vo
1 CONC te until a “revolt” in the 1680s.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dionis’ husband overshadowed her in court records — and in Nantucket history. In his 1924 History of Nantucket: County, Island, and Town Including Genealogies of the First Settlers, Alexander Starbuck wrote, “If any man may be con
1 CONC sidered the patriarch of Nantucket. . . that man is Tristram Coffin.” Tristram and the nine other original partners bought the Indian rights to Nantucket from Watertown merchant Thomas Mayhew for “30 pounds of silver and two beave
1 CONC r hats one for myself and one for my wife.” Under the system of proprietorship they devised, the Nantucket founding families were free to recreate the
1 CONT
1 CONT English manorial system. With freedom for entrepreneurship, Tristram and Dionis’s daughter, Mary Coffin Starbuck (born in 1645), undertook first alone, and later with her eldest son Nathaniel, a number of commercial ventures, incl
1 CONC uding a country store, extensive land trading and investment in the nascent Nantucket whaling industry.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mary not only knew how to read and write; she also mastered Latin and Greek, and was “well read in the Scriptures.”[5] Despite the fact that children growing up in the Bay Colony were required to attend school, Mary’s level of edu
1 CONC cation would have been unusual. The ability to read and write classical languages was usually a skill possessed only by young men studying for the ministry at Harvard College. All sources point to Mary being precocious and charism
1 CONC atic.
1 CONT
1 CONT In a match possibly arranged by her parents, sixteen- year-old Mary was betrothed to her father’s business partner, Nathaniel Starbuck. Mary had probably known Nathaniel for years; her brother Peter had married Nathaniel’s sister 
1 CONC Dorcas when Mary was a child. The only surviving son of Baptist lay minister Edward Starbuck and Katherine Reynolds (“through whom almost all Starbucks in the United States trace their ancestry”),[6] Nathaniel used a mark to sign 
1 CONC his name — often, but not necessarily, an indication of illiteracy.[ 7] Because of his position in the proprietary division of the island, Nathaniel was guaranteed several shares on which to farm and raise sheep. Ten years Mary’s 
1 CONC senior, he had worked for years in the lumber business in what is now Dover, New Hampshire. The couple’s marriage on Nantucket in 1661 was the first English wedding on the island; and their daughter Mary, born a year later, was th
1 CONC e first English child born on Nantucket.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mary Coffin Starbuck was not afraid to “borrow” some of her husband’s male authority to lend clout and credibility to her own words at town meetings. She often spoke for both of them: “My husband and I, having considered the subje
1 CONC ct, think. . . .” Describing the Starbucks in 1702, itinerant Quaker preacher John Richardson noted in his journal: “He [Nathaniel] appeared not a Man of Mean Parts, but she [Mary] so exceeded him in the soundness of judgment, cle
1 CONC arness of Understanding, and an elegant way of expressing herself, and that not in an affected Strain, but one very natural to her, that it tended to lessen the Qualifications of her Husband.”[8]
1 CONT
1 CONT Even as she held town meetings at home (locally known as “Parliament House” because of its size and utility), and raised ten children — eight of whom lived to maturity — Mary began a trading post or “country store,” probably in a 
1 CONC shed attached to the house, in 1661.
1 CONT
1 CONT Historian Daniel Vickers outlined how Mary and her family used her customers, the Algonquian Indians native to Nantucket, to their own ends. An account book, kept by the Starbuck family between 1683 and 1757 and now owned by the N
1 CONC antucket Historical Association, documents this trade. “Indians were eager to trade because they recognized the low cost and technological superiority of English manufacturers. Shoes, coats, woolen cloth, fishhooks, lines, powder 
1 CONC and shot, and plowshares were the most desired items. Settlers needed Indian commodities such as feathers, grain, and fi sh, to send to Boston to trade for goods the island could not produce. To virtually every Indian customer the
1 CONC  Starbucks advanced enough goods (but never more than £10 worth) to oblige him to continue bringing items to trade or paying debt by labor. In the nascent shore-whaling industry, Indians were needed to row whale-boats commanded by
1 CONC  English settlers. The extension of credit bound the Native population more tightly when liquor was introduced as a trade good. English traders used alcohol and credit extension to gain control over Indian labor, producing a de fa
1 CONC cto system of debt peonage.”[9]
1 CONT
1 CONT A page from Mary Coffin Starbuck's account book. Courtesy of the Nantucket Historical Association, MS10-475-122.On Nantucket, Mary was allowed not only to sell what she wanted, but to worship as she pleased. Possessing a Puritan w
1 CONC ork ethic, she was nonetheless free of Bay Colony theocracy. The original English settlers of Nantucket were dissenters from Puritanism: Baptists, such as Edward Starbuck, two non-practicing Quakers, and “electarians,” a group tha
1 CONC t rejected paid leadership (depending instead on “inward leanings”), into which Mary was baptized by her spiritual mentor Peter Folger in 1663. Rejection of Bay Colony Puritanism
1 CONT
1 CONT was paramount for these early Nantucketers; both for philosophic and economic reasons, they rejected the establishment of a settled minister.[10] Itinerant ministers of many groups saw the Island as ripe for proselytizing. Chief a
1 CONC mong the visiting preachers were the Society of Friends, or Quakers, of England. Here was a religion that emphasized the individual “Inner Light” or “that of God in every man” — or woman. Quakerism radically redefined women’s role
1 CONC  in worship and the running of Meeting, using strong biblical examples such as Ruth in their “openings.” As with many English women in the colonies, the Quaker faith appealed strongly to independent Mary Coffin Starbuck.
1 CONT
1 CONT “At the first Sight of her it sprang in my Heart, To this Woman is the everlasting Love of God,” wrote John Richardson, a Friend (Quaker) who met Mary in Nantucket in 1702. “[The] Islanders esteemed [her] a Judge among them, for l
1 CONC ittle was done there without her, as I understood.” Richardson held a meeting for which Mary volunteered her home.[11] Two years later Mary further demonstrated her commitment to the Quaker faith. While noting “[h]er husband being
1 CONC  freely passive in such things,” visiting Friend Thomas Story initiated a weekly “First Day,” or Sunday Quaker meeting, with Mary, her children, and neighbors. The official Nantucket Meeting was formed in 1708, and Mary served as 
1 CONC a minister or elder. “Quakerism, with its emphasis on individual experience, lay ministry, and inward Light of Christ in every person, closely expressed many of the ideas” of the religious “dissenters” of Nantucket.[12] Mary’s son
1 CONC , Nathaniel Starbuck, Jr., clerk of the Meeting and the town of Nantucket, granted two acres for the construction of a meetinghouse and a Quaker graveyard in 1711. By the opening of the second meetinghouse in 1736, seven hundred Q
1 CONC uakers lived on the Island. The Society of Friends dominated religious life on Nantucket for more than a century.[13] No records exist of an Indian on the Island joining the Friends. Membership in the Society for the English inhab
1 CONC itants often led to intermarriage and joint business partnerships. A Starbuck-Coffin-Macy family merger came to dominate the offshore and later deep-sea whaling industry. Due to Nathaniel’s success in farming, Mary’s in trading, a
1 CONC nd especially the couple’s shares in offshore whaling companies, the elder Nathaniel Starbuck is sometimes considered Nantucket’s first “millionaire.”
1 CONT
1 CONT Mother and daughter Dionis Stevens Coffin and Mary Coffin Starbuck, only a generation apart, operated in wholly different spheres. Dionis’s life during the Great Migration era was one of upheaval and struggle in a new and hostile 
1 CONC environment. The Massachusetts Bay Colony allowed women to run their own businesses,
1 CONT
1 CONT but strictly regimented individual action. Nantucket’s more limited governance, with fewer constraints and regulations, greater economic opportunities, provided by the presence of the Native population, allowed Mary to flourish. A
1 CONC nti-establishment Quakerism, embraced by Mary over the Puritan faith of her parents, also promoted greater individualism and autonomy, particularly for women. A successful entrepreneur and “matriarch,” or “founder” of Quakerism on
1 CONC  Nantucket, Mary Coffin Starbuck “had her say” until her death on “13 day of 9 month” 1717, aged 72.[14]
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes 1 Mary Lovering Holman, Ancestry of Charles Stinson Pillsbury and John Sargent Pillsbury (Concord, N.H.: Rumford Press, 1938), 605.
