0 @N6000000000383550308@ NOTE !Per Note 1, p.317-318:"Edward Starbuck was born in 1604, and is said to have
1 CONT come to Dover from Derbyshire, in England.  He is first mentioned receiving,
1 CONT 30th,6 mo.,1643, a grant of 40 acres of land on each side of Fresh River, at
1 CONT 'Cutchchechoe,next above the lot of John Baker, at the little water brooke, and
1 CONT also one plat of marsh above Cutchechoe great Marsh, that the brook that runs
1 CONT out of the great river runs through, first discovered by Richard Walderne,
1 CONT Edward Colcord, Edward Starbuck, and William Furber."       He had other grants
1 CONT at different times, and became one of the Elders of the Church at an early per-
1 CONT iod.  He was Representative in the General Court, in 1643 and 1646, and enjoyed
1 CONT various other tokens of respect that were given him by his fellow citizens.  In
1 CONT his later career he appears to have become a heretic, as regards some of the
1 CONT ways of conducting Church matters.  We cannot, of course, ascertain with
1 CONT certainty what particulars the Elder held, as an Anabaptist, but it is probable
1 CONT that he differed from it in the matter of the baptism of children, he supposing
1 CONT that immersion was the Scripture method of performing this rite, and that child-
1 CONT ren were not fit subjects for its performance.  But the Elder managed to get
1 CONT along with but little disquietude for quite a number of years.     In 1669 Elder
1 CONT Starbuck went off on an exploring expedition.  In the course of his travels he
1 CONT met Thomas Macey, and his family, James Coffin, a youth of nineteen, and Isaac
1 CONT Coleman, a boy of twelve.  These adventurers set sail in an open boat in the
1 CONT autumn of 1659, and in due time arrived at the Island of Nantucket.  They
1 CONT settled first at Mantical, but afterwards moved to a more central place, now
1 CONT called Cambridge.     The next spring the Elder came back to Dover to get his
1 CONT family.  His daughters, Sarah and Abigail, were married and remained in Dover;
1 CONT but his wife Katherine, went with him; also Nathaniel, Dorcas and Jethro, the
1 CONT remaining children, went with him.  Elder Starbuck became one of the leading and
1 CONT prosperous citizens of that new settlement.  He died there 4th Dec. 1690."
0 @N6000000003077310338@ NOTE In 1641, he sued Hansard Knollys for slander but the details and outcome hace been lost or never recorded.  Twice he was elected deputy (Representative) of the Massachusetts General Court (Legislature) 1643 and 1646.  He and  two 
1 CONC others were appointed Wearsmen or official river fishermen for Dover for life and were required to supple the town and its church from their catches.  In 1647 he was given permissoin to erect a sawmill and went into the lumber bus
1 CONC iness with Richard Waldrom.  For the "great misdemeanor of professint Anabaptism" he was heavily fined.  There was no seperation of church and statem and the Puritans who ran the colony were intolerant of people like the Anabaptis
1 CONC ts who refused to baptize children and insisted on the baptism of adults instead.  In 1653, Edward sold half of his timber and water rights to his son-in-law, Peter Coffin.
0 @N6000000001722426794@ NOTE
1 CONT Came from England to Dover, NH abt 1640 and then moved to Nantucket, MA. abt 1659 along with adult children Nathaniel, Jethro, and Dorcas.,  Prosecuted for joining the Baptist faith.  Later became a Quaker.,    Alternate death dat
1 CONC e Dec 4, 1690 at age 86.
