1 CONT
1 CONT [143983.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT  John Cooke was
1 CONT  baptized at Leyden,
1 CONT  Holland between January
1 CONT  and March 1607/8 and
1 CONT  was, thus, about thirteen
1 CONT  years old on arrival at
1 CONT  Plymouth with his father,
1 CONT  Francis Cooke in 1620 on
1 CONT  the Mayflower. He
1 CONT  married in Plymouth on
1 CONT  March 28, 1634, Sarah
1 CONT  Warren, daughter of
1 CONT  Pilgrim Richard and
1 CONT  Elizabeth Warren of the
1 CONT  Mayflower.
1 CONT
1 CONT  John became a deacon of
1 CONT  the Plymouth Church in
1 CONT  the 1630's, but he was
1 CONT  excommunicated from
1 CONT  the church ca. 1757.
1 CONT  Probably around the time
1 CONT  he was excommunicated,
1 CONT  he became a Baptist. He
1 CONT  was a Baptist preacher
1 CONT  and about 1680
1 CONT  established a Baptist
1 CONT  church in what is now
1 CONT  Tiverton, near Adamsville.
1 CONT  This was the beginning of
1 CONT  the society which has
1 CONT  included members of the
1 CONT  Wilcox and allied families
1 CONT  since its founding and
1 CONT  which has worshipped in
1 CONT  the Old Stone Church
1 CONT  since it was built in 1841.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT                                        Photo © 1995, Sam Behling
1 CONT
1 CONT He served on juries and on various special assignments, and was a long-time
1 CONT Plymouth deputy. In 1664 he was granted 15 acres of land near Dartmouth
1 CONT and he had other significant land dealings in the area, owning 3/34 of the
1 CONT original grant. Sometime between 1653 and 1660, he moved to that part of
1 CONT Dartmouth now known as Oxford Village, Fairhaven. In 1672, the town of
1 CONT Dartmouth gave him Ram (now Pope's) Island in recompense for former
1 CONT services.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1666 he was a deputy for Dartmouth. John was one of the advisers for the
1 CONT defense of Dartmouth against the Indians. He became a magistrate for
1 CONT Dartmouth authorized to marry, to administer oaths, and to issue warrants for
1 CONT cour trials at Plymouth. He served the town several years as a selectman and
1 CONT was ten times a representative to the General Court. He was one of the
1 CONT owners of the first vessels built in Plymouth. He was a constant trader in
1 CONT lands at Plymouth and Dartmouth and owned lands at Puncatest.
1 CONT
1 CONT Prior to the Indian uprising (King Phillip's War) in 1675 he converted his home
1 CONT into a garrison house. This was the haven of safety of the inhabitants in the
1 CONT early spring of 1676. His home was later burned by the savages.
1 CONT
1 CONT John Cooke was the last surviving male passenger of the Mayflower. John
1 CONT died 23 Nov 1695, having lived through the entire life of the Plymouth Colony.
1 CONT The will of John Cooke of Dartmouth, County of Bristol dated November 9,
1 CONT 1694, proved April 16, 1696, mentions wife Sarah; son-in-law Arthur Hathaway
1 CONT and his wife Sarah his daughter; son-in-law Stephen West and his wife Mercy
1 CONT his daughter.; Jonathan Delano; grandson Thomas Taber; and
1 CONT granddaughter Hester Perry. On December 7, 1696 widow Sarah Cooke
1 CONT presented the inventory.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Fairhaven a short distance north of the New Bedford-Fairhaven bridge,
1 CONT near the river, is a large boulder to which is attached a bronze table, on
1 CONT which, below a representation of the Mayflower is an inscription. A picture of
1 CONT this appears on this page. The exact location of the grave of John Cooke is,
1 CONT however, a matter of doubt. Somewhere in Fairhaven is about as definite as it
1 CONT can now be determined. The boulder with its inscription was placed 200 years
1 CONT after his death. That he as the "first white settler of this town" applies to
1 CONT Fairhaven, rather than old Dartmouth.
