0 @N6000000005584467707@ NOTE
1 CONT EDWARD PERRY1 of Sandwich, MA, is supposed to have been born about 1630-1632a,j in Devonshire, England;a and he died before 9 Mar 1694/5 in Sandwich, MA, when the inventory of his estate was dated.l He was buried at Spring Hill, M
1 CONC A.j He married about 1653,j MARY FREEMAN (See Family # FM2).a 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Rev. Calbraith Bourn Perry reports that Edward Perry persuaded "his future father-in-law that the marriage ceremony, although to be performed in the Gubernatorial mansion, should conform to the simple Quaker rite.  Governor Freema
1 CONC n, as a magistrate, had been commanded by the General Court to perform the ceremony.  This departure from Puritan forms of religion was so offensive to the lawmakers of Plymouth, that Perry was annually condemned for many years to
1 CONC  pay a fine, the largest, except one ever imposed in the Colony."f
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Most printed accounts of the Perry’s suggest that the following other early Perry inhabitants of Sandwich were siblings of Edward Perry, specifically: Ezra Perry, who married 12 Feb 1651, Elizabeth Burges; Margaret Perry, who marr
1 CONC ied 18 July 1651, as his second wife, Edmond Freeman, Jr. (See Family # FM2); Hannah Perry, who married 24 June 1652, Henry Dillingham; and Deborah Perry, who married 9 May 1654, Robert Harper (See Family # HR1).f
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward Perry was a prominent member of the Sandwich Society of Friends as were Hannah (Perry) Dillingham and Deborah (Perry) Harper.  Yet, neither Ezra Perry nor Margaret (Perry) Freeman seem to have identified themselves with the
1 CONC  Quakers.j
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Rosemary Canfield writes that "The Plymouth Colony Court Records contain this entry: 7 Jun 1659 - 'Ezra Perry is allowed by the Court to be Executor of the Estate of Sarah Perry, there being noe other (although she hath many frien
1 CONC ds in the Country), that claimeth interest to the Estate, having put in securities into the Court to be accountable for the Estate encase it shall be required by any that hath better title thereto . . .’ The wording of this entry 
1 CONC demonstrates clearly that Sarah Perry had no blood relatives in Sandwich, but may have been closely connected to Ezra, and perhaps Edward, by marriage.  It has been suggested that she was step-mother to Ezra,h and that he had clai
1 CONC m to her estate based on the right to a dower residue."j
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward Perry is recorded in Sandwich as a grand juryman in 1653.  As early as 1658, and from that date until the persecution of the Quakers ceased, he was repeatedly fined and otherwise punished for his religion and the conduct ar
1 CONC ising out of his beliefs.a, f
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT At the age of forty-six, Edward Perry published, "Warning to New England" condemning the sins of the day.  In this pamphlet he wrote, "O, what lamentation shall be taken up for New England to countervail or equalize Abominations o
1 CONC f drunkenness, swearing, lying, stealing, whoredoms, adultery and fornication, with many other Abominations..."f On 29 Dec 1694, Edward Perry made his will, which was proved 12 Apr 1695, naming his nine children who survived him.a
1 CONC
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT These children, all born in Sandwich were:j
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT i. DORCASa, 2 b 9 May 1661; d 16 Jan 1746; m 23 Aug 1679, JOHN EASTON, son of Peter and Ann (Coggeshall) (See Family # CO1) Easton of Newport, RI.a
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT ii. MARY, b prob in 1663; m 22 Sep 1685, JOHN WING, son of Stephen and Sarah (Briggs) Wing.a He was b 22, 9th month, 1661;i d in 1728.j
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT iii. SAMUEL,a b 2 Mar 1664/5;c m MARY TUCKER.a
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT iv. DEBORAH, b prob in 1667; m CHRISTOPHER GIFFORD.a
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT v. SARAH, b prob in 1669; m 1 Sep 1690, TIMOTHY DAVIS, son of John and Hannah (Linnell) Davis.  He was a Quaker and lived in Rochester, MA.  He was b in 1662 and d in 1723.a
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT vi. PEACE, b in 1671;a d 9 Nov 1740;a m 20 Oct 1697,a JOHN MUMFORD, son of Stephen and Ann Mumfordg of Newport.a
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT vii. EDWARD, b prob in 1673;a m poss as his second wife, ELIPHAL SMITH.a
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT viii. REST, b in 1675; m JACOB MOTT of Portsmouth.a
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT ix. BENJAMIN, b prob in 1677;a m (2) SUSANNA BARBER.a, e
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [SOURCES: a. Gen of RI Fam, (1983), 1:667,674-679; b. Austin, 160, p 273; c. Benns; d. TAG, 50:158; e. Harding, p 89; f. Rev. C. B. Perry, p 43,45-46; g. Austin, Gen Dict, p 136; h. NEHGR, 115:86; i. Wing, pp 56,66 j. Canfield, Pe
1 CONC rry, pp 1-3; k. Schroeder, p 3; l. Brown, Perry, p 8.]”
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward'a birth is estimated to be ca 1630-1632. He m ca 1653 Mary Freeman. He d in Sandwich, MA, 9 Mar 1694/5. The earliest mention of his name that I have seen is his appointment as a grand juryman in Sandwich in 1653. It would a
1 CONC ppear that the birthdate estimate is derived from this appointment. The marriage date may be more speculative. I show 9 children with the eldest born 9 May 1661 and the youngest, Benjamin with an estimated birthdate of 1677. Benja
1 CONC min was named in Edward's will dated 29 Dec 1694 recorded Sandwich and Benjamin was under age.
1 CONT
1 CONT From the Grand Inquest, 7 Jun 1653, "Edward Perry, for unorderly proceeding contrary to order of Court, about his marriage, is fined five pounds. And Mr. Prence is ordered by the Court to see his marriage ratified as hee goeth hom
1 CONC e.... Thomas Tupper, for his negligence in not causing Edward Perry of Sandwich to bee by him orderly married, being by the Court appointed to mary persons there, was required hence forth to desist, and is not entrusted with that 
1 CONC business any more." Later, the statement, "Wee understand that the honored Court hath taken notice of the marriage of Edward Perry, and therefore for the presen t wee leave it."
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT dward Perry, unlike his brother Ezra, who seems to have belonged to the established religion of the Colony, was, as we have seen, a Quaker, but under the demure habit and demeanor of his sect he must have possessed an indomitable 
1 CONC will and some of the fighting spirit that has characterized his descendants. At the age of forty-six he published a "Warning to New England," a series of woeful visions and wrathful prophesyings against the sins of his day, disclo
1 CONC sures which may surprise those who are accustomed to look back to the Puritans for example of all godliness of living. We are not surprised that the stern Puritans fined him fifty pounds, a small fortune in those days, for such wo
1 CONC rds as the following, contained in his "Warning":
1 CONT
1 CONT "The Voice that called unto me: Blood toucheth Blood, and Blood for Blood. The Word spoken: O, what lamentation shall be taken up for New England to countervail or equalize Abominations in drunkenness, swearing, lying, stealing, w
1 CONC horedoms, adultery and fornication, with many other Abominations, but above all Blood, Blood, even the Blood of My Children, and servants which by cruelty and cruel hands have been shed in the midst of her." A terrible arraignment
1 CONC  of the Assembly of the Saints!
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1665 he was again fined for writing "a Railing Letter to the Court of Plymouth." In the year 1658-60 Edward Perry's fines amounted to eighty-nine pounds, eighteen shillings and seventeen head of cattle—it is to be remembered th
1 CONC at at this time five pounds was a considerable fortune. Edward's fines were the heaviest imposed in the colony. The fine that came nearest to that of Edward Perry was that of Matthew Allen's, eighty-six pounds, seventeen shillings
1 CONC  and sixteen head of cattle. Robert Harper was fined forty-four pounds, house and land and nine head of cattle; William Gifford, whose son married Edward Perry's daughter, was fined fifty-seven pounds, nineteen shillings, one-half
1 CONC  of house, one-half of his pigs, and fifteen head of cattle. The fines of other Quakers ranged from one pound to forty pounds.
1 CONT
1 CONT Notwithstanding these persecutions, Edward Perry seems to have stood well in town affairs. He does not appear in the list of freemen, 1643, as ne was probably not then of age. He was on the grand jury, 1653; Surveyor of Highways, 
1 CONC 1657-58 and in 1674; was elected Recorder of Deeds, 1674; grand juror again, 1667; in 1666 appointed to act for the town in time of trouble with the Indians; 1682 appointed to settle town boundaries; 1687 chosen Town Commissioner.
1 CONC  In 1680 he and his brother-in-law, Edward Freeman, Jr., were appointed to act in behalf of the town in regard to division of certain lands. All the whales caught in the harbor were bought by three men, they paying sixteen pounds 
1 CONC per whale, Edward Perry and three others to be judges of the whale before it was cut into, and to determine the share of each man. He was chosen and elected to many other town offices, showing that his fellow-townsmen highly regar
1 CONC ded him as a man, regardless of the magisterial views of his religion.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward Perry's will was dated December 29, 1694, and proved April 12, 1695, thus approximately fixing the time of his death. That his wife survived him appears in the following entry, made in the Journal of Thomas Story, "5th mont
1 CONC h, 2nd day, 1704": "We had a meeting at Mary Perry's, she being ancient and sick and not able to go to the meeting house." In view of this record of his stormy life, it is not surprising that at least two of his children sought re
1 CONC fuge in the "Providence Plantations" of Roger Williams. Samuel, the oldest son, established himself upon a farm on the western shore of Narragansett Bay, in what later became the township of South Kingstown. The younger brother, B
1 CONC enjamin, soon followed Samuel's example, and built a home for himself upon a farm adjoining his brother's. It is this "Perry homestead" which is honored and frequently visited as the birthplace of Benjamin Perry's distinguished gr
1 CONC eat-grandson, Commodore Oliver Hazard Perry. We shall see later that the original house built by Benjamin Perry, and in which his children, grandchildren and oldest great-grandchildren, including the hero of Lake Erie, were born, 
1 CONC has been replaced by a later farm house built partly on the same foundation by Com. O. H. Perry himself.
1 CONT
1 CONT On the early records of Sandwich appear the names of Sarah Perry, a widow, and Ezra, Edward, Margaret and Deborah Perry. The last four are believed to be children of Sarah, the widow of an Edmund Perry of Devonshire. This relation
1 CONC ship cannot be proven conclusively from the records, but it is implied in several entries. Ezra Perry is appointed "exequitor of the Estate of Sarah Perry." Family tradition supports this inference from the records. Dr. George Haz
1 CONC ard Perry, who at the time of his recent death was probably the oldest surviving descendant of Edward Perry, writes that it "was so generally held in the time of his grandfather," George Hazard Perry, a great-grandson of Edward Pe
1 CONC rry of Sandwich. Mrs. Smith writes in her notes: "Ezra, Edward, Margaret and Deborah seem to have been possessed of both education and property, and to have belonged to the very best class of emigrants—to have been among the gentr
1 CONC y, and they all seem to have married in this country into families holding a similar rank."
1 CONT
1 CONT in an address written in 1689 Edward Perry says: 'I have been in the Province of New England fifty years.' This fixes the immigration of the Perry family in the year 1639. Edward was born in 1630, so that he was but nine years old
1 CONC  when he came; it is probable that he was the youngest of his family."
1 CONT
1 CONT Margaret Perry was the second wife of Edmund Freeman, Jr., son of the Governor. Her husband thus became a broth- in-law of her brother, Edward Perry. The first wife of Edmund Freeman, Jr., had been Rebecca Prince, daughter of Gove
1 CONC rnor Prince of Plymouth Colony, and granddaughter of Elder Brew- ster. A daughter by this first marriage, Rebecca Freeman, married Ezra, son of Ezra and Eliz. (Burgess) Perry.
1 CONT
1 CONT Deborah Perry married May 9, 1654, Robert Harper, of a family of famous Quaker preachers. One of their children, Stephen Harper, married the widow of his cousin, Edward Perry, Jr. These marriages and intermarriages corroborate the
1 CONC  note of Mrs. Smith, and strengthened the predominance of Perry blood and Perry traits in the family.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT son of Ezra Perry of Sandwich Mass. 1644, d. there on Apr. 18, 1690, it is probable that he was b. about 1615 in Devonshire Eng., and it is thought that he, Margaret, Deborah, Edward and perhaps Hannah Perry, all of Sandwich durin
1 CONC g this period, were brothers and sisters, Edward m. Mary, dau. of Edward Freeman, vice-governor of the colony, Margaret m. Edward Freeman, whose first wife was Rebecca, dau. of Gov. Prence, Ezra Jr., son of Ezra Sr, m. Rebecca, da
1 CONC u. of Edward Freeman Jr. and Rebecca Prence, and Erra Sr. and Edward each had among their children a Samuel, Ben- jajnin, Deborah and Sarah, Edward was the ancestor of the commodores Oliver Hazard and Mathew Galbraith (Ezra m. Feb
1 CONC . 12, 1651 Elizabeth, dau. of Thomas Burgess, one of the first men of the colony, his children were: Ezra, b. F««b. n, 1652, Deborah, b. Nov. 28, 1654, John, b. Jan. i, 1656, Samuel, b. Mar. 15, 1667, Benjamin, b. Feb. 15, 1670, R
1 CONC emember, b. Jan. I, 1676, and Sarah), Ezra's name frequently appears in the annals of the colony, and always as an honored citizen or
1 CONT 00
