1 CONT [143983.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT William Hodges first appears in this country in Salem, MA, when he was appointed on the jury at the court held at Salem, Marech 27, 1638.  He went from Salem to Taunton, soon after the latter town was purchased and is on the secon
1 CONC d list of early settlers of Taunton.  His name first appears of town records in August, 1643, in the list of men at Taunton, between the ages of 16 and 60 able to bear arms.  On March 24, 1643-44 the town voted that a sufficient c
1 CONC arteway be made from the houses into the woods behind the ground of William Hodges.  He was appointed constable at Taunton and was on the grand jury.  He was one of the original stockholders of the first Taunton Iron Works, and pa
1 CONC id twenty pounds per share.  He seem to have owned considerable property.
0 @N6000000000337357850@ NOTE Source: IBID:
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT MRS. HANNAH (Lake) GALLUP, came to this country with her mother, in the ship Abigail, arriving October 6, 1635, after a passage of ten weeks. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Mrs. Margaret Lake, with her daughters Hannah and Martha, accompanied her sister Elizabeth (Read) Winthrop, the new wife of Gov. John Winthrop, Jr., who returned to America in this ship with commissions from Lord's Say, Brook and 
1 CONC others. She left a son in England who never came to Americ
1 CONT
1 CONT As this was the first marriage in the male line of the emigrant ancestors, of the Gallop family, which took place in this country, and the commencement of the American line of descent, the lineage of the wife is here given: 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT ANCESTRY OF HANNAH (LAKE) GALLOP:
1 CONT John Lake, the father of Hannah (Lake) Gallop descended from the Lakes of Normantown, Yorkshire, who claimed descent through the Cailleys, from the Albinis, Earls of Arundel and Sussex, from the Counts of Louvaine, (the right line
1 CONC  of Charlamagne) and from William the Conquerer. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Margaret (Read) Lake, mother of Hannah (Lake) Gallop, born in England, was the daughter of Edmund Read of Wickford, Essex County. Margaret (Read) Lake of Wickford, died in Ipswich, Mass., 1672. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The youngest sister of Margaret Lake, was the second wife of Gov. John Winthrop, Jr., and the mother of all his children. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Martha Epps, her eldest sister (widow) married for her second husband, Deputy Governor Samuel Symonds, of Ipswich, Mass. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas Read, the brother who came to this country and settled at Salem, Mass., where he was an ensign, returned to England, and entered Cromwell's army, was made a colonel, and assisted General Monk in the restoration of Charles I
1 CONC I., was placed in command of Sterling Castle. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Martha, the second daughter of Margaret Lake, married Thomas Harris of Ipswich, Mass., November 15, 1647. 
1 CONT
1 CONT John Lake, the husband of Margaret, never came to this country, died in England. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Margaret Lake died in Ipswich, Mass, 1672. Her will and inventory of her estate are in the appendix. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Source#2: Robert Charles Anderson. The Great Migration Begins: Immigrants to New England, 1620-1633 [database online] Provo, UT: Ancestry.com, 2000. Original data: Robert Charles Anderson. The Great Migration Begins: Immigrants to
1 CONC  New England, 1620-1633, vols. 1-3. Boston, MA: New England Historic Genealogical Society, 1995
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #3:  Walter Goodwin Davis, "Ancestry of John Lake, Husband of Margaret (READE) Lake"  in The New England Historic Genealogical Register (NEHGR 84: 304-317), July, 1930.
