1 CONT [143983.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT The Great Migration Begins  Vol 1 p 467
1 CONT
1 CONT Origin:  Leiden, Holland
1 CONT Migration:  1620 in Mayflower
1 CONT First Residence:  Plymouth
1 CONT
1 CONT Freeman:  in 1633 Plymouth list
1 CONT Able to bear arms in Plymouth section of 1643 Plymouth list.
1 CONT
1 CONT Birth:  in or shortly after 1583
1 CONT Death Plymouth 7 April 1663
1 CONT Marriage:  Leiden 20 Jul or shortly thereafter Hester Mahieu
1 CONT
1 CONT In his accounting of the pasengers on the Mayflower Bradford includes "Francis Cooke and his son John; but his wife and other children came afterwards"; in the summary of these families as of 1651, Bradford tells us that "Francis 
1 CONC Cooke is still living, a very old man and hath seen his children's children have children.  After his wife came over with other of his children; he hath three still living by her, all married and have five children, so their incre
1 CONC ase is eight.  And his son John which came over with him is married, and hath four children living.  Francis Cooke signed the Mayflower Compact.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT FRANCIS COOKE OF THE MAYFLOWER   p. 4  Francis Cooke and his son John embarked on the SPEEDWELL at Delfshaven, Holland in July 1620, leaving behind Hester and the other children.  At Southampton or Plymouth, England they were tran
1 CONC sferred to the Mayflower and in her set sail from Plymouth on Wednesday, 6 September 1620 (16 Sep 1620 new style)
1 CONT
1 CONT On 28 Dec. 1620 after the common house had been completed except for the thatching, "we took notice how many families there were...single men...to join with some family...that we might build fewer houses...and we reduced them to n
1 CONC ineteen families". Each of these 19 received a parcel of land about fifty feet deep.  Frontage was proportional to the number in the family, about eight feet per person. Lots were cast to decide each family's position in the "two 
1 CONC rows of houses" which were to be built "for more safety".  This allotment was for their use for only the year, to build houses and plant gardens, and they could not pass the land down to their heirs.  Francis Cook appears on a dia
1 CONC gram of "meersteads & garden plots of which came first laid out 1620" with his plot being on the south side of the street, with Isaac Allerton and Edward Winslow on his east and west.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT       Francis Cooke. Born, circa 1583, in England[1]. Died, 7 Apr
1 CONT 1663, in Plymouth, Massachusetts[2].
1 CONT
1 CONT       Nothing definite can be found on the ancestry of Francis
1 CONT Cooke. A  deliberate attempt to falsify his ancestry was made in
1 CONT "New light on the Pilgrim Story," by Thomas W. Mason [London :
1 CONT Congregational Union of England and Wales, 1920], p. 55. This
1 CONT fantastic account has been effectively disposed of (The English
1 CONT Ancestry and Homes of the Pilgrim Fathers, by Charles Edward
1 CONT Banks [Baltimore : Genealogical Publishing Co., 1962 reprint],
1 CONT pp. 47-48).
1 CONT
1 CONT       The Dutch records in Leyden show the names Franachoic Couck
1 CONT and bride  Hester Mahieu. Witnesses to the marriage were Walloons
1 CONT and Hester is probably of that race. With this evidence, it is
1 CONT fair to doubt any English ancestry of Francis Cooke (ibid)?
1 CONT
1 CONT       Francis Cooke may have been born in England of alien
1 CONT parents and returned to Holland six years before the arrival of
1 CONT the Pilgrim group. It is clear he had no prior association with
1 CONT the Pilgrims before their arrival in Holland (ibid).
1 CONT
1 CONT       The marriage of Francis Cooke is recorded in the Dutch
1 CONT records; the exact date of marriage is not given but probably 30
1 CONT June 1603. Frances Cooke was a woolcomber, originally from
1 CONT England. His wife Hester Mahieu came from Canterbury, England.
1 CONT She had a sister and mother both named Jennie (Families of the
1 CONT Pilgrims, p. 65).
1 CONT
1 CONT       Francis Cooke and his son John Left Holland in July 1620 on
1 CONT the  Speedwell, leaving behind his wife and other children. In
1 CONT England, father and son transferred to the Mayflower and they set
1 CONT sail from Plymouth, England in September 1620 (Ibid, p. 67).
1 CONT
1 CONT       After arriving at Plymouth, Massachusetts, Francis Cooke
1 CONT probably was  engaged in farming. His service on the grand
1 CONT inquest and trial juries indicates he had the respect and
1 CONT confidence of the community (Ibid, p. 66).
1 CONT
1 CONT       The Descendants of Edward Small contains a lengthy sketch
1 CONT on Francis  Cooke and family, starting on page 601 (Descendants
1 CONT of Edward Small of New England, by Lora Altine Woodbury Underhill
1 CONT [Boston and New York : Houghton Mifflin Company, revised edition,
1 CONT 1934].
1 CONT
1 CONT       Jeremy Dupertuis Bangs wrote an eighteen page essay in the
1 CONT New England  Historical and Genealogical Register entitled "The
1 CONT Pilgrims and other English in Leiden Records: Some new Pilgrim
1 CONT Documents." Dr. Bangs at the time was Chief Curator of Plimoth
1 CONT Plantation, and former Curator of the Leiden Pilgrim Documents
1 CONT Center in Leiden, The Netherlands (NEHGR, 143:Jul 1989:195-212).
1 CONT
1 CONT       The Leiden records show the betrothal of Francis Cooke and
1 CONT Hester Mahieu, both of England, dated 30 June 1603. "Franchoijs
1 CONT Couck" is identified as a bachelor from England, occupation
1 CONT woolcomber. Hester is described as an unmarried from Canterbury,
1 CONT England. Couck was accompanied by two witnesses, Phillippe de
1 CONT Veau and Raphael Roelandt; Hester was accompanied by her mother
1 CONT and her sister, both named Jenne (Jeanne) Mahieu (ibid, pp.
1 CONT 195-196).
1 CONT
1 CONT       The betrothal record was in a register listing couples
1 CONT whose weddings  were solemnized in the Dutch Reformed Church or
1 CONT the Walloon Reformed Church. This register was not used by
1 CONT members of English Separatist or Pilgrim congregations. Although
1 CONT the Cooke-Mahieu marriage banns was published, the actual
1 CONT marriage is not recorded in the Dutch Reformed Church records, as
1 CONT was customary. The marriage was probably solemnized in the Leiden
1 CONT Walloon Reformed Church, but there is no proof (ibid, p. 196).
1 CONT
1 CONT       The records show that the Cooke-Mahieu couple lived in
1 CONT Norwich among the  Walloons there, probably from 8 August 1606
1 CONT until 1 January 1608. Before and after this Norwich residence,
1 CONT they were members of the Leiden Walloon congregation (ibid, p.
1 CONT 197).
1 CONT
1 CONT       Hester Mahieu and Francis Cooke appear much less English
1 CONT than their names and birthplaces indicate (ibid 199).
1 CONT
1 CONT       He married Hester Mahieu, daughter of --------- Mahieu and
1 CONT Jennie  ---------, 1603, in Leyden, Holland[3]. Children:
1 CONT
1 CONT             i.  John Cooke. Born, circa 1605, in Holland[4].
1 CONT Died, 23 Nov 1695, in Dartmouth, Bristol, Massachusetts[4].
1 CONT Baptism: 1607, in Leyden, Holland[5]. Prob of Est: 16 Apr 1696,
1 CONT in Dartmouth, Bristol, Massachusetts[6]. He married Sarah Warren,
1 CONT 28 Mar 1634, in Plymouth, Massachusetts[4].
1 CONT
1 CONT            ii.  Elizabeth Cooke. Born, circa 1611, in Leiden,
1 CONT Holland[7].  Baptism: 26 Dec 1611, in Leiden, Holland[7].
1 CONT
1 CONT           iii.  Jane Cooke. Born, before 1613, in Holland[4].
1 CONT Died, before  1651[8]. She married Experience Mitchell.
1 CONT
1 CONT            iv.  Jacob Cooke. Born, circa 1618, in Holland[4].
1 CONT Died, Dec 1675,  in Plymouth, Massachusetts[4]. He married,
1 CONT first, Damaris Hopkins, 1647[4]. He married, second, Elizabeth
1 CONT Lettice, 18 Nov 1669, in Plymouth, Massachusetts[4].
1 CONT
1 CONT             v.  Hester Cooke. Born, circa 1620, in Holland[5].
1 CONT Died, after  1669[9]. She married Richard Wright, 1644, in
1 CONT Plymouth, Massachusetts[10].
1 CONT
1 CONT            vi.  Mary Cooke. Born, circa 1624, in Plymouth,
1 CONT Massachusetts[10].  Died, 21 Mar 1713/4, in Middleborough,
1 CONT Plymouth, Massachusetts[11]. She married John Tomson, 26 Dec
1 CONT 1645, in Plymouth, Massachusetts[10].
1 CONT
