1 CONT
1 CONT  John Cooke was
1 CONT  baptized at Leyden,
1 CONT  Holland between January
1 CONT  and March 1607/8 and
1 CONT  was, thus, about thirteen
1 CONT  years old on arrival at
1 CONT  Plymouth with his father,
1 CONT  Francis Cooke in 1620 on
1 CONT  the Mayflower. He
1 CONT  married in Plymouth on
1 CONT  March 28, 1634, Sarah
1 CONT  Warren, daughter of
1 CONT  Pilgrim Richard and
1 CONT  Elizabeth Warren of the
1 CONT  Mayflower.
1 CONT
1 CONT  John became a deacon of
1 CONT  the Plymouth Church in
1 CONT  the 1630's, but he was
1 CONT  excommunicated from
1 CONT  the church ca. 1757.
1 CONT  Probably around the time
1 CONT  he was excommunicated,
1 CONT  he became a Baptist. He
1 CONT  was a Baptist preacher
1 CONT  and about 1680
1 CONT  established a Baptist
1 CONT  church in what is now
1 CONT  Tiverton, near Adamsville.
1 CONT  This was the beginning of
1 CONT  the society which has
1 CONT  included members of the
1 CONT  Wilcox and allied families
1 CONT  since its founding and
1 CONT  which has worshipped in
1 CONT  the Old Stone Church
1 CONT  since it was built in 1841.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT    Sam Behling
1 CONT
1 CONT He served on juries and on various special assignments, and was a long-time
1 CONT Plymouth deputy. In 1664 he was granted 15 acres of land near Dartmouth
1 CONT and he had other significant land dealings in the area, owning 3/34 of the
1 CONT original grant. Sometime between 1653 and 1660, he moved to that part of
1 CONT Dartmouth now known as Oxford Village, Fairhaven. In 1672, the town of
1 CONT Dartmouth gave him Ram (now Pope's) Island in recompense for former
1 CONT services.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1666 he was a deputy for Dartmouth. John was one of the advisers for the
1 CONT defense of Dartmouth against the Indians. He became a magistrate for
1 CONT Dartmouth authorized to marry, to administer oaths, and to issue warrants for
1 CONT cour trials at Plymouth. He served the town several years as a selectman and
1 CONT was ten times a representative to the General Court. He was one of the
1 CONT owners of the first vessels built in Plymouth. He was a constant trader in
1 CONT lands at Plymouth and Dartmouth and owned lands at Puncatest.
1 CONT
1 CONT Prior to the Indian uprising (King Phillip's War) in 1675 he converted his home
1 CONT into a garrison house. This was the haven of safety of the inhabitants in the
1 CONT early spring of 1676. His home was later burned by the savages.
1 CONT
1 CONT John Cooke was the last surviving male passenger of the Mayflower. John
1 CONT died 23 Nov 1695, having lived through the entire life of the Plymouth Colony.
1 CONT The will of John Cooke of Dartmouth, County of Bristol dated November 9,
1 CONT 1694, proved April 16, 1696, mentions wife Sarah; son-in-law Arthur Hathaway
1 CONT and his wife Sarah his daughter; son-in-law Stephen West and his wife Mercy
1 CONT his daughter.; Jonathan Delano; grandson Thomas Taber; and
1 CONT granddaughter Hester Perry. On December 7, 1696 widow Sarah Cooke
1 CONT presented the inventory.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Fairhaven a short distance north of the New Bedford-Fairhaven bridge,
1 CONT near the river, is a large boulder to which is attached a bronze table, on
1 CONT which, below a representation of the Mayflower is an inscription. A picture of
1 CONT this appears on this page. The exact location of the grave of John Cooke is,
1 CONT however, a matter of doubt. Somewhere in Fairhaven is about as definite as it
1 CONT can now be determined. The boulder with its inscription was placed 200 years
1 CONT after his death. That he as the "first white settler of this town" applies to
1 CONT Fairhaven, rather than old Dartmouth.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Francis Cooke was a 1620 Mayflower passenger originally from Blythe,
1 CONT Yorkshire, England and later of Kent County. The records in Holland describe
1 CONT him as a woolcomber and he appeared there before the arrival of the
1 CONT Clyfton/Robinson Separatists.
1 CONT
1 CONT The date of his marriage to Hester Mahieu in Leyden, Holland has often been
1 CONT printed incorrectly (e.g., 30 June 1603). However, an article in Mayflower
1 CONT Descendant 27:145-55 (New Light on Francis Cooke and His Wife Hester
1 CONT Mahieu and Their Son John) goes to great pains to give an estimated date
1 CONT and states the the previous published date was incorrect. Marriage intentions
1 CONT were entered July 4, 1603 and Luly 5, 1603 which means the three banns were
1 CONT proclaimed July 6, July 13 and July 20 (three successive Sundays); therefore,
1 CONT the marriage took place on or after July 20, 1603. Hester Mahieu, the daugther
1 CONT of Jennie le Mahieu of Canterbury, England died. after June 8, 1666 in
1 CONT Plymouth.
1 CONT
1 CONT Francis Cooke appears frequently in Plymouth records on grand and trial
1 CONT juries, as a surveyor of the highways, on various ad hoc committees, and in a
1 CONT number of land transactions. He came to Plymouth with son John, and his
1 CONT wife and their daughter, Jane and son, Jacob arrived on the ship Anne in
1 CONT 1623. Two more children, Hester and Mary, were born at Plymouth. Francis
1 CONT died April 7, 1663 "above 80" years of age.
1 CONT
1 CONT William Bradford's 1651 account says, "Francis Cooke is still living, a very
1 CONT olde man, and hath seene his children's children have children; after his wife
1 CONT came over (with other of this children) he hath 3 still livng by her, all maried
1 CONT and have 5 children so their increase is 8. And his sone John which came over
1 CONT with him, is maried and hath 4 children living."
1 CONT
1 CONT The will of Francis Cooke of Plymouth, dated 7th 10th mo. 1659, exhibited 5
1 CONT June 1663, names wife Hester and son John. His inventory is dated May 1,
1 CONT 1663. An agreement made June 8, 1666 between John Cooke, Jacob Cooke,
1 CONT Hester Wright the wife of Richard Wright and Mary Tomson the wife of John
1 CONT Tompson disposed of the land of Francis Cooke. The agreement mentions
1 CONT Hester Cookie is still living. On the same date John Cooke confirmed to
1 CONT Richard Wright and Thomas Mitchell, in equal shares, sixty acres of upland,
1 CONT near Jones River Meadow formerly given them by Francis Cooke. On July 5,
1 CONT 1670 a court record mentions that land called "old Cookes Holes," lying at
1 CONT Jones River was given by Francis Cooke to Richard Wright and Thomas
1 CONT Mitchell and since his (Francis) decease confirmed unto Richard Wright and
1 CONT Thomas Mitchell by John Cooke. On 1 Aug. 1672 Thomas Mitchell of Duxbury
1 CONT sold to Richard Wright of Plymouth his share in the above grant and states it
1 CONT was given to him by his grandfather, Francis Cooke.
1 CONT
1 CONT                            Will of Francis Cooke
1 CONT
1 CONT The last Will and Testament of ffrancis Cooke of Plymouth late Deceased,
1 CONT exhibited before the Court held att Plymouth aforsaid the fifth day of June
1 CONT 1663 on the oathes of mr John Aldin and mr John howland;
1 CONT
1 CONT The Last Will and Testament of ffrancis Cooke made this seaventh of the
1 CONT tenth month 1659.
1 CONT
1 CONT I being att prsent weake and Infeirme in body yett in prfect memory throw
1 CONT mercy Doe comitt my soule unto god that gave it and my body to the earthe;
1 CONT which my will is should bee Intered in a Decent and comly manner; As for
1 CONT such goods and lands as I stand posessed of I Doe will and bequeath as
1 CONT followeth;
1 CONT
1 CONT My will is that hester my Dear and loveing wife shall have all my moveable
1 CONT goods and all my Cattle of all kinds; viz: neat Cattle horsekind sheep and
1 CONT swine to be att her Dispose.
1 CONT
1 CONT My will is that hester my wife shall have and Injoy my lands both upland and
1 CONT meddow lands which att prsent I posesse During her life.
1 CONT
1 CONT I Doe ordaine and appoint my Deare wife and my son John Cooke Joynt
1 CONT exequitors of this my said will.
1 CONT
1 CONT Witnes
1 CONT John Aldin
1 CONT ffrancis Cooke
1 CONT John howland
1 CONT  John Cooke was
1 CONT  baptized at Leyden,
1 CONT  Holland between January
1 CONT  and March 1607/8 and
1 CONT  was, thus, about thirteen
1 CONT  years old on arrival at
1 CONT  Plymouth with his father,
1 CONT  Francis Cooke in 1620 on
1 CONT  the Mayflower. He
1 CONT  married in Plymouth on
1 CONT  March 28, 1634, Sarah
1 CONT  Warren, daughter of
1 CONT  Pilgrim Richard and
1 CONT  Elizabeth Warren of the
1 CONT  Mayflower.
1 CONT
1 CONT  John became a deacon of
1 CONT  the Plymouth Church in
1 CONT  the 1630's, but he was
1 CONT  excommunicated from
1 CONT  the church ca. 1757.
1 CONT  Probably around the time
1 CONT  he was excommunicated,
1 CONT  he became a Baptist. He
1 CONT  was a Baptist preacher
1 CONT  and about 1680
1 CONT  established a Baptist
1 CONT  church in what is now
1 CONT  Tiverton, near Adamsville.
1 CONT  This was the beginning of
1 CONT  the society which has
1 CONT  included members of the
1 CONT  Wilcox and allied families
1 CONT  since its founding and
1 CONT  which has worshipped in
1 CONT  the Old Stone Church
1 CONT  since it was built in 1841.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT                                        Photo © 1995, Sam Behling
1 CONT
1 CONT He served on juries and on various special assignments, and was a long-time
1 CONT Plymouth deputy. In 1664 he was granted 15 acres of land near Dartmouth
1 CONT and he had other significant land dealings in the area, owning 3/34 of the
1 CONT original grant. Sometime between 1653 and 1660, he moved to that part of
1 CONT Dartmouth now known as Oxford Village, Fairhaven. In 1672, the town of
1 CONT Dartmouth gave him Ram (now Pope's) Island in recompense for former
1 CONT services.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1666 he was a deputy for Dartmouth. John was one of the advisers for the
1 CONT defense of Dartmouth against the Indians. He became a magistrate for
1 CONT Dartmouth authorized to marry, to administer oaths, and to issue warrants for
1 CONT cour trials at Plymouth. He served the town several years as a selectman and
1 CONT was ten times a representative to the General Court. He was one of the
1 CONT owners of the first vessels built in Plymouth. He was a constant trader in
1 CONT lands at Plymouth and Dartmouth and owned lands at Puncatest.
1 CONT
1 CONT Prior to the Indian uprising (King Phillip's War) in 1675 he converted his home
1 CONT into a garrison house. This was the haven of safety of the inhabitants in the
1 CONT early spring of 1676. His home was later burned by the savages.
1 CONT
1 CONT John Cooke was the last surviving male passenger of the Mayflower. John
1 CONT died 23 Nov 1695, having lived through the entire life of the Plymouth Colony.
1 CONT The will of John Cooke of Dartmouth, County of Bristol dated November 9,
1 CONT 1694, proved April 16, 1696, mentions wife Sarah; son-in-law Arthur Hathaway
1 CONT and his wife Sarah his daughter; son-in-law Stephen West and his wife Mercy
1 CONT his daughter.; Jonathan Delano; grandson Thomas Taber; and
1 CONT granddaughter Hester Perry. On December 7, 1696 widow Sarah Cooke
1 CONT presented the inventory.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Fairhaven a short distance north of the New Bedford-Fairhaven bridge,
1 CONT near the river, is a large boulder to which is attached a bronze table, on
1 CONT which, below a representation of the Mayflower is an inscription. A picture of
1 CONT this appears on this page. The exact location of the grave of John Cooke is,
1 CONT however, a matter of doubt. Somewhere in Fairhaven is about as definite as it
1 CONT can now be determined. The boulder with its inscription was placed 200 years
1 CONT after his death. That he as the "first white settler of this town" applies to
1 CONT Fairhaven, rather than old Dartmouth.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
