0 @N6000000001870029793@ NOTE Came to America on the Mayflower.
0 @N6000000003796671329@ NOTE Mayflower Passenger, Richard Warren
1 CONT URL:  http://homepages.rootsweb.com/~sam/rwarren.html
1 CONT
1 CONT A 1620 Mayflower passenger, Richard Warren was a merchant of London, England who joined the Pilgrims at Southampton and became the twelfth signer of the Mayflower Compact. Although William Bradford in his "decreasings and increasi
1 CONC ngs" gives Richard Warren the honorific title "Mr.", he does not mention him at all in the text of his history of Plymouth Plantation, and very little is known about him except for a few brief mentions elsewhere. Bradford says of 
1 CONC him, "Grave Richard Warren, a man of integrity, justice and uprightness, of piety and serious religion, a useful citizen, bearing a deep share of the difficulties and troubles of the plantation."
1 CONT
1 CONT In Mourt's Relation Edward Winslow lists ten men on an early expedition at Cape Cod, including Richard Warren, who were from London. He was "a useful instrument; bore a deep share in the difficulties and troubles of settlement," a
1 CONC ccording to Nathaniel Morton in his New England's Memorial. Judging from land transactions of his widow, Elizabeth, the family appears to have been one of the wealthier ones at Plymouth. In the division of cattle in 1627 shares we
1 CONC re given to him, to his wife Elizabeth, and to their children.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard Warren was probably born in England between 1580 and 1590 and died in Plymouth in 1628. He married prior to 1610 Elizabeth. Despite what has previously appeared in print (e.g. Marsh, Jowett) her maiden name is unknown. She
1 CONC  was born about 1580 and died in Plymouth October 2, 1673 aged above 90 years. She came over in 1623 on the ship Anne with daughters Abigail, Anne, Elizabeth, Mary and Sarah. Richard and Elizabeth Warren had two sons born at Plymo
1 CONC uth, Nathaniel, who married Sarah Walker, and Joseph, who married Priscilla Faunce. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Further Suggested Reading
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Of Plymouth Plantation; William Bradford; Modern Library College Edition, 1981
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Saints and Strangers; George F. Willison; 1945
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Plymouth Colony, Its History & People, 1620-1691; Eugene Aubrey Stratton; Ancestry Inc., Salt Lake City, UT; 1986
1 CONT
1 CONT     * New England's Memorial; Nathaniel Morton; 1669
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Mourt's Relation: A Journal of the Pilgrims at Plymouth; Edward Winslow & William Bradford; 1621
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT ============================================
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT  Richard Warren
1 CONT
1 CONT BORN:   England.  Called "of London" in Mourt's Relation (1622).
1 CONT DIED:   1628, Plymouth.  
1 CONT MARRIED:   Elizabeth (---).  She died 2 October 1673, Plymouth "aged above ninety".
1 CONT
1 CONT CHILDREN:
1 CONT NAME BIRTH DEATH MARRIAGE
1 CONT Mary c1610, England 27 March 1683, Plymouth Robert Bartlett, after 22 May 1627
1 CONT Anna c1612, England after 19 February 1675/6, Marshfield, MA Thomas Little, 19 April 1633, Plymouth
1 CONT Sarah c1614, England after 15 July 1696 John Cooke, 28 March 1634, Plymouth
1 CONT Elizabeth c1616, England 9 March 1669/70, Hingham, MA Richard Church, before 14 March 1635, Plymouth
1 CONT Abigail c1618, England after 3 January 1692/3, Marshfield, MA Anthony Snow, 8 November 1639, Plymouth
1 CONT Nathaniel c1625, Plymouth between 16 July and 21 October 1667, Plymouth Sarah Walker, 19 November 1645, Plymouth
1 CONT Joseph c1626, Plymouth 4 May 1689, Plymouth Priscilla Faunce, c1652, Plymouth
1 CONT
1 CONT ANCESTRAL SUMMARY:
1 CONT
1 CONT More erroneous information has been published about Richard Warren than any other Mayflower passenger, probably because he has so many descendants (note that all seven of his children grew up and married).  It is time here to debu
1 CONC nk many of the mistakes that have been published over the past hundred years.
1 CONT
1 CONT Common mistake #1.  Richard Warren's wife is not Elizabeth (Jewett/Jonatt/Juett) Marsh.  This is easily disproven.  Elizabeth (Jewett) Marsh was born in 1614, which makes her not only younger than Richard Warren's two oldest child
1 CONC ren, but also makes her only fourteen years old when Richard Warren died.  [Mayflower Descendant 2:63].
1 CONT
1 CONT Common mistake #2.  Richard Warren is not a proven descendant of any royalty, whether it be Sir John de Warrene or Charlemagne.  Richard Warren's parents have not even been identified, despite extensive searches in the records of 
1 CONC England (see the Mayflower Quarterly, 51:109-112 for a summary of one such search).
1 CONT
1 CONT The only concrete things we know about Richard Warren's ancestry are that he was a merchant of London--whether he was born there or not is an entirely different question.  We also know that his wife was named Elizabeth.  He had fi
1 CONC ve daughters baptized in England somewhere, and perhaps the true records will some day be brought to light.  
1 CONT
1 CONT There is a Richard Warren who married an Elizabeth Evans on 1 January 1592/3 in St. Leonards, and a Richard Warren who married an Elizabeth Doucke on 1 November 1596 in Sidmouth, Devon.  However, since Richard's first child was bo
1 CONC rn about 1610, a marriage in 1592 or 1596 seems most unlikely.  
1 CONT
1 CONT The I.G.I. lists the following baptisms of Richard Warrens from its parish register abstractions (1570-1588):
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 January 1570, St. Columb Minor, Cornwall (son of Lawrence)
1 CONT     * 25 March 1571, St. Matthew Friday Street, London
1 CONT     * 20 January 1572, Bishops Nympton, Devon (son of Baldwine)
1 CONT     * 18 January 1573, Burbage, Leicester (son of John)
1 CONT     * 2 October 1576, Phillack, Cornwall (son of John)
1 CONT     * 9 August 1580, Sandy, Bedford (son of William)
1 CONT     * 24 January 1580, St. Peter Cornhill, London (son of John)
1 CONT     * 12 June 1580, St. Giles Cripplegate, London (son of John)
1 CONT     * 1 October 1581, Harrow on the Hill, London
1 CONT     * 26 January 1583, Haughley, Suffolk (son of George)
1 CONT     * 11 August 1583, Darford, Kent (son of William)
1 CONT     * 28 August 1585, Redruth, Cornwall (son of Benet)
1 CONT     * 12 April 1585, Abbey, St. Albans, Hertford (son of William)
1 CONT     * 2 March 1586, St. Mary, Stoke Newington, London (son of Robert)
1 CONT     * 25 December 1587, Shillington, Bedford (son of Robert)
1 CONT     * 3 September 1587, St. Andrew by the Wardrobe, London 
1 CONT
1 CONT As should be plainly obvious, there were many Richard Warrens in England.  If the true Richard Warren is ever identified, it will almost certainly be because the baptisms of his five daughters which should be somewhere in England.
1 CONC  There are a few other small clues which may aid researchers looking for records.  First, early Charlestown settler Ralph Spague married Joanna Warren, daughter of a Richard Warren from Fordington St. George, Dorset.  This Richard
1 CONC  Warren died in Fordington, Dorset before 1638.  And early Watertown settler John Warren was baptized in Nayland, Suffolk, England in 1585, and he apparently had an uncle named Richard Warren.
1 CONT
1 CONT BIOGRAPHICAL SUMMARY:
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard Warren appears to have been a merchant, who resided in London, and became associated with the Pilgrims and the Mayflower through the Merchant Adventurers.  Richard Warren participated in several of the early explorations m
1 CONC ade by the Pilgrims in 1620, while looking for a place to settle.  He appears by land records to have been fairly well-to-do.  
1 CONT
1 CONT When he came over on the Mayflower, he left behind his wife and five daughters, planning to have them sent over after things were more settled in the Colony.  His wife and daughters arrived in America in 1623, on the ship Anne.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nathaniel Morton wrote in his book New England's Memorial, first published in 1669, the following about Richard Warren:  
1 CONT
1 CONT This year [1628] died Mr. Richard Warren, who was an useful instrument and during his life bare a deep share in the difficulties and troubles of the first settlement of the Plantation of New Plymouth.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard Warren is an ancestor to many famous Americans.  Among them are Presidents Ulysses S. Grant and Franklin D. Roosevelt; and Alan B. Shepard, Jr., the first American in space and fifth man to walk on the moon.  A published l
1 CONC ineage showing Winston Churchill as a descendant of Richard Warren has a questionable generation and is most likely in error.  However, Winston Churchill does appear to be a descendant of Mayflower passenger John Howland's brother
1 CONC  Arthur.
1 CONT
1 CONT SOURCES:
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert S. Wakefield, Mayflower Families in Progress: Richard Warren for Four Generations (Plymouth: General Society of Mayflower Descendants, 1991).
1 CONT
1 CONT Ruth Berg Walsh, "The Search for Pilgrim Richard Warren's Parentage," Mayflower Quarterly, 51:109-112.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eugene Aubrey Stratton, Plymouth Colony, Its History and Its People, 1620-1691 (Salt Lake City: Ancestor Publishers, 1986).
1 CONT
1 CONT Nathaniel Morton, New England's Memorial (Cambridge, 1669).
1 CONT
1 CONT William Bradford, Of Plymouth Plantation, ed. Samuel Morison (New York: Random House, 1952).
1 CONT
1 CONT Mayflower Web Pages.  Caleb Johnson © 1998
