0 @N6000000007417918827@ NOTE Son of (Christopher, Thomas, John) of South Kilworth, co. Leicester, Eng la nd, and of Saugus (Lynn), Mass., Sandwich in the Plymouth Colony, and P ort smouth, R.I., gentleman, only son of his father, born, probably at Dun ton- Ba
1 CONC ssett or South Kilworth, co. Leicester, about 1600.*Died, probab ly at Portsmouth, R.I., between 28 Feb 1676/7, when he made h is will, a nd 23 Apr 1677, when his will was proved.He was in the Massachusetts Bay C olony in 1631, re
1 CONC turned later to Englan d, and with his wife Audrey and t wo children, Anna (or Annis) and Christop her, embarked again for New Engl and in the "Abigail" in 1635.* According to Pope's Pioneers of Massachuset ts he deposed 20 (4) 16
1 CONC 54, ag ed about 53 years.
0 @N6000000003490614860@ NOTE
1 CONT He is first mentioned in Plymouth Colony records in March of 1636/37 when Francis Cooke brought charges against him and some other young men who had abused Cooke's cattle. (PCR 7:5)  He became a freeman in 1654 (PCR3:48)  
0 @N6000000003076523675@ NOTE [Brøderbund WFT European Origins Vol. E1, Ed. 1, Tree #0003, Date of Import: Dec 25, 1999]
1 CONT
1 CONT he was a cooper from Norwich Parish, Norfolk, England
1 CONT he had gone to Holland in his early childhood and lived in Rotterdam where he was a brewer
1 CONT He and his wife had come to America in August of 1623 with 3 children
1 CONT he cam to America on the Little James, a 44 ton ship that had been built for the Pilrgims in Leyden
1 CONT His will is printed in The Mayflower Descendant
1 CONT dated December 28, 1643
1 CONT he names his wife and 5 children including Samuel and Abigail, his eldest daughter
1 CONT she was to "Dwell one full year with Mr. Charles Chauncey of Scituate before marrying Henry Wood."
1 CONT Sarah, his widow was one of the first to purchase Dartmouth in 1652.
