0 @N6000000004549573297@ NOTE Title Descendants of John (1) and Sarah (Carey) Jenney through Ansel (8) and Elizabeth (Brown) Jenne and their children 
1 CONT Authors Carroll, Virginia E. (Virginia Eleanor), 1909- (Main Author)    929.273 J431c 
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes Cover title: A genealogy of the Jenne family.
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Bibliography interspersed.
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Includes index (listing Jene, Jeney, Jenney and Jenny as variant spellings of Jenne).
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Colored illustration attached to leaf 75.
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT "John Jenny, the first Jenney ancestor in America, was born in England about 1584, and died in Plymouth, Massachusetts in May 1644. It is thought he may be the son of Henry and Mary (Smyth(e)) Jenny. He was from Norwich, County No
1 CONC rfolk."--P. 16. He later fled to Holland to avoid religious persecution. While there living in Leyden, John married Sarah Carey on 1 November 1614. "Sarah was from Monk Soham (Moncksoon)which is twenty-five miles south of Norwich 
1 CONC and in County Suffolk [England]. John and his family left " ... Holland sailing on the [ship] Little James, arriving in Plymouth on the 23rd of August 1623."--P. 16. John's will was proved on 30 June 1644. His wive Sarah "lived te
1 CONC n to eleven years longer than he."--P. 18. Descendants lived in New England, New York, Ohio, Iowa, Florida and elsewhere. Includes ancestors in England to the 1300s.
1 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Includes Jenne, Jenney, Jenny, Griffin, Lindsley, Lockwood, McIlrath, Pope, Wing, Allen, Appleson, Aufmuth, Badcock, Barnes, Benson, Block, Brainard, Brigham, Carroll, Clark, Conger, Cook, Croll, Driscoll, Eirons, Eller, Fair, For
1 CONC tman, Frabotta, Hearn, Heth, Hicks, Hopkins, Kenney, Kuder, Landon, LoPresti, Lyons, McQueen, Marsico, Matzek, Miller, Mitchell, Nelson, Parr, Perry, Quinn, Richmond, Rider, Rigg, Roberts, Shafer, Shearman, Shiltz, Smith, Snyder, 
1 CONC Spencer, Tahl, Tobey, Vaughn, Ward, Wilson, Wood, Wright and related families. 
0 @N6000000002329577139@ NOTE John Jenney was from Norwich, England. He had moved to Leyden by 1614, when he married Sarah Carey of Monk Sohan, Suffolk, England.  John Jenney was a brewer and a miller. He and his wife, with their 2 living children, journeyed t
1 CONC o Plymouth on 
1 CONT the Little James in 1623. Another son was born on board ship.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT John and Sarah had 7 children in all: Samuel, who was born in Leiden and journeyed with his parents to Plymouth; an unnamed child who died as an infant and is buried in Leiden; Abigail, who also journeyed with her parents to Plymo
1 CONC uth; an 
1 CONT unnamed son who was born in 1623 aboard the Little James but who died before 1627; Sarah, John and Susanna, all born in Plymouth.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Nathaniel Morton characterized Jenney as "a godly, though otherwise a plain man, yet singular for publicness of spirit, setting himself to seek and promote the common good of the plantation of new Plimouth." Jenney was involved in
1 CONC  refinancing 
1 CONT the Plantation in 1626 and served several terms as an Assistant to the Governor as well as in other positions of resonsibility within the Colony.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT John Jenney is best known, however, for operating Plymouth Colony’s third (and most successful) corn mill. The earliest corn mill had been located near Billington Sea, a distance from town. Then, in 1632, the General Court authori
1 CONC zed Stephen 
1 CONT Deane to set up a water-powered corn mill on Town Brook; this mill was only in operation for about 2 years when Stephen Deane died. In 1636, Jenney was authorized to "erect a mill for grinding and beating of corn upon the brook of
1 CONC  Plymouth."
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT John operated the mill until his death in 1644. After his death, Sarah operated the corn mill. The mill was then carried on by their son Samuel and then by outsiders, until its demise in 1847. Sarah died in late 1655 or early 1666
1 CONC . We have 
1 CONT wills and inventories for both John and Sarah. John’s inventory includes several books and a "smale globe," indicating some education and a curiosity, perhaps, about the world. Sarah Jenney’s inventory included these same books, b
1 CONC ut the globe
1 CONT is not listed.
0 @N6000000003075903402@ NOTE [Brøderbund WFT European Origins Vol. E1, Ed. 1, Tree #0003, Date of Import: Dec 25, 1999]
