0 @N6000000000337381266@ NOTE Source #1:  Walter Goodwin Davis, "Ancestry of John Lake, Husband of Margaret (READE) Lake"  in The New England Historic Genealogical Register (NEHGR 84: 304-317), July, 1930.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eldest son and heir.  Also was a legatee in the will of his uncle, Thomas Sandell, dated 10 Octobet 1593.
1 CONT
1 CONT By 1622 he had sold the properties in Basildon.  It would seem that he disposed also of the North Benfleet manors before 1636.  Sometime between 1631 and 1635 John Lake's wife, Mrs. Margaret (Reade) Lake, left him and emigrated wi
1 CONC th her sister and their families to New England, taking with her her two daughters, Ann (called Hannah in the New England records) and Martha Lake.  There were three sons by the marriage, one who died in infancy.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1654 Rev. Hugh Peter, Mrs. Lake's stepfather, wrote from London to John Winthrop, Jr.: "John Lake is alive and lusty;"  and in 1657 he stated to the same correspondent: "John Lake live(s) still."  In January, 1661/2, Mrs. Lake 
1 CONC wrote to her brother-in-law, Governor Winthrop, who was then in London, asking him to look into the particulars of her husband's death and estate, making mention of her cousin, Thomas Cooke.
