0 @N6000000000337357943@ NOTE Source #1: Gallup, John D. Genealogy Of The Gallup Family. Press of the Hartford Printing Co. Hartford Conneticut. 1893. 
1 CONT
1 CONT John Gallop, the ancestor of most of the families of that name in this country, came to America from the Parish of Mosterne, County Dorset, England, in the year 1630. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He was son of John Gollop, who married (???) Crabbe, who was son of Thomas and Agnes (Watkins) Gollop, of North Bowood and Strode, and whose descendants still own and occupy the manors of Strode. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He was thirty-three years old at the time of the visitation of Dorset, 1623. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Christobel, whose full name does not appear, and careful research has failed to discover it. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He sailed from Plymouth, England, March 20, 1630, in the ship Mary and John, arriving at Nantasket, now Hull, May 30th. His wife and children following in 1633. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He went first to Dorchester, but was soon after a resident of Boston. He was admitted to the First Church, January 6, 1634. His wife, Christobel, June 22, 1634. He was made a freeman in April of same year. "He was one of the earli
1 CONC est grantees of land at the northerly part of the town, where he had a wharf-right and house." The locality was known by the name of Gallop's point, and was the southeast part of the peninsula, at the north end of the town. 
1 CONT
1 CONT We find his name first in the town records in 1636, on page 10, where occurs the following entry: "Item: It is ordered that John Gallop shall remove his payles at his yarde ende within 14 days, and to rainge them even with the cor
1 CONC ner of his house, for the preserving of the way upon the Sea Bancke." This was probably the origin of Middle street, now known as Hanover street.--Extract from an article in Boston Transcript, April 13, 1883, by James H. Stark. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He owned Gallup's Island, where he had a snug farm, with a meadow on Long Island, a sheep pasture on Nix Mate, and a house in Boston. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a skilful mariner, well acquainted with the harbor around Boston, and in the habit of making frequent trading expeditions along the coast in his own vessels. One of these expeditions was made forever memorable by the encoun
1 CONC ter of the murderers of his friend, John Oldham. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [For full account of this first naval exploit off Block Island, July, 1636, see Appendix.]
1 CONT After the settlement of Rhode Island and Connecticut, his vessel furnished about the only means of communication between the two colonies. At one time there was considerable anxiety in the Rhode Island colony, for John Gallup was 
1 CONC delayed in his trip. Soon after, Roger Williams writes Governor Winthrop, beginning in this manner: "God be praised John Gallop has arrived." 
1 CONT He achieved great distinction by piloting in the ship Griffin, a ship of 300 tons, in September, 1633, through a new found channel, when she had on board the Rev. John Cotton, the Rev. Thos. Hooker, Rev. Mr. Stone, and other fathe
1 CONC rs of New England among her two hundred passengers. Besides these, it is supposed that Mr. Gallop's wife and children were on board. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He died in Boston, January 11, 1650. His wife, Christobel, died in Boston, September 27, 1655. His will and that of his wife's are among the earliest on record, and may be found in the Appendix.(*) 
1 CONT
1 CONT In this connection, an extract from a letter written by Governor Winthrop to the Rev. John White, of England, referring to John of Masterne, will be enjoyed: 
1 CONT
1 CONT (+) "I have much difficultye to keepe John Gallop here, by reason his wife will not come. I marvayle at the woman's weaknesse. I pray, persuade and further her coming by all means. If she will come let her have the remainder of hi
1 CONC s wages; if not, let it be bestowed to bring over his children, for so he desired. It would be about œ40 losse to him to come for he
1 CONT
1 CONT            Your assured in the Lord's worke,              J. WINTHRO
1 CONT     MASSACHUSETTS, July 4, 1632." 
1 CONT Possibly, Governor Winthrop's pleasure, that the "woman's weaknesse" had been overcome, enabling her to join her husband, prompted the gift of Gallop's-Island in commemoration of the successful piloting, by her happy husband, of t
1 CONC he ship which bore her and so many distinguished ones safely into Boston Harbor. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT (*)Gathered from Winthrop's History. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT (+)Found among Gov. Winthrop's papers. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #2:  The Gallup Family Association, Inc, "Gallup Genealogy" (Provo, Utah:  Brigham Young University, Family History and Genealogy Research Services, 2nd edition, 1987), pp. 3-16
1 CONT
1 CONT Source#3: Robert Charles Anderson. The Great Migration Begins: Immigrants to New England, 1620-1633 [database online] Provo, UT: Ancestry.com, 2000. Original data: Robert Charles Anderson. The Great Migration Begins: Immigrants to
1 CONC  New England, 1620-1633, vols. 1-3. Boston, MA: New England Historic Genealogical Society, 1995
1 CONT
1 CONT   JOHN GALLOP 
1 CONT
1 CONT ORIGIN: Bridport, Dorsetshire
1 CONT
1 CONT MIGRATION: 1630
1 CONT
1 CONT FIRST RESIDENCE: Boston
1 CONT
1 CONT OCCUPATION: Fisherman. Mariner. "John Gallop hath written to some of your neighbors for twelve doz. of cod lines, if he provide them and bring them to you I pray deliver him this bill," 4 July 1632 [WP 3:87]
1 CONT
1 CONT CHURCH MEMBERSHIP: "John Gallop fisherman" admitted to Boston church 5 January 1633/4 [BChR 17].
1 CONT
1 CONT FREEMAN: 1 April 1634 (as third in a group of six Dorchester men) [MBCR 1:368].
1 CONT
1 CONT EDUCATION: Son Nathaniel could write most imaginatively [WP 6:65], but John made his mark to his will.
1 CONT
1 CONT ESTATE: On 8 September 1636 there are "twelve acres of land granted to John Galop, upon Nixes Island, to enjoy to him and his heirs forever, if the island be so much" [MBCR 1:179]. John Gallop was granted forty-nine acres in the a
1 CONC llotment at Rumney Marsh and Pullen Point in 1637 [BTR 1:27].
1 CONT    In his will, dated 20 December 1649 and proved 9 February 1649[/50], "John Gallop of Boston" made "my wife whole executor" and directed her to distribute the following bequests: to "my son John Gallop my now shallop"; to "my da
1 CONC ughter Joane" my heifer; "my two youngest sons shall employ my bark the first year after my decease wholly for their mother & after one year to have two thirds for themselves and one third for their mother; "my wife" to have the u
1 CONC se of livestock and after her death equally divided among "my two youngest sons Samuell Gollop & Nathaniel Gollop, if they are obedient children, but if they are rebellious, then my wife to have liberty to dispose of all as she sh
1 CONC all think good; if one son die before their mother, all to remain to the other; to "John Joy my daughter's son" £5 at age twenty-one and if he die then to "his brother Joseph"; 40s. to the building of the new meetinghouse [SPR Cas
1 CONC e #88].
1 CONT
1 CONT    The inventory of "John Gallupe" was taken 26 February 1649[/50] and totalled £311 10s. 8d., including real estate valued at £120: "one house and ground lying in Boston," £100; "Gallupe's Island containing about sixteen acres," 
1 CONC £12; and "four acres of meadow," £8 [SPR Case #88].
1 CONT
1 CONT    In her will, dated 24 July 1655 and proved 31 October 1655, "Christovell Gallop, being in perfect memory though weak in body," bequeathed to "my son John Gallop half my money which is about £15" with some moveables, including "
1 CONC a great Bible"; "half my wearing clothes I do give Hannah my son John Gallop's wife"; to "my daughter Joane Joy" the other half of the money, the other half of the wearing clothes, and other moveables; and to "my son Sam[ue]ll Gal
1 CONC lop & my son Nathaniell Gallop" the residue to be equally divided between them [SPR 1:136]. The inventory of the goods of "Christobell Gallop deceased" was taken about December 1655 (undated) and totalled £36 14s. including no rea
1 CONC l estate [SPR 3:35].
1 CONT
1 CONT BIRTH: By about 1593 based on date of marriage.
1 CONT
1 CONT DEATH: Boston January 1649/50 [BVR 29]. "Goodman Gallop is dead of a great griping in his bowels" (letter of Adam Winthrop to John Winthrop Jr. from Boston, 10 February 1649/50 [WP 6:17]).
1 CONT
1 CONT MARRIAGE: Bridport, Dorsetshire, 19 January 1617[/8] Christabell Brushett. "Christovell Gallopp the wife of our brother John Gallopp" was admitted to Boston church 22 June 1634 [BChR 18]. She died at Boston 27 September 1655 [BVR 
1 CONC 52].
1 CONT
1 CONT CHILDREN:
1 CONT
1 CONT     i   JOAN, bp. Bridport 20 September 1618; m. by 1637 Thomas Joy [TAG 68:13]. 
1 CONT
1 CONT     ii   JOHN, bp. Bridport 25 January 1620[/1]; m. by 1644 Hannah Lake, daughter of John and Margaret (Reade) Lake [TAG 68:13; Bethia Harris Anc 55; NEHGR 84:316]. 
1 CONT
1 CONT     iii   WILLIAM, bp. Bridport 4 August 1622; predeceased his father, evidently unmarried. 
1 CONT
1 CONT     iv   FRANCIS, bp. Bridport 27 July 1625; bur. there 18 November 162
1 CONT
1 CONT     v   SAMUEL, bp. Bridport 16 August 1629; m. Boston 20 January 1650[/1] Mary Phillips [BVR 31]. 
1 CONT
1 CONT     vi   NATHANIEL, bp. Bridport 16 August 1629; m. Boston 11 June 1652 "Margaret Eveley [Eveleth]" [BVR 38], daughter of Sylvester Eveleth [NEHGR 134:302]. 
1 CONT
1 CONT     vii   JOHN, bp. Bridport 11 July 1630; presumably died young, since John calls Samuel and Nathaniel his "two youngest sons." (See TAG 68:12 for further discussion on this son.) 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT COMMENTS: On 4 July 1632 John Winthrop wrote "I have much difficulty to keep John Galloppe here by reason his wife will not come. I marvel at the woman's weakness that she will live miserably with her children there, when she migh
1 CONC t live comfortably here with her husband. I pray persuade and further her coming by all means: if she will come let her have the remainder of his wages, if not, let it be bestowed to bring over his children, for so he desires: it 
1 CONC would be above £40 loss to him to come for her" [WP 3:87-88].
1 CONT
1 CONT    Later letters make it clear that John Gallop was a coastal trader, moving up and down from Maine to Connecticut, carrying goods for the Winthrops and others [WP 3:141, 156, 170, 275-76; 4:118, 190; 5:312]. In July of 1636 John 
1 CONC Gallop played a crucial role in the events leading up to the Pequot War. On a trading voyage to Long Island, Gallop came upon the boat of JOHN OLDHAM, which had been boarded by Indians, who killed Oldham. Gallop took countermeasur
1 CONC es, as a result of which several Indians were drowned [WJ 1:225-28].
1 CONT
1 CONT    He and his supplies were very gratefully received by Roger Williams at "New Prov~idence" about June 1637 [WP 3:433]. He saved the wife of Mononotto and the wounded Indians with her, coming "in a happy hour, to bring them victua
1 CONC ls, and to carry their wounded men to the pinnace where our chief surgeon was ..." during the Pequot war about July 1637 [WP 3:457].
1 CONT
1 CONT    William Hook at Agamenticus, 28 January 1639/40, spoke ill of him, by reference to "this poor man the bearer hereof which hath been much damnified by the master of Gallop's pinnace. Sir I leave the matter unto the party to rela
1 CONC te unto your worship. To my knowledge hath carried himself here amongst us very shamefully, the master and Gallope['s] son too, invited some of our neighbors aboard his pinnace and made them drunk and drunk themselves too" [WP 4:1
1 CONC 84].
1 CONT
1 CONT    Many sources state that John Gallop resided briefly in Dorchester but we may doubt that he ever lived there. The one reference in Dorchester records to a "Mr. Gallup" pertains to HUMPHREY GALLOP. The one record that may indicat
1 CONC e Dorchester residence is puzzling. As noted above, when he is made freeman John Gallop appears as the fourth name in a sequence of six men, all the rest of whom are from Dorchester. But Gallop had just been admitted to Boston chu
1 CONC rch a few months earlier, and he was certainly residing at Boston at this time. Perhaps he did set down in Dorchester for a time, but it cannot have been for long.
1 CONT
1 CONT BIBLIOGRAPHIC NOTE: In 1960 John Dorrance Morrell published some parish register extracts which demonstrated that the immigrant was from Bridport, Dorsetshire [TAG 36:108-09]. Gerald Parsons examined the same records more thorough
1 CONC ly and in 1993 published an article which extended and revised the work done by Morrell [TAG 68:11-13].
1 CONT
1 CONT    In 1950 Louis Effingham deForest prepared a comprehensive sketch of the immigrant, quoting at length from several sources [Bainbridge Anc 82-97 (the section on English ancestry has been superseded and should be ignored)]. In 19
1 CONC 66 the Gallup Family Association published a genealogy which treats the descendants of this immigrant [Darwin C. Gallup and Josephine Middleton Peck, Gallup Genealogy: Gallop, Galloup, Galloupe, Gallupe (n.p., 1966)]. 
0 @N6000000000491142578@ NOTE [Himes.Core.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT [143983.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Captain John Gallop, SR. was educated in military school in Holland and arrived in New England from Mosterne Dorsetshire in the MARY AND JOHN, 30 May 1630.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hi wife Christabel, son John, two other sons and a daughter came in the GRIFFIN, 04 Sept 1633.
1 CONT
1 CONT John Gallop came to America from the Parish of Mosterne, Co. Dorset, England in the year 1630. He sailed from Plymouth, England, March 20, 1630, in the ship "Mary and John," arriving at Nantucket, now Hull, May 30th.  His wife and
1 CONC  children following in 1633.  He went first to Dorchester, but was soon after a resident of Boston.  He was admitted to the First Church Jan 6, 1634.  He was made a freeman in April of the same year.  "He was one of the earliest g
1 CONC rantees of land at the northerly part of the town, where he had a wharf-right and house".  The locality was known by the name of Gallop's point, and was the southeast part of the peninsula, at the north end of the town.
1 CONT
1 CONT He owned Gallup's Island, where he had a snug farm, with a meadow on Long Island, a sheep pasture in ANix Maste, and a house in Boston.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the settlement of Rhode Island and Connecticut, his vessel furnished about the only means of communication between the two colonies.  At one time there was considerable anxiety in the Rhode Island colony, for John Gallup was
1 CONC  delayed in his trip.  Soon after, Roger Williams writes Governor Winthrop, beginning in this manner:  "God be praised John Gallop has arrived.
