0 @N6000000001539112043@ NOTE John Howland and Elizabeth Tilley were "Mayflower" Passengers
0 @N6000000002932571864@ NOTE [dporter.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the 13th Signer of the Mayflower Compact. !Information source:
1 CONT "Plymouth Colony', by Eugene Stratton, c. 1986.
1 CONT
1 CONT RELATIONSHIP:  File, "Howlan.pdg", downloaded from Compuserv (not
1 CONT verified).Note & Source:John Howland of Fenstanton, Huntingdonshire,
1 CONT England, a passenger on the famous ship Mayflower, which sailed from
1 CONT Plymouth, England in the autumn of 1620, was the indentured manservent
1 CONT of Mr. John Carver, a wealthy Londoner,  who became the first Governor
1 CONT of New Plimoth Colony in Massachusetts.On Nov.11,1620, as the ship lay
1 CONT at anchor in Cape Cod Bay, John Howland was the 13th. man to sign the
1 CONT MAYFLOWER COMPACT, the agreement which laid the foundation for the new
1 CONT town that the able-bodied men on board the Mayflower planned to create
1 CONT when the group landed in
1 CONT what was to become Plymouth, Massachusetts.
1 CONT
1 CONT (1) The son of Henry & Mararet Howland, John Howland was born about
1 CONT 1592 and grew up in Fenstanto, a town about nine miles northwest of
1 CONT Cambridge on the old Roman Road. No Baptismal record has been found
1 CONT for John Howland but was said to above 80 when
1 CONT he died in Rocky Nook, Kingston, near Plymouth, 23 Feb 1672. His
1 CONT Father Henry Howland, Yeoman, died in Fenstanton 17 May, 1635, and his
1 CONT mother, Mararet, was buried there 31 July,1629. The identity of this
1 CONT family is proved by the probate records of John's brother, Humphrey
1 CONT Howland, a draper, who settled in St.Swithin's Parish in London.
1 CONT Humphrey Howland ,In his will written in London 28 May,1646 and proved
1 CONT 10 July,1646 by his second wife, Anne. mentioned his brothers, Arthur,
1 CONT John Henry, his sister,Mararett, Wife of Richard Phillips of
1 CONT Fenstanton, shoemaker, his "nephew",Simon Howland,and his "niece,
1 CONT "Hannah Howland "Simon's  sister. Additional information about John
1 CONT Howland's
1 CONT family is found the records of the intestate estate of another brother
1 CONT George Howland, a merchant of St. Dunstan's,East London,who had died
1 CONT two years earlier,10 Feb,1643/4.His
1 CONT estate was administered by Humphry Howland's wife,Anne, 11 July
1 CONT 1646.Simon Howland was baptized in Fenstanton 19 Aug. 1604, called the
1 CONT "son of Henrye," and was probably The Simon Howland  who was
1 CONT apprenticed 19 March 1622, to Humphrey Howland,"citizen and draper of
1 CONT London." (2)
1 CONT
1 CONT REFERENCES (1)Mayflower Descendant(hereater MD)1:9Franklyn Howland,
1 CONT History of Arthur,Henry, and John Howland and their descendants (New
1 CONT Bedford, Mass.1885) ,315.(2):"English Reseach," The Howland
1 CONT Quarterly(Jan-Apr 1964)28:6-7, Fenstanton, "(July 1968)32:2-4,include
1 CONT a brother, Simon,in this family, but Simon Howland is called "nephew"
1 CONT in Humphrey Howland's will as ststed in "The Will of Humphrey Howland,
1 CONT " (June 1964) 28:2-3.
1 CONT
1 CONT John Howland's Will
1 CONT
1 CONT The Last Will and Testament of mr John howland of Plymouth late
1 CONT Deceased, exhibited to the Court held att Plymouth the fift Day of
1 CONT March Anno Dom 1672 on the oathes of mr Samuell ffuller and mr William
1 CONT Crow as followeth
1 CONT Know all men to whom these prsents shall Come That I John howland
1 CONT senir of the Towne of New Plymouth in the Collonie of New Plymouth in
1 CONT New England in America, this twenty ninth Day of May one thousand six
1 CONT hundred seaventy and two being of whole mind, and in Good and prfect
1 CONT memory and Remembrance praised be God; being now Grown aged; haveing
1 CONT many Infeirmities of body upon mee; and not Knowing how soon God will
1 CONT call mee out of this world, Doe make and ordaine these prsents to be
1 CONT my Testament Containing herein my last Will in manor and forme
1 CONT following;
1 CONT
1 CONT Imp I Will and bequeath my body to the Dust and my soule to God that
1 CONT Gave it in hopes of a Joyfull Resurrection unto Glory; and as
1 CONT Concerning my temporall estate, I Dispose thereof as followeth;
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I Doe give and bequeath unto John howland my eldest sonne besides
1 CONT what lands I have alreddy given him, all my Right and Interest To that
1 CONT one hundred acres of land graunted mee by the Court lying on the
1 CONT eastern side of Tauton River; between Teticutt
1 CONT and Taunton bounds and all the appurtenances and privilidges Therunto
1 CONT belonging, T belonge to him and his heirs and assignes for ever; and
1 CONT if that Tract should faile, then to have all my Right title and
1 CONT Interest by and in that Last Court graunt to mee in any other place,
1 CONT To belonge to him his heires and assignes for ever;
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my son Jabez howland all those my upland
1 CONT and Meadow That I now posesse at Satuckett and Paomett, and places
1 CONT adjacent, with all the appurtenances and privilidges, belonging
1 CONT therunto, and all my right title and Interest therin, To belonge to
1 CONT him his heires and assignes for ever,
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I Give and bequeath unto my son Jabez howland all that my one
1 CONT peece of land that I have lying on the southsyde of the Mill brooke,
1 CONT in the Towne of Plymouth aforsaid; be it more or lesse; and is on the
1 CONT Northsyde of a feild that is now Gyles Rickards senir To
1 CONT belonge to the said Jabez his heirs and assignes for ever;
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto Isacke howland my youngest sonne all
1 CONT those my uplands and meddows Devided and undivided with all the
1 CONT appurtenances and priviliges unto them belonging, lying and being in
1 CONT the Towne of Middlebery, and in a tract of Land Called the Majors
1 CONT Purchase near Namassakett Ponds; which I have bought and purchased of
1 CONT William White of Marshfeild in the Collonie of New Plymouth; which may
1 CONT or shall appeer by any Deed or writinges Together with the
1 CONT aformentioned prticulares To belonge to the said Isacke his heirs and
1 CONT assignes for ever;
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my said son Isacke howland the one halfe
1 CONT of my twelve acree lott of Meddow That I now have att Winnatucsett
1 CONT River within the Towne of Plymouth aforsaid To belonge to him and said
1 CONT Isacke howland his heires and assignes for
1 CONT ever,
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I Will and bequeath unto my Deare and loveing wife Elizabeth
1 CONT howland the use and benifitt of my now Dwelling house in Rockey nooke
1 CONT in the Township of Plymouth aforsaid, with the outhousing lands, That
1 CONT is uplands uplands [sic] and meddow lands and all
1 CONT appurtenances and privilidges therunto belonging in the Towne of
1 CONT Plymouth and all other Lands housing and meddowes that I have in the
1 CONT said Towne of Plymouth excepting what meddow and upland I have before
1 CONT given To my sonnes Jabez and Isacke howland During
1 CONT her naturall life to Injoy make use of and Improve for her benifitt
1 CONT and Comfort;
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my son Joseph howland after the Decease
1 CONT of my loveing wife Elizabeth howland my aforsaid Dwelling house att
1 CONT Rockey nooke together with all the outhousing uplands and Medowes
1 CONT appurtenances and privilidges belonging therunto; and all other
1 CONT housing uplands and meddowes appurtenances and privilidges That I have
1 CONT within the aforsaid Towne of New Plymouth excepting what lands and
1 CONT meadowes I have before Given To my two sonnes Jabez and Isacke; To
1 CONT belong to him the said Joseph howland To him and his heires and
1 CONT assignes for ever;
1 CONT
