0 @N6000000003490628289@ NOTE Passenger who fell off the Mayflower while crossing the Atlantic.  He had gone up on deck during a terrible storm and was swept overboard.  Luckily for him, there was some rigging dragging behind the ship and he was able to catch 
1 CONC hold of it and was then dragged back on board. He was John Carver's servant.  
1 CONT
1 CONT His Epitaph, featured in the book "Over Their Dead Bodies: Yankee Epitaphs & History", reads: 
1 CONT
1 CONT Here ended ye Pilgrimage of John Howland
1 CONT  who died Feby 23d 1672-3
1 CONT Aged above 80 yeares.  He married
1 CONT Elizabeth daur of John Tilley who
1 CONT came with him in ye shipp Mayflower
1 CONT Decr. 1620.  From them are
1 CONT descended numerous posterity.
1 CONT
1 CONT He presided as Magistrate over one of the two Plymouth Colony witch trials.  The wife of William Holmes, Standish's Lieutenant, was tried on the complaint of one Dinah Sylvester.
1 CONT "What evidence have you of the fact?" he asked the complaintent.
1 CONT "She appeared to me as a witch."
1 CONT "In what shape?"
1 CONT "In the shape of a bear, your honor."
1 CONT "How far off was the bear?"
1 CONT "About a stone's throw from the highway."
1 CONT "What manner of tail did the bear have?"
1 CONT " I could not tell, your honor, as his head was towards me."
1 CONT To discourage such nonsense, Dinah was fined 5 pounds and whipped.  And that was the end of witchcraft in Plymouth Colony.  (Saints and Strangers, pg 320)
