0 @N6000000000490901266@ NOTE [Himes.Core.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT [143983.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT HISTORY OF THE TOWN OF MIDDLEBORO
1 CONT John Howland was one of the passengers in the Mayflower.  He married Elizabeth Tilley, a daughter of John Tilley, of the Mayflower.  The Plymouth Colony Records speak of him as a "godly man, an ancient professor in the ways of Chr
1 CONC ist, and an instrument of good in his place." He was the last male survivor of those who came in the Mayflower, who remained in Plymouth.  He died March 5, 1673, at the age of eighty.  He was one of the persons for whose benefit t
1 CONC he Sixteen Shilling and the Purchade Purchases were made.  He probably sold that portion of his land included in theTwenty-six Men's Purchase before the breaking out of King Philip's War.  He left four sons; Isaac lived in Middleb
1 CONC oro.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: THE GREAT MIGRATION BEGINS   Vol 2  p 1021
1 CONT Origin:  Fenstanton, Huntingdonshire
1 CONT Migration:  1620 on Mayflower
1 CONT First Residence:  Plymouth
1 CONT Freeman:  1633 list of Plymouth Freemen John Howland is near the head of the list, among the councillors.
1 CONT Education:  Inventory listed many books.
1 CONT Held many offices.
1 CONT In charge of the fur trading post at Kennebec.  Committee on the fur trade, 3 October 1659.
1 CONT In the Plymouth section of the 1643 Plymouth Colony list of men able to bear arms.
1 CONT
1 CONT Birth:  Say 1592, son of Henry and Margaret ____Howland of Fenstanton.
1 CONT Death:  Plymouth 23 Feb 1672/3 "Above eighty years"
1 CONT Marriage:  Plymouth by about 1624 Elizabeth Tilley, baptized Henlow, Huntingdonshire, 30 Aug 1607, daughter of John Tilley.  She died at Swansea 22 Dec 1687, aged eighty.
1 CONT Brothers Henry Howland and Arthur Howland.
1 CONT Comments:  In his list of passengers on the Mayflower Bradford tells us that John Howland was one of the "manservants" of John Carver [Bradford 441].  During a particularly bad storm on the crossing John Howland (characterized by 
1 CONC Bradford as 'a lusty young man') went above deck and was swept overboard, but "it pleased God that he caught hold of the topsail halyards which hung overboard and ran out at length.  Yet he held his hold  (though he was sundry fat
1 CONC homs under water) till he was hauled up by the same rope to the brim of the water, and then with a boat hook and other means got into the ship again and his life saved.  And though he was something ill with it, yet he lived many y
1 CONC ears after and became a profitable member both in church and commonwealth. [Bradford 59].
1 CONT      In his 1651 accounting on the family of John Carver, Bradford reported that '[h]is servant John Howland married the daughter of John Tilley, Elizabeth, and they are both now living, and their eldest daughter hath four childre
1 CONC n; and their second daughter one, all living, and other of their children marriageable [Bradford 444].
1 CONT      In an undated deposition we learn that in April 1634 John Hocking came to Kennebec and challenged the rights of the Plymouth men to their exclusive trade in that place.  Mr. John Howland, in charge of the trading post, went o
1 CONC ut in their bark with several other men and warned Hocking off, but was taunted and defied.  Howland 'bid three of his men to cut his cable [Hocking's anchor]," but the flow of the stream Was too strong and Howland called them bac
1 CONC k and added Moses Talbot to the crew. Hocking, seeing that their intent Was to cut the cable, "presently put his peice almost to Moyses Talbott's head, which Mr. Howland seeing called to him desiring him not to shoot his man but t
1 CONC ake himself for his mark saying his men did but that which he commanded them and therefore desired him not to hurt any of them, if any wrong wa one it as himself that did it and therefore called again to him to take him for hi mar
1 CONC k saying he stood very fair, but Hocking would not hear not look towards our bark, but presently shooteth Moyses in the head, and presently took up his pistol in his hand but the Lord stayed him from doing any further hurt by a sh
1 CONC ot from our bark himself was presently struck dead being shot near the same place in the head where he had murderously shot Moyses" [MD 2:10-11]
1 CONT      [Bibliographic note:  Because of the multitude of descendants of John Howland, through all ten of his children, the publication of the first five generations of descent from John Howland will occupy many volumes.  Elizabeth P
1 CONC earson White has prepared the first two volumes in this series:  concerning the lives of John Howland s first child Desire Howland and second child John (2) Howland.]
1 CONT      In her first volume White argues that John Howland lived for several years in Maine, and that three of his children were born there.  Robert S. Wakefield has gathered the evidence that this could not have been the case. [MD 4
1 CONC 2:15-16].
1 CONT PLYMOUTH COLONY VITAL RECORDS
1 CONT      "The 23th of February 1672 Mr John Howland senir of the Towne of Plymouth Deceased; hee was a Godly man and an ancient professor in the wayes of Christ hee lived untill hee attained above eighty yeares in the world, hee was o
1 CONC ne of the first Comers into this land and proved a usefull instrument of Good in his place and was the last man that was left of those that Came over in the shipp Called the May Flower, that lived in Plymouth hee was with honor In
1 CONC terned att the Towne of Plymouth on the 25 of February 1672."
1 CONT      John Howland:  Records of Colony of New Plymouth (1633-1689)
1 CONT #136 Pre-1790 Census/tax records Plymouth Colony, MA 1632
1 CONT Plymouth Colony Its history and People 1620-1691 by Eugene Aubrey Stratton Ancestry Publishing, SLC, 1986
1 CONT Christening:  Mayflower Quarterly, 47: 146-147
1 CONT Plymouth Church Records, Abstracts of Bristol County Probate
1 CONT      Further information is in:  JOHN HOWLAND OF THE MAYFLOWER, VOL 2
1 CONT      Sources and proofs are listed with each family.  I have a copy of this book.
1 CONT CALEB JOHNSON'S WEB PAGE
1 CONT      * The traditional date that has been ascribed to John
1 CONT Howland's birth is "about 1592", and this has never really
1 CONT been questioned.  However, a birth about 1599 is clearly a
1 CONT better estimate for the following reasons:
1 CONT
1 CONT     John Howland is called a "manservant" in William
1 CONT     Bradford's passenger list.  Servants were contracted
1 CONT     out until the age of 25.  Thus, Howland must have been
1 CONT     under 25 in 1620, meaning he had to have been born
1 CONT     after 1595.  Since Howland signed the Mayflower
1 CONT     Compact, he must have been born sometime before
1 CONT     1600 to have been legally old enough to sign.
1 CONT     John Howland's wife was born in 1607, and it is most
1 CONT     unlikely that he, at the age of 32, married a 17 year
1 CONT     old girl as his first wife.  Most men married about age
1 CONT     25, and since his marriage occurred about 1624, this
1 CONT     would place his likely birth at 1599.
1 CONT     John Howland's last child was born in 1649.  If the
1 CONT     1592 date were accepted, he would have fathered a
1 CONT     child at the age of 57, a most unlikely circumstance.
1 CONT     William Bradford writes in that John Howland was a
1 CONT     "lusty young man" in 1620.  It is unlikely that Bradford
1 CONT     would call a 28-year old a "young man".  The only
1 CONT     other person Bradford called a "young man" in 1620
1 CONT     was John Alden, who was born in 1599.
0 @N6000000006348426486@ NOTE Source: Caleb Johnson: MayflowerHistory.com
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT John Howland was born about 1599, probably in Fenstanton, Huntington.  He came on the Mayflower in 1620 as a manservant for Governor John Carver. During the Mayflower's voyage, Howland fell overboard during a storm, and was almost
1 CONC  lost at sea--but luckily for his millions of descendants living today (including Presidents George Bush and George W. Bush, and Mrs. Theodore Roosevelt) he managed to grab ahold of the topsail halyards, giving the crew enough tim
1 CONC e to rescue him with a boathook.
1 CONT
1 CONT It has been traditionally reported that John Howland was born about 1592, based on his reported age at death in the Plymouth Church Records. However, ages at death were often overstated, and that is clearly the case here.  John Ho
1 CONC wland came as a servant for John Carver, which means he was under 25 years old at the time (i.e. he was born after 1595). William Bradford, in the falling-overboard incident, refers to Howland as a "lusty young man", a term that w
1 CONC ould not likely have applied to a 28-year old given that Bradford himself was only 30--Bradford did call 21-year old John Alden a "young man" though.  Howland's wife Elizabeth was born in 1607: a 32-year old marrying a 17-year old
1 CONC  is an unlikely circumstance.  Howland's last child was born in 1649: a 57-year old Howland would be an unlikely father.  All these taken together demonstrate that Howland's age was likely overstated by at least 5 years.  Since he
1 CONC  signed the Mayflower Compact, we can assume he was probably about 21 in 1620, so the best estimate for his birth would be about 1599.
1 CONT
1 CONT John Howland had several brothers who also came to New England, namely Henry Howland (an ancestor to both Presidents Richard Nixon and Gerald Ford) and Arthur Howland (an ancestor to Winston Churchill).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Will of John Howland
1 CONT 29 May 1672
1 CONT The Last Will and Testament of mr John howland of Plymouth late Deceased, exhibited to the Court held att Plymouth the fift Day of March Anno Dom 1672 on the oathes of mr Samuell ffuller and mr William Crow as followeth
1 CONT Know all men to whom these prsents shall Come That I John howland senir of the Towne of New Plymouth in the Collonie of New Plymouth in New England in America, this twenty ninth Day of May one thousand six hundred seaventy and two
1 CONC  being of whole mind, and in Good and prfect memory and Remembrance praised be God; being now Grown aged; haveing many Infeirmities of body upon mee; and not Knowing how soon God will call mee out of this world, Doe make and ordai
1 CONC ne these prsents to be my Testament Containing herein my last Will in manor and forme following;
1 CONT
1 CONT Imp I Will and bequeath my body to the Dust and my soule to God that Gave it in hopes of a Joyfull Resurrection unto Glory; and as Concerning my temporall estate, I Dispose thereof as followeth;
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I Doe give and bequeath unto John howland my eldest sonne besides what lands I have alreddy given him, all my Right and Interest To that one hundred acres of land graunted mee by the Court lying on the eastern side of Tauton 
1 CONC River; between Teticutt and Taunton bounds and all the appurtenances and privilidges Therunto belonging, T belonge to him and his heirs and assignes for ever; and if that Tract should faile, then to have all my Right title and Int
1 CONC erest by and in that Last Court graunt to mee in any other place, To belonge to him his heires and assignes for ever;
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my son Jabez howland all those my upland and Meadow That I now posesse at Satuckett and Pamet, and places adjacent, with all the appurtenances and privilidges, belonging therunto, and all my right tit
1 CONC le and Interest therin, To belonge to him his heires and assignes for ever,
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I Give and bequeath unto my son Jabez howland all that my one peece of land that I have lying on the southsyde of the Mill brooke, in the Towne of Plymouth aforsaid; be it more or lesse; and is on the Northsyde of a feild tha
1 CONC t is now Gyles Rickards senir To belonge to the said Jabez his heirs and assignes for ever;
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto Isacke howland my youngest sonne all those my uplands and meddows Devided and undivided with all the appurtenances and priviliges unto them belonging, lying and being in the Towne of Middlebery, and i
1 CONC n a tract of Land Called the Majors Purchase near Namassakett Ponds; which I have bought and purchased of William White of Marshfeild in the Collonie of New Plymouth; which may or shall appeer by any Deed or writinges Together wit
1 CONC h the aformentioned prticulares To belonge to the said Isacke his heirs and assignes for ever;
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my said son Isacke howland the one halfe of my twelve acree lott of Meddow That I now have att Winnatucsett River within the Towne of Plymouth aforsaid To belonge to him and said Isacke howland his he
1 CONC ires and assignes for ever;
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I Will and bequeath unto my Deare and loveing wife Elizabeth howland the use and benifitt of my now Dwelling house in Rockey nooke in the Township of Plymouth aforsaid, with the outhousing lands, That is uplands uplands [sic]
1 CONC  and meddow lands and all appurtenances and privilidges therunto belonging in the Towne of Plymouth and all other Lands housing and meddowes that I have in the said Towne of Plymouth excepting what meddow and upland I have before 
1 CONC given To my sonnes Jabez and Isacke howland During her naturall life to Injoy make use of and Improve for her benifitt and Comfort;
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my son Joseph howland after the Decease of my loveing wife Elizabeth howland my aforsaid Dwelling house att Rockey nooke together with all the outhousing uplands and Medowes appurtenances and privilid
1 CONC ges belonging therunto; and all other housing uplands and meddowes appurtenances and privilidges That I have within the aforsaid Towne of New Plymouth excepting what lands and meadowes I have before Given To my two sonnes Jabez an
1 CONC d Isacke; To belong to him the said Joseph howland To him and his heires and assignes for ever;
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my Daughter Desire Gorum twenty shillings
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath To my Daughter Hope Chipman twenty shillings
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my Daughter Elizabeth Dickenson twenty shillings
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my Daughter Lydia Browne twenty shillings
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give & bequeath to my Daughter Hannah Bosworth twenty shillings
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my Daughter Ruth Cushman twenty shillings
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I give to my Grandchild Elizabeth Howland The Daughter of my son John Howland twenty shillings
1 CONT
1 CONT Item my will is That these legacyes Given to my Daughters, be payed by my exequitrix in such species as shee thinketh meet;
1 CONT
1 CONT Item I will and bequeath unto my loveing wife Elizabeth Howland, my Debts and legacyes being first payed my whole estate: vis: lands houses goods Chattles; or any thing else that belongeth or appertaineth unto mee, undisposed of b
1 CONC e it either in Plymouth Duxburrow or Middlbery or any other place whatsoever; I Doe freely and absolutly give and bequeath it all to my Deare and loveing wife Elizabeth howland whom I Doe by these prsents, make ordaine and Constit
1 CONC ute to be the sole exequitrix of this my Last will and Testament to see the same truely and faithfully prformed according to the tenour therof; In witness whereof I the said John howland senir have heerunto sett my hand and seale 
1 CONC the aforsaid twenty ninth Day of May, one thousand six hundred seaventy and two 1672
1 CONT
1 CONT Signed and sealed in the
1 CONT prsence of Samuel ffuller John Howland
1 CONT William Crow And a seale
1 CONT
1 CONT The following is from:
1 CONT Robert Charles Anderson. The Great Migration Begins: Immigrants to New England, 1620-1633, vols. 1-3. Boston, Suffolk Co., MA: New England Historic Genealogical Society, 1995.
1 CONT
