0 @N6000000000437741776@ NOTE Alias:<ALIA> Mayflower /Passenger/ REFERENCE: 3334 Complete Mayflower Passenger List John Alden, John Carver, Richard Gardinar, Ellen More, Edward Tilley,Isa ac Allerton, Katherine (White) Carver, John Goodman, Jasper More,  Ann (
1 CONC Co oper) Tilley, Mary (Norris) Allerton, James Chilton, William Holbeck, Rich ard More, John Tilley,  Bartholomew Allerton, Mrs. Chilton, John Hooke, Ma ry More, Joan (Hurst) Tilley,  Remember Allerton, Mary Chilton, Stephen Ho pk
1 CONC ins,William Mullins, Elizabeth Tilley, Mary Allerton, Richard Clarke, E lizabeth (Fisher) Hopkins, Mrs. Alice Mullins, Thomas Tinker, wife, and so n, John Allerton, Francis Cooke, Constance Hopkins, Priscilla Mullins, Wil liam Tre
1 CONC vore, John Billington, John Cooke, Giles Hopkins, Joseph Mullin s, John Turner, and two sons, Eleanor Billington, Humility Cooper, Damar is Hopkins, Degory Priest,Richard Warren, John Billington, John Cracksto n, Oceanus Hopkins, 
1 CONC Solomon Prower, William White, Francis Billington, Jo hn Crackston, John Howland, John Rigsdale, Mrs. Susanna White, William Bra dford, Edward Doty, John Langmore, Alice Rigsdale, Resolved White, Dorot hy (May) Bradford, Francis E
1 CONC aton, William,Latham Thomas Rogers, Roger Wil der, William Brewster, Mrs. Sarah Eaton, Edward Leister, Joseph Rogers, Th omas Williams, Mrs. Mary Brewster, Samuel Eaton,Edmund Margesson, Henry S amson, Edward Winslow, Love Brewste
1 CONC r, Thomas English, Christopher Marti n, George Soule, Elizabeth (Barker) Winslow, Wrestling Brewster, Moses Fle tcher, Mary (Prower) Martin, Myles Standish, Gilbert Winslow,Richard Brit teridge, Edward Fuller, Desire Minter, Mrs. 
1 CONC Rose Standish, PeterBrowne, M rs. Fuller, Elias Story, "Mr. Ely", William Button, Samuel Fuller,Edwa rd Thompson, Dorothy, Carver's maidservant, Robert Carter, Samuel Fuller Biographical Summary John Howland was born about 1599, p
1 CONC robably in Fenstanton, Huntingto n.  He came on the Mayflower in 1620 as a manservant for GovernorJohn Car ver.  During the Mayflower's voyage, Howland fell overboard during astor m, and was almost lost at sea--but luckily for his
1 CONC  millions of descendan ts living today (including Presidents George Bush and George W. Bush, a nd Mrs. Theodore Roosevelt) he managed to grab ahold of the topsail halyar ds, giving the crew enough time to rescue him with a boathoo
1 CONC k. It has been traditionally reported that John Howland was born about 159 2, based on his reported ageat death in the Plymouth Church Records.  How ever, ages at death were often overstated, and that is clearly the case he re.  J
1 CONC ohn Howland came as a servantfor John Carver, which means he was un der 25 years old at the time (i.e. hewas born after 1595).  William Bradf ord, in the falling-overboard incident, refers to Howland as a "lusty you ng man", a ter
1 CONC m that would not likely have applied to a 28-year old giv en that Bradford himself was only 30--Bradford didcall 21-year old John A lden a "young man" though.  Howland's wife Elizabeth was born in 1607: a 3 2-year old marrying a 1
1 CONC 7-year old is an unlikely circumstance.  Howland 's last child was born in 1649: a 57-year old Howland would bean unlike ly father.  All these taken together demonstrate that Howland's agewas li kely overstated by at least 5 years
1 CONC .  Since he signed the Mayflower Compac t, we can assume he was probably about 21 in 1620, so the best estimate f or his birth would be about 1599. John Howland had several brothers who alsocame to New England, namely Hen ry Howla
1 CONC nd (an ancestor to both Presidents Richard Nixon and Gerald For d) and Arthur Howland (an ancestor to Winston Churchill).
0 @N6000000003877704993@ NOTE John Howland was the son of Henry Howland and was born about 1592/3. He died at Plymouth Colony, Massachusetts, February 23, 1672/3. Plymouth Colony records state:
1 CONT
1 CONT “The 23th of February Mr. John Howland Senir of the Towne of Plymouth Deceased…Hee lived until hee attained about eighty yeaes in the world…and was the last man that was left of those that Came over in the ship Called the May flow
1 CONC er, that lived in Plymouth hee was with honor Intered att the Towne of Plymouth on the 25 of February 1672.”
1 CONT
1 CONT The John Howland Memorial Stone
1 CONT Burial Hill, Plymouth, MA
1 CONT
1 CONT On Burial Hill is a monument to John Howland erected in 1897 with funds raised by Mrs. Joseph Howland. This replaces a stone erected about 1836 by John and Henry Howland of Providence, Rhode Island. The earlier stone was buried un
1 CONC der the new one. This earlier stone stated that John Howland’s wife was “a daughter of Governor Carver”, but after the discovery in 1856 of Governor William Bradford’s manuscript Of Plimoth Plantation, it was known that he married
1 CONC  Elizabeth Tilley, daughter of John and Joan Tilley who were also passengers of the Mayflower.
1 CONT
1 CONT John Howland boarded the Mayflower in England in September 1620, arrived in Provincetown Harbor, November 21, 1620 and, although called a man-servant of Governor Carver, he was the thirteenth signer of the Mayflower Compact in Ply
1 CONC mouth Harbor on December 21, 1620.
1 CONT
1 CONT Within a few years he married Elizabeth Tilley, built a house on First Street and gradually as land was allotted to each family, he acquired four acres on Watson’s Hill, Plymouth and considerable acreage in Duxbury. February 2, 16
1 CONC 38/9 he bought from John Jenny the property called Rocky Nook (Kingston). Some of this land is still owned by our Society.
1 CONT
1 CONT He served in the General court of Plymouth as Committeeman in 1637, 1639-1652 and as Deputy 1652, 1659, 1661-1668 and 1670.
1 CONT
1 CONT He had two brothers, Arthur and Henry who arrived a few years later. Arthur Howland married Margaret Reed, settled in Marshfield and had five children. Sir Winston Churchill, an honorary member of the Pilgrim John Howland Society,
1 CONC  was one of his descendants. Henry Howland married Mary (Newland) and lived in Duxbury. They had eight children. Both brothers joined the Society of Friends. For many generations the descendants of these two men remained Quakers, 
1 CONC many settled around Dartmouth, MA where they became very prosperous.
1 CONT
1 CONT TIMELINE
1 CONT
1 CONT 1620 - John Howland and Elizabeth arrive on the Mayflower.
1 CONT 1632 - They went to Maine.
1 CONT 1638/9 - Bought the Rocky Nook farm.
1 CONT 1670 - Jabez Howland bought the house at Plymouth. John and Elizabeth winter there.
1 CONT 1672/3 - John Howland died in the Plymouth home of his son.
1 CONT Circa 1675 - The Rocky Nook Farm house burned to the ground. Elizabeth makes her home with Jabez' family.
1 CONT 1680/1 - Jabez sells the Plymouth house. Elizabeth signed the deed and moved to Swansea to live with her daughter, Lydia Brown.
1 CONT 1687 - Elizabeth Tilley Howland died and was buried in the Brown Family plot.
0 @N6000000003490633142@ NOTE Deacon William Crocker (the first of this line in America) came to New England with Rev Mr. Lothrop and his church in 1639.  For a time he was in Roxbury, but soon settled in Barnstable.  He joind Mr Lothrop's church in Scituate i
1 CONC n 1636 and then came to Barnstable in 1639, among the first settlers of the town.  He built a frame house in Scituate in 1636, the forty-fourth in the town.  He was constable in 1644, grand juror in 1654, 55, 57, 61, 67, adn 75, s
1 CONC alesman in 1668, deputy to the General Court at plymouth in 1670, 71, and 74.  He was one of the leading citizens of his day, often employed in the business of the town and in settling estates.  He acquired a large landed estate a
1 CONC nd for many years was the richest man in the town.
