0 @N6000000000491151411@ NOTE [Himes.Core.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT [143983.GED]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Baptised in St. Mary's the Virgin Church, Henlow, Bedfordshire, Eng.  Re. Mayflower Quarterly v. 57:$:p342.
1 CONT
1 CONT Daughter Joan Rogers baptised at the same place 13 Mar 1567/8.
1 CONT
1 CONT John and Joan were m. same church and town, 20 Sep 1596 where John and his ancestors had been members for generations.  Records also show that Joan and John Tilley had the following children bapt. there.
1 CONT Ref: " Redding Family and It's Relatives" by Billie Redding Lewis, 1984, 3rd ed
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Mayflower Quarterly  Feb. 1996
1 CONT p. 56-58
1 CONT
1 CONT Mrs. John Tilley - Mystery Matron of the Mayflower
1 CONT .  It was not until 1976 that the Tilley records were discovered in the
1 CONT Henlow parish church proving that Elizabeth Tilley, the bride of John
1 CONT Howland, was the daughter of Joan Hurst.
1 CONT
1 CONT The records show that Joan was baptized March 3, 1567 and probably was born
1 CONT in Henlow, Bedfordshire, England.  Henlow was also the home of several other
1 CONT Mayflower passengers including John Tilley, his brother Edward and Edward's
1 CONT wife, Ann Cooper, plus Humility Cooper and Henry Samson.
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan was first married to Thomas Rogers in Henlow on June 18, 1594.  They
1 CONT had one child, John, who was baptized in Henlow May 26, 1594.  Thomas Rogers
1 CONT died about 1594/5 and Joan remarried fairly quickly, wedding John Tilley I
1 CONT Henlow on Sept. 20, 1596.
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan's parents were William Hurst (1530-1571) and Rose _______.  After her
1 CONT husband's death, Rose married John Rush on Feb. 4, 1571/2.  She was buried
1 CONT in Henlow July 23, 1601.
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan had two Hurst brothers, Henry, who was identified as "an ancient
1 CONT bachelor," and John, plus a sister, Agnes, who married William Albone.
1 CONT There was another sister Joan who died young.  Mother Rose and No. 2 husband
1 CONT John Rush had five children so Joan also had three half-brothers and two
1 CONT half-sisters.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nothing is known about Joan's early life in Henlow or how she and her second
1 CONT husband John Tilley became involved in the Pilgrim saga.  We do know that
1 CONT they left several children behind when they sailed on the Mayflower in 1620.
1 CONT The records show that Joan and John Tilley were the parents of :
1 CONT 1.  Rose, baptized in Henlow Oct. 23, 1597
1 CONT 2.  John, baptized in Henlow Aug. 26, 1599
1 CONT 3.  Rose, baptized in Henlow Feb. 28, 1601/2
1 CONT 4.  Robert, baptized in Henlow Nov. 25, 1604
1 CONT 5.  Elizabeth, baptized in Henlow Aug. 30, 1607
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan's older children may have been married when the Mayflower sailed in
1 CONT 1620 and preferred to remain at home in England.  Daughter Joan Rogers, if
1 CONT still alive, was 26.  John Tilley Jr. was 21.  Rose was 19 and Robet 16 and
1 CONT perhaps were left with relatives but they have, for the present, disappeared
1 CONT into history.
0 @N6000000003490628381@ NOTE Mayflower passenger
0 @N6000000006348426533@ NOTE John and Joan (Hurst) Tilley came on the Mayflower in 1620, bringing with them daughter Elizabeth.  Joan, along with her husband, died the first winter at Plymouth, orphaning their 13-year old daughter Elizabeth in the New World. 
1 CONC  Elizabeth would later marry to Mayflower passenger John Howland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan may have had an earlier marriage to a man named Rogers, but some researchers dispute this.
